window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class9-physics', board:'federal', title:'Physics', urdu:'طبیعیات', class:'Class 9', icon:'⚛️', desc:'FBISE Class 9 Physics — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch1', title:'Physical Quantities and Measurement', urdu:'طبیعی مقداریں اور پیمائش', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch1-l1', title:'Physical Quantities and SI Units', html:'<h3>Physical Quantities and SI Units</h3><p>A <strong>physical quantity</strong> is anything that can be measured, such as length, mass or time. Every measurement has a <strong>number</strong> and a <strong>unit</strong>. Physics uses the <strong>International System of Units (SI)</strong>, whose seven <strong>base units</strong> are: metre (m) for length, kilogram (kg) for mass, second (s) for time, ampere (A) for current, kelvin (K) for temperature, mole (mol) for amount of substance, and candela (cd) for luminous intensity. Quantities built from these, like speed (m/s) or force (newton = kg·m/s²), are <strong>derived quantities</strong>.</p><p>SI prefixes shrink or grow units: milli (10⁻³), centi (10⁻²), kilo (10³), mega (10⁶), micro (10⁻⁶), nano (10⁻⁹). So 1 km = 1000 m and 1 mm = 0.001 m.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch1-l2', title:'Measuring Instruments and Least Count', html:'<h3>Measuring Instruments and Least Count</h3><p><strong>Least count</strong> is the smallest measurement an instrument can make. A <strong>metre rule</strong> has least count 1 mm. A <strong>Vernier caliper</strong> measures to <strong>0.1 mm (0.01 cm)</strong>: reading = main-scale reading + (vernier division coinciding × 0.1 mm). A <strong>screw gauge / micrometer</strong> measures to <strong>0.01 mm</strong>: reading = pitch-scale reading + (circular-scale coincidence × 0.01 mm). Common error: forget the <strong>zero error</strong> — subtract it if positive, add it if negative.</p><p>Time is measured with a <strong>stopwatch</strong> (digital: 0.01 s). Volume of an irregular solid is found by <strong>displacement</strong> in a measuring cylinder: volume = final level − initial level.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch1-l3', title:'Precision, Accuracy and Significant Figures', html:'<h3>Precision, Accuracy and Significant Figures</h3><p><strong>Precision</strong> is how close repeated measurements are to each other; <strong>accuracy</strong> is how close they are to the true value. A faulty scale can be precise but not accurate. <strong>Significant figures</strong> are the reliably known digits: e.g. 0.00450 has 3 significant figures (leading zeros never count; trailing zeros count only after a decimal point). A result cannot be more precise than the least precise measurement used.</p><p><strong>Error</strong> is unavoidable: <strong>systematic error</strong> (e.g. zero error, wrong calibration) shifts every reading the same way, while <strong>random error</strong> varies and is reduced by averaging repeats.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch1-l4', title:'Scalars and Vectors', html:'<h3>Scalars and Vectors</h3><p>A <strong>scalar</strong> has magnitude only (distance, speed, mass, energy, temperature). A <strong>vector</strong> has magnitude <em>and</em> direction (displacement, velocity, force, weight). Vectors are drawn as arrows: length = magnitude, arrowhead = direction. <strong>Adding vectors:</strong> place them head-to-tail; the <strong>resultant</strong> runs from the first tail to the last head. Vectors at right angles combine by Pythagoras: R = √(A² + B²). Multiplying a vector by a number scales its length (negative reverses direction).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'How many base units are there in the SI system?', options:['5','6','7','9'], answer:2, explain:'SI has 7 base units: m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd.'},
{q:'The SI unit of mass is the:', options:['gram','pound','kilogram','tonne'], answer:2, explain:'The kilogram (kg) is the SI base unit of mass.'},
{q:'The least count of a Vernier caliper is:', options:['1 mm','0.5 mm','0.1 mm','0.01 mm'], answer:2, explain:'A Vernier caliper reads to 0.1 mm (0.01 cm).'},
{q:'The least count of a screw gauge is:', options:['1 mm','0.1 mm','0.01 mm','0.001 mm'], answer:2, explain:'A screw gauge / micrometer screw reads to 0.01 mm.'},
{q:'Which of the following is a derived quantity?', options:['length','time','force','mass'], answer:2, explain:'Force (newton = kg·m/s²) is derived from base units.'},
{q:'1 microsecond is equal to:', options:['10⁻³ s','10⁻⁶ s','10⁻⁹ s','10³ s'], answer:1, explain:'The prefix micro means 10⁻⁶.'},
{q:'How many significant figures are in 0.00450?', options:['2','3','4','5'], answer:1, explain:'Leading zeros do not count; the trailing zero after the decimal counts, giving 3.'},
{q:'Precision refers to:', options:['closeness to the true value','closeness of repeated readings to each other','size of the instrument','number of digits'], answer:1, explain:'Precision is repeatability; accuracy is closeness to the true value.'},
{q:'Which of these is a vector quantity?', options:['speed','distance','velocity','temperature'], answer:2, explain:'Velocity has both magnitude and direction, so it is a vector.'},
{q:'Zero error of an instrument is an example of:', options:['random error','systematic error','human error','no error'], answer:1, explain:'Zero error shifts every reading the same way, so it is systematic.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch2', title:'Kinematics', urdu:'حرکت شناسی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch2-l1', title:'Rest, Motion and Types of Motion', html:'<h3>Rest, Motion and Types of Motion</h3><p>A body is at <strong>rest</strong> if its position does not change with time, and in <strong>motion</strong> if it changes. Rest and motion are <strong>relative</strong>: a passenger in a moving bus is at rest relative to the bus but in motion relative to the road. <strong>Translatory motion</strong> moves the whole body from one place to another (a car); <strong>rotatory motion</strong> spins about an axis (a fan blade); <strong>vibratory motion</strong> oscillates about a mean position (a swing). Motion can be a combination, like a rolling wheel.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch2-l2', title:'Distance, Displacement, Speed and Velocity', html:'<h3>Distance, Displacement, Speed and Velocity</h3><p><strong>Distance</strong> is the total path length (scalar); <strong>displacement</strong> is the straight-line change of position from start to finish (vector). Round-trip distance can be large while displacement is zero. <strong>Speed</strong> = distance ÷ time (m/s). <strong>Velocity</strong> = displacement ÷ time, with direction. <strong>Acceleration</strong> = change in velocity ÷ time, <strong>a = (v − u)/t</strong>, in m/s². Deceleration (retardation) is negative acceleration.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> a car goes from rest to 20 m/s in 4 s: a = (20 − 0)/4 = 5 m/s².</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch2-l3', title:'Equations of Motion', html:'<h3>Equations of Motion</h3><p>For motion with <strong>uniform (constant) acceleration</strong>, three equations link initial velocity u, final velocity v, acceleration a, time t and displacement S:</p><p style="text-align:center"><strong>v = u + at</strong> &nbsp;•&nbsp; <strong>S = ut + ½at²</strong> &nbsp;•&nbsp; <strong>v² = u² + 2aS</strong></p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> a ball dropped from rest (u = 0) falls with a = 10 m/s² for 2 s. v = 0 + 10×2 = 20 m/s; S = 0 + ½×10×4 = 20 m. Freely falling bodies accelerate at <strong>g ≈ 10 m/s²</strong> downward (9.8 m/s² in precise work).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch2-l4', title:'Motion Graphs', html:'<h3>Motion Graphs</h3><p>A <strong>distance–time graph</strong> for uniform motion is a straight line whose <strong>slope = speed</strong>; a horizontal line means rest, and a curve means changing speed. A <strong>speed–time (velocity–time) graph</strong> is richer: its <strong>slope = acceleration</strong>, and the <strong>area under the graph = distance/displacement</strong>. A horizontal velocity–time line means uniform velocity (zero acceleration); a line through the origin means uniform acceleration from rest.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Displacement is a:', options:['scalar quantity','vector quantity','unit of speed','form of energy'], answer:1, explain:'Displacement has magnitude and direction, so it is a vector.'},
{q:'A car completes one lap of a circular track and returns to the start. Its displacement is:', options:['equal to the track length','twice the track length','zero','equal to the diameter'], answer:2, explain:'Start and finish positions coincide, so displacement is zero.'},
{q:'The SI unit of acceleration is:', options:['m/s','m/s²','m²/s','N'], answer:1, explain:'Acceleration = velocity change ÷ time, so m/s².'},
{q:'Which equation gives displacement with uniform acceleration?', options:['v = u + at','S = ut + ½at²','v² = u² + 2aS','a = (v−u)/t'], answer:1, explain:'S = ut + ½at² links displacement, time and acceleration.'},
{q:'A body falls freely from rest for 2 s (g = 10 m/s²). Its speed is:', options:['10 m/s','20 m/s','40 m/s','5 m/s'], answer:1, explain:'v = u + at = 0 + 10×2 = 20 m/s.'},
{q:'On a velocity–time graph, the area under the line gives:', options:['acceleration','force','displacement','velocity'], answer:2, explain:'Area under a v–t graph = velocity × time = displacement.'},
{q:'On a distance–time graph, the slope gives:', options:['acceleration','speed','force','displacement'], answer:1, explain:'Slope = distance ÷ time = speed.'},
{q:'A horizontal line on a velocity–time graph means:', options:['increasing speed','rest','uniform velocity','uniform acceleration'], answer:2, explain:'Constant velocity means zero slope, hence zero acceleration.'},
{q:'If v² = u² + 2aS, with u = 0, a = 10, S = 20, then v equals:', options:['10 m/s','20 m/s','200 m/s','400 m/s'], answer:1, explain:'v² = 0 + 2×10×20 = 400, so v = 20 m/s.'},
{q:'Rest and motion are:', options:['absolute','relative','the same thing','impossible together'], answer:1, explain:'Whether a body is at rest depends on the observer’s frame of reference.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch3', title:'Dynamics - I', urdu:'قوت شناسی اول', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch3-l1', title:'Force, Inertia and Newton’s First Law', html:'<h3>Force, Inertia and Newton’s First Law</h3><p>A <strong>force</strong> is a push or pull that can change a body’s state of rest, motion, shape or direction. SI unit: the <strong>newton (N)</strong>. <strong>Inertia</strong> is the tendency of a body to resist change in its state: a book stays put until pushed. <strong>Newton’s first law:</strong> a body remains at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless acted on by a net external force. This is why seatbelts matter — in a sudden stop your body keeps moving forward by inertia.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch3-l2', title:'Newton’s Second Law and Momentum', html:'<h3>Newton’s Second Law and Momentum</h3><p><strong>Momentum</strong> p = mass × velocity (<strong>p = mv</strong>), in kg·m/s. <strong>Newton’s second law:</strong> the rate of change of momentum of a body is proportional to the applied force, giving <strong>F = ma</strong>. So 1 N accelerates 1 kg at 1 m/s². <strong>Example:</strong> a 2 kg mass accelerated at 3 m/s² needs F = 2 × 3 = 6 N. Heavier bodies and faster bodies have more momentum, which is why a truck is harder to stop than a bicycle at the same speed.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch3-l3', title:'Newton’s Third Law', html:'<h3>Newton’s Third Law</h3><p><strong>Newton’s third law:</strong> to every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. The two forces act on <strong>different bodies</strong>, so they never cancel. Examples: a rocket pushes exhaust gases down; the gases push the rocket up. You push the ground backward when walking; the ground pushes you forward. A book on a table pushes down on the table; the table pushes up on the book (the normal force).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch3-l4', title:'Friction', html:'<h3>Friction</h3><p><strong>Friction</strong> opposes relative motion between surfaces in contact. <strong>Static friction</strong> acts before motion starts; <strong>kinetic (sliding) friction</strong> acts during motion and is smaller; <strong>rolling friction</strong> is smaller still. Friction lets us walk and grip, but wastes energy as heat in machines. It is reduced with <strong>lubricants</strong> (oil, grease) and <strong>ball bearings</strong>, and increased with rough surfaces or treads (shoe soles, tyres).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of force is the:', options:['joule','newton','watt','pascal'], answer:1, explain:'Force is measured in newtons (N).'},
{q:'According to F = ma, a 5 kg mass needs what force for 2 m/s² acceleration?', options:['2.5 N','7 N','10 N','25 N'], answer:2, explain:'F = 5 × 2 = 10 N.'},
{q:'Inertia is the tendency of a body to:', options:['speed up','resist change in its state of motion','fall down','heat up'], answer:1, explain:'Inertia opposes any change in rest or uniform motion.'},
{q:'Newton’s first law is also called the law of:', options:['action–reaction','inertia','gravitation','moments'], answer:1, explain:'The first law describes inertia.'},
{q:'The momentum of a 4 kg object moving at 3 m/s is:', options:['7 kg·m/s','12 kg·m/s','1.33 kg·m/s','0.75 kg·m/s'], answer:1, explain:'p = mv = 4 × 3 = 12 kg·m/s.'},
{q:'In Newton’s third law, action and reaction act on:', options:['the same body','different bodies','no body','only the Earth'], answer:1, explain:'The pair acts on two different interacting bodies.'},
{q:'Which friction is the smallest?', options:['static','sliding','rolling','limiting'], answer:2, explain:'Rolling friction is much smaller than sliding or static friction.'},
{q:'Friction can be reduced by:', options:['roughening surfaces','using lubricants','increasing weight','removing wheels'], answer:1, explain:'Lubricants and ball bearings reduce friction.'},
{q:'A rocket moves forward because:', options:['air pushes it','exhaust gases push back on it (reaction)','it is light','of gravity'], answer:1, explain:'Expelled gases push down; the reaction pushes the rocket up.'},
{q:'Seatbelts protect passengers mainly because of:', options:['friction','inertia','gravity','air pressure'], answer:1, explain:'By inertia the body keeps moving forward when the car stops suddenly.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch4', title:'Dynamics - II', urdu:'قوت شناسی دوم', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch4-l1', title:'Moment of Force (Torque)', html:'<h3>Moment of Force (Torque)</h3><p>The <strong>moment of a force</strong> (torque) is its turning effect about a pivot: <strong>τ = F × d</strong>, where d is the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of force. SI unit: <strong>newton-metre (N·m)</strong>. A longer spanner turns a bolt more easily because d is larger. Clockwise and anticlockwise moments <strong>balance</strong> when equal: a see-saw balances when F₁d₁ = F₂d₂. <strong>Example:</strong> 50 N at 0.4 m gives τ = 20 N·m.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch4-l2', title:'Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity', html:'<h3>Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity</h3><p>The <strong>centre of mass</strong> is the point where the whole mass of a body can be considered concentrated. For uniform regular objects it is the geometric centre. <strong>Centre of gravity</strong> is the point where the whole weight acts — practically the same point near the Earth’s surface. It can be found for a flat lamina by <strong>plumb-line suspension</strong>: hang it from two points and mark the verticals; their intersection is the centre of gravity.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch4-l3', title:'Equilibrium and Stability', html:'<h3>Equilibrium and Stability</h3><p>A body is in <strong>equilibrium</strong> when the net force <em>and</em> net turning effect on it are both zero. <strong>First condition:</strong> ΣF = 0. <strong>Second condition:</strong> Στ = 0 (clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments). Stability depends on the centre of gravity: <strong>stable</strong> (centre of gravity rises if tilted — returns), <strong>unstable</strong> (centre of gravity falls — topples), <strong>neutral</strong> (no change — a ball on flat ground). A racing car is stable because its centre of gravity is low and its base is wide.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch4-l4', title:'Gravitation', html:'<h3>Gravitation</h3><p><strong>Newton’s law of gravitation:</strong> every mass attracts every other with F = G·m₁m₂/r², where <strong>G = 6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²</strong>. Near Earth this gives <strong>g ≈ 10 m/s²</strong>; g decreases with altitude and depth. <strong>Weight</strong> W = mg is the gravitational force on a body — unlike mass, it changes from planet to planet. Satellites stay in orbit because gravity provides the centripetal pull while their forward motion keeps them falling <em>around</em> the Earth, which is why astronauts feel <strong>weightless</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The moment of a force is calculated as:', options:['F + d','F × d','F ÷ d','F − d'], answer:1, explain:'Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot.'},
{q:'The SI unit of moment of force is:', options:['N','N·m','J','Pa'], answer:1, explain:'Moment is measured in newton-metres.'},
{q:'A see-saw balances when:', options:['both sides weigh the same','clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments','one side is heavier','it is level'], answer:1, explain:'Rotational equilibrium needs Στ = 0.'},
{q:'The centre of gravity of a uniform metre rod is at:', options:['0 cm','25 cm','50 cm','100 cm'], answer:2, explain:'For a uniform regular body it is at the geometric centre.'},
{q:'A racing car is very stable because it has:', options:['a high centre of gravity','a low centre of gravity and wide base','small wheels','a powerful engine'], answer:1, explain:'Low centre of gravity plus wide base gives high stability.'},
{q:'The value of the gravitational constant G is about:', options:['6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²','9.8 N/kg','3 × 10⁸ m/s','1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C'], answer:0, explain:'G = 6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg².'},
{q:'Weight differs from mass because weight:', options:['is measured in kg','depends on gravity','never changes','is a scalar'], answer:1, explain:'W = mg, so weight changes where g changes; mass does not.'},
{q:'Astronauts in orbit feel weightless because:', options:['there is no gravity','they are in continuous free fall around Earth','they wear special suits','the air is thin'], answer:1, explain:'Orbit is perpetual free fall; gravity still acts as the centripetal force.'},
{q:'The second condition of equilibrium states:', options:['net force is zero','net moment is zero','velocity is zero','mass is zero'], answer:1, explain:'Both ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0 are needed for complete equilibrium.'},
{q:'60 N applied 0.5 m from a pivot produces a moment of:', options:['30 N·m','60 N·m','120 N·m','0.5 N·m'], answer:0, explain:'τ = 60 × 0.5 = 30 N·m.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch5', title:'Pressure and Deformation in Solids', urdu:'دباؤ اور ٹھوس اجسام کی شکل میں تبدیلی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch5-l1', title:'Pressure', html:'<h3>Pressure</h3><p><strong>Pressure</strong> is force per unit area: <strong>P = F/A</strong>, in <strong>pascals (Pa)</strong>, where 1 Pa = 1 N/m². A sharp knife cuts because its tiny edge area concentrates force into huge pressure. <strong>Example:</strong> a 600 N person on one foot of area 0.02 m² exerts P = 600 ÷ 0.02 = 30,000 Pa. Lying on a bed of nails is survivable because the force spreads over hundreds of points, keeping pressure low.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch5-l2', title:'Pressure in Fluids and Atmospheric Pressure', html:'<h3>Pressure in Fluids and Atmospheric Pressure</h3><p>Fluids exert pressure in all directions, increasing with depth: P = ρgh. <strong>Pascal’s law:</strong> pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted <strong>equally in all directions</strong> — the principle of the <strong>hydraulic press, hydraulic lift and car brakes</strong>. The air’s weight gives <strong>atmospheric pressure ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa (1 atm)</strong> at sea level, measured by a <strong>barometer</strong>. It falls with altitude, so water boils below 100 °C on high mountains.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch5-l3', title:'Deformation: Stress, Strain and Hooke’s Law', html:'<h3>Deformation: Stress, Strain and Hooke’s Law</h3><p>Forces deform solids: <strong>stress</strong> = force ÷ area (Pa), <strong>strain</strong> = extension ÷ original length (no units). <strong>Hooke’s law:</strong> within the <strong>elastic limit</strong>, extension is directly proportional to the stretching force — <strong>F = kx</strong>, where k is the spring constant (N/m). Remove the load and the solid springs back; beyond the elastic limit it stays permanently deformed. <strong>Young’s modulus</strong> E = stress ÷ strain measures stiffness — steel’s is far higher than rubber’s.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of pressure is the:', options:['newton','pascal','joule','watt'], answer:1, explain:'1 Pa = 1 N/m².'},
{q:'Pressure is defined as:', options:['force × area','force ÷ area','mass ÷ volume','force × distance'], answer:1, explain:'P = F/A.'},
{q:'A hydraulic lift works on:', options:['Hooke’s law','Pascal’s law','Archimedes’ principle','Ohm’s law'], answer:1, explain:'Pascal’s law: enclosed-fluid pressure is transmitted equally.'},
{q:'Atmospheric pressure at sea level is about:', options:['1.01 × 10³ Pa','1.01 × 10⁵ Pa','1.01 × 10⁷ Pa','101 Pa'], answer:1, explain:'Standard atmosphere ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.'},
{q:'Atmospheric pressure is measured with a:', options:['thermometer','barometer','ammeter','hydrometer'], answer:1, explain:'A barometer measures air pressure.'},
{q:'Hooke’s law states that within the elastic limit:', options:['stress equals strain','extension is proportional to force','pressure equals force','strain has units'], answer:1, explain:'F = kx: extension ∝ applied force.'},
{q:'Strain is defined as:', options:['force ÷ area','extension ÷ original length','stress × strain','force × area'], answer:1, explain:'Strain is a ratio of lengths, so it has no unit.'},
{q:'Young’s modulus is the ratio of:', options:['force to extension','stress to strain','pressure to volume','mass to volume'], answer:1, explain:'E = stress ÷ strain.'},
{q:'A sharp knife cuts easily because:', options:['it is heavy','its small edge area gives large pressure','steel is strong','it is long'], answer:1, explain:'P = F/A: tiny area means huge pressure for the same force.'},
{q:'Beyond the elastic limit, a stretched wire:', options:['returns fully','stays permanently deformed','breaks into gas','cools down'], answer:1, explain:'Deformation beyond the elastic limit is permanent (plastic).'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch6', title:'Work and Energy', urdu:'کام اور توانائی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch6-l1', title:'Work', html:'<h3>Work</h3><p>In physics, <strong>work</strong> is done when a force moves a body in the force’s direction: <strong>W = F × d</strong>. SI unit: the <strong>joule (J)</strong> = 1 N·m. No displacement, no work — holding a heavy bag still does no mechanical work, however tiring. Work is <strong>positive</strong> when force and displacement align (lifting), <strong>negative</strong> when they oppose (friction). <strong>Example:</strong> pushing with 50 N over 4 m does W = 200 J.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch6-l2', title:'Kinetic and Potential Energy', html:'<h3>Kinetic and Potential Energy</h3><p><strong>Energy</strong> is the capacity to do work, also in joules. <strong>Kinetic energy</strong> of motion: <strong>K.E. = ½mv²</strong>. <strong>Potential energy</strong> of position: gravitational <strong>P.E. = mgh</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> a 2 kg ball at 10 m height has P.E. = 2 × 10 × 10 = 200 J; dropped, it converts to K.E. <strong>Conservation of energy:</strong> energy is never created or destroyed, only transformed — the falling ball’s lost P.E. exactly equals its gained K.E. (ignoring air resistance).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch6-l3', title:'Power and Efficiency', html:'<h3>Power and Efficiency</h3><p><strong>Power</strong> is the rate of doing work: <strong>P = W/t</strong>, in <strong>watts (W)</strong> = 1 J/s. A 100 W bulb uses 100 J every second. 1 horsepower ≈ 746 W. <strong>Efficiency</strong> = (useful energy output ÷ total energy input) × 100%. No machine is 100% efficient — some energy always becomes waste heat through friction. <strong>Example:</strong> a motor giving 800 J of useful work from 1000 J input is 80% efficient.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch6-l4', title:'Forms and Sources of Energy', html:'<h3>Forms and Sources of Energy</h3><p>Energy forms include <strong>mechanical, heat, light, sound, electrical, chemical</strong> (food, fuels) and <strong>nuclear</strong>. <strong>Renewable sources</strong> — solar, wind, hydro, biomass, geothermal — replenish naturally; <strong>non-renewable</strong> fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas) took millions of years to form and release CO₂ when burnt. Pakistan’s Tarbela and Mangla dams harvest hydroelectric energy: falling water’s K.E. spins turbines that generate electricity.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of work is the:', options:['watt','joule','newton','pascal'], answer:1, explain:'1 J = 1 N·m.'},
{q:'Work is calculated as:', options:['F ÷ d','F × d','m × v','P × t'], answer:1, explain:'W = force × displacement in the force’s direction.'},
{q:'Kinetic energy of a 2 kg mass moving at 4 m/s is:', options:['8 J','16 J','32 J','4 J'], answer:1, explain:'K.E. = ½ × 2 × 16 = 16 J.'},
{q:'Potential energy of a 5 kg object at 2 m height (g = 10) is:', options:['10 J','50 J','100 J','25 J'], answer:2, explain:'P.E. = mgh = 5 × 10 × 2 = 100 J.'},
{q:'Power is defined as:', options:['work × time','work ÷ time','force × velocity','energy × time'], answer:1, explain:'P = W/t, measured in watts.'},
{q:'100 W means:', options:['100 J of work done','100 J used per second','100 N of force','100 m moved'], answer:1, explain:'1 watt = 1 joule per second.'},
{q:'A machine with 80% efficiency wastes:', options:['80% of input','20% of input','100% of input','0% of input'], answer:1, explain:'Useful output is 80%, so 20% is lost, mostly as heat.'},
{q:'Which is a renewable energy source?', options:['coal','natural gas','solar energy','petrol'], answer:2, explain:'Solar energy replenishes naturally; fossil fuels do not.'},
{q:'Holding a heavy suitcase still does:', options:['maximum work','no mechanical work','negative work','double work'], answer:1, explain:'No displacement means W = F × d = 0.'},
{q:'Energy can be:', options:['created from nothing','destroyed completely','transformed from one form to another','used up entirely'], answer:2, explain:'Conservation of energy: it only changes form.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch7', title:'Density and Temperature', urdu:'کثافت اور درجہ حرارت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch7-l1', title:'Density and Relative Density', html:'<h3>Density and Relative Density</h3><p><strong>Density</strong> ρ = mass ÷ volume (<strong>ρ = m/V</strong>), SI unit <strong>kg/m³</strong>. Water’s density is 1000 kg/m³. Objects denser than a fluid sink; less dense ones float — a steel ship floats because its <em>average</em> density, hull plus air, is less than water’s. <strong>Relative density</strong> = density of substance ÷ density of water; it has no units. It is measured with a <strong>relative-density bottle</strong> or hydrometer.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch7-l2', title:'Archimedes’ Principle and Flotation', html:'<h3>Archimedes’ Principle and Flotation</h3><p><strong>Archimedes’ principle:</strong> a body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward <strong>upthrust</strong> equal to the <strong>weight of the fluid it displaces</strong> — which is why you feel lighter in a swimming pool. <strong>Law of flotation:</strong> a floating body displaces a weight of fluid equal to its own weight. Submarines dive by flooding ballast tanks (increasing average density) and surface by blowing them out with air.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch7-l3', title:'Temperature and Thermometers', html:'<h3>Temperature and Thermometers</h3><p><strong>Temperature</strong> measures how hot or cold a body is — the average kinetic energy of its particles. Common scales: <strong>Celsius (°C)</strong> and <strong>kelvin (K)</strong>, related by <strong>K = °C + 273.15</strong> (so 0 °C = 273 K). A <strong>laboratory thermometer</strong> uses mercury or alcohol expansion; a <strong>clinical thermometer</strong> has a kink to hold the reading and ranges about 35–42 °C. Heat is energy in transit; temperature is its intensity.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of density is:', options:['kg/m³','g/cm³','N/m³','kg·m³'], answer:0, explain:'Density = mass ÷ volume, so kg/m³ in SI.'},
{q:'Relative density:', options:['has unit kg/m³','has no unit','is measured in newtons','equals weight'], answer:1, explain:'It is a ratio of two densities, so the units cancel.'},
{q:'A ship made of steel floats because:', options:['steel is lighter than water','its average density is less than water’s','engines hold it up','water is solid'], answer:1, explain:'The hollow hull makes average density less than water’s.'},
{q:'Archimedes’ principle states that upthrust equals:', options:['the weight of the body','the weight of displaced fluid','the volume of the body','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Buoyant force = weight of fluid pushed aside.'},
{q:'0 °C on the kelvin scale is:', options:['0 K','100 K','273 K','373 K'], answer:2, explain:'K = °C + 273, so 0 °C = 273 K.'},
{q:'Temperature is a measure of:', options:['total heat energy','average kinetic energy of particles','mass','pressure'], answer:1, explain:'Temperature reflects the average particle kinetic energy.'},
{q:'A clinical thermometer has a kink to:', options:['break easily','hold the mercury thread in place','measure faster','look nice'], answer:1, explain:'The constriction stops mercury falling back before reading.'},
{q:'A submarine surfaces by:', options:['firing engines','blowing water out of ballast tanks','dropping weight','heating the hull'], answer:1, explain:'Expelling water lowers average density below water’s.'},
{q:'The density of water is:', options:['100 kg/m³','1000 kg/m³','10,000 kg/m³','1 kg/m³'], answer:1, explain:'Water’s density is 1000 kg/m³ (1 g/cm³).'},
{q:'Heat and temperature differ because heat is:', options:['measured in kelvin','energy in transit','always 100 °C','a vector'], answer:1, explain:'Heat is energy flowing due to temperature difference; temperature is its measure.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch8', title:'Magnetism', urdu:'مقناطیسیت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch8-l1', title:'Magnets and Magnetic Materials', html:'<h3>Magnets and Magnetic Materials</h3><p>Every magnet has a <strong>north and south pole</strong>; like poles <strong>repel</strong>, unlike poles <strong>attract</strong>. A pole never exists alone — cutting a magnet gives two smaller magnets. <strong>Magnetic materials</strong> (iron, steel, nickel, cobalt) are attracted to magnets; <strong>non-magnetic</strong> ones (wood, plastic, copper) are not. <strong>Soft iron</strong> magnetises and demagnetises easily (used in electromagnets); <strong>steel</strong> keeps magnetism (permanent magnets).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch8-l2', title:'Magnetic Field and Field Lines', html:'<h3>Magnetic Field and Field Lines</h3><p>The <strong>magnetic field</strong> is the region around a magnet where its force acts. <strong>Field lines</strong> map it: they leave the north pole, enter the south pole, never cross, and are closest together where the field is <strong>strongest</strong> (near the poles). Sprinkle iron filings around a bar magnet and they trace these lines. A freely suspended magnet always points <strong>north–south</strong> — the principle of the <strong>compass</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch8-l3', title:'Domain Theory and Electromagnetism', html:'<h3>Domain Theory and Electromagnetism</h3><p><strong>Domain theory:</strong> magnetic materials contain tiny regions (<strong>domains</strong>) that act like mini-magnets; in unmagnetised iron they point randomly, while stroking with a magnet aligns them. A magnet loses magnetism by <strong>heating, hammering or dropping</strong>. An <strong>electromagnet</strong> is a coil around soft iron carrying current — its magnetism switches on and off with the current. Uses: electric bells, cranes lifting scrap iron, relays. <strong>Earth itself</strong> behaves like a giant magnet, which is why compass needles align north–south.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Like magnetic poles:', options:['attract','repel','have no effect','merge'], answer:1, explain:'Like poles repel; unlike poles attract.'},
{q:'Which material is magnetic?', options:['copper','aluminium','iron','wood'], answer:2, explain:'Iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are magnetic materials.'},
{q:'Magnetic field lines:', options:['cross each other','never cross','start at the south pole','are equally spaced'], answer:1, explain:'Field lines never cross; each point has one field direction.'},
{q:'Field lines are closest together where the field is:', options:['weakest','strongest','zero','changing'], answer:1, explain:'Line density shows field strength — densest near poles.'},
{q:'A compass needle points north–south because:', options:['of gravity','Earth acts like a giant magnet','of wind','of electric current'], answer:1, explain:'Earth’s magnetic field aligns the needle.'},
{q:'Soft iron is preferred for electromagnets because it:', options:['is cheap','magnetises and demagnetises easily','never rusts','is heavy'], answer:1, explain:'Temporary magnetism is ideal for switchable electromagnets.'},
{q:'A magnet can be demagnetised by:', options:['cooling','heating strongly','painting','wrapping'], answer:1, explain:'Heating, hammering or dropping disrupts domain alignment.'},
{q:'Cutting a bar magnet in half gives:', options:['one north and one south piece','two complete smaller magnets','no magnetism','only a north pole'], answer:1, explain:'Poles always come in pairs; each piece is a full magnet.'},
{q:'Electric bells and scrap cranes use:', options:['permanent magnets','electromagnets','compasses','batteries only'], answer:1, explain:'Switchable magnetism is needed in both.'},
{q:'According to domain theory, unmagnetised iron has domains that are:', options:['absent','aligned','randomly oriented','frozen'], answer:2, explain:'Random domains cancel out, giving no net magnetism.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch9', title:'Nature of Science and Physics', urdu:'سائنس اور طبیعیات کی ماہیت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch9-l1', title:'Science and the Scientific Method', html:'<h3>Science and the Scientific Method</h3><p><strong>Science</strong> is systematic knowledge gained through observation and experiment. The <strong>scientific method</strong> runs: observe → ask a question → form a <strong>hypothesis</strong> (testable explanation) → experiment → analyse data → conclude. A <strong>theory</strong> is a well-tested explanation (e.g. kinetic theory); a <strong>law</strong> describes what happens (e.g. Newton’s laws). Theories can be refined when new evidence arrives — that self-correction is science’s strength.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch9-l2', title:'Branches of Physics', html:'<h3>Branches of Physics</h3><p>Physics is the study of <strong>matter, energy and their interactions</strong>. Its branches: <strong>mechanics</strong> (motion and forces), <strong>thermodynamics</strong> (heat), <strong>electromagnetism</strong> (electricity, magnetism, light), <strong>optics</strong> (light behaviour), <strong>sound/acoustics</strong>, <strong>atomic and nuclear physics</strong> (atoms, nuclei, radioactivity), and <strong>quantum/particle physics</strong>. Physics underlies all engineering: bridges need mechanics, power stations need electromagnetism and thermodynamics.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-phy-ch9-l3', title:'Physics, Technology and Society', html:'<h3>Physics, Technology and Society</h3><p>Physics drives technology: electromagnetism gave us motors, generators and mobile phones; nuclear physics gave both power stations and weapons; optics gave lasers and fibre-optic internet. Muslim scientists laid foundations: <strong>Ibn al-Haytham</strong> founded experimental optics, <strong>Al-Khwarizmi’s</strong> algebra underpins physics maths, and <strong>Ibn Sina</strong> wrote on motion. Responsible use matters — the same nuclear physics can light cities or destroy them, so scientists and societies must weigh benefits against risks.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Physics is the study of:', options:['living things','matter, energy and their interactions','chemicals only','stars only'], answer:1, explain:'Physics covers matter, energy and how they interact.'},
{q:'A hypothesis is a:', options:['proven fact','testable explanation','random guess','law'], answer:1, explain:'A hypothesis must be testable by experiment.'},
{q:'Which branch of physics deals with heat?', options:['optics','thermodynamics','acoustics','mechanics'], answer:1, explain:'Thermodynamics studies heat and temperature.'},
{q:'Ibn al-Haytham is famous for work in:', options:['electricity','optics','nuclear physics','sound'], answer:1, explain:'He founded experimental optics with work on light and vision.'},
{q:'A scientific law:', options:['describes what happens','explains why in full detail','is a guess','never uses maths'], answer:0, explain:'Laws describe observed patterns; theories explain them.'},
{q:'The first step of the scientific method is:', options:['conclusion','hypothesis','observation','experiment'], answer:2, explain:'Careful observation raises the question to investigate.'},
{q:'Mobile phones and generators come from:', options:['mechanics','electromagnetism','optics','acoustics'], answer:1, explain:'Electromagnetism underlies electricity, motors and radio waves.'},
{q:'Which is a strength of science?', options:['never changing','self-correction with new evidence','ignoring experiments','avoiding maths'], answer:1, explain:'Science revises theories when better evidence appears.'},
{q:'The study of motion and forces is:', options:['thermodynamics','mechanics','optics','nuclear physics'], answer:1, explain:'Mechanics covers kinematics and dynamics.'},
{q:'Fibre-optic internet is an application of:', options:['sound','optics','magnetism','heat'], answer:1, explain:'It guides light through glass fibres — applied optics.'}
]}
]});

/* ================= CLASS 9 — CHEMISTRY (FBISE / NBF, 19 chapters) ================= */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class9-chemistry', board:'federal', title:'Chemistry', urdu:'کیمسٹری', class:'Class 9', icon:'🧪', desc:'FBISE Class 9 Chemistry — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch1', title:'Nature of Science in Chemistry', urdu:'کیمسٹری میں سائنس کی ماہیت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch1-l1', title:'What Chemistry Studies', html:'<h3>What Chemistry Studies</h3><p><strong>Chemistry</strong> is the study of <strong>matter</strong> — its composition, structure, properties and the changes it undergoes. Its branches: <strong>physical chemistry</strong> (energy and rates), <strong>organic chemistry</strong> (carbon compounds), <strong>inorganic chemistry</strong> (other elements), <strong>analytical chemistry</strong> (what is present, how much), <strong>biochemistry</strong> (chemistry of life), and <strong>environmental chemistry</strong>. Chemistry links to physics, biology and geology, and underpins medicines, fertilisers, plastics and fuels.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch1-l2', title:'The Scientific Method in Chemistry', html:'<h3>The Scientific Method in Chemistry</h3><p>Chemists work by the <strong>scientific method</strong>: make observations, ask questions, propose a <strong>hypothesis</strong>, design <strong>controlled experiments</strong> (changing one <strong>independent variable</strong> at a time while keeping others constant), record <strong>dependent-variable</strong> data, and draw conclusions. Repeatability builds confidence; a widely confirmed explanation becomes a <strong>theory</strong> (e.g. atomic theory), while a concise description of behaviour becomes a <strong>law</strong> (e.g. law of conservation of mass).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch1-l3', title:'Lab Safety and Measurement', html:'<h3>Lab Safety and Measurement</h3><p>Safe lab habits: wear <strong>goggles</strong>, never taste chemicals, waft vapours toward the nose, point test tubes away from people, and know the <strong>eyewash and fire extinguisher</strong> locations. Measurements use SI units: mass in <strong>kg/g</strong>, volume in <strong>dm³/cm³</strong>, temperature in <strong>°C/K</strong>. Every measurement carries <strong>uncertainty</strong>; chemists record to the instrument’s precision and keep results honest with correct <strong>significant figures</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Chemistry is the study of:', options:['living organisms','matter and its changes','forces and motion','the stars'], answer:1, explain:'Chemistry studies composition, structure, properties and changes of matter.'},
{q:'The study of carbon compounds is:', options:['inorganic chemistry','organic chemistry','physical chemistry','analytical chemistry'], answer:1, explain:'Organic chemistry deals with carbon-based compounds.'},
{q:'A testable explanation in science is a:', options:['law','theory','hypothesis','fact'], answer:2, explain:'A hypothesis is proposed and then tested by experiment.'},
{q:'In a controlled experiment, the scientist changes:', options:['all variables','one independent variable at a time','only the dependent variable','nothing'], answer:1, explain:'Only the independent variable changes; others are kept constant.'},
{q:'Which is a branch of chemistry?', options:['astrology','biochemistry','geology','zoology'], answer:1, explain:'Biochemistry studies the chemistry of living things.'},
{q:'In the lab you should never:', options:['wear goggles','taste chemicals','label bottles','wash hands'], answer:1, explain:'Tasting chemicals is strictly forbidden for safety.'},
{q:'The SI unit of volume commonly used in labs is:', options:['cm³','metre','newton','joule'], answer:0, explain:'Lab volumes are measured in cm³ (mL) or dm³ (L).'},
{q:'Wafting means:', options:['pouring quickly','gently waving vapours toward the nose','heating strongly','shaking hard'], answer:1, explain:'Wafting lets you smell vapours safely without inhaling directly.'},
{q:'A theory differs from a law because a theory:', options:['is a guess','explains why, backed by wide evidence','cannot be tested','is always wrong'], answer:1, explain:'Theories are well-confirmed explanations; laws describe behaviour.'},
{q:'Analytical chemistry answers:', options:['how fast reactions go','what is present and how much','how proteins fold','how plastics form'], answer:1, explain:'Analytical chemistry identifies substances and measures quantities.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch2', title:'Matter', urdu:'مادہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch2-l1', title:'States of Matter', html:'<h3>States of Matter</h3><p><strong>Matter</strong> is anything with mass and volume. Its three states: <strong>solid</strong> (fixed shape and volume, particles tightly packed and vibrating), <strong>liquid</strong> (fixed volume, takes container’s shape, particles slide past each other), <strong>gas</strong> (fills the container, particles far apart and fast). <strong>Kinetic particle theory:</strong> particles are always moving; hotter means faster. Heating curves flatten during melting and boiling because energy breaks bonds (<strong>latent heat</strong>) instead of raising temperature.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch2-l2', title:'Mixtures and Pure Substances', html:'<h3>Mixtures and Pure Substances</h3><p>A <strong>pure substance</strong> has fixed composition (an <strong>element</strong> like oxygen, or a <strong>compound</strong> like water). A <strong>mixture</strong> blends substances physically and can vary in composition: <strong>homogeneous</strong> (uniform, e.g. salt solution) or <strong>heterogeneous</strong> (e.g. sand and water). Mixtures are separated by <strong>physical methods</strong> — filtration, evaporation, distillation — while compounds need <strong>chemical change</strong> to split.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch2-l3', title:'Elements, Compounds and Changes', html:'<h3>Elements, Compounds and Changes</h3><p><strong>Elements</strong> are the simplest substances (118 known); <strong>compounds</strong> form when elements bond chemically in fixed ratios (H₂O is always 2 H : 1 O by atoms). A <strong>physical change</strong> (melting, dissolving) alters state or form, not composition; a <strong>chemical change</strong> (burning, rusting) makes new substances, shown by colour change, gas, precipitate or temperature change. <strong>Law of conservation of mass:</strong> mass is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Which is a pure substance?', options:['air','sea water','distilled water','brass'], answer:2, explain:'Distilled water is a compound with fixed composition.'},
{q:'Particles in a solid:', options:['move freely','vibrate about fixed positions','are far apart','do not exist'], answer:1, explain:'Solids have tightly packed particles that only vibrate.'},
{q:'A flat section on a heating curve during boiling shows:', options:['thermometer error','latent heat being absorbed','cooling','no energy input'], answer:1, explain:'Energy breaks intermolecular bonds without raising temperature.'},
{q:'Salt dissolved in water is a:', options:['compound','heterogeneous mixture','homogeneous mixture','element'], answer:2, explain:'The solution is uniform throughout.'},
{q:'Which separates a mixture?', options:['electrolysis','filtration','decomposition','burning'], answer:1, explain:'Filtration is a physical separation method.'},
{q:'Rusting of iron is a:', options:['physical change','chemical change','phase change','no change'], answer:1, explain:'A new substance (iron oxide) forms.'},
{q:'Water is a compound because:', options:['it can be filtered','hydrogen and oxygen bond in a fixed ratio','it is a liquid','it boils at 100 °C'], answer:1, explain:'Compounds have elements in fixed chemical ratios.'},
{q:'Law of conservation of mass means:', options:['mass increases in reactions','mass is conserved in reactions','gases have no mass','atoms disappear'], answer:1, explain:'Total mass of reactants equals total mass of products.'},
{q:'Which is an element?', options:['carbon dioxide','oxygen','sugar','steel'], answer:1, explain:'Oxygen is a pure element; the rest are compounds or mixtures.'},
{q:'Dissolving sugar in tea is a:', options:['chemical change','physical change','nuclear change','no change'], answer:1, explain:'No new substance forms; sugar can be recovered by evaporation.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch3', title:'Atomic Structure', urdu:'ایٹمی ساخت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch3-l1', title:'From Dalton to Rutherford', html:'<h3>From Dalton to Rutherford</h3><p><strong>Dalton</strong> proposed atoms as indivisible spheres. <strong>J.J. Thomson</strong> found the <strong>electron</strong> (negative) with cathode rays and suggested the plum-pudding model. <strong>Rutherford’s gold-foil experiment:</strong> most alpha particles passed straight through, but a few bounced back — proving the atom is mostly <strong>empty space</strong> with a tiny, dense, <strong>positive nucleus</strong> where electrons orbit. <strong>Protons</strong> (positive, in nucleus) and later <strong>neutrons</strong> (neutral, in nucleus) were identified; electrons occupy shells around the nucleus.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch3-l2', title:'Subatomic Particles and Isotopes', html:'<h3>Subatomic Particles and Isotopes</h3><p><strong>Atomic number (Z)</strong> = number of protons (defines the element). <strong>Mass number (A)</strong> = protons + neutrons. <strong>Isotopes</strong> are atoms of the same element with different neutron numbers, e.g. carbon-12 and carbon-14. Relative atomic mass is the <strong>weighted average</strong> of isotopic masses — chlorine is ~35.5 because of ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl. Isotope uses: carbon-14 in dating, cobalt-60 in cancer treatment, uranium-235 as fuel.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch3-l3', title:'Bohr Model and Electronic Configuration', html:'<h3>Bohr Model and Electronic Configuration</h3><p><strong>Bohr</strong> placed electrons in fixed <strong>shells</strong> (n = 1, 2, 3…) holding 2, 8, 8… electrons. <strong>Electronic configuration</strong> lists electrons per shell: sodium (11) = 2,8,1; chlorine (17) = 2,8,7. The outer-shell electrons are <strong>valence electrons</strong> — they decide bonding and reactivity: sodium loses 1 to form Na⁺, chlorine gains 1 to form Cl⁻. Elements in the same group share the same valence-electron count, hence similar chemistry.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Rutherford’s experiment proved the atom has a:', options:['solid core of electrons','tiny dense positive nucleus','uniform positive charge','no empty space'], answer:1, explain:'Alpha particles bouncing back revealed the small dense nucleus.'},
{q:'The atomic number of an element equals its number of:', options:['neutrons','protons','electrons + neutrons','nucleons'], answer:1, explain:'Z = proton count, which defines the element.'},
{q:'Isotopes of an element differ in:', options:['protons','electrons','neutrons','atomic number'], answer:2, explain:'Isotopes have the same Z but different neutron numbers.'},
{q:'The electronic configuration of sodium (Z = 11) is:', options:['2,8','2,8,1','2,7,2','2,8,2'], answer:1, explain:'11 electrons fill as 2 in shell 1, 8 in shell 2, 1 in shell 3.'},
{q:'Chlorine’s relative atomic mass is 35.5 because:', options:['it has 35.5 protons','it is a mixture of ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl isotopes','of experimental error','it loses electrons'], answer:1, explain:'Relative atomic mass is the weighted average of isotopic masses.'},
{q:'Who discovered the electron?', options:['Dalton','Thomson','Rutherford','Bohr'], answer:1, explain:'J.J. Thomson identified the electron using cathode rays.'},
{q:'Valence electrons are:', options:['inner-shell electrons','outer-shell electrons','protons','neutrons'], answer:1, explain:'Outer-shell electrons take part in bonding.'},
{q:'The mass number A equals:', options:['protons only','neutrons only','protons + neutrons','electrons + protons'], answer:2, explain:'A counts the nucleons in the nucleus.'},
{q:'Bohr’s model places electrons in:', options:['random clouds','fixed shells','the nucleus','pairs only'], answer:1, explain:'Bohr proposed fixed energy shells n = 1, 2, 3.'},
{q:'Carbon-14 is useful for:', options:['fuel','archaeological dating','cooking','batteries'], answer:1, explain:'Its steady decay rate dates ancient organic remains.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch4', title:'Periodic Table and Periodicity of Properties', urdu:'پیریڈک ٹیبل اور خصوصیات کی دوریت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch4-l1', title:'Development of the Periodic Table', html:'<h3>Development of the Periodic Table</h3><p><strong>Döbereiner’s triads</strong> and <strong>Newlands’ octaves</strong> first spotted patterns. <strong>Mendeleev</strong> arranged elements by <strong>atomic mass</strong>, leaving gaps for undiscovered elements and predicting their properties — a triumph when gallium and germanium matched his predictions. The <strong>modern periodic law</strong> arranges by <strong>atomic number</strong>: <strong>periods</strong> (7 rows = shells filled) and <strong>groups</strong> (18 columns = same valence electrons, similar chemistry).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch4-l2', title:'Groups, Periods and Blocks', html:'<h3>Groups, Periods and Blocks</h3><p><strong>Group 1 (alkali metals):</strong> soft, very reactive, form +1 ions. <strong>Group 2:</strong> alkaline earth metals, +2 ions. <strong>Groups 3–12:</strong> transition metals — hard, high melting points, coloured compounds, variable valency. <strong>Group 17 (halogens):</strong> reactive non-metals, −1 ions. <strong>Group 18 (noble gases):</strong> full outer shells, almost inert. <strong>Period 3</strong> runs Na → Ar: metallic character fades left to right as shells fill identically but nuclear charge grows.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch4-l3', title:'Periodic Trends', html:'<h3>Periodic Trends</h3><p><strong>Atomic size</strong> decreases across a period (stronger nuclear pull) and increases down a group (new shells). <strong>Ionisation energy</strong> (energy to remove an electron) increases across a period and decreases down a group — sodium loses its electron easily; neon holds electrons tightly. <strong>Electronegativity</strong> (electron-pulling power in a bond) rises across a period toward fluorine, the most electronegative element. These trends explain why reactivity of metals grows down Group 1 while halogen reactivity grows up Group 17.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Mendeleev arranged elements by:', options:['atomic number','atomic mass','density','colour'], answer:1, explain:'Mendeleev ordered by atomic mass, leaving predictive gaps.'},
{q:'The modern periodic law is based on:', options:['atomic mass','atomic number','melting point','abundance'], answer:1, explain:'Elements are ordered by proton number (atomic number).'},
{q:'Elements in the same group have:', options:['same mass','same valence electrons','same density','same state'], answer:1, explain:'Equal valence electrons give similar chemical behaviour.'},
{q:'Noble gases are unreactive because they have:', options:['no electrons','full outer shells','high density','low mass'], answer:1, explain:'Complete outer shells make them stable and inert.'},
{q:'Atomic size across a period (left to right):', options:['increases','decreases','stays same','doubles'], answer:1, explain:'Greater nuclear charge pulls shells closer.'},
{q:'Which is the most electronegative element?', options:['oxygen','chlorine','fluorine','sodium'], answer:2, explain:'Fluorine tops the electronegativity scale.'},
{q:'Transition metals are found in groups:', options:['1–2','3–12','13–16','17–18'], answer:1, explain:'Groups 3–12 hold the transition metals.'},
{q:'Ionisation energy down a group:', options:['increases','decreases','stays constant','becomes zero'], answer:1, explain:'Outer electrons are farther and more shielded, so easier to remove.'},
{q:'Halogens are in group:', options:['1','2','17','18'], answer:2, explain:'Group 17 holds F, Cl, Br, I — the halogens.'},
{q:'Mendeleev’s success was proved when:', options:['oxygen was found','gallium matched his prediction','atoms were split','noble gases reacted'], answer:1, explain:'Predicted elements like gallium were discovered with his forecast properties.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch5', title:'Chemical Bonding', urdu:'کیمیائی بانڈ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch5-l1', title:'Why Atoms Bond: The Octet Rule', html:'<h3>Why Atoms Bond: The Octet Rule</h3><p>Atoms bond to reach a <strong>stable electron arrangement</strong> — usually a full outer shell of 8 (<strong>octet rule</strong>), like the noble gases. Sodium (2,8,1) <strong>loses</strong> one electron → Na⁺; chlorine (2,8,7) <strong>gains</strong> one → Cl⁻. The resulting electrostatic attraction is an <strong>ionic bond</strong>. Atoms can also <strong>share</strong> electron pairs (covalent) or <strong>donate</strong> a pair (coordinate/dative covalent, as in NH₄⁺).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch5-l2', title:'Ionic vs Covalent Compounds', html:'<h3>Ionic vs Covalent Compounds</h3><p><strong>Ionic compounds</strong> (NaCl, MgO) form giant lattices: high melting/boiling points, <strong>conduct electricity when molten or dissolved</strong> (mobile ions) but not as solids, and are often soluble in water. <strong>Covalent compounds</strong> (H₂O, CH₄, CO₂) share electrons: usually low melting points (simple molecules) and <strong>do not conduct</strong> electricity. Giant covalent structures (diamond, graphite, silicon dioxide) are extremely hard with very high melting points; <strong>graphite conducts</strong> due to delocalised electrons.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch5-l3', title:'Bond Polarity, Shapes and Intermolecular Forces', html:'<h3>Bond Polarity, Shapes and Intermolecular Forces</h3><p>When bonding atoms differ in <strong>electronegativity</strong>, the bond is <strong>polar</strong> (e.g. H–Cl); water’s bent shape makes the whole molecule polar. <strong>Intermolecular forces</strong> — <strong>hydrogen bonding</strong> (strongest, in H₂O, NH₃, HF), dipole–dipole, and <strong>van der Waals</strong> forces — decide boiling points and solubility: polar dissolves polar (“like dissolves like”). Metallic bonding (positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons) explains metals’ conductivity, malleability and ductility.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Atoms bond mainly to achieve:', options:['more protons','a stable outer electron arrangement','higher mass','radioactivity'], answer:1, explain:'A full outer shell (octet) gives noble-gas stability.'},
{q:'In NaCl, the bond is:', options:['covalent','ionic','metallic','hydrogen'], answer:1, explain:'Electron transfer from Na to Cl gives ionic bonding.'},
{q:'Ionic compounds conduct electricity when:', options:['solid','molten or aqueous','cold','powdered'], answer:1, explain:'Mobile ions carry charge; fixed ions in solids cannot.'},
{q:'Which substance has a giant covalent structure?', options:['sodium chloride','diamond','methane','water'], answer:1, explain:'Diamond is a giant 3D network of carbon atoms.'},
{q:'Graphite conducts electricity because of:', options:['ions','delocalised electrons','protons','holes'], answer:1, explain:'Each carbon contributes a delocalised electron that moves freely.'},
{q:'A coordinate (dative) bond forms when:', options:['electrons transfer','one atom donates both shared electrons','atoms share equally','ions attract'], answer:1, explain:'One atom supplies the whole shared pair, as in NH₄⁺.'},
{q:'Water has a high boiling point mainly due to:', options:['van der Waals forces','hydrogen bonding','ionic bonds','metallic bonds'], answer:1, explain:'Strong H-bonds between water molecules need much energy to break.'},
{q:'A polar bond forms between atoms with:', options:['equal sharing','different electronegativities','no electrons','same mass'], answer:1, explain:'Unequal pull gives partial charges (δ⁺, δ⁻).'},
{q:'Metals are malleable because of:', options:['ionic lattices','sea of delocalised electrons','covalent networks','hydrogen bonds'], answer:1, explain:'Mobile electrons let ion layers slide without breaking bonds.'},
{q:'“Like dissolves like” means:', options:['solids dissolve solids','polar solvents dissolve polar solutes','hot dissolves cold','acids dissolve bases'], answer:1, explain:'Matching polarity lets solvent–solute attractions replace each other.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch6', title:'Stoichiometry', urdu:'سٹوکیومیٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch6-l1', title:'The Mole and Molar Mass', html:'<h3>The Mole and Molar Mass</h3><p>The <strong>mole</strong> is chemistry’s counting unit: 1 mol = <strong>6.022 × 10²³ particles</strong> (<strong>Avogadro’s number</strong>). <strong>Molar mass</strong> = mass of 1 mol (g/mol), numerically equal to relative atomic/molecular mass: H₂O = 18 g/mol. Key conversions: <strong>moles = mass ÷ molar mass</strong>; <strong>particles = moles × 6.022 × 10²³</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 36 g of water = 36 ÷ 18 = 2 mol = 1.204 × 10²⁴ molecules.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch6-l2', title:'Empirical and Molecular Formulae', html:'<h3>Empirical and Molecular Formulae</h3><p>The <strong>empirical formula</strong> is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms (CH₂O for glucose); the <strong>molecular formula</strong> gives actual numbers (C₆H₁₂O₆). Method: % → mass → ÷ atomic mass → moles → ÷ smallest → ratio. <strong>Percentage composition</strong> = (atoms × atomic mass ÷ molar mass) × 100. Molecular formula = empirical formula × n, where n = molar mass ÷ empirical mass.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch6-l3', title:'Calculations from Equations', html:'<h3>Calculations from Equations</h3><p>A <strong>balanced equation</strong> gives mole ratios: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O means 2 mol H₂ needs 1 mol O₂. Steps: mass → moles → mole ratio → moles of product → mass. The <strong>limiting reactant</strong> runs out first and caps the product; excess reactant remains. <strong>Percentage yield</strong> = (actual yield ÷ theoretical yield) × 100 — always ≤ 100% because of side reactions and losses.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Avogadro’s number is:', options:['6.022 × 10²³','3.00 × 10⁸','1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹','9.11 × 10⁻³¹'], answer:0, explain:'One mole contains 6.022 × 10²³ particles.'},
{q:'The molar mass of CO₂ is:', options:['28 g/mol','32 g/mol','44 g/mol','12 g/mol'], answer:2, explain:'12 + 2×16 = 44 g/mol.'},
{q:'How many moles are in 36 g of water (M = 18)?', options:['1','2','18','36'], answer:1, explain:'moles = 36 ÷ 18 = 2.'},
{q:'The empirical formula of glucose C₆H₁₂O₆ is:', options:['C₆H₁₂O₆','CH₂O','C₂H₄O₂','CHO'], answer:1, explain:'Simplest whole-number ratio is 1:2:1.'},
{q:'The limiting reactant is the one that:', options:['is cheapest','runs out first','is a gas','is in excess'], answer:1, explain:'It limits how much product can form.'},
{q:'Percentage yield equals:', options:['theoretical ÷ actual × 100','actual ÷ theoretical × 100','actual + theoretical','moles × 100'], answer:1, explain:'Yield compares what you got with the maximum possible.'},
{q:'In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, 4 mol H₂ needs:', options:['4 mol O₂','2 mol O₂','1 mol O₂','8 mol O₂'], answer:1, explain:'Mole ratio H₂:O₂ = 2:1, so 4 mol H₂ needs 2 mol O₂.'},
{q:'Moles = ', options:['mass × molar mass','mass ÷ molar mass','molar mass ÷ mass','mass + molar mass'], answer:1, explain:'n = m/M is the core stoichiometry conversion.'},
{q:'Percentage yield is always:', options:['over 100%','100% or less','exactly 100%','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Losses and side reactions keep actual yield below theoretical.'},
{q:'One mole of any gas at STP occupies about:', options:['1 dm³','22.4 dm³','44 dm³','6 dm³'], answer:1, explain:'Molar gas volume at STP ≈ 22.4 dm³ (22.4 L).'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch7', title:'Electrochemistry', urdu:'الیکٹرو کیمسٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch7-l1', title:'Oxidation Numbers and Redox', html:'<h3>Oxidation Numbers and Redox</h3><p><strong>Oxidation</strong> is loss of electrons (or gain of oxygen); <strong>reduction</strong> is gain of electrons (or loss of oxygen) — remember <strong>OIL RIG</strong>. <strong>Oxidation numbers</strong> track electron shifts: free elements = 0, Group 1 = +1, oxygen usually −2, hydrogen usually +1. In 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, Mg goes 0 → +2 (oxidised) and O goes 0 → −2 (reduced). The <strong>oxidising agent</strong> causes oxidation and is itself reduced.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch7-l2', title:'Electrochemical Cells', html:'<h3>Electrochemical Cells</h3><p>A <strong>galvanic (voltaic) cell</strong> turns chemical energy into electrical energy: oxidation at the <strong>anode</strong> (negative), reduction at the <strong>cathode</strong> (positive) — remember <strong>An Ox, Red Cat</strong>. In the <strong>Daniel cell</strong>, zinc dissolves (anode) while copper deposits (cathode); electrons flow Zn → Cu through the wire. The <strong>electrochemical series</strong> ranks metals by reactivity: a more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one from its salt solution (Zn displaces Cu²⁺).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch7-l3', title:'Electrolysis and Batteries', html:'<h3>Electrolysis and Batteries</h3><p><strong>Electrolysis</strong> uses electricity to drive a non-spontaneous reaction: cations move to the <strong>cathode</strong> (negative) and gain electrons; anions move to the <strong>anode</strong> (positive) and lose electrons. Uses: <strong>electroplating</strong> (coating with chromium/nickel), <strong>purification of copper</strong>, extraction of aluminium. <strong>Batteries</strong> store chemical energy: dry cells and lead-acid accumulators power torches and cars; <strong>corrosion (rusting)</strong> is unwanted electrochemistry, prevented by painting, oiling or <strong>galvanising</strong> (zinc coating).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'OIL RIG means:', options:['oil floats on water','oxidation is loss, reduction is gain of electrons','oxygen is light','ions lose reactivity'], answer:1, explain:'Oxidation Is Loss (of electrons); Reduction Is Gain.'},
{q:'In a galvanic cell, oxidation occurs at the:', options:['cathode','anode','salt bridge','wire'], answer:1, explain:'An Ox — oxidation at the anode.'},
{q:'The oxidation number of oxygen in H₂O is:', options:['0','+1','−2','+2'], answer:2, explain:'Oxygen is −2 in most compounds (except peroxides).'},
{q:'During electrolysis, cations move to the:', options:['anode','cathode','battery','electrolyte'], answer:1, explain:'Positive ions are attracted to the negative cathode.'},
{q:'Electroplating is used to:', options:['melt metals','coat objects with a thin metal layer','make alloys','purify water'], answer:1, explain:'A metal layer is deposited for protection or shine.'},
{q:'Rusting of iron is prevented by:', options:['heating','galvanising with zinc','adding water','scratching'], answer:1, explain:'Zinc coating (or paint/oil) blocks air and moisture.'},
{q:'A more reactive metal will:', options:['never react','displace a less reactive metal from its salt','dissolve in water only','form no ions'], answer:1, explain:'The electrochemical series predicts displacement reactions.'},
{q:'In the Daniel cell, electrons flow:', options:['Cu → Zn','Zn → Cu','both ways','nowhere'], answer:1, explain:'From the oxidised zinc anode to the copper cathode.'},
{q:'The oxidising agent is itself:', options:['oxidised','reduced','unchanged','destroyed'], answer:1, explain:'It accepts electrons, so it is reduced.'},
{q:'Which is a use of electrolysis?', options:['making steel','purification of copper','burning fuel','photosynthesis'], answer:1, explain:'Impure copper anodes dissolve and pure copper deposits at the cathode.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch8', title:'Energetics', urdu:'انرجیٹکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch8-l1', title:'Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions', html:'<h3>Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions</h3><p><strong>Exothermic</strong> reactions release heat to the surroundings (combustion, neutralisation, respiration) — the flask feels <strong>hot</strong>, ΔH <strong>negative</strong>. <strong>Endothermic</strong> reactions absorb heat (photosynthesis, thermal decomposition of limestone) — the flask feels <strong>cold</strong>, ΔH <strong>positive</strong>. <strong>Enthalpy change (ΔH)</strong> = heat content of products − heat content of reactants. Energy-level diagrams show reactants higher than products for exothermic reactions.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch8-l2', title:'Bond Energies and Hess’s Law', html:'<h3>Bond Energies and Hess’s Law</h3><p>Breaking bonds <strong>absorbs</strong> energy; forming bonds <strong>releases</strong> energy. ΔH ≈ Σ(bond energies broken) − Σ(bond energies formed). A reaction is exothermic when more energy is released forming products than absorbed breaking reactants. <strong>Hess’s law:</strong> the total enthalpy change is <strong>independent of the route</strong> — whether carbon burns directly to CO₂ or via CO, ΔH is the same. This lets chemists calculate ΔH for reactions that cannot be measured directly.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch8-l3', title:'Fuels and Energy in Daily Life', html:'<h3>Fuels and Energy in Daily Life</h3><p><strong>Fuels</strong> release energy by combustion: natural gas (methane), petrol, coal. <strong>Calorific value</strong> measures energy per gram — hydrogen has the highest, which is why it fuels rockets. <strong>Activation energy</strong> is the minimum energy needed to start a reaction (the spark that lights the match); <strong>catalysts</strong> lower it without being used up. Food <strong>calories</strong> are chemistry too: respiration (C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O) is exothermic, releasing energy for the body.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'An exothermic reaction:', options:['absorbs heat','releases heat','has ΔH = 0','needs light'], answer:1, explain:'Exo = out; heat flows to the surroundings.'},
{q:'For an exothermic reaction, ΔH is:', options:['positive','negative','zero','infinite'], answer:1, explain:'Products have less enthalpy than reactants.'},
{q:'Breaking chemical bonds:', options:['releases energy','absorbs energy','needs no energy','creates matter'], answer:1, explain:'Energy must be supplied to pull atoms apart.'},
{q:'Hess’s law states that ΔH:', options:['depends on the route','is independent of the route','is always zero','cannot be measured'], answer:1, explain:'Enthalpy is a state function — only start and end matter.'},
{q:'Activation energy is:', options:['total energy released','minimum energy to start a reaction','energy of products','heat of solution'], answer:1, explain:'Reactants must clear this barrier before reacting.'},
{q:'A catalyst works by:', options:['being used up','lowering activation energy','raising temperature','adding energy'], answer:1, explain:'It provides an easier pathway and is regenerated.'},
{q:'Which process is endothermic?', options:['combustion','respiration','photosynthesis','neutralisation'], answer:2, explain:'Photosynthesis absorbs sunlight energy.'},
{q:'Hydrogen is a good rocket fuel because of its:', options:['low cost','high calorific value','blue flame','abundance'], answer:1, explain:'It releases the most energy per gram of any fuel.'},
{q:'In an energy-level diagram of an exothermic reaction, products are:', options:['higher than reactants','lower than reactants','equal to reactants','absent'], answer:1, explain:'Energy is lost as heat, so products sit lower.'},
{q:'Respiration is:', options:['endothermic','exothermic','neutral','physical'], answer:1, explain:'Glucose oxidation releases energy for the body.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch9', title:'Chemical Equilibrium', urdu:'کیمیائی توازن', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch9-l1', title:'Reversible Reactions and Dynamic Equilibrium', html:'<h3>Reversible Reactions and Dynamic Equilibrium</h3><p>Many reactions are <strong>reversible</strong> (⇌): e.g. N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. In a <strong>closed system</strong>, a point comes where the <strong>forward and reverse rates are equal</strong> — <strong>dynamic equilibrium</strong>. It is <em>dynamic</em> because both reactions continue; concentrations stay constant, not because reaction stops, but because formation and breakdown balance. Equilibrium needs a closed system so nothing escapes.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch9-l2', title:'Le Chatelier’s Principle', html:'<h3>Le Chatelier’s Principle</h3><p><strong>Le Chatelier’s principle:</strong> if a system at equilibrium is disturbed, it shifts to <strong>oppose the change</strong>. <strong>Concentration:</strong> adding reactant shifts right (more product). <strong>Pressure</strong> (gases): increased pressure shifts toward <strong>fewer moles of gas</strong>. <strong>Temperature:</strong> heating shifts toward the <strong>endothermic</strong> direction. A <strong>catalyst</strong> speeds both directions equally — it reaches equilibrium faster but does <em>not</em> change the position.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch9-l3', title:'Equilibrium in Industry: The Haber Process', html:'<h3>Equilibrium in Industry: The Haber Process</h3><p>The <strong>Haber process</strong> makes ammonia: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (ΔH negative, exothermic). Conditions chosen by Le Chatelier: <strong>~450 °C</strong> (compromise — lower favours yield but is too slow), <strong>~200 atm</strong> (high pressure favours 4 gas moles → 2), and an <strong>iron catalyst</strong> (faster equilibrium, same yield). Ammonia becomes fertilisers feeding billions — equilibrium chemistry with world-changing impact.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Dynamic equilibrium means:', options:['reactions have stopped','forward and reverse rates are equal','only products exist','temperature is zero'], answer:1, explain:'Both reactions continue at equal rates, so concentrations stay constant.'},
{q:'Equilibrium can only be reached in a:', options:['open system','closed system','vacuum','hot system'], answer:1, explain:'Products must not escape, or the reverse reaction cannot balance.'},
{q:'Increasing pressure on N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ shifts equilibrium:', options:['left','right','nowhere','to solids'], answer:1, explain:'Right side has fewer gas moles (2 vs 4), favoured by high pressure.'},
{q:'A catalyst at equilibrium:', options:['increases yield','speeds both directions equally, yield unchanged','shifts right','shifts left'], answer:1, explain:'Catalysts change rate, not the equilibrium position.'},
{q:'Heating an exothermic reversible reaction shifts it:', options:['toward products','toward reactants','nowhere','to completion'], answer:1, explain:'Heat favours the endothermic (reverse) direction.'},
{q:'The Haber process makes:', options:['sulphuric acid','ammonia','nitric acid','ethanol'], answer:1, explain:'N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ over an iron catalyst.'},
{q:'High pressure is used in the Haber process because:', options:['it is cheap','fewer gas moles on the product side','ammonia is a liquid','iron needs it'], answer:1, explain:'4 gas moles → 2, so pressure drives the forward reaction.'},
{q:'Le Chatelier’s principle predicts the effect of:', options:['catalyst colour','disturbing an equilibrium','stirring speed','container shape'], answer:1, explain:'The system shifts to oppose concentration, pressure or temperature changes.'},
{q:'At equilibrium, concentrations are:', options:['equal','constant but not necessarily equal','zero','changing'], answer:1, explain:'Constant concentrations, though amounts may differ.'},
{q:'The ⇌ symbol means the reaction is:', options:['fast','reversible','complete','dangerous'], answer:1, explain:'It shows forward and reverse reactions both occur.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch10', title:'Acids, Bases and Salts', urdu:'تیزاب، اساس اور نمکیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch10-l1', title:'Theories of Acids and Bases', html:'<h3>Theories of Acids and Bases</h3><p><strong>Arrhenius:</strong> acids release <strong>H⁺</strong> in water, bases release <strong>OH⁻</strong>. <strong>Brønsted–Lowry:</strong> acids are <strong>proton donors</strong>, bases <strong>proton acceptors</strong> (NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻). <strong>Lewis:</strong> acids accept electron pairs, bases donate them. Properties: acids taste sour, turn <strong>blue litmus red</strong>, pH < 7; bases feel soapy, turn <strong>red litmus blue</strong>, pH > 7. <strong>Strong</strong> acids (HCl, H₂SO₄) fully ionise; <strong>weak</strong> acids (CH₃COOH) partly ionise.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch10-l2', title:'pH Scale and Indicators', html:'<h3>pH Scale and Indicators</h3><p><strong>pH</strong> = −log[H⁺]: 0–6 acidic, 7 neutral, 8–14 basic. Each pH unit is a <strong>10× change</strong> in H⁺ concentration — pH 3 is ten times more acidic than pH 4. <strong>Indicators</strong> show pH by colour: <strong>litmus</strong> (red/blue), <strong>methyl orange</strong> (red in acid, yellow in base), <strong>phenolphthalein</strong> (colourless in acid, pink in base), and <strong>universal indicator</strong> (full rainbow scale). Soil pH, stomach acid (pH ~2) and blood (pH 7.4) all matter biologically.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch10-l3', title:'Neutralisation and Salts', html:'<h3>Neutralisation and Salts</h3><p><strong>Neutralisation:</strong> acid + base → <strong>salt + water</strong>. E.g. HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O. <strong>Salts</strong> also form by acid + metal → salt + hydrogen, and acid + carbonate → salt + water + CO₂. Salt types: <strong>normal</strong> (NaCl), <strong>acidic</strong> (NaHSO₄), <strong>basic</strong>, <strong>double salts</strong> and <strong>hydrated salts</strong> (CuSO₄·5H₂O, blue; anhydrous is white — used to test for water). Everyday salts: table salt, washing soda, plaster of Paris.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'A Brønsted–Lowry acid is a:', options:['proton donor','proton acceptor','electron donor','hydroxide donor'], answer:0, explain:'Acids donate H⁺; bases accept it.'},
{q:'Which is a strong acid?', options:['ethanoic acid','citric acid','hydrochloric acid','carbonic acid'], answer:2, explain:'HCl ionises completely in water.'},
{q:'Blue litmus turns ___ in acid:', options:['blue','red','green','yellow'], answer:1, explain:'Acids turn blue litmus paper red.'},
{q:'pH 3 compared with pH 5 is:', options:['2× more acidic','20× more acidic','100× more acidic','less acidic'], answer:2, explain:'Each pH unit is a 10× change: 10 × 10 = 100×.'},
{q:'Phenolphthalein in a basic solution turns:', options:['red','pink','blue','orange'], answer:1, explain:'Colourless in acid, pink in alkali.'},
{q:'Neutralisation of HCl by NaOH gives:', options:['NaH + ClOH','NaCl + H₂O','NaO + HCl','H₂ + Cl₂'], answer:1, explain:'Acid + base → salt + water.'},
{q:'Acid + carbonate gives:', options:['salt + hydrogen','salt + water + CO₂','salt + oxygen','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Carbonates fizz, releasing carbon dioxide.'},
{q:'Anhydrous copper(II) sulphate turns blue when it meets:', options:['acid','water','base','oil'], answer:1, explain:'White anhydrous CuSO₄ hydrates to blue — a test for water.'},
{q:'A Lewis base is an:', options:['proton donor','electron-pair donor','electron-pair acceptor','neutron donor'], answer:1, explain:'Lewis bases donate an electron pair.'},
{q:'Stomach acid has a pH of about:', options:['7','2','10','12'], answer:1, explain:'Dilute HCl in the stomach is strongly acidic, pH 1–3.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch11', title:'Environmental Chemistry - Air', urdu:'ماحولیاتی کیمسٹری ۔ ہوا', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch11-l1', title:'Composition of Air and the Atmosphere', html:'<h3>Composition of Air and the Atmosphere</h3><p>Dry air is about <strong>78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.93% argon, 0.04% carbon dioxide</strong> plus variable water vapour. The <strong>ozone layer</strong> (stratosphere) absorbs harmful <strong>UV radiation</strong>; <strong>CFCs</strong> (old refrigerants) destroy ozone — the Montreal Protocol phased them out. The <strong>greenhouse effect</strong> is natural (CO₂, methane, water vapour trap heat), but excess emissions cause <strong>global warming</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch11-l2', title:'Air Pollutants', html:'<h3>Air Pollutants</h3><p>Key pollutants: <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> (incomplete combustion; binds haemoglobin, poisonous), <strong>sulphur dioxide</strong> (fossil fuels, power plants), <strong>nitrogen oxides</strong> (vehicle engines, lightning), <strong>particulates/smoke</strong> and <strong>lead</strong> (old petrol). <strong>Acid rain</strong> forms when SO₂ and NOₓ dissolve in rain (pH < 5.6), damaging buildings (limestone), crops and lakes. <strong>Photochemical smog</strong> — NOₓ + sunlight + hydrocarbons — chokes cities like Lahore in winter.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch11-l3', title:'Controlling Air Pollution', html:'<h3>Controlling Air Pollution</h3><p>Solutions: <strong>catalytic converters</strong> turn CO and NOₓ into CO₂ and N₂; <strong>scrubbers</strong> remove SO₂ from factory chimneys; <strong>electrostatic precipitators</strong> trap dust; unleaded petrol and <strong>Euro-standard fuels</strong> cut emissions. Individuals help by using <strong>public transport</strong>, saving electricity and planting trees — trees absorb CO₂ and release O₂. Pakistan’s anti-smog measures include brick-kiln zigzag technology and vehicle emission testing.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Air is about ___ nitrogen:', options:['21%','50%','78%','90%'], answer:2, explain:'Nitrogen dominates dry air at ~78%.'},
{q:'The ozone layer protects us from:', options:['visible light','UV radiation','infrared','radio waves'], answer:1, explain:'Ozone absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays.'},
{q:'CFCs are harmful because they:', options:['cause rain','destroy ozone','cool the Earth','make oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'Chlorine from CFCs breaks down stratospheric ozone.'},
{q:'Carbon monoxide is dangerous because it:', options:['smells bad','binds haemoglobin, blocking oxygen','is acidic','explodes'], answer:1, explain:'CO binds haemoglobin ~200× more strongly than oxygen.'},
{q:'Acid rain is mainly caused by:', options:['CO₂ only','SO₂ and NOₓ','oxygen','dust'], answer:1, explain:'These dissolve in rainwater forming sulphuric and nitric acids.'},
{q:'Catalytic converters change CO and NOₓ into:', options:['oxygen','CO₂ and N₂','acid rain','ozone'], answer:1, explain:'They convert pollutants to less harmful gases.'},
{q:'Photochemical smog needs:', options:['rain','sunlight + NOₓ + hydrocarbons','snow','wind only'], answer:1, explain:'Sunlight drives the reactions forming ground-level ozone.'},
{q:'The natural greenhouse effect:', options:['is always harmful','keeps Earth warm enough for life','cools the planet','destroys ozone'], answer:1, explain:'Without it Earth would average about −18 °C.'},
{q:'Scrubbers in chimneys remove:', options:['CO₂','SO₂','oxygen','nitrogen'], answer:1, explain:'They wash sulphur dioxide from flue gases.'},
{q:'Planting trees helps air quality because trees:', options:['make smoke','absorb CO₂ and release O₂','trap nitrogen','block wind'], answer:1, explain:'Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide from the air.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch12', title:'Environmental Chemistry - Water', urdu:'ماحولیاتی کیمسٹری ۔ پانی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch12-l1', title:'Water: Properties and the Water Cycle', html:'<h3>Water: Properties and the Water Cycle</h3><p>Water’s <strong>hydrogen bonding</strong> gives it unusual properties: high boiling point, high <strong>specific heat capacity</strong> (stabilises climate), and <strong>maximum density at 4 °C</strong> — ice floats, insulating lakes so aquatic life survives winter. The <strong>water cycle</strong>: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → collection. Water is the <strong>universal solvent</strong>, which is why pure water is rare in nature — it dissolves minerals as it flows.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch12-l2', title:'Hard Water and Water Pollution', html:'<h3>Hard Water and Water Pollution</h3><p><strong>Hard water</strong> contains dissolved <strong>Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺</strong>: <strong>temporary hardness</strong> (bicarbonates — removed by boiling) and <strong>permanent hardness</strong> (sulphates/chlorides — removed by washing soda or ion exchange). Hard water wastes soap (forms scum) and furs kettles. <strong>Water pollutants:</strong> sewage (pathogens → cholera, typhoid), fertilisers (algal blooms, <strong>eutrophication</strong>), industrial chemicals and oil spills.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch12-l3', title:'Purifying Water', html:'<h3>Purifying Water</h3><p>Safe drinking water needs treatment: <strong>sedimentation</strong> (settling), <strong>filtration</strong> (sand beds), <strong>chlorination</strong> (kills germs) and sometimes <strong>fluoridation</strong>. At home: <strong>boil</strong> water 1–3 minutes to kill pathogens. <strong>Desalination</strong> (distillation/reverse osmosis) makes seawater drinkable but is energy-hungry. Conserving water — fixing leaks, drip irrigation — matters hugely in water-stressed Pakistan.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Ice floats because water:', options:['is warm','is densest at 4 °C','has no bonds','is a gas'], answer:1, explain:'Maximum density at 4 °C makes ice less dense than liquid water.'},
{q:'Temporary hardness of water is caused by:', options:['calcium sulphate','calcium/magnesium bicarbonates','sodium chloride','iron'], answer:1, explain:'Bicarbonates decompose on boiling, removing temporary hardness.'},
{q:'Permanent hardness can be removed by:', options:['boiling','washing soda / ion exchange','filtering','cooling'], answer:1, explain:'Sulphates and chlorides need chemical softening.'},
{q:'Hard water wastes soap by forming:', options:['lather','scum','bubbles','foam'], answer:1, explain:'Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ precipitate soap as insoluble scum.'},
{q:'Eutrophication is caused by excess:', options:['oxygen','fertiliser nutrients in water','sand','salt'], answer:1, explain:'Nitrates/phosphates feed algal blooms that deplete oxygen.'},
{q:'Chlorination of water:', options:['removes hardness','kills germs','adds minerals','softens'], answer:1, explain:'Chlorine disinfects drinking water.'},
{q:'Water is called the universal solvent because it:', options:['dissolves everything','dissolves many substances','is tasteless','boils at 100 °C'], answer:1, explain:'Its polarity lets it dissolve a huge range of substances.'},
{q:'Sewage in drinking water can cause:', options:['flu','cholera and typhoid','malaria','diabetes'], answer:1, explain:'Waterborne pathogens cause cholera, typhoid and dysentery.'},
{q:'The water cycle’s correct order is:', options:['precipitation → evaporation → condensation','evaporation → condensation → precipitation','condensation → collection → evaporation','collection → precipitation → condensation'], answer:1, explain:'Water evaporates, condenses into clouds, then falls as precipitation.'},
{q:'Boiling drinking water for 1–3 minutes:', options:['adds minerals','kills pathogens','removes salt','cools it'], answer:1, explain:'Heat destroys disease-causing microorganisms.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch13', title:'Organic Chemistry', urdu:'نامیاتی کیمسٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch13-l1', title:'What Makes Chemistry “Organic”', html:'<h3>What Makes Chemistry “Organic”</h3><p><strong>Organic chemistry</strong> studies <strong>carbon compounds</strong> (with exceptions like CO₂ and carbonates). Carbon is special: it forms <strong>4 covalent bonds</strong> (<strong>tetravalency</strong>) and links into long <strong>chains, branches and rings</strong> (<strong>catenation</strong>). <strong>Vital force theory</strong> (organics need a “life force”) was demolished when <strong>Wöhler</strong> made urea from inorganic ammonium cyanate in 1828. Sources of organics: petroleum, natural gas, coal, and living things.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch13-l2', title:'Functional Groups and Homologous Series', html:'<h3>Functional Groups and Homologous Series</h3><p>A <strong>functional group</strong> is the reactive part of a molecule: –OH (alcohol), –COOH (carboxylic acid), –CHO (aldehyde), C=O (ketone), –NH₂ (amine), C=C (alkene). A <strong>homologous series</strong> is a family with the same functional group, each member differing by <strong>CH₂</strong>: alkanes (methane, ethane…), alkenes, alcohols. Members show <strong>gradual trends</strong> (boiling point rises with size) and similar chemistry.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch13-l3', title:'Naming and Drawing Organic Molecules', html:'<h3>Naming and Drawing Organic Molecules</h3><p><strong>IUPAC naming</strong> basics: find the longest carbon chain (meth-1, eth-2, prop-3, but-4, pent-5…), number to give substituents lowest numbers, name substituents (methyl, ethyl, chloro…), then the parent with a suffix (-ane, -ene, -ol, -oic acid). <strong>Structural formulae</strong> show every bond; <strong>condensed formulae</strong> (CH₃CH₂OH) are shorthand. <strong>Isomers</strong> share a molecular formula but differ in arrangement — butane and methylpropane are isomers.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Organic chemistry is the study of:', options:['all chemicals','carbon compounds','metals','gases'], answer:1, explain:'Organic = carbon-based compounds (with a few exceptions).'},
{q:'Carbon forms ___ covalent bonds:', options:['2','3','4','6'], answer:2, explain:'Carbon is tetravalent.'},
{q:'Wöhler’s synthesis of urea disproved:', options:['atomic theory','vital force theory','octet rule','evolution'], answer:1, explain:'He made an organic compound from inorganic starting materials.'},
{q:'The functional group –COOH belongs to:', options:['alcohols','carboxylic acids','aldehydes','amines'], answer:1, explain:'–COOH is the carboxyl group.'},
{q:'Members of a homologous series differ by:', options:['CH₄','CH₂','H₂O','O₂'], answer:1, explain:'Each step adds one CH₂ unit.'},
{q:'The –OH functional group makes a compound an:', options:['alkane','alcohol','acid','alkene'], answer:1, explain:'Hydroxyl group = alcohol family.'},
{q:'In IUPAC, “prop-” means ___ carbons:', options:['2','3','4','5'], answer:1, explain:'meth-1, eth-2, prop-3, but-4.'},
{q:'Butane and methylpropane are:', options:['isotopes','isomers','allotropes','polymers'], answer:1, explain:'Same formula C₄H₁₀, different arrangement.'},
{q:'Catenation is carbon’s ability to:', options:['form ions','link into long chains','conduct electricity','dissolve'], answer:1, explain:'C–C bonds build chains, branches and rings.'},
{q:'Which is a source of organic compounds?', options:['sand','petroleum','granite','salt'], answer:1, explain:'Petroleum and natural gas are rich organic sources.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch14', title:'Hydrocarbons', urdu:'ہائیڈرو کاربن', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch14-l1', title:'Alkanes', html:'<h3>Alkanes</h3><p><strong>Alkanes</strong> (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂) have only <strong>single bonds</strong> — they are <strong>saturated</strong>: methane (CH₄), ethane, propane, butane. They burn with a clean blue flame: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O (complete combustion); limited oxygen gives poisonous <strong>CO</strong> and soot. Alkanes undergo <strong>substitution</strong> with chlorine in UV light (CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl). Uses: natural gas fuel, LPG (propane/butane), petrol.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch14-l2', title:'Alkenes and Alkynes', html:'<h3>Alkenes and Alkynes</h3><p><strong>Alkenes</strong> (CₙH₂ₙ) contain a <strong>C=C double bond</strong> — <strong>unsaturated</strong>: ethene (CH₂=CH₂). They decolourise <strong>bromine water</strong> (orange → colourless) and <strong>acidified KMnO₄</strong> — tests for unsaturation. They undergo <strong>addition</strong>: hydrogenation (→ alkane, Ni catalyst), hydration (→ ethanol). <strong>Alkynes</strong> (CₙH₂ₙ₋₂) have a <strong>triple bond</strong>; ethyne (acetylene) burns at ~3000 °C in oxy-acetylene welding torches.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch14-l3', title:'Petroleum, CNG and LPG', html:'<h3>Petroleum, CNG and LPG</h3><p><strong>Petroleum</strong> (crude oil) is separated by <strong>fractional distillation</strong> into refinery gas, petrol, kerosene, diesel, lubricating oil and bitumen — smaller molecules boil off first. <strong>CNG</strong> (compressed natural gas, mainly <strong>methane</strong>) and <strong>LPG</strong> (liquefied propane/butane) are cleaner vehicle fuels than petrol. Burning fossil fuels releases <strong>CO₂</strong> (global warming) and, if incomplete, <strong>CO</strong> — proper ventilation and maintenance save lives.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The general formula of alkanes is:', options:['CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ₊₂','CₙH₂ₙ₋₂','CₙHₙ'], answer:1, explain:'Alkanes are saturated: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.'},
{q:'Ethene decolourises bromine water because it is:', options:['saturated','unsaturated','an acid','a salt'], answer:1, explain:'The C=C double bond adds bromine across it.'},
{q:'Complete combustion of methane gives:', options:['CO + H₂','CO₂ + H₂O','C + H₂O','CO₂ + H₂'], answer:1, explain:'CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.'},
{q:'Alkynes contain a:', options:['single bond','double bond','triple bond','ionic bond'], answer:2, explain:'General formula CₙH₂ₙ₋₂ with a C≡C triple bond.'},
{q:'Petroleum is separated by:', options:['filtration','fractional distillation','evaporation','chromatography'], answer:1, explain:'Fractions separate by boiling point in the fractionating tower.'},
{q:'CNG is mainly:', options:['propane','methane','butane','ethene'], answer:1, explain:'Compressed natural gas is mostly methane.'},
{q:'Incomplete combustion of fuels produces poisonous:', options:['CO₂','CO','H₂O','N₂'], answer:1, explain:'Carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin and can kill.'},
{q:'Alkanes react with chlorine in UV light by:', options:['addition','substitution','neutralisation','cracking'], answer:1, explain:'A hydrogen is substituted by chlorine (free-radical reaction).'},
{q:'Acetylene (ethyne) is used in:', options:['cooking','welding torches','batteries','paints'], answer:1, explain:'Oxy-acetylene flames reach ~3000 °C.'},
{q:'LPG consists mainly of:', options:['methane','propane and butane','ethene','hydrogen'], answer:1, explain:'Liquefied petroleum gas = propane/butane mixture.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch15', title:'Biochemistry', urdu:'بائیو کیمسٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch15-l1', title:'Carbohydrates and Lipids', html:'<h3>Carbohydrates and Lipids</h3><p><strong>Carbohydrates</strong> (Cₓ(H₂O)ᵧ) are energy foods: <strong>monosaccharides</strong> (glucose, fructose), <strong>disaccharides</strong> (sucrose, maltose, lactose) and <strong>polysaccharides</strong> (starch in plants, glycogen in animals, cellulose for fibre). <strong>Lipids</strong> (fats/oils) store <strong>twice the energy per gram</strong> of carbs, insulate the body and carry vitamins A, D, E, K. <strong>Saturated</strong> fats (ghee) pack tightly; <strong>unsaturated</strong> oils (mustard, olive) have C=C bonds and are heart-friendlier.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch15-l2', title:'Proteins and Nucleic Acids', html:'<h3>Proteins and Nucleic Acids</h3><p><strong>Proteins</strong> are chains of <strong>amino acids</strong> (20 types) joined by <strong>peptide bonds</strong>: they build muscle, make <strong>enzymes</strong>, haemoglobin and antibodies. <strong>Nucleic acids</strong> store heredity: <strong>DNA</strong> (double helix; bases A–T, G–C; sugar deoxyribose) and <strong>RNA</strong> (single strand; uracil replaces thymine). The sequence of bases is the <strong>genetic code</strong> directing protein synthesis.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch15-l3', title:'Vitamins, Minerals and Enzymes', html:'<h3>Vitamins, Minerals and Enzymes</h3><p><strong>Vitamins:</strong> A (vision), B-complex (energy metabolism), C (immunity, scurvy prevention), D (bones, from sunlight), E and K (blood clotting). <strong>Minerals:</strong> calcium and phosphorus (bones/teeth), iron (haemoglobin — deficiency causes <strong>anaemia</strong>), iodine (thyroid — deficiency causes <strong>goitre</strong>). <strong>Enzymes</strong> are protein <strong>biocatalysts</strong>: each has an <strong>active site</strong> fitting its substrate like lock and key; they denature with extreme heat or pH.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Glucose is a:', options:['disaccharide','monosaccharide','polysaccharide','lipid'], answer:1, explain:'Glucose is a simple single-unit sugar.'},
{q:'Starch is stored in:', options:['animals','plants','bacteria only','fungi only'], answer:1, explain:'Plants store glucose as starch; animals use glycogen.'},
{q:'Proteins are made of:', options:['fatty acids','amino acids','glucose units','nucleotides'], answer:1, explain:'Amino acids join by peptide bonds into proteins.'},
{q:'In DNA, adenine pairs with:', options:['guanine','cytosine','thymine','uracil'], answer:2, explain:'A–T and G–C base pairing in the double helix.'},
{q:'Iron deficiency causes:', options:['goitre','anaemia','scurvy','rickets'], answer:1, explain:'Iron is needed for haemoglobin in red blood cells.'},
{q:'Vitamin D is important for:', options:['vision','bones','clotting','immunity'], answer:1, explain:'It helps absorb calcium for bones and teeth.'},
{q:'Enzymes are:', options:['carbohydrates','protein biocatalysts','lipids','minerals'], answer:1, explain:'Enzymes are proteins that speed reactions without being used up.'},
{q:'Unsaturated fats differ from saturated by having:', options:['more calories','C=C double bonds','no carbon','more protein'], answer:1, explain:'Double bonds kink the chains so they stay liquid.'},
{q:'Iodine deficiency causes:', options:['anaemia','goitre','scurvy','beriberi'], answer:1, explain:'The thyroid needs iodine; deficiency swells the gland.'},
{q:'The genetic code is carried by the sequence of:', options:['amino acids','nitrogenous bases','fatty acids','minerals'], answer:1, explain:'Base order in DNA/RNA codes for proteins.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch16', title:'Empirical Data Collection and Analysis', urdu:'تجرباتی ڈیٹا کا حصول اور تجزیہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch16-l1', title:'Planning an Investigation', html:'<h3>Planning an Investigation</h3><p>Good experiments start with a clear <strong>aim</strong> and <strong>hypothesis</strong>. Identify the <strong>independent variable</strong> (what you change), <strong>dependent variable</strong> (what you measure) and <strong>control variables</strong> (kept constant for a fair test). A <strong>control experiment</strong> (e.g. no catalyst) isolates the effect being studied. Decide the <strong>range and repeats</strong> in advance — repeats reveal random error and let you average.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch16-l2', title:'Recording and Presenting Data', html:'<h3>Recording and Presenting Data</h3><p>Record results in a <strong>table</strong>: independent variable in the first column, units in headers, readings to the instrument’s precision. Plot the independent variable on the <strong>x-axis</strong> and dependent on the <strong>y-axis</strong>; use sensible scales, label axes with units, and draw a <strong>line of best fit</strong> (not dot-to-dot). <strong>Anomalous results</strong> (outliers) are circled and repeated or ignored with a stated reason.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch16-l3', title:'Errors, Accuracy and Conclusions', html:'<h3>Errors, Accuracy and Conclusions</h3><p><strong>Random errors</strong> (reading fluctuations) are reduced by repeats and averaging; <strong>systematic errors</strong> (faulty balance, zero error) bias every reading and need recalibration. A <strong>conclusion</strong> answers the aim using the data pattern (“as temperature rose, rate increased because…”). Honest science reports <strong>uncertainty</strong>, suggests <strong>improvements</strong> (more repeats, better control) and never hides anomalous data.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The independent variable is:', options:['what you measure','what you deliberately change','kept constant','the conclusion'], answer:1, explain:'It is the factor the experimenter varies.'},
{q:'A fair test keeps unchanged the:', options:['independent variable','dependent variable','control variables','hypothesis'], answer:2, explain:'Controls stay constant so only one factor varies.'},
{q:'On a graph, the independent variable goes on the:', options:['y-axis','x-axis','title','key'], answer:1, explain:'Convention: independent on x, dependent on y.'},
{q:'An anomalous result should be:', options:['hidden','repeated or noted with reason','deleted silently','averaged in always'], answer:1, explain:'Honest science acknowledges outliers openly.'},
{q:'Repeating measurements mainly reduces:', options:['systematic error','random error','cost','time'], answer:1, explain:'Averaging repeats smooths out random fluctuations.'},
{q:'A zero error on a balance is:', options:['random error','systematic error','no error','human error'], answer:1, explain:'It shifts every reading the same way.'},
{q:'A line of best fit:', options:['joins every dot','shows the overall trend','is always straight','ignores data'], answer:1, explain:'It reveals the pattern, balancing points above and below.'},
{q:'A control experiment:', options:['tests everything','isolates the effect by omitting the factor studied','is unscientific','needs no aim'], answer:1, explain:'Comparing with/without the factor shows its true effect.'},
{q:'Units in a results table go:', options:['in each cell','in the column headers','nowhere','in the title only'], answer:1, explain:'Headers carry units once, keeping cells clean numbers.'},
{q:'A good conclusion:', options:['restates the aim only','answers the aim using the data pattern','lists apparatus','hides errors'], answer:1, explain:'It explains what the evidence shows and why.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch17', title:'Separation Techniques', urdu:'علیحدگی کی تکنیکیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch17-l1', title:'Filtration, Evaporation and Crystallisation', html:'<h3>Filtration, Evaporation and Crystallisation</h3><p><strong>Filtration</strong> separates an <strong>insoluble solid from a liquid</strong> (sand from water): the <strong>residue</strong> stays on the filter paper, the <strong>filtrate</strong> passes through. <strong>Evaporation</strong> recovers a dissolved solid by boiling off the solvent (salt from seawater). <strong>Crystallisation</strong> gives purer crystals: make a hot saturated solution, cool slowly, filter and dry the crystals — cooling lowers solubility so pure solute crystallises out.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch17-l2', title:'Distillation', html:'<h3>Distillation</h3><p><strong>Simple distillation</strong> separates a liquid from dissolved solids or liquids with very different boiling points: boil, condense vapours in a <strong>Liebig condenser</strong> (cold water in at the bottom, out at the top), collect the <strong>distillate</strong>. <strong>Fractional distillation</strong> separates <strong>miscible liquids</strong> with close boiling points (ethanol/water, crude oil): the <strong>fractionating column</strong> gives repeated condensation–evaporation, so the lowest-boiling component distils first. Thermometer position at the still head reads the vapour temperature.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch17-l3', title:'Sublimation, Separating Funnel and Magnetism', html:'<h3>Sublimation, Separating Funnel and Magnetism</h3><p><strong>Sublimation</strong> separates solids where one sublimes (solid → gas): heat a mixture of <strong>ammonium chloride and sand</strong>; NH₄Cl vapour re-solidifies on a cool surface. A <strong>separating funnel</strong> splits <strong>immiscible liquids</strong> (oil and water): run off the denser lower layer, closing the tap at the boundary. A <strong>magnet</strong> pulls iron from mixtures. Choose the method by <strong>property difference</strong>: particle size, solubility, boiling point, density or magnetism.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Filtration separates:', options:['two liquids','insoluble solid from liquid','dissolved salt from water','gases'], answer:1, explain:'The filter traps solids; liquid passes as filtrate.'},
{q:'The liquid passing through filter paper is the:', options:['residue','filtrate','distillate','solvent'], answer:1, explain:'Residue stays behind; filtrate passes through.'},
{q:'Crystallisation gives purer solids because:', options:['heating destroys impurities','cooling lowers solubility so pure solute crystallises','filter paper is fine','water evaporates fast'], answer:1, explain:'Slow cooling deposits pure crystals from saturated solution.'},
{q:'Fractional distillation is used for:', options:['sand and water','ethanol and water','iron and sulphur','oil and water'], answer:1, explain:'Close boiling points need the fractionating column’s repeated separation.'},
{q:'In a condenser, cold water enters at the:', options:['top','bottom','middle','still head'], answer:1, explain:'Bottom entry keeps the jacket full for efficient cooling.'},
{q:'Sublimation can separate:', options:['salt and water','ammonium chloride and sand','oil and water','iron and copper'], answer:1, explain:'NH₄Cl sublimes; sand does not.'},
{q:'A separating funnel separates:', options:['miscible liquids','immiscible liquids','solids','gases'], answer:1, explain:'Density layers let you run off the lower liquid.'},
{q:'The thermometer in distillation sits:', options:['in the flask liquid','at the still head','in the condenser','outside'], answer:1, explain:'It measures the vapour temperature entering the condenser.'},
{q:'A magnet separates mixtures using:', options:['density','magnetism','boiling point','solubility'], answer:1, explain:'Magnetic iron is pulled from non-magnetic material.'},
{q:'To get salt from seawater, use:', options:['filtration','evaporation','sublimation','magnet'], answer:1, explain:'Evaporating the water leaves dissolved salt behind.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch18', title:'Qualitative Analysis', urdu:'معیاری تجزیہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch18-l1', title:'Flame Tests for Metal Ions', html:'<h3>Flame Tests for Metal Ions</h3><p>Dip a clean <strong>nichrome/platinum wire</strong> in concentrated HCl, then the sample, and hold it in a <strong>non-luminous Bunsen flame</strong>. Colours identify cations: <strong>lithium – crimson red</strong>, <strong>sodium – golden yellow</strong>, <strong>potassium – lilac</strong> (viewed through blue glass to mask sodium), <strong>calcium – brick red/orange-red</strong>, <strong>copper(II) – blue-green</strong>. Clean the wire in acid between tests to avoid mixed colours.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch18-l2', title:'Tests for Anions', html:'<h3>Tests for Anions</h3><p><strong>Carbonate:</strong> add dilute acid → <strong>CO₂</strong> fizzes; turns <strong>limewater milky</strong>. <strong>Sulphate:</strong> add <strong>barium chloride + dilute HCl</strong> → <strong>white precipitate</strong> of BaSO₄. <strong>Chloride:</strong> add <strong>silver nitrate + dilute HNO₃</strong> → <strong>white precipitate</strong> of AgCl. <strong>Nitrate:</strong> brown-ring test with FeSO₄ and concentrated H₂SO₄. Always add the acid first to remove interfering carbonate/sulphite.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch18-l3', title:'Tests for Gases and Water', html:'<h3>Tests for Gases and Water</h3><p><strong>Hydrogen:</strong> squeaky <strong>pop</strong> with a lit splint. <strong>Oxygen:</strong> relights a <strong>glowing splint</strong>. <strong>Carbon dioxide:</strong> turns <strong>limewater milky</strong>. <strong>Ammonia:</strong> turns damp <strong>red litmus blue</strong>; white smoke with HCl. <strong>Chlorine:</strong> bleaches damp litmus. <strong>Water:</strong> <strong>anhydrous CuSO₄</strong> white → blue, or <strong>cobalt chloride paper</strong> blue → pink. Record observations precisely — colour changes are the evidence.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Sodium gives a flame test colour of:', options:['lilac','golden yellow','crimson','brick red'], answer:1, explain:'Na⁺ colours the flame golden yellow.'},
{q:'Potassium’s lilac flame is viewed through blue glass to:', options:['see better','mask sodium contamination','protect eyes','cool the flame'], answer:1, explain:'Blue glass absorbs sodium’s strong yellow.'},
{q:'Carbonate ions are tested with:', options:['silver nitrate','dilute acid, then limewater','barium chloride','flame test'], answer:1, explain:'CO₂ fizzes off and turns limewater milky.'},
{q:'Sulphate ions give with BaCl₂:', options:['blue solution','white precipitate','brown gas','no change'], answer:1, explain:'Insoluble white BaSO₄ precipitates.'},
{q:'Hydrogen is tested with a:', options:['glowing splint','lit splint (squeaky pop)','limewater','litmus'], answer:1, explain:'H₂ burns with a characteristic squeaky pop.'},
{q:'Oxygen:', options:['pops','relights a glowing splint','turns limewater milky','bleaches litmus'], answer:1, explain:'O₂ rekindles the glowing splint.'},
{q:'Anhydrous copper(II) sulphate tests for:', options:['acid','water','ammonia','chlorine'], answer:1, explain:'White → blue shows water present.'},
{q:'Calcium’s flame colour is:', options:['lilac','brick red','green','yellow'], answer:1, explain:'Ca²⁺ gives a brick-red/orange-red flame.'},
{q:'Ammonia turns damp red litmus:', options:['white','blue','green','colourless'], answer:1, explain:'NH₃ is basic, turning red litmus blue.'},
{q:'The wire for flame tests is cleaned with:', options:['water','concentrated HCl','oil','sand'], answer:1, explain:'Acid removes old deposits before each test.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch19', title:'Chromatography', urdu:'کرومیٹوگرافی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch19-l1', title:'Principles of Chromatography', html:'<h3>Principles of Chromatography</h3><p><strong>Chromatography</strong> separates mixtures of <strong>soluble</strong> substances. A <strong>mobile phase</strong> (solvent) carries the mixture over a <strong>stationary phase</strong> (paper). Components separate because they differ in <strong>solubility</strong> in the solvent and <strong>attraction</strong> to the paper: the most soluble / least attracted travels farthest. Uses: checking ink composition, food dyes, and forensic analysis.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch19-l2', title:'Paper Chromatography Method', html:'<h3>Paper Chromatography Method</h3><p>Draw a <strong>pencil baseline</strong> (ink would dissolve and confuse results) near the bottom of the paper. Spot the mixture on the line and dip the paper in a <strong>shallow solvent layer — below the baseline</strong>. Seal the chamber so the atmosphere saturates. The <strong>solvent front</strong> rises by capillary action, dragging components into separate spots. Remove before the front reaches the top; mark it immediately and let dry. <strong>Locating agents</strong> (e.g. ninhydrin for amino acids) reveal colourless spots.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-che-ch19-l3', title:'Rf Values and Interpretation', html:'<h3>Rf Values and Interpretation</h3><p>The <strong>retention factor</strong> identifies each substance: <strong>Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent front</strong> (always < 1). <strong>Example:</strong> spot travels 3 cm, front travels 10 cm → Rf = 0.3. Compare with known values run under identical conditions. <strong>Two spots</strong> = mixture of two substances; a <strong>single spot</strong> suggests purity. More spots higher up mean greater solubility in that solvent.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Chromatography separates mixtures of:', options:['insoluble solids','soluble substances','gases only','metals'], answer:1, explain:'Components must dissolve in the mobile phase to move.'},
{q:'The baseline is drawn in pencil because:', options:['it is cheap','ink would dissolve and interfere','pencil is colourful','it absorbs solvent'], answer:1, explain:'Pencil (graphite) is insoluble and will not separate.'},
{q:'The solvent level must stay:', options:['above the baseline','below the baseline','at the top','changing'], answer:1, explain:'Otherwise spots dissolve into the reservoir instead of separating.'},
{q:'Rf is calculated as:', options:['solvent ÷ spot','spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance','spot × solvent','front − spot'], answer:1, explain:'Rf = distance of spot ÷ distance of solvent front.'},
{q:'A spot travels 4 cm; the front travels 8 cm. Rf =', options:['2.0','0.5','0.25','32'], answer:1, explain:'Rf = 4 ÷ 8 = 0.5 (always less than 1).'},
{q:'Two spots on a chromatogram show the sample:', options:['is pure','is a mixture of two substances','is an element','evaporated'], answer:1, explain:'Each component forms its own spot.'},
{q:'The most soluble component travels:', options:['least far','farthest','nowhere','downward'], answer:1, explain:'Greater solubility in the mobile phase carries it further.'},
{q:'Ninhydrin is used as a:', options:['solvent','locating agent for colourless spots','drying agent','baseline'], answer:1, explain:'It stains amino acids purple so spots become visible.'},
{q:'Separation happens because components differ in:', options:['mass only','solubility and attraction to the paper','colour','temperature'], answer:1, explain:'Different partition between phases gives different speeds.'},
{q:'A single spot suggests a substance is:', options:['a mixture','pure','a gas','an acid'], answer:1, explain:'One component gives one spot (confirmed by matching Rf).'}
]}
]});

/* ================= CLASS 9 — BIOLOGY (FBISE / NBF, 10 chapters) ================= */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class9-biology', board:'federal', title:'Biology', urdu:'حیاتیات', class:'Class 9', icon:'🧬', desc:'FBISE Class 9 Biology — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch1', title:'The Science of Biology', urdu:'حیاتیات کی سائنس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch1-l1', title:'What Biology Studies', html:'<h3>What Biology Studies</h3><p><strong>Biology</strong> (bios = life, logos = study) is the science of <strong>living organisms</strong> — their structure, function, growth, origin and evolution. Major fields: <strong>zoology</strong> (animals), <strong>botany</strong> (plants), <strong>microbiology</strong> (microbes), <strong>genetics</strong> (heredity), <strong>ecology</strong> (organisms and environment), <strong>biotechnology</strong> and <strong>molecular biology</strong>. Biology connects with chemistry (biochemistry), physics (biophysics) and mathematics (biostatistics) — modern biology is deeply interdisciplinary.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch1-l2', title:'The Scientific Method: Solving a Biological Problem', html:'<h3>The Scientific Method: Solving a Biological Problem</h3><p>Biologists follow the <strong>scientific method</strong>: recognise a problem → make <strong>observations</strong> (qualitative: colour; quantitative: measured) → formulate a <strong>hypothesis</strong> → deduce predictions → design <strong>controlled experiments</strong> → collect data → draw conclusions → report. The classic example: <strong>malaria</strong> — observations of fever patterns led to the hypothesis that mosquitoes transmit it; experiments with <strong>Anopheles</strong> mosquitoes confirmed it, guiding control measures that save millions of lives.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch1-l3', title:'Careers and the Muslim Scientific Heritage', html:'<h3>Careers and the Muslim Scientific Heritage</h3><p>Biology opens careers: <strong>doctor, pharmacist, agriculturist, biotechnologist, ecologist, forensic scientist</strong>. Muslim scholars pioneered biology: <strong>Al-Jahiz</strong> wrote on animal adaptation and food chains, <strong>Ibn Sina’s</strong> <em>Canon of Medicine</em> guided physicians for centuries, and <strong>Ibn al-Nafis</strong> described pulmonary blood circulation. Studying biology is also an act of reflecting on creation — the Quran repeatedly invites observation of living things as signs.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The word biology comes from Greek meaning:', options:['study of rocks','study of life','study of stars','study of chemicals'], answer:1, explain:'bios = life, logos = study.'},
{q:'The study of heredity is called:', options:['ecology','genetics','botany','zoology'], answer:1, explain:'Genetics studies genes and inheritance.'},
{q:'A hypothesis is:', options:['a proven law','a testable proposed explanation','a random guess','the final conclusion'], answer:1, explain:'It must be testable through experiments.'},
{q:'Malaria is transmitted by:', options:['houseflies','Anopheles mosquitoes','dirty water only','fleas'], answer:1, explain:'Female Anopheles mosquitoes carry the Plasmodium parasite.'},
{q:'Quantitative observations are:', options:['descriptions of colour','measured with numbers','opinions','predictions'], answer:1, explain:'Quantitative = numerical measurement.'},
{q:'Who described pulmonary blood circulation?', options:['Ibn Sina','Ibn al-Nafis','Al-Jahiz','Al-Khwarizmi'], answer:1, explain:'Ibn al-Nafis described it centuries before Harvey.'},
{q:'In a controlled experiment, the factor kept constant is the:', options:['independent variable','dependent variable','control variable','hypothesis'], answer:2, explain:'Controls stay fixed so only one factor varies.'},
{q:'Microbiology studies:', options:['large animals','microorganisms','fossils','ecosystems'], answer:1, explain:'Microbes: bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa.'},
{q:'The last step of the scientific method is to:', options:['guess','report findings','ignore data','repeat the hypothesis'], answer:1, explain:'Results are published for verification by others.'},
{q:'Ecology is the study of:', options:['cells','organisms and their environment','genes','fossils'], answer:1, explain:'Ecology examines interactions between organisms and surroundings.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch2', title:'Biodiversity', urdu:'حیاتیاتی تنوع', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch2-l1', title:'What Biodiversity Means', html:'<h3>What Biodiversity Means</h3><p><strong>Biodiversity</strong> is the variety of life at three levels: <strong>genetic diversity</strong> (variation within a species), <strong>species diversity</strong> (number of species) and <strong>ecosystem diversity</strong> (habitats). It matters: diverse ecosystems give <strong>food, medicines, raw materials</strong> and stable services like pollination. Pakistan hosts snow leopards, Indus dolphins and juniper forests — yet habitat loss, pollution and overhunting threaten this heritage, making <strong>conservation</strong> urgent.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch2-l2', title:'Classification Systems', html:'<h3>Classification Systems</h3><p><strong>Classification</strong> groups organisms by similarities. Early two-kingdom systems gave way to <strong>Whittaker’s five kingdoms</strong>: Monera (bacteria), Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia. The modern <strong>three-domain system</strong> (Woese) uses genetic evidence: <strong>Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya</strong>. The taxonomic hierarchy runs: <strong>domain → kingdom → phylum → class → order → family → genus → species</strong> — “species” is the basic unit; members can interbreed.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch2-l3', title:'Binomial Nomenclature and Viruses', html:'<h3>Binomial Nomenclature and Viruses</h3><p><strong>Binomial nomenclature</strong> (Linnaeus) gives every species a two-part Latin name: <em>Genus species</em> — e.g. <em>Homo sapiens</em>, <em>Mangifera indica</em> (mango). Rules: genus capitalised, species lowercase, both <strong>italicised</strong> (or underlined). <strong>Viruses</strong> complicate classification: they are <strong>acellular</strong>, only reproduce inside host cells, and sit on the border of living and non-living — most systems place them outside the kingdoms.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Biodiversity includes diversity at the level of:', options:['genes, species and ecosystems','only animals','only plants','rocks and soil'], answer:0, explain:'All three levels together make biodiversity.'},
{q:'Whittaker’s system has ___ kingdoms:', options:['two','three','five','seven'], answer:2, explain:'Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.'},
{q:'The three domains are:', options:['plants, animals, fungi','Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya','land, sea, air','producers, consumers, decomposers'], answer:1, explain:'Woese’s genetic-based three-domain system.'},
{q:'The scientific name of humans is:', options:['Homo erectus','Homo sapiens','Sapiens homo','Human sapiens'], answer:1, explain:'Genus Homo, species sapiens.'},
{q:'In binomial nomenclature, the genus name is:', options:['lowercase','capitalised','in Urdu','optional'], answer:1, explain:'Genus capitalised, species lowercase, both italicised.'},
{q:'Viruses are hard to classify because they:', options:['are too big','are acellular and need hosts to reproduce','have nuclei','photosynthesise'], answer:1, explain:'They show living traits only inside host cells.'},
{q:'Species is defined as a group that can:', options:['look alike','interbreed and produce fertile offspring','live anywhere','eat anything'], answer:1, explain:'Reproductive compatibility defines a species.'},
{q:'The correct hierarchy order (large to small) is:', options:['species → genus → family','kingdom → phylum → class → order','order → kingdom → genus','family → domain → species'], answer:1, explain:'Domain → kingdom → phylum → class → order → family → genus → species.'},
{q:'Mangifera indica is the scientific name of:', options:['wheat','mango','rose','neem'], answer:1, explain:'Mangifera is the mango genus.'},
{q:'Genetic diversity refers to variation:', options:['between ecosystems','within a species','between kingdoms','of rocks'], answer:1, explain:'Differences among individuals of the same species.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch3', title:'Cell', urdu:'خلیہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch3-l1', title:'Cell Theory and Cell Types', html:'<h3>Cell Theory and Cell Types</h3><p><strong>Cell theory</strong> (Schleiden, Schwann, Virchow): all living things are made of cells; the cell is the <strong>basic unit of life</strong>; all cells arise from <strong>pre-existing cells</strong>. <strong>Prokaryotic</strong> cells (bacteria) lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles — DNA floats in the cytoplasm. <strong>Eukaryotic</strong> cells (plants, animals, fungi) have a true <strong>nucleus</strong> and organelles. Cells were first seen by <strong>Robert Hooke</strong> (cork, 1665).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch3-l2', title:'Organelles and Their Functions', html:'<h3>Organelles and Their Functions</h3><p>Key organelles: <strong>cell membrane</strong> (selectively permeable boundary), <strong>cell wall</strong> (plants; cellulose support), <strong>nucleus</strong> (controls cell via DNA), <strong>mitochondria</strong> (release energy — <strong>powerhouse</strong>), <strong>chloroplasts</strong> (plants; photosynthesis), <strong>ribosomes</strong> (protein synthesis), <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> (transport), <strong>Golgi body</strong> (packaging), <strong>lysosomes</strong> (digestion — <strong>suicide bags</strong>), <strong>vacuole</strong> (storage; large central one in plants), <strong>cytoskeleton</strong> (shape and movement).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch3-l3', title:'Plant vs Animal Cells; Movement Across Membranes', html:'<h3>Plant vs Animal Cells; Movement Across Membranes</h3><p><strong>Plant cells</strong> have a <strong>cell wall, chloroplasts and a large central vacuole</strong>; <strong>animal cells</strong> lack these but may have <strong>centrioles</strong>. Substances cross membranes by: <strong>diffusion</strong> (high → low concentration), <strong>osmosis</strong> (water across a <strong>semi-permeable membrane</strong>), and <strong>active transport</strong> (against the gradient, using <strong>ATP energy</strong>). Root hairs absorb water by osmosis; plant cells become <strong>turgid</strong> (firm) in water and <strong>plasmolysed</strong> in strong salt solution.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Cell theory does NOT state that:', options:['all organisms are made of cells','the cell is the basic unit of life','cells arise from pre-existing cells','cells are visible to the naked eye'], answer:3, explain:'Most cells need a microscope; the other three are cell-theory tenets.'},
{q:'Prokaryotic cells lack a:', options:['cell membrane','true nucleus','cytoplasm','ribosome'], answer:1, explain:'Their DNA lies free in the cytoplasm.'},
{q:'The powerhouse of the cell is the:', options:['nucleus','mitochondrion','ribosome','vacuole'], answer:1, explain:'Mitochondria release energy from glucose (respiration).'},
{q:'Chloroplasts are found in:', options:['animal cells','plant cells','both','neither'], answer:1, explain:'They carry chlorophyll for photosynthesis.'},
{q:'Lysosomes are called suicide bags because they:', options:['kill the cell','digest worn-out parts and invaders','make energy','store water'], answer:1, explain:'Their enzymes digest debris and can digest the cell itself.'},
{q:'Osmosis is the movement of:', options:['solutes only','water across a semi-permeable membrane','proteins','air'], answer:1, explain:'Water moves from high to low water concentration.'},
{q:'Active transport needs:', options:['no energy','ATP energy','light','heat'], answer:1, explain:'Moving against the gradient costs energy.'},
{q:'A plant cell in pure water becomes:', options:['plasmolysed','turgid','dead','smaller'], answer:1, explain:'Water enters by osmosis, firming the cell against its wall.'},
{q:'Who first observed cells in cork?', options:['Schleiden','Robert Hooke','Virchow','Brown'], answer:1, explain:'Hooke coined “cell” in 1665.'},
{q:'Ribosomes make:', options:['lipids','proteins','carbohydrates','ATP'], answer:1, explain:'They assemble amino acids into proteins.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch4', title:'Cell Cycle', urdu:'خلیے کا دور', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch4-l1', title:'The Cell Cycle and Interphase', html:'<h3>The Cell Cycle and Interphase</h3><p>The <strong>cell cycle</strong> is the ordered series of events from one division to the next: <strong>interphase</strong> (growth) + <strong>M phase</strong> (division). Interphase has <strong>G₁</strong> (cell grows), <strong>S</strong> (DNA <strong>replicates</strong> — chromosomes duplicate), and <strong>G₂</strong> (prepares for division). Most of a cell’s life is interphase; division is comparatively brief. <strong>Checkpoints</strong> pause the cycle if DNA is damaged — failures here can lead to <strong>cancer</strong> (uncontrolled division).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch4-l2', title:'Mitosis', html:'<h3>Mitosis</h3><p><strong>Mitosis</strong> divides one nucleus into two <strong>genetically identical</strong> nuclei. Stages: <strong>prophase</strong> (chromosomes condense, nuclear membrane breaks down), <strong>metaphase</strong> (chromosomes line up at the equator, spindle attaches), <strong>anaphase</strong> (sister <strong>chromatids separate</strong> to opposite poles), <strong>telophase</strong> (two nuclei reform), then <strong>cytokinesis</strong> splits the cytoplasm. Uses: <strong>growth, repair and asexual reproduction</strong> — your skin heals by mitosis.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch4-l3', title:'Meiosis', html:'<h3>Meiosis</h3><p><strong>Meiosis</strong> produces <strong>gametes</strong> (sperm, egg): one diploid cell → <strong>four haploid</strong> cells, each with <strong>half the chromosomes</strong>. Two divisions (meiosis I and II) follow one DNA replication. <strong>Crossing over</strong> in prophase I swaps segments between homologous chromosomes, creating <strong>genetic variation</strong>. <strong>Mistakes</strong> (non-disjunction) cause disorders like <strong>Down syndrome</strong> (extra chromosome 21). Mitosis = identical copies; meiosis = variation for sexual reproduction.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'During which phase does DNA replicate?', options:['G₁','S','G₂','M'], answer:1, explain:'S (synthesis) phase duplicates the chromosomes.'},
{q:'Mitosis produces:', options:['4 haploid cells','2 genetically identical diploid cells','2 different cells','gametes'], answer:1, explain:'One division gives two identical daughter nuclei.'},
{q:'Chromosomes line up at the equator in:', options:['prophase','metaphase','anaphase','telophase'], answer:1, explain:'Metaphase: chromosomes align at the cell equator.'},
{q:'Sister chromatids separate during:', options:['prophase','metaphase','anaphase','interphase'], answer:2, explain:'Anaphase pulls chromatids to opposite poles.'},
{q:'Cytokinesis is:', options:['nuclear division','division of the cytoplasm','DNA replication','cell growth'], answer:1, explain:'It splits the cytoplasm after nuclear division.'},
{q:'Meiosis produces:', options:['2 diploid cells','4 haploid cells','2 haploid cells','4 diploid cells'], answer:1, explain:'Two divisions halve the chromosome number twice over.'},
{q:'Crossing over causes:', options:['cell death','genetic variation','chromosome loss','no change'], answer:1, explain:'Swapped segments create new allele combinations.'},
{q:'Uncontrolled cell division leads to:', options:['healing','cancer','growth','repair'], answer:1, explain:'Checkpoint failure lets damaged cells divide relentlessly.'},
{q:'Down syndrome results from:', options:['infection','extra chromosome 21','missing gene','bacteria'], answer:1, explain:'Non-disjunction in meiosis gives trisomy 21.'},
{q:'Mitosis is used for:', options:['making gametes','growth and repair','reducing chromosomes','fertilisation'], answer:1, explain:'It makes identical cells for growth, healing and asexual reproduction.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch5', title:'Tissues, Organ and Organ Systems', urdu:'ٹشوز، عضو اور عضوی نظام', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch5-l1', title:'Plant Tissues', html:'<h3>Plant Tissues</h3><p>A <strong>tissue</strong> is a group of similar cells performing one function. <strong>Meristematic tissue</strong> (root/shoot tips) divides actively for growth. <strong>Permanent tissues:</strong> <strong>epidermis</strong> (protection), <strong>parenchyma</strong> (photosynthesis/storage), <strong>collenchyma</strong> and <strong>sclerenchyma</strong> (support; sclerenchyma is dead and lignified), <strong>xylem</strong> (water transport upward) and <strong>phloem</strong> (food transport both ways). Tissues organise into <strong>organs</strong> (leaf, root) and <strong>systems</strong> (shoot system).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch5-l2', title:'Animal Tissues', html:'<h3>Animal Tissues</h3><p>Four animal tissue types: <strong>epithelial</strong> (covers surfaces; skin, gut lining), <strong>connective</strong> (blood, bone, cartilage, tendons — supports and joins), <strong>muscular</strong> (<strong>skeletal</strong>: voluntary, striated; <strong>smooth</strong>: involuntary, e.g. gut; <strong>cardiac</strong>: heart, involuntary, never tires), and <strong>nervous</strong> (neurons carry impulses). <strong>Blood</strong> is a fluid connective tissue: red cells carry O₂, white cells fight infection, platelets clot blood.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch5-l3', title:'Levels of Organisation', html:'<h3>Levels of Organisation</h3><p>Life organises as: <strong>cell → tissue → organ → organ system → organism</strong>. Example: muscle cells → muscular tissue → <strong>heart</strong> (organ) → <strong>circulatory system</strong> (organ system) → human. Human systems include digestive, respiratory, circulatory, nervous, excretory, skeletal, muscular, reproductive and immune. <strong>Emergent properties</strong> appear at each level — a heart pumps, though no single heart cell can.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Xylem transports:', options:['food','water and minerals upward','hormones','oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'Xylem vessels carry water from roots to leaves.'},
{q:'Phloem transports:', options:['water','food (sugars)','air','minerals only'], answer:1, explain:'Phloem translocates sugars both up and down.'},
{q:'Actively dividing plant tissue is:', options:['parenchyma','meristematic','sclerenchyma','epidermis'], answer:1, explain:'Meristems at tips drive growth.'},
{q:'Cardiac muscle is:', options:['voluntary','involuntary and striated','smooth','found in the gut'], answer:1, explain:'Heart muscle is involuntary and never fatigues.'},
{q:'Blood is classified as:', options:['epithelial tissue','connective tissue','muscular tissue','nervous tissue'], answer:1, explain:'It is a fluid connective tissue.'},
{q:'White blood cells:', options:['carry oxygen','fight infection','clot blood','carry hormones'], answer:1, explain:'Leukocytes defend against pathogens.'},
{q:'The correct order of organisation is:', options:['organ → cell → tissue','cell → tissue → organ → system','tissue → system → cell','system → organ → cell'], answer:1, explain:'Cells build tissues, tissues build organs, organs build systems.'},
{q:'Sclerenchyma is:', options:['living and soft','dead, lignified support tissue','photosynthetic','dividing'], answer:1, explain:'Lignified dead cells give plants strength.'},
{q:'Neurons belong to:', options:['muscular tissue','nervous tissue','epithelial tissue','connective tissue'], answer:1, explain:'Nervous tissue carries electrical impulses.'},
{q:'Tendons join:', options:['bone to bone','muscle to bone','skin to muscle','nerve to muscle'], answer:1, explain:'Tendons are connective tissue linking muscle to bone.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch6', title:'Molecular Biology', urdu:'سالماتی حیاتیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch6-l1', title:'DNA Structure', html:'<h3>DNA Structure</h3><p><strong>DNA</strong> (deoxyribonucleic acid) is the hereditary molecule. <strong>Watson and Crick (1953)</strong>, using Franklin’s X-ray data, revealed the <strong>double helix</strong>: two strands of <strong>nucleotides</strong> (deoxyribose sugar + phosphate + nitrogenous base). Bases pair specifically — <strong>A–T</strong> (2 hydrogen bonds), <strong>G–C</strong> (3 hydrogen bonds). The <strong>sequence of bases</strong> is the genetic code. DNA is packed into <strong>chromosomes</strong> in the nucleus.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch6-l2', title:'DNA Replication', html:'<h3>DNA Replication</h3><p>Before division, DNA <strong>replicates semi-conservatively</strong>: the helix <strong>unzips</strong> (enzyme helicase), each strand acts as a <strong>template</strong>, and <strong>DNA polymerase</strong> adds complementary nucleotides (A–T, G–C). Result: two identical DNA molecules, each with one old and one new strand. Accurate copying preserves genetic information; rare <strong>mutations</strong> (copying errors) create variation — the raw material of evolution.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch6-l3', title:'From DNA to Protein: The Central Dogma', html:'<h3>From DNA to Protein: The Central Dogma</h3><p><strong>Central dogma: DNA → RNA → protein.</strong> <strong>Transcription:</strong> a gene’s code is copied into <strong>mRNA</strong> in the nucleus. <strong>Translation:</strong> ribosomes read mRNA in <strong>codons</strong> (3-base units); <strong>tRNA</strong> delivers matching <strong>amino acids</strong>, building a protein. <strong>RNA</strong> differs from DNA: single-stranded, ribose sugar, and <strong>uracil (U)</strong> instead of thymine. One gene → one protein: this is how DNA controls the cell.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The double helix model of DNA was proposed by:', options:['Darwin and Wallace','Watson and Crick','Mendel','Lamarck'], answer:1, explain:'1953, using Franklin’s X-ray crystallography data.'},
{q:'In DNA, adenine pairs with:', options:['guanine','cytosine','thymine','uracil'], answer:2, explain:'A–T with two hydrogen bonds; G–C with three.'},
{q:'A nucleotide consists of:', options:['sugar + phosphate + base','amino acids','fatty acids','two sugars'], answer:0, explain:'These three parts form each DNA building block.'},
{q:'DNA replication is semi-conservative because each new molecule has:', options:['two new strands','one old and one new strand','two old strands','no strands'], answer:1, explain:'Each parental strand templates one new strand.'},
{q:'The central dogma is:', options:['RNA → DNA → protein','DNA → RNA → protein','protein → RNA → DNA','DNA → protein → RNA'], answer:1, explain:'Information flows from DNA via RNA to protein.'},
{q:'Transcription produces:', options:['DNA','mRNA','protein','amino acids'], answer:1, explain:'A gene is copied into messenger RNA.'},
{q:'Codons are read during:', options:['replication','transcription','translation','mutation'], answer:2, explain:'Ribosomes translate mRNA codons into amino acids.'},
{q:'RNA contains uracil instead of:', options:['adenine','thymine','guanine','cytosine'], answer:1, explain:'U replaces T in RNA; A pairs with U.'},
{q:'Mutations are:', options:['always harmful','changes in DNA sequence','impossible','only in RNA'], answer:1, explain:'Copying errors alter the base sequence.'},
{q:'Genes are located on:', options:['ribosomes','chromosomes','cell membrane','cytoplasm'], answer:1, explain:'DNA packed into chromosomes carries the genes.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch7', title:'Metabolism', urdu:'میٹابولزم', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch7-l1', title:'What Metabolism Is', html:'<h3>What Metabolism Is</h3><p><strong>Metabolism</strong> is the sum of all chemical reactions in the body. <strong>Anabolism</strong> builds complex molecules (protein synthesis — needs energy); <strong>catabolism</strong> breaks molecules down (digestion, respiration — releases energy). The energy currency is <strong>ATP</strong> (adenosine triphosphate): breaking its terminal phosphate releases energy for muscle contraction, active transport and biosynthesis. Metabolism keeps every cell fuelled and repaired.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch7-l2', title:'Enzymes', html:'<h3>Enzymes</h3><p><strong>Enzymes</strong> are protein <strong>biocatalysts</strong> that speed reactions by <strong>lowering activation energy</strong> — without being used up. Each enzyme has an <strong>active site</strong> shaped for its <strong>substrate</strong> (<strong>lock-and-key</strong> / induced-fit model). They are <strong>specific</strong> (amylase digests starch only) and sensitive: extreme <strong>temperature or pH denatures</strong> them (shape lost, function gone). Examples: <strong>amylase</strong> (starch → maltose), <strong>pepsin</strong> (protein, acidic stomach), <strong>catalase</strong> (H₂O₂ → water + O₂).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch7-l3', title:'Photosynthesis and Respiration', html:'<h3>Photosynthesis and Respiration</h3><p><strong>Photosynthesis</strong> (chloroplasts): <strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong> (light energy trapped by <strong>chlorophyll</strong>). <strong>Aerobic respiration</strong> (mitochondria) reverses it: <strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy (ATP)</strong>. <strong>Anaerobic respiration</strong> without oxygen gives far less energy: in yeast → ethanol + CO₂ (brewing); in muscles → <strong>lactic acid</strong> (cramps). Together they cycle carbon and energy through life.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Metabolism is:', options:['only digestion','the sum of all chemical reactions in the body','only breathing','muscle movement'], answer:1, explain:'Every cellular chemical reaction is part of metabolism.'},
{q:'Anabolism:', options:['breaks molecules down','builds complex molecules','releases heat only','needs no energy'], answer:1, explain:'Building up (e.g. protein synthesis) consumes ATP.'},
{q:'The energy currency of the cell is:', options:['glucose','ATP','DNA','oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'ATP’s phosphate bonds store and release usable energy.'},
{q:'Enzymes speed reactions by:', options:['raising temperature','lowering activation energy','being used up','adding substrate'], answer:1, explain:'They provide an easier reaction pathway.'},
{q:'An enzyme denatured by heat has:', options:['more activity','lost its shape and function','become a substrate','multiplied'], answer:1, explain:'Shape determines function; unfolding destroys it.'},
{q:'Amylase digests:', options:['protein','starch','fat','fibre'], answer:1, explain:'Salivary and pancreatic amylase break starch to maltose.'},
{q:'The word equation for photosynthesis is:', options:['glucose + oxygen → CO₂ + water','CO₂ + water → glucose + oxygen','oxygen → glucose','water → hydrogen + oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ using light energy.'},
{q:'Anaerobic respiration in human muscle produces:', options:['ethanol','lactic acid','oxygen','starch'], answer:1, explain:'Without enough O₂, glucose → lactic acid (less ATP).'},
{q:'Chlorophyll’s role is to:', options:['store water','trap light energy','release oxygen','make glucose directly'], answer:1, explain:'It captures sunlight to power photosynthesis.'},
{q:'Aerobic respiration occurs in the:', options:['chloroplast','mitochondrion','nucleus','ribosome'], answer:1, explain:'Mitochondria release ATP from glucose using oxygen.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch8', title:'Plant Physiology', urdu:'نباتی فعلیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch8-l1', title:'Water Uptake and Transport', html:'<h3>Water Uptake and Transport</h3><p><strong>Root hairs</strong> (huge surface area) absorb water by <strong>osmosis</strong> and minerals by <strong>active transport</strong>. Water travels up through <strong>xylem vessels</strong> pulled by the <strong>transpiration stream</strong>: water evaporates from leaves (<strong>transpiration</strong>), creating suction. <strong>Transpiration</strong> is faster in heat, wind, dry air and light; a <strong>potometer</strong> measures its rate. Wilting happens when water loss exceeds uptake.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch8-l2', title:'Translocation and Mineral Nutrition', html:'<h3>Translocation and Mineral Nutrition</h3><p><strong>Translocation</strong> moves sugars (and amino acids) through <strong>phloem</strong> from <strong>sources</strong> (leaves) to <strong>sinks</strong> (roots, fruits) — both directions. Plants need minerals: <strong>nitrogen</strong> (proteins; deficiency → yellow leaves), <strong>phosphorus</strong> (roots/flowers), <strong>potassium</strong> (disease resistance), <strong>magnesium</strong> (chlorophyll — deficiency → yellow leaves with green veins), <strong>iron</strong> and <strong>calcium</strong>. Fertilisers replace what harvests remove.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch8-l3', title:'Plant Hormones and Responses', html:'<h3>Plant Hormones and Responses</h3><p>Plant <strong>hormones</strong> coordinate growth: <strong>auxins</strong> (cell elongation, <strong>phototropism</strong> — bending toward light), <strong>gibberellins</strong> (stem elongation, seed germination), <strong>cytokinins</strong> (cell division), <strong>abscisic acid</strong> (dormancy, closes stomata in drought), <strong>ethene</strong> (fruit ripening). <strong>Tropisms</strong> are directional growth responses: <strong>phototropism</strong> (light), <strong>geotropism</strong> (gravity — roots positive), <strong>hydrotropism</strong> (water).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Root hairs absorb water by:', options:['active transport','osmosis','diffusion of minerals','transpiration'], answer:1, explain:'Water moves into the concentrated root-hair cell sap.'},
{q:'Water moves up the stem in the:', options:['phloem','xylem','cortex','pith'], answer:1, explain:'Xylem vessels carry the transpiration stream upward.'},
{q:'Transpiration is fastest in:', options:['cold still air','hot windy dry conditions','humid night','rain'], answer:1, explain:'Heat, wind and dry air all speed evaporation from leaves.'},
{q:'A potometer measures:', options:['photosynthesis','transpiration rate','growth','respiration'], answer:1, explain:'It tracks water uptake, approximating transpiration.'},
{q:'Translocation moves:', options:['water up','sugars through phloem','minerals only','oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'Phloem carries food from sources to sinks.'},
{q:'Nitrogen deficiency in plants causes:', options:['purple stems','yellow leaves (chlorosis)','wilting only','no effect'], answer:1, explain:'Nitrogen is needed for chlorophyll and proteins.'},
{q:'Magnesium is needed for:', options:['roots','chlorophyll','flowers','wood'], answer:1, explain:'Mg²⁺ sits at the centre of the chlorophyll molecule.'},
{q:'Bending of a shoot toward light is:', options:['geotropism','phototropism','hydrotropism','chemotropism'], answer:1, explain:'Phototropism, controlled by auxin redistribution.'},
{q:'Fruit ripening is triggered by:', options:['auxin','ethene','gibberellin','cytokinin'], answer:1, explain:'Ethene (ethylene) is the ripening hormone.'},
{q:'Abscisic acid:', options:['promotes growth','causes dormancy and closes stomata','ripens fruit','divides cells'], answer:1, explain:'It is the stress/dormancy hormone.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch9', title:'Plant Reproduction', urdu:'نباتاتی تولید', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch9-l1', title:'Asexual Reproduction in Plants', html:'<h3>Asexual Reproduction in Plants</h3><p><strong>Asexual reproduction</strong> needs one parent and no gametes — offspring are <strong>genetically identical clones</strong>. Natural methods: <strong>runners</strong> (strawberry), <strong>bulbs</strong> (onion), <strong>tubers</strong> (potato eyes), <strong>rhizomes</strong> (ginger), <strong>suckers</strong>. Artificial <strong>vegetative propagation</strong>: <strong>cuttings</strong> (rose, sugarcane), <strong>grafting</strong> (mango — scion on stock), <strong>layering</strong>, and <strong>tissue culture</strong> (micropropagation for virus-free plants). Fast and uniform, but no variation for adapting to disease.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch9-l2', title:'The Flower and Pollination', html:'<h3>The Flower and Pollination</h3><p>A flower’s parts: <strong>sepals</strong> (protect bud), <strong>petals</strong> (attract pollinators), <strong>stamens</strong> (male: anther makes <strong>pollen</strong>), <strong>carpel</strong> (female: stigma, style, ovary with <strong>ovules</strong>). <strong>Pollination</strong> transfers pollen to stigma: <strong>self-pollination</strong> (same flower) vs <strong>cross-pollination</strong> (different plant — more variation). Agents: <strong>insects</strong> (bright, scented flowers), <strong>wind</strong> (small dull flowers, huge pollen output, feathery stigmas — wheat, maize).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch9-l3', title:'Fertilisation, Seeds and Germination', html:'<h3>Fertilisation, Seeds and Germination</h3><p><strong>Fertilisation:</strong> the pollen tube grows down the style; male gamete fuses with the egg → <strong>zygote</strong> (→ embryo); the ovule becomes the <strong>seed</strong>, the ovary wall the <strong>fruit</strong>. Seeds <strong>disperse</strong> by wind (light, winged), water (coconut), animals (hooks, tasty fruit) or explosion. <strong>Germination</strong> needs <strong>water, oxygen and suitable temperature</strong>: the seed absorbs water, the <strong>radicle</strong> (root) emerges first, then the <strong>plumule</strong> (shoot).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Asexual reproduction produces offspring that are:', options:['all different','genetically identical','half the chromosomes','always seeds'], answer:1, explain:'No gametes fuse, so clones result.'},
{q:'Potato tubers reproduce by:', options:['seeds','eyes (buds) sprouting','flowers','spores'], answer:1, explain:'Each eye is a bud that grows into a new plant.'},
{q:'Grafting joins a:', options:['root to leaf','scion to stock','seed to fruit','pollen to stigma'], answer:1, explain:'The scion (desired variety) grows on the stock’s roots.'},
{q:'Pollen is produced in the:', options:['ovary','anther','stigma','style'], answer:1, explain:'Anthers of the stamens make pollen grains.'},
{q:'The female part of a flower is the:', options:['stamen','carpel','petal','sepal'], answer:1, explain:'Carpel = stigma + style + ovary.'},
{q:'Wind-pollinated flowers typically have:', options:['bright petals','feathery stigmas and lots of pollen','sweet scent','nectar'], answer:1, explain:'They maximise catching airborne pollen.'},
{q:'After fertilisation, the ovule becomes the:', options:['fruit','seed','flower','pollen'], answer:1, explain:'Ovule → seed; ovary wall → fruit.'},
{q:'Germination needs:', options:['light only','water, oxygen and warmth','soil only','darkness only'], answer:1, explain:'These three trigger the embryo to grow.'},
{q:'In germination, the first structure to emerge is the:', options:['plumule','radicle','leaf','flower'], answer:1, explain:'The radicle (young root) anchors and absorbs first.'},
{q:'Tissue culture is useful for producing:', options:['seeds','large numbers of identical, virus-free plants','new species','fruits'], answer:1, explain:'Micropropagation clones plants rapidly in sterile conditions.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch10', title:'Evolution', urdu:'ارتقا', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch10-l1', title:'Ideas About Evolution', html:'<h3>Ideas About Evolution</h3><p><strong>Evolution</strong> is change in inherited traits of populations over generations. <strong>Lamarck</strong> proposed <strong>inheritance of acquired characteristics</strong> (giraffes stretching necks) — disproved, since body changes are not inherited. <strong>Darwin and Wallace</strong> proposed <strong>natural selection</strong>: variation exists → more offspring than can survive → <strong>struggle for existence</strong> → best-adapted survive (<strong>survival of the fittest</strong>) → pass on useful traits. Over time, populations evolve; given long enough, new species form.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch10-l2', title:'Evidence for Evolution', html:'<h3>Evidence for Evolution</h3><p>Evidence: <strong>fossils</strong> (transitional forms like <em>Archaeopteryx</em>, reptile–bird link; horse series), <strong>homologous structures</strong> (same bone pattern in human arm, bat wing, whale flipper — common ancestor), <strong>vestigial organs</strong> (human appendix, whale hip bones), <strong>embryology</strong> (vertebrate embryos look alike), <strong>biochemistry/DNA</strong> (closer DNA = closer relation; humans share ~98% DNA with chimpanzees), and observed evolution (antibiotic-resistant bacteria, pesticide-resistant insects).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-bio-ch10-l3', title:'Variation, Selection and Speciation', html:'<h3>Variation, Selection and Speciation</h3><p><strong>Variation</strong> comes from <strong>mutation</strong> and <strong>sexual reproduction</strong> (crossing over, independent assortment). <strong>Natural selection</strong> acts on it: e.g. industrial melanism — dark peppered moths survived better on sooty trees. <strong>Artificial selection</strong> (humans choosing traits) created dog breeds and high-yield wheat. <strong>Speciation</strong> occurs when populations become reproductively isolated (e.g. by geography) and diverge until they cannot interbreed — Darwin’s finches on the Galápagos are the classic example.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Evolution is:', options:['change in an individual’s lifetime','change in inherited traits of populations over generations','extinction only','growth of an organism'], answer:1, explain:'Populations evolve; individuals develop.'},
{q:'Lamarck proposed:', options:['natural selection','inheritance of acquired characteristics','genetics','DNA structure'], answer:1, explain:'Use-and-disuse inheritance — later disproved.'},
{q:'“Survival of the fittest” means:', options:['strongest always win','best-adapted individuals reproduce more','only large animals survive','fitness training'], answer:1, explain:'Reproductive success of the well-adapted drives evolution.'},
{q:'Archaeopteryx is evidence as a:', options:['living fossil','transitional form between reptiles and birds','mammal','plant'], answer:1, explain:'It had feathers and teeth — a reptile–bird link.'},
{q:'Homologous structures show:', options:['same function','common ancestry','convergent evolution','no relation'], answer:1, explain:'Same underlying structure, different uses, one ancestor.'},
{q:'Humans share about ___ of DNA with chimpanzees:', options:['50%','75%','98%','100%'], answer:2, explain:'Molecular evidence of close common ancestry.'},
{q:'Antibiotic resistance in bacteria is an example of:', options:['artificial selection','natural selection in action','Lamarckism','extinction'], answer:1, explain:'Resistant variants survive treatment and multiply.'},
{q:'Variation mainly comes from:', options:['exercise','mutation and sexual reproduction','eating well','ageing'], answer:1, explain:'These create the differences selection acts upon.'},
{q:'Speciation needs:', options:['more food','reproductive isolation of populations','bigger bodies','migration only'], answer:1, explain:'Isolated populations diverge until they cannot interbreed.'},
{q:'Darwin’s finches illustrate:', options:['extinction','adaptive radiation on the Galápagos','artificial selection','fossils'], answer:1, explain:'One ancestor diversified into many beak forms.'}
]}
]});

