window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class9-mathematics', board:'federal', title:'Mathematics', urdu:'ریاضی', class:'Class 9', icon:'📐', desc:'FBISE Class 9 Mathematics — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch1', title:'Real Numbers', urdu:'حقیقی اعداد', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch1-l1', title:'Rational and Irrational Numbers', html:'<h3>Rational and Irrational Numbers</h3><p><strong>Rational numbers</strong> can be written as p/q (q ≠ 0): integers, terminating and recurring decimals (0.333… = 1/3). <strong>Irrational numbers</strong> cannot: non-terminating, non-recurring decimals like <strong>√2, √3, π</strong>. Together they form the <strong>real numbers</strong>. <strong>Proof sketch:</strong> if √2 = p/q in lowest terms, then p² = 2q² forces p even, then q even — contradiction. Real numbers fill the <strong>number line</strong> completely.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch1-l2', title:'Laws of Exponents and Radicals', html:'<h3>Laws of Exponents and Radicals</h3><p><strong>Laws:</strong> aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; (ab)ⁿ = aⁿbⁿ; a⁰ = 1 (a ≠ 0); a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. <strong>Radicals:</strong> √a = a^(1/2); ∛a = a^(1/3). Simplify by extracting perfect squares: <strong>√50 = √(25×2) = 5√2</strong>. <strong>Rationalise</strong> denominators: 1/√2 = √2/2 (multiply top and bottom by √2). <strong>Example:</strong> (2³)² × 2⁻¹ = 2⁶⁻¹ = 2⁵ = 32.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch1-l3', title:'Scientific Notation and Number Systems', html:'<h3>Scientific Notation and Number Systems</h3><p><strong>Scientific notation:</strong> a × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ a < 10 — e.g. 6,020,000 = 6.02 × 10⁶; 0.00045 = 4.5 × 10⁻⁴. Used for huge/small quantities (Avogadro’s number, atomic sizes). Number systems nest: <strong>natural ⊂ whole ⊂ integers ⊂ rational ⊂ real</strong>, plus irrationals completing the reals. <strong>Absolute value</strong> |x| is distance from zero: |−7| = 7. Real-number properties: closure, commutativity, associativity, distributivity, identities (0, 1) and inverses.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Which is an irrational number?', options:['0.75','22/7','√2','−5'], answer:2, explain:'√2 is non-terminating and non-recurring.'},
{q:'aᵐ × aⁿ equals:', options:['aᵐⁿ','aᵐ⁺ⁿ','aᵐ⁻ⁿ','(am)ⁿ'], answer:1, explain:'Add exponents when multiplying same bases.'},
{q:'(3²)³ equals:', options:['3⁵','3⁶','9⁶','3⁸'], answer:1, explain:'(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ: 3⁶ = 729.'},
{q:'5⁻² equals:', options:['−25','−10','1/25','25'], answer:2, explain:'a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ, so 1/25.'},
{q:'√72 simplified is:', options:['6√2','8√3','36√2','7√2'], answer:0, explain:'√72 = √(36×2) = 6√2.'},
{q:'Rationalising 1/√3 gives:', options:['3/√3','√3/3','√3','1/3'], answer:1, explain:'Multiply by √3/√3: √3/3.'},
{q:'3.6 × 10⁻⁴ in ordinary form is:', options:['36000','0.00036','0.0036','3600'], answer:1, explain:'Move the decimal 4 places left: 0.00036.'},
{q:'|−9| + |4| equals:', options:['−5','5','13','−13'], answer:2, explain:'9 + 4 = 13.'},
{q:'0.454545… is:', options:['irrational','rational','imaginary','prime'], answer:1, explain:'Recurring decimals are rational (here 5/11).'},
{q:'The multiplicative inverse of 7 is:', options:['−7','1/7','0','7'], answer:1, explain:'7 × 1/7 = 1, the multiplicative identity.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch2', title:'Logarithms', urdu:'لوگارتھم', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch2-l1', title:'What a Logarithm Is', html:'<h3>What a Logarithm Is</h3><p>If <strong>aˣ = y</strong>, then <strong>x = log_a(y)</strong> — the logarithm is the <strong>exponent</strong>. So log₁₀(1000) = 3 because 10³ = 1000. <strong>Common logarithms</strong> use base 10 (written log). Key values: log 1 = 0, log 10 = 1, log 100 = 2. Logarithms turn <strong>multiplication into addition</strong>, which is why they once powered all scientific calculation (slide rules, log tables).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch2-l2', title:'Laws of Logarithms', html:'<h3>Laws of Logarithms</h3><p><strong>Product:</strong> log(mn) = log m + log n. <strong>Quotient:</strong> log(m/n) = log m − log n. <strong>Power:</strong> log(mⁿ) = n·log m. Also log_a(a) = 1 and log_a(1) = 0. <strong>Example:</strong> log 2 + log 5 = log 10 = 1. <strong>Change of base:</strong> log_a(b) = log(b)/log(a). <strong>Common trap:</strong> log(m + n) does NOT split — there is no such law.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch2-l3', title:'Characteristic, Mantissa and Antilog', html:'<h3>Characteristic, Mantissa and Antilog</h3><p>Write log N = <strong>characteristic</strong> (integer part) + <strong>mantissa</strong> (decimal part, always positive, from tables). The characteristic = (digits before decimal) − 1; for 0.0045 it is −3 (written 3̄). <strong>Example:</strong> log 452 = 2.6551. The <strong>antilog</strong> reverses it: if log x = 2.6551, then x = antilog(2.6551) = 452. Before calculators, scientists multiplied huge numbers via log → add → antilog.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'If 2ˣ = 32, then x =', options:['4','5','6','16'], answer:1, explain:'log₂(32) = 5 since 2⁵ = 32.'},
{q:'log₁₀(100000) equals:', options:['4','5','6','10'], answer:1, explain:'10⁵ = 100000.'},
{q:'log(mn) equals:', options:['log m × log n','log m + log n','log m − log n','(log m)ⁿ'], answer:1, explain:'The product law of logarithms.'},
{q:'log(8) − log(2) equals:', options:['log 6','log 4','log 10','4'], answer:1, explain:'Quotient law: log(8/2) = log 4.'},
{q:'log(5³) equals:', options:['3 + log 5','3·log 5','log 15','log 5 ÷ 3'], answer:1, explain:'Power law: log(mⁿ) = n·log m.'},
{q:'log 1 (any base) equals:', options:['1','0','10','undefined'], answer:1, explain:'a⁰ = 1, so log(1) = 0.'},
{q:'In log 452 = 2.6551, the characteristic is:', options:['0.6551','2','452','6'], answer:1, explain:'The integer part, 2 (digits − 1).'},
{q:'The mantissa is always:', options:['negative','positive','zero','an integer'], answer:1, explain:'Tables give mantissa as a positive decimal.'},
{q:'Antilog reverses:', options:['addition','the logarithm','squaring','division'], answer:1, explain:'If log x = y, then x = antilog(y).'},
{q:'Which has NO logarithm law?', options:['log(mn)','log(m/n)','log(mⁿ)','log(m+n)'], answer:3, explain:'Sums inside a log cannot be split.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch3', title:'Sets and Relations', urdu:'سیٹ اور تعلق', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch3-l1', title:'Sets and Notation', html:'<h3>Sets and Notation</h3><p>A <strong>set</strong> is a well-defined collection of distinct objects (<strong>elements</strong>), written in braces: A = {1, 2, 3}. <strong>∈</strong> means “is an element of”. Sets are described by <strong>listing</strong>, <strong>set-builder</strong> ({x | x is even}) or <strong>Venn diagrams</strong>. Special sets: <strong>∅</strong> (empty), <strong>U</strong> (universal), <strong>N, W, Z, Q, R</strong>. <strong>Cardinality</strong> n(A) counts elements. A set with n elements has <strong>2ⁿ subsets</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch3-l2', title:'Operations on Sets', html:'<h3>Operations on Sets</h3><p><strong>Union</strong> A ∪ B: elements in A or B. <strong>Intersection</strong> A ∩ B: in both. <strong>Difference</strong> A − B: in A but not B. <strong>Complement</strong> A′: in U but not A. <strong>Disjoint</strong> sets share nothing (A ∩ B = ∅). <strong>De Morgan’s laws:</strong> (A ∪ B)′ = A′ ∩ B′ and (A ∩ B)′ = A′ ∪ B′. <strong>Inclusion–exclusion:</strong> n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B) — essential for survey problems with Venn diagrams.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch3-l3', title:'Relations and Functions', html:'<h3>Relations and Functions</h3><p>The <strong>Cartesian product</strong> A × B is the set of all ordered pairs (a, b). A <strong>relation</strong> is any subset of A × B. A <strong>function</strong> is a relation where each input has <strong>exactly one</strong> output (vertical-line test on graphs). <strong>Domain</strong> = all inputs; <strong>range</strong> = actual outputs. <strong>Example:</strong> f(x) = 2x + 1 has domain all reals; f(3) = 7. Types: <strong>one-to-one, onto, bijective</strong> (both) — only bijective functions have inverses.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Which is a well-defined set?', options:['tall students','{2, 4, 6, 8}','nice books','big cities'], answer:1, explain:'Membership must be unambiguous.'},
{q:'If A = {1,2,3} and B = {3,4}, then A ∪ B =', options:['{3}','{1,2,3,4}','{1,2}','{4}'], answer:1, explain:'Union collects elements from both.'},
{q:'A ∩ B for the sets above is:', options:['{3}','{1,2,3,4}','∅','{1,2,4}'], answer:0, explain:'Only 3 is common to both.'},
{q:'A set with 4 elements has ___ subsets:', options:['8','12','16','4'], answer:2, explain:'2ⁿ = 2⁴ = 16.'},
{q:'De Morgan’s law: (A ∪ B)′ equals:', options:['A′ ∪ B′','A′ ∩ B′','A ∩ B','A ∪ B'], answer:1, explain:'Complement of union = intersection of complements.'},
{q:'n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B) is used to:', options:['avoid double-counting','count subsets','find complements','draw axes'], answer:0, explain:'Shared elements are counted twice without the correction.'},
{q:'A relation is a function if each input has:', options:['at least one output','exactly one output','no output','two outputs'], answer:1, explain:'The defining property of a function.'},
{q:'For f(x) = 3x − 2, f(4) =', options:['10','12','6','14'], answer:0, explain:'3×4 − 2 = 10.'},
{q:'A × B where A = {1,2}, B = {a} is:', options:['{(1,a),(2,a)}','{(1,2),(a)}','{1,2,a}','{(1,a)}'], answer:0, explain:'All ordered pairs: (1,a) and (2,a).'},
{q:'The empty set is denoted:', options:['{0}','∅','U','{}0'], answer:1, explain:'∅ (or {}) is the set with no elements.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch4', title:'Factorization and Algebraic Manipulation', urdu:'تجزی اور الجبری عمل', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch4-l1', title:'Algebraic Identities', html:'<h3>Algebraic Identities</h3><p>Core identities: <strong>(a+b)² = a² + 2ab + b²</strong>; <strong>(a−b)² = a² − 2ab + b²</strong>; <strong>a² − b² = (a+b)(a−b)</strong>; <strong>(a+b)³ = a³ + 3a²b + 3ab² + b³</strong>; <strong>a³ + b³ = (a+b)(a² − ab + b²)</strong>; <strong>a³ − b³ = (a−b)(a² + ab + b²)</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 103² = (100+3)² = 10000 + 600 + 9 = 10609 — identities speed mental maths too.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch4-l2', title:'Factorization Methods', html:'<h3>Factorization Methods</h3><p>Strategy order: (1) take out the <strong>common factor</strong>; (2) spot <strong>identities</strong> (difference of squares, perfect-square trinomials); (3) factor <strong>trinomials x² + bx + c</strong> by finding two numbers with product c and sum b — x² + 7x + 12 = (x+3)(x+4); (4) <strong>grouping</strong>: ax + ay + bx + by = a(x+y) + b(x+y) = (a+b)(x+y); (5) <strong>sum/difference of cubes</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 2x² − 8 = 2(x² − 4) = 2(x+2)(x−2).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch4-l3', title:'HCF, LCM and Simplification', html:'<h3>HCF, LCM and Simplification</h3><p><strong>HCF</strong> of expressions = product of common factors with lowest powers; <strong>LCM</strong> = product of all factors with highest powers. <strong>Example:</strong> HCF of 12x²y and 18xy² is 6xy; LCM is 36x²y². Simplify rational expressions by factoring first: (x²−9)/(x²+5x+6) = (x−3)(x+3)/((x+2)(x+3)) = <strong>(x−3)/(x+2)</strong>. Never cancel terms — only <strong>factors</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'(a − b)² expands to:', options:['a² − b²','a² − 2ab + b²','a² + 2ab + b²','a² + b²'], answer:1, explain:'Square of a difference: a² − 2ab + b².'},
{q:'x² − 16 factorises as:', options:['(x−4)²','(x+4)(x−4)','(x−8)(x+8)','(x−4)(x−4)'], answer:1, explain:'Difference of squares: a² − b² = (a+b)(a−b).'},
{q:'x² + 5x + 6 factorises as:', options:['(x+2)(x+3)','(x+1)(x+6)','(x−2)(x−3)','(x+5)(x+1)'], answer:0, explain:'Two numbers with product 6 and sum 5: 2 and 3.'},
{q:'a³ + b³ equals:', options:['(a+b)³','(a+b)(a²−ab+b²)','(a−b)(a²+ab+b²)','(a+b)(a+b)²'], answer:1, explain:'Sum of cubes identity.'},
{q:'Factorising 3x² − 12 fully gives:', options:['3(x²−4)','3(x+2)(x−2)','(3x+6)(x−2)','3(x−2)²'], answer:1, explain:'Take out 3, then difference of squares.'},
{q:'The HCF of 12x²y and 18xy² is:', options:['36x²y²','6xy','6x²y²','12xy'], answer:1, explain:'Common factors with lowest powers: 6xy.'},
{q:'Grouping: ax + ay + bx + by =', options:['(a+x)(b+y)','(a+b)(x+y)','ab(x+y)','(a−b)(x−y)'], answer:1, explain:'Group as a(x+y) + b(x+y).'},
{q:'103² using an identity is:', options:['10609','10009','10900','10300'], answer:0, explain:'(100+3)² = 10000 + 600 + 9 = 10609.'},
{q:'When simplifying fractions, you may cancel:', options:['terms','only common factors','anything','nothing'], answer:1, explain:'Only factors common to numerator and denominator cancel.'},
{q:'x³ − 8 factorises as:', options:['(x−2)³','(x−2)(x²+2x+4)','(x+2)(x²−2x+4)','(x−2)(x−2)(x−2)'], answer:1, explain:'Difference of cubes: (a−b)(a²+ab+b²).'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch5', title:'Linear Equations and Inequalities', urdu:'خطی مساواتیں اور غیر مساواتیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch5-l1', title:'Linear Equations in One Variable', html:'<h3>Linear Equations in One Variable</h3><p>A <strong>linear equation</strong> has the form ax + b = 0 (a ≠ 0). Solve by <strong>inverse operations</strong>: 3x + 7 = 22 → 3x = 15 → x = 5. Equations with <strong>fractions</strong>: multiply through by the LCM — x/2 + x/3 = 5 → 3x + 2x = 30 → x = 6. <strong>Word problems:</strong> define the variable, translate (“5 more than twice a number” = 2x + 5), solve, and <strong>check</strong> the answer in the original statement.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch5-l2', title:'Simultaneous Linear Equations', html:'<h3>Simultaneous Linear Equations</h3><p>Two equations, two unknowns: <strong>elimination</strong> (add/subtract to kill one variable), <strong>substitution</strong> (express one variable, substitute), or <strong>graphical</strong> (intersection point of the two lines). <strong>Example (elimination):</strong> x + y = 10 and x − y = 4 → adding gives 2x = 14, x = 7, y = 3. Systems can have <strong>one solution</strong> (intersecting lines), <strong>none</strong> (parallel) or <strong>infinitely many</strong> (same line).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch5-l3', title:'Linear Inequalities', html:'<h3>Linear Inequalities</h3><p><strong>Inequalities</strong> use <, >, ≤, ≥. Solve like equations, with one golden rule: <strong>multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses the sign</strong> — −2x > 6 → x < −3. Show solutions on a <strong>number line</strong> (open circle for <,>, filled for ≤,≥) or in <strong>interval notation</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 2x − 5 ≤ 9 → 2x ≤ 14 → x ≤ 7. Double inequalities like 1 < 2x + 3 ≤ 9 are solved in one chain: −1 < x ≤ 3.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The solution of 4x − 7 = 9 is:', options:['x = 2','x = 4','x = 16','x = 3'], answer:1, explain:'4x = 16, so x = 4.'},
{q:'Solving x/3 + x/4 = 7: multiply by 12 to get:', options:['4x + 3x = 7','4x + 3x = 84','7x = 7','x = 12'], answer:1, explain:'LCM 12 clears denominators: 4x + 3x = 84, x = 12.'},
{q:'For x + y = 9 and x − y = 3, x equals:', options:['3','6','9','12'], answer:1, explain:'Adding: 2x = 12, x = 6 (y = 3).'},
{q:'Parallel lines as a system have:', options:['one solution','no solution','two solutions','infinite solutions'], answer:1, explain:'Parallel lines never meet.'},
{q:'If −3x ≥ 12, then:', options:['x ≥ −4','x ≤ −4','x ≥ 4','x ≤ 4'], answer:1, explain:'Dividing by −3 reverses the sign.'},
{q:'On a number line, x > 2 is shown with:', options:['filled circle at 2','open circle at 2, shading right','open circle at 2, shading left','no circle'], answer:1, explain:'Strict inequality = open circle; greater = right.'},
{q:'The solution of 5x + 3 ≤ 28 is:', options:['x ≤ 5','x ≥ 5','x < 5','x = 5'], answer:0, explain:'5x ≤ 25, so x ≤ 5.'},
{q:'“5 more than twice a number is 17” gives:', options:['2x + 5 = 17','5x + 2 = 17','2(x+5) = 17','x + 10 = 17'], answer:0, explain:'Twice the number plus 5 equals 17; x = 6.'},
{q:'Substitution method means:', options:['adding equations','expressing one variable and substituting','graphing','guessing'], answer:1, explain:'Solve one equation for a variable, plug into the other.'},
{q:'The solution set of 1 < 2x + 3 ≤ 9 is:', options:['−1 < x ≤ 3','0 < x < 3','−2 < x < 6','x > 3'], answer:0, explain:'Subtract 3: −2 < 2x ≤ 6, then halve.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch6', title:'Trigonometry and Bearing', urdu:'مثلثیات اور بیرنگ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch6-l1', title:'Trigonometric Ratios', html:'<h3>Trigonometric Ratios</h3><p>In a right triangle with angle θ: <strong>sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse</strong>, <strong>cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse</strong>, <strong>tan θ = opposite/adjacent</strong> — remember <strong>SOH CAH TOA</strong>. Standard angles: sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2, tan 45° = 1, sin 60° = √3/2. <strong>Example:</strong> opposite = 3, hypotenuse = 5 → sin θ = 3/5, so θ ≈ 37°. Ratios depend only on the angle, never the triangle’s size.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch6-l2', title:'Identities and Angle of Elevation', html:'<h3>Identities and Angle of Elevation</h3><p>Key identities: <strong>sin²θ + cos²θ = 1</strong>; <strong>tan θ = sin θ / cos θ</strong>. <strong>Angle of elevation</strong> is measured <strong>up</strong> from the horizontal (looking at a minaret top); <strong>angle of depression</strong> is measured <strong>down</strong> (looking at a boat from a cliff). They are <strong>equal</strong> for the same two points (alternate angles). <strong>Example:</strong> a 20 m tower subtends 30° elevation from a point: distance = 20/tan 30° = 20√3 ≈ 34.6 m.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch6-l3', title:'Bearings', html:'<h3>Bearings</h3><p><strong>Bearings</strong> give direction as a <strong>three-figure angle clockwise from north</strong>: east = 090°, south = 180°, west = 270°. <strong>Example:</strong> a ship sails on bearing 060° for 10 km: northward component = 10·cos 60° = 5 km; eastward = 10·sin 60° ≈ 8.66 km. Back-bearings differ by 180°. Scale drawings or trigonometry both solve bearing problems — draw north lines at every point to avoid angle errors.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'sin θ equals:', options:['adjacent/hypotenuse','opposite/hypotenuse','opposite/adjacent','hypotenuse/opposite'], answer:1, explain:'SOH: sine = opposite over hypotenuse.'},
{q:'tan 45° equals:', options:['0','1/2','1','√3'], answer:2, explain:'In a 45° isosceles right triangle, opposite = adjacent.'},
{q:'sin²θ + cos²θ equals:', options:['0','1','2','tan θ'], answer:1, explain:'The fundamental Pythagorean identity.'},
{q:'The angle of depression is measured:', options:['up from horizontal','down from horizontal','from the vertical','in radians'], answer:1, explain:'Depression looks downward from the horizontal.'},
{q:'A bearing of 090° points:', options:['north','east','south','west'], answer:1, explain:'Bearings run clockwise from north: 090° = east.'},
{q:'If opposite = 6 and hypotenuse = 10, sin θ =', options:['3/4','3/5','4/5','5/3'], answer:1, explain:'6/10 simplifies to 3/5.'},
{q:'The back-bearing of 060° is:', options:['120°','240°','060°','300°'], answer:1, explain:'Add 180°: 060° + 180° = 240°.'},
{q:'sin 30° equals:', options:['√3/2','1/2','1','√2/2'], answer:1, explain:'Standard value from the 30°–60°–90° triangle.'},
{q:'Angle of elevation and depression between two points are:', options:['supplementary','equal','complementary','unrelated'], answer:1, explain:'They are alternate interior angles.'},
{q:'tan θ in terms of sin and cos is:', options:['sin θ + cos θ','sin θ / cos θ','cos θ / sin θ','sin θ × cos θ'], answer:1, explain:'tan θ = opposite/adjacent = sin θ/cos θ.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch7', title:'Coordinate Geometry', urdu:'مختص جیومیٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch7-l1', title:'The Cartesian Plane', html:'<h3>The Cartesian Plane</h3><p>Points are located by <strong>ordered pairs (x, y)</strong>: x along the horizontal axis, y along the vertical. The axes divide the plane into <strong>four quadrants</strong> (I: +,+, II: −,+, III: −,−, IV: +,−). <strong>Plotting:</strong> move x units horizontally, then y vertically. The <strong>origin</strong> is (0, 0). Points on the x-axis have y = 0; on the y-axis, x = 0.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch7-l2', title:'Distance and Midpoint Formulae', html:'<h3>Distance and Midpoint Formulae</h3><p><strong>Distance</strong> between A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂): <strong>d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²)</strong> (Pythagoras in disguise). <strong>Example:</strong> (1, 2) to (4, 6): d = √(9+16) = 5. <strong>Midpoint</strong> M = <strong>((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2)</strong> — average the coordinates. <strong>Example:</strong> midpoint of (2, 8) and (6, 4) is (4, 6). Use distance to test for isosceles/equilateral triangles or to find a circle’s radius.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch7-l3', title:'Section Formula and Slope', html:'<h3>Section Formula and Slope</h3><p>A point dividing AB in ratio <strong>m:n</strong> is <strong>((mx₂+nx₁)/(m+n), (my₂+ny₁)/(m+n))</strong> — weighted average. <strong>Slope (gradient)</strong> m = <strong>(y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁)</strong> = rise/run. Positive slope climbs left-to-right; zero slope is horizontal; <strong>undefined</strong> slope is vertical. <strong>Parallel lines</strong> share slopes; <strong>perpendicular lines</strong> have slopes multiplying to <strong>−1</strong> (m₁·m₂ = −1).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The point (−3, 5) lies in quadrant:', options:['I','II','III','IV'], answer:1, explain:'x negative, y positive = quadrant II.'},
{q:'Distance between (0, 0) and (3, 4) is:', options:['5','7','12','25'], answer:0, explain:'√(9+16) = 5 (3-4-5 triangle).'},
{q:'Midpoint of (2, 6) and (8, 10) is:', options:['(5, 8)','(10, 16)','(3, 5)','(6, 4)'], answer:0, explain:'((2+8)/2, (6+10)/2) = (5, 8).'},
{q:'Slope of the line through (1, 2) and (5, 10) is:', options:['1','2','3','4'], answer:1, explain:'(10−2)/(5−1) = 8/4 = 2.'},
{q:'Two perpendicular lines have slopes:', options:['equal','multiplying to −1','adding to 0','both zero'], answer:1, explain:'m₁·m₂ = −1 for perpendicular lines.'},
{q:'A horizontal line has slope:', options:['1','0','undefined','−1'], answer:1, explain:'No rise means slope 0.'},
{q:'Points with y = 0 lie on the:', options:['y-axis','x-axis','origin','quadrant I'], answer:1, explain:'The x-axis is the line y = 0.'},
{q:'The section formula gives:', options:['a midpoint only','a point dividing a segment in a ratio','a slope','an area'], answer:1, explain:'It is the weighted average for ratio m:n (midpoint is the 1:1 case).'},
{q:'Parallel lines have:', options:['different slopes','equal slopes','slopes multiplying to −1','zero slope'], answer:1, explain:'Equal gradients never meet.'},
{q:'Distance between (1, 1) and (1, 5) is:', options:['4','5','6','√17'], answer:0, explain:'Vertical segment: |5−1| = 4.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch8', title:'Geometry of Straight Lines', urdu:'سیدھی لکیروں کی جیومیٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch8-l1', title:'Angles and Parallel Lines', html:'<h3>Angles and Parallel Lines</h3><p>Angle pairs: <strong>complementary</strong> (sum 90°), <strong>supplementary</strong> (sum 180°), <strong>vertically opposite</strong> (equal). When a <strong>transversal</strong> cuts <strong>parallel lines</strong>: <strong>corresponding angles</strong> are equal (F-pattern), <strong>alternate interior angles</strong> are equal (Z-pattern), and <strong>co-interior angles</strong> sum to 180° (C-pattern). Converses hold: equal alternate angles <strong>prove</strong> lines parallel.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch8-l2', title:'Angle Chasing in Triangles', html:'<h3>Angle Chasing in Triangles</h3><p><strong>Angle sum:</strong> interior angles of a triangle total <strong>180°</strong>. <strong>Exterior angle theorem:</strong> an exterior angle equals the <strong>sum of the two opposite interior angles</strong>. Strategy: mark known angles, use parallel-line rules and triangle sums step by step. <strong>Example:</strong> two angles 50° and 60° → third = 180 − 110 = 70°; its exterior = 50 + 60 = 110°.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch8-l3', title:'Congruence and Proofs', html:'<h3>Congruence and Proofs</h3><p><strong>Congruent</strong> figures match exactly. Triangle congruence tests: <strong>SSS</strong> (three sides), <strong>SAS</strong> (two sides + included angle), <strong>ASA/AAS</strong> (two angles + a side), <strong>RHS</strong> (right angle, hypotenuse, one side). <strong>Proof structure:</strong> state the given, the required, then a logical chain citing reasons (“alternate angles”, “SSS”). <strong>Example:</strong> the base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal — prove via SAS on the two halves.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Two angles summing to 90° are:', options:['supplementary','complementary','opposite','adjacent'], answer:1, explain:'Complementary = 90°; supplementary = 180°.'},
{q:'Alternate interior angles formed by parallel lines are:', options:['supplementary','equal','complementary','90°'], answer:1, explain:'The Z-pattern gives equal alternate angles.'},
{q:'Co-interior angles sum to:', options:['90°','180°','360°','0°'], answer:1, explain:'The C-pattern: interior angles on the same side sum to 180°.'},
{q:'The angles of a triangle sum to:', options:['90°','180°','270°','360°'], answer:1, explain:'The triangle angle-sum theorem.'},
{q:'An exterior angle of a triangle equals:', options:['the adjacent interior angle','the sum of the two opposite interior angles','90°','180°'], answer:1, explain:'The exterior angle theorem.'},
{q:'Which proves triangle congruence?', options:['AAA','SSS','SSA','AAA only'], answer:1, explain:'SSS, SAS, ASA/AAS and RHS are valid tests; AAA gives similarity only.'},
{q:'Vertically opposite angles are:', options:['supplementary','equal','complementary','adjacent'], answer:1, explain:'Opposite angles at an intersection are equal.'},
{q:'If two angles of a triangle are 40° and 75°, the third is:', options:['65°','75°','55°','115°'], answer:0, explain:'180 − 40 − 75 = 65°.'},
{q:'Equal corresponding angles prove lines are:', options:['perpendicular','parallel','equal','curved'], answer:1, explain:'The converse of the parallel-lines theorem.'},
{q:'RHS congruence needs:', options:['three angles','right angle, hypotenuse and one side','two sides only','three sides'], answer:1, explain:'Right angle–Hypotenuse–Side for right triangles.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch9', title:'Geometry and Polygons', urdu:'جیومیٹری اور کثیر الاضلاع', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch9-l1', title:'Polygons and Angle Sums', html:'<h3>Polygons and Angle Sums</h3><p>A <strong>polygon</strong> is a closed straight-sided figure: triangle (3), quadrilateral (4), pentagon (5), hexagon (6), octagon (8). <strong>Interior angle sum = (n − 2) × 180°</strong> — a pentagon’s is 540°. <strong>Exterior angles</strong> (one per vertex) always sum to <strong>360°</strong>. A <strong>regular</strong> polygon has equal sides and angles: each interior angle = (n−2)×180°/n; each exterior = 360°/n. <strong>Example:</strong> regular hexagon: exterior 60°, interior 120°.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch9-l2', title:'Quadrilaterals', html:'<h3>Quadrilaterals</h3><p><strong>Parallelogram:</strong> opposite sides parallel and equal; opposite angles equal; <strong>diagonals bisect each other</strong>. Special cases: <strong>rectangle</strong> (all angles 90°, diagonals equal), <strong>rhombus</strong> (all sides equal, diagonals perpendicular bisectors), <strong>square</strong> (both). <strong>Trapezium:</strong> one pair of parallel sides; <strong>kite:</strong> two pairs of adjacent equal sides, one diagonal bisected. <strong>Test:</strong> a quadrilateral with diagonals bisecting each other is a parallelogram.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch9-l3', title:'Circles: Basic Properties', html:'<h3>Circles: Basic Properties</h3><p>Parts: <strong>centre, radius, diameter</strong> (= 2r), <strong>chord</strong>, <strong>arc</strong>, <strong>sector</strong>, <strong>segment</strong>, <strong>tangent</strong> (touches at one point), <strong>circumference = 2πr</strong>, <strong>area = πr²</strong>. Key facts: the <strong>perpendicular from the centre bisects a chord</strong>; equal chords are equidistant from the centre; a <strong>tangent is perpendicular to the radius</strong> at the point of contact. <strong>Example:</strong> r = 7 cm → area = 22/7 × 49 = 154 cm².</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The interior angle sum of a pentagon is:', options:['360°','540°','720°','180°'], answer:1, explain:'(5−2) × 180° = 540°.'},
{q:'Exterior angles of any polygon sum to:', options:['180°','360°','540°','(n−2)×180°'], answer:1, explain:'One exterior per vertex always totals 360°.'},
{q:'Each interior angle of a regular hexagon is:', options:['60°','120°','108°','135°'], answer:1, explain:'(6−2)×180°/6 = 120°.'},
{q:'Diagonals of a parallelogram:', options:['are equal','bisect each other','are perpendicular','are parallel'], answer:1, explain:'Bisection is the defining diagonal property.'},
{q:'A rhombus has diagonals that are:', options:['equal','perpendicular bisectors','parallel','unequal always'], answer:1, explain:'They bisect each other at right angles.'},
{q:'Area of a circle with r = 7 cm (π = 22/7) is:', options:['44 cm²','154 cm²','308 cm²','616 cm²'], answer:1, explain:'22/7 × 49 = 154 cm².'},
{q:'A tangent to a circle is ___ to the radius at contact:', options:['parallel','perpendicular','equal','adjacent'], answer:1, explain:'Tangent–radius angle is 90°.'},
{q:'The perpendicular from the centre to a chord:', options:['is the diameter','bisects the chord','is a tangent','equals the radius'], answer:1, explain:'It cuts the chord into two equal parts.'},
{q:'A trapezium has:', options:['no parallel sides','one pair of parallel sides','two pairs of parallel sides','all sides equal'], answer:1, explain:'Exactly one pair of parallel sides.'},
{q:'Which quadrilateral has all angles 90° and all sides equal?', options:['rectangle','rhombus','square','kite'], answer:2, explain:'The square combines both properties.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch10', title:'Practical Geometry', urdu:'عملی جیومیٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch10-l1', title:'Basic Constructions', html:'<h3>Basic Constructions</h3><p>Use only <strong>compass and straightedge</strong> (no protractor measuring). <strong>Perpendicular bisector:</strong> arcs of equal radius from both endpoints, joined — it cuts the segment at 90° at its midpoint. <strong>Angle bisector:</strong> arc from the vertex, then arcs from the two crossing points; the join bisects the angle. <strong>Perpendicular at a point</strong> on a line and <strong>parallel through a point</strong> (copy an angle) complete the toolkit. Keep construction arcs light but visible.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch10-l2', title:'Constructing Triangles', html:'<h3>Constructing Triangles</h3><p><strong>SSS:</strong> draw the base, then arcs of the other two side-lengths from each end; their intersection is the third vertex. <strong>SAS:</strong> draw one side, construct the included angle at its end, mark the second side, join. <strong>ASA:</strong> draw the base, construct both base angles at the ends; the rays meet at the third vertex. <strong>RHS:</strong> right angle, hypotenuse arc, then the remaining side. Always label vertices and write the given data.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch10-l3', title:'Constructing Special Angles and Figures', html:'<h3>Constructing Special Angles and Figures</h3><p><strong>60°:</strong> arc from the vertex, then an arc of the same radius from where it crosses the ray — the join makes 60°. <strong>Bisect</strong> for 30°; <strong>90°</strong> via perpendicular, bisect for 45°. Combine: 75° = 60° + 15°. <strong>Circles:</strong> circumscribe a triangle via <strong>perpendicular bisectors</strong> of sides (meet at circumcentre); inscribe via <strong>angle bisectors</strong> (meet at incentre). A <strong>regular hexagon</strong> is six equilateral triangles around a centre.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The perpendicular bisector of a segment:', options:['is parallel to it','cuts it at 90° at its midpoint','bisects an angle','is a circle'], answer:1, explain:'Equal-radius arcs from both ends give the bisector.'},
{q:'To construct a triangle given SSS, you use:', options:['a protractor','arcs of the two side lengths from each base end','angle bisectors','parallel lines'], answer:1, explain:'The arcs’ intersection is the third vertex.'},
{q:'Bisecting a 60° angle gives:', options:['20°','30°','45°','15°'], answer:1, explain:'60° ÷ 2 = 30°.'},
{q:'A 45° angle is constructed by:', options:['trisecting 90°','bisecting 90°','doubling 60°','bisecting 60°'], answer:1, explain:'Half of a constructed right angle.'},
{q:'The circumcentre is found by:', options:['angle bisectors','perpendicular bisectors of sides','medians','altitudes'], answer:1, explain:'It is equidistant from all vertices.'},
{q:'The incentre is the meeting point of:', options:['medians','angle bisectors','perpendicular bisectors','sides'], answer:1, explain:'It is equidistant from all sides (inscribed circle centre).'},
{q:'A regular hexagon is made of:', options:['six squares','six equilateral triangles','four triangles','a circle'], answer:1, explain:'Central angles of 60° give equilateral triangles.'},
{q:'Practical geometry uses:', options:['protractor only','compass and straightedge','calculator','freehand'], answer:1, explain:'Classical constructions forbid measuring.'},
{q:'To draw a parallel through a point, you:', options:['guess','copy a corresponding angle','draw any line','use a set square only'], answer:1, explain:'Equal corresponding angles guarantee parallelism.'},
{q:'75° can be constructed as:', options:['60° + 15°','90° − 10°','45° + 45°','30° × 3'], answer:0, explain:'Construct 60°, bisect the 30° gap to 90° for 15°, and add.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch11', title:'Basic Statistics', urdu:'بنیادی شماریات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch11-l1', title:'Data and Frequency Tables', html:'<h3>Data and Frequency Tables</h3><p><strong>Statistics</strong> collects, organises and interprets data. <strong>Raw data</strong> is unorganised; a <strong>frequency table</strong> counts occurrences. For grouped data, use <strong>class intervals</strong> (e.g. 10–20) with <strong>class boundaries</strong> (9.5–20.5 for continuous data) and <strong>class marks</strong> (midpoints). <strong>Tally marks</strong> (groups of five: 𝥂) speed manual counting. <strong>Example:</strong> marks 12, 15, 12, 18 → 12 appears twice.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch11-l2', title:'Mean, Median and Mode', html:'<h3>Mean, Median and Mode</h3><p><strong>Mean</strong> = sum ÷ count (average). <strong>Median</strong> = middle value when ordered (average the two middles if even count). <strong>Mode</strong> = most frequent value. <strong>Example:</strong> 4, 7, 7, 9, 13 → mean = 40/5 = 8, median = 7, mode = 7. The mean is pulled by <strong>extreme values</strong>; the median resists them — median income describes a town better than mean income when billionaires live there.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-mat-ch11-l3', title:'Graphs: Bar, Histogram and Pie Charts', html:'<h3>Graphs: Bar, Histogram and Pie Charts</h3><p><strong>Bar charts</strong> compare categories (separate bars). <strong>Histograms</strong> show continuous grouped data (bars touch; area ∝ frequency). A <strong>frequency polygon</strong> joins class-mark points. <strong>Pie charts</strong> show parts of a whole: angle = (value/total) × 360°. <strong>Example:</strong> 25% of 200 students = 50 students → 90° sector. Choose the graph that fits: trends → line/bar; composition → pie; distribution → histogram.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The mean of 5, 8, 12 is:', options:['8','8.33','25','12'], answer:1, explain:'(5+8+12)/3 = 25/3 ≈ 8.33.'},
{q:'The median of 3, 9, 7, 1, 5 is:', options:['3','5','7','9'], answer:1, explain:'Ordered: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 — middle is 5.'},
{q:'The mode of 2, 3, 3, 5, 7, 3, 2 is:', options:['2','3','5','7'], answer:1, explain:'3 occurs most often (three times).'},
{q:'A histogram differs from a bar chart because:', options:['bars touch (continuous data)','it is 3D','bars are round','it has no axes'], answer:0, explain:'Histograms show continuous grouped data with touching bars.'},
{q:'In a pie chart, a value that is 1/4 of the total gets:', options:['45°','90°','180°','120°'], answer:1, explain:'(1/4) × 360° = 90°.'},
{q:'Class mark of 10–20 is:', options:['10','15','20','5'], answer:1, explain:'Midpoint: (10+20)/2 = 15.'},
{q:'Which average resists extreme values?', options:['mean','median','range','total'], answer:1, explain:'The median depends on position, not magnitude.'},
{q:'Tally marks are grouped in:', options:['threes','fives','tens','twos'], answer:1, explain:'Four strokes crossed by a fifth: 𝥂.'},
{q:'A frequency polygon joins:', options:['bar tops','class marks at frequencies','pie sectors','tallies'], answer:1, explain:'Points at (class mark, frequency) are connected.'},
{q:'Raw data means data that is:', options:['cooked','unorganised','graphed','averaged'], answer:1, explain:'Collected but not yet organised or summarised.'}
]}
]});

/* ================= CLASS 9 — ENGLISH (FBISE / NBF, 12 units) ================= */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class9-english', board:'federal', title:'English', urdu:'انگریزی', class:'Class 9', icon:'📖', desc:'FBISE Class 9 English — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch1', title:'Hazrat Muhammad Rasulullah — A Mercy for All Creation', urdu:'حضرت محمد رسول اللہ ﷺ ۔ تمام مخلوق کے لیے رحمت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch1-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit presents the Holy Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as <strong>“a mercy for all creation”</strong> (Quran 21:107) — mercy not only for humans but for animals, plants and the environment. It recalls his kindness to the weak, forgiveness of enemies (e.g. the conquest of Makkah), care for animals, and teaching that even planting a tree is charity. <strong>Central idea:</strong> true greatness lies in compassion; following his example builds a just, caring society.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch1-l2', title:'Vocabulary and Reading Skills', html:'<h3>Vocabulary and Reading Skills</h3><p>Key words: <strong>mercy</strong> (compassion), <strong>creation</strong> (all that is made), <strong>forgiveness</strong>, <strong>compassion</strong>, <strong>embodiment</strong> (living example). Reading skill: <strong>skimming</strong> for the main idea, <strong>scanning</strong> for details. <strong>Context clues</strong> unlock unknown words — the sentence around “mercy” shows it means kindness. Practise <strong>reference words</strong>: “he”, “this” point back to earlier nouns.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch1-l3', title:'Grammar: Tenses Review', html:'<h3>Grammar: Tenses Review</h3><p><strong>Present simple</strong> for habits and universal truths: “He <em>forgives</em> his enemies.” <strong>Past simple</strong> for completed events: “He <em>migrated</em> to Madinah.” <strong>Present perfect</strong> for past actions with present relevance: “His teachings <em>have guided</em> millions.” <strong>Signal words:</strong> every day → present simple; yesterday → past simple; since/for → present perfect. Keep tense <strong>consistent</strong> within a paragraph.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The unit describes the Prophet ﷺ as a mercy for:', options:['Muslims only','all creation','Arabs only','his family only'], answer:1, explain:'Quran 21:107 — mercy for all creation.'},
{q:'Skimming is reading to find the:', options:['every detail','main idea quickly','difficult words','spelling'], answer:1, explain:'Skimming gets the gist; scanning finds specifics.'},
{q:'“He forgives his enemies” is in the:', options:['past simple','present simple','future','present perfect'], answer:1, explain:'Habitual truth → present simple.'},
{q:'“His teachings have guided millions” is:', options:['past simple','present perfect','present continuous','past perfect'], answer:1, explain:'Past action with present relevance → present perfect.'},
{q:'The word “embodiment” means:', options:['hiding','a living example','destruction','weakness'], answer:1, explain:'One who embodies a quality shows it fully.'},
{q:'“Yesterday” signals the:', options:['present simple','past simple','future','present perfect'], answer:1, explain:'Finished past time → past simple.'},
{q:'A reference word like “he” points to:', options:['the future','an earlier noun','a verb','nothing'], answer:1, explain:'Pronouns refer back to previously named nouns.'},
{q:'Planting a tree, in the unit’s spirit, is:', options:['wasteful','an act of charity','forbidden','useless'], answer:1, explain:'Care for creation is presented as charity.'},
{q:'Context clues help you:', options:['skip reading','guess word meanings from surroundings','memorise grammar','write faster'], answer:1, explain:'Surrounding sentences reveal an unknown word’s sense.'},
{q:'Which sentence keeps tense consistent?', options:['He came and forgives','He came and forgave','He comes and forgave','He will come and forgave'], answer:1, explain:'Both verbs in past simple match.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch2', title:'The Art of Muslim Women’s Entrepreneurship', urdu:'مسلم خواتین کی کاروباری مہارت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch2-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit celebrates <strong>Muslim women entrepreneurs</strong> — from <strong>Hazrat Khadijah (RA)</strong>, the successful merchant who employed the Prophet ﷺ, to modern women running online businesses from home. <strong>Central idea:</strong> Islam honours women’s economic participation; with skill, honesty (<strong>amanah</strong>) and digital tools, women can earn while managing family life. Entrepreneurship needs <strong>idea + courage + persistence</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch2-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Business Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Business Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>entrepreneur</strong> (one who starts a business), <strong>enterprise</strong>, <strong>investment</strong>, <strong>profit</strong>, <strong>customer</strong>, <strong>marketing</strong>. <strong>Word formation:</strong> entrepreneur → entrepreneurship; employ → employment; create → creative → creativity. <strong>Synonyms:</strong> venture ≈ enterprise; earnings ≈ income. <strong>Antonyms:</strong> profit ≠ loss; success ≠ failure.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch2-l3', title:'Writing: A Paragraph', html:'<h3>Writing: A Paragraph</h3><p>A good paragraph has <strong>unity</strong> (one idea), a <strong>topic sentence</strong> (main idea first), <strong>supporting details</strong> (examples, facts) and <strong>coherence</strong> (logical flow with linkers: <em>firstly, moreover, however, finally</em>). <strong>Example topic sentence:</strong> “Online business has opened new doors for women in Pakistan.” Support it with 3–4 sentences, then a closing line. Keep 80–120 words for exams.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Hazrat Khadijah (RA) is mentioned as a:', options:['scholar','successful merchant','poet','traveller'], answer:1, explain:'She ran a trading business and employed the Prophet ﷺ.'},
{q:'An entrepreneur is one who:', options:['works for others','starts and runs a business','saves money only','studies business'], answer:1, explain:'Entrepreneurs take initiative and risk to create ventures.'},
{q:'The noun form of “create” is:', options:['creative','creation','creator','creating'], answer:1, explain:'Create → creation (noun); creative (adjective); creator (person).'},
{q:'The antonym of “profit” is:', options:['income','loss','gain','revenue'], answer:1, explain:'Profit and loss are opposites.'},
{q:'A paragraph’s first sentence is usually the:', options:['example','topic sentence','conclusion','title'], answer:1, explain:'It states the controlling idea.'},
{q:'Unity in a paragraph means:', options:['many topics','one central idea','long sentences','difficult words'], answer:1, explain:'Every sentence supports the single topic.'},
{q:'Which is a coherence linker?', options:['moreover','apple','quickly','very'], answer:0, explain:'Linkers like moreover, however, finally connect ideas.'},
{q:'Amanah in business means:', options:['profit','honesty and trustworthiness','advertising','speed'], answer:1, explain:'Trustworthiness is the Islamic business ethic.'},
{q:'The synonym of “venture” is:', options:['adventure trip','enterprise','danger','journey'], answer:1, explain:'Both mean a business undertaking.'},
{q:'Supporting details in a paragraph give:', options:['new topics','examples and facts backing the idea','the title','questions'], answer:1, explain:'They develop and prove the topic sentence.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch3', title:'Modern World and Age of Digital Globalization', urdu:'جدید دنیا اور ڈیجیٹل عالمگیریت کا دور', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch3-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit explores <strong>digital globalization</strong>: the internet has shrunk the world into a <strong>global village</strong>. A student in Islamabad can learn from teachers worldwide, sell crafts online, and video-call relatives abroad. <strong>Benefits:</strong> knowledge access, e-commerce, remote work. <strong>Challenges:</strong> misinformation, screen addiction, cultural pressure. <strong>Central idea:</strong> use technology <strong>wisely and ethically</strong> — a tool, not a master.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch3-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Technology Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Technology Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>globalization</strong>, <strong>digital</strong>, <strong>innovation</strong>, <strong>connectivity</strong>, <strong>virtual</strong>, <strong>cyberspace</strong>. <strong>Prefixes:</strong> cyber- (cybercrime), e- (e-learning), tele- (telemedicine). <strong>Collocations:</strong> “digital divide”, “social media”, “artificial intelligence”. <strong>Fact vs opinion:</strong> “5 billion people use the internet” (fact) vs “the internet is harmful” (opinion) — exams test this distinction.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch3-l3', title:'Grammar: Active and Passive Voice', html:'<h3>Grammar: Active and Passive Voice</h3><p><strong>Active:</strong> “Students <em>use</em> smartphones.” <strong>Passive:</strong> “Smartphones <em>are used</em> by students.” Form: <strong>be + past participle</strong> (is written, was invented, will be launched). Use passive when the <strong>doer is unknown or unimportant</strong>: “The internet <em>was invented</em> for sharing research.” <strong>Interchange:</strong> “Bell invented the telephone” → “The telephone was invented by Bell.”</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Global village” means the world has become:', options:['a real village','interconnected by technology','smaller in size','rural'], answer:1, explain:'Instant communication links everyone.'},
{q:'Which is a FACT?', options:['Phones are bad','5 billion people use the internet','Technology ruins life','Online learning is boring'], answer:1, explain:'It is verifiable data; the rest are opinions.'},
{q:'“Smartphones are used by students” is:', options:['active voice','passive voice','direct speech','a question'], answer:1, explain:'be + past participle with the doer in a by-phrase.'},
{q:'Passive of “They launched the app” is:', options:['They launch the app','The app was launched by them','The app launches them','They were launched'], answer:1, explain:'Object becomes subject: was + launched.'},
{q:'The prefix in “telemedicine” means:', options:['against','at a distance','new','false'], answer:1, explain:'Tele- = distant, as in telephone, television.'},
{q:'A challenge of digital life mentioned is:', options:['misinformation','too many books','slow post','no schools'], answer:0, explain:'False information spreads fast online.'},
{q:'“Digital divide” refers to:', options:['a river','the gap between tech haves and have-nots','a phone model','a subject'], answer:1, explain:'Unequal access to digital technology.'},
{q:'Use passive voice when the doer is:', options:['famous','unknown or unimportant','angry','present'], answer:1, explain:'Focus shifts to the action’s receiver.'},
{q:'“The internet was invented for research” — the doer is:', options:['stated','omitted as unimportant here','wrong','the internet'], answer:1, explain:'Passive lets us skip the agent.'},
{q:'A collocation is:', options:['a grammar rule','words that naturally go together','a spelling','a tense'], answer:1, explain:'E.g. “social media”, “artificial intelligence”.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch4', title:'Nothing is Impossible — The Construction of the Brooklyn Bridge', urdu:'کچھ بھی ناممکن نہیں ۔ بروکلین پل کی تعمیر', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch4-l1', title:'Story Summary', html:'<h3>Story Summary</h3><p>In 1869, engineer <strong>John Roebling</strong> dreamed of a bridge linking Manhattan and Brooklyn; experts called it <strong>impossible</strong>. After his death, his son <strong>Washington Roebling</strong> continued, then fell paralysed by decompression sickness — yet directed the work through his wife <strong>Emily</strong>, tapping codes on her arm. Completed in <strong>1883</strong>, the Brooklyn Bridge became a wonder of the world. <strong>Moral:</strong> vision + perseverance defeats “impossible”.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch4-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Determination Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Determination Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>vision</strong> (dream/plan), <strong>perseverance</strong> (steady effort), <strong>obstacle</strong>, <strong>triumph</strong>, <strong>determination</strong>. <strong>Idioms:</strong> “against all odds”, “never give up”, “where there’s a will there’s a way”. <strong>Sequencing:</strong> first, then, after that, finally — story questions test order of events. <strong>Character sketch:</strong> describe Emily as courageous, intelligent and devoted, with evidence from the text.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch4-l3', title:'Writing: Story Writing', html:'<h3>Writing: Story Writing</h3><p>A story needs <strong>setting</strong> (where/when), <strong>characters</strong>, <strong>plot</strong> (beginning → conflict → climax → resolution) and a <strong>moral</strong>. Use the <strong>past tense</strong> and <strong>dialogue</strong> (“‘We can do it,’ he said.”). <strong>Plan:</strong> 1) outline events, 2) add descriptive details, 3) end with the lesson. <strong>Exam tip:</strong> give a <strong>title</strong> and keep the moral in the final line.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The Brooklyn Bridge was completed in:', options:['1869','1883','1901','1850'], answer:1, explain:'Fourteen years after Roebling’s dream began.'},
{q:'Washington Roebling directed construction despite being:', options:['blind','paralysed','deaf','abroad'], answer:1, explain:'Decompression sickness paralysed him; Emily conveyed his instructions.'},
{q:'Emily Roebling’s role shows:', options:['weakness','courage and intelligence','luck','wealth'], answer:1, explain:'She learned engineering and supervised the project.'},
{q:'The moral of the story is:', options:['bridges are costly','perseverance defeats “impossible”','dreams are useless','engineers are rich'], answer:1, explain:'Vision plus steady effort achieved the “impossible”.'},
{q:'“Where there’s a will there’s a way” is a:', options:['fact','proverb','question','tense'], answer:1, explain:'A traditional saying expressing a truth.'},
{q:'Story writing uses mainly the:', options:['present tense','past tense','future tense','passive voice'], answer:1, explain:'Narratives recount completed events.'},
{q:'The climax of a story is its:', options:['beginning','most exciting turning point','title','first line'], answer:1, explain:'Peak tension before the resolution.'},
{q:'Sequencing words include:', options:['apple, ball','first, then, finally','very, much','he, she'], answer:1, explain:'They order events in time.'},
{q:'A character sketch needs:', options:['only the name','traits with evidence from the text','pictures','dialogue only'], answer:1, explain:'Qualities must be supported by story details.'},
{q:'Perseverance means:', options:['giving up','steady continued effort','luck','speed'], answer:1, explain:'Persisting despite difficulties.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch5', title:'Lessons for Success (Jeff Bezos)', urdu:'کامیابی کے اسباق', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch5-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit distils lessons from <strong>Jeff Bezos’s</strong> 27 years leading Amazon: <strong>obsess over customers</strong> (not competitors), <strong>invent and willingly fail</strong> (most experiments fail — that is fine), <strong>think long term</strong> (decisions for years, not quarters), <strong>hire builders</strong>, and keep <strong>Day 1 energy</strong> (stay hungry, never complacent). <strong>Central idea:</strong> success comes from customer focus, experimentation and patience — principles any student or entrepreneur can apply.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch5-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Success Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Success Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>obsess</strong> (think constantly about), <strong>innovate</strong>, <strong>persistent</strong>, <strong>stumble</strong> (fail temporarily), <strong>frugality</strong> (careful spending). <strong>Synonyms:</strong> invent ≈ create; determined ≈ resolute. <strong>Antonyms:</strong> success ≠ failure; patient ≠ hasty. <strong>One-word substitution:</strong> “one who starts a company” = entrepreneur; “fear of failure” holds many back.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch5-l3', title:'Grammar: Direct and Indirect Speech', html:'<h3>Grammar: Direct and Indirect Speech</h3><p><strong>Direct:</strong> He said, “I will invent.” <strong>Indirect:</strong> He said that he <em>would</em> invent. Rules: <strong>present → past</strong> (“I work” → he said he <em>worked</em>), <strong>will → would</strong>, <strong>can → could</strong>, pronouns and time words shift (“today” → “that day”). <strong>Questions:</strong> He asked, “Are you ready?” → He asked <em>if</em> I was ready. <strong>Commands:</strong> “Work hard.” → He <em>told</em> me <em>to</em> work hard.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Bezos’s first lesson is to obsess over:', options:['competitors','customers','profits','ads'], answer:1, explain:'Customer obsession drives Amazon’s decisions.'},
{q:'“Day 1 energy” means:', options:['resting','staying hungry and inventive','celebrating','retiring'], answer:1, explain:'Never become complacent; keep startup urgency.'},
{q:'Indirect speech of “I can do it,” he said:', options:['he said he can do it','he said he could do it','he says he could do it','he said I could do it'], answer:1, explain:'Can → could; pronoun shifts to he.'},
{q:'He said, “I will come” → indirect:', options:['he said he will come','he said he would come','he says he would come','he said I would come'], answer:1, explain:'Will → would in reported speech.'},
{q:'The synonym of “determined” is:', options:['lazy','resolute','confused','slow'], answer:1, explain:'Both mean firm in purpose.'},
{q:'Frugality means:', options:['wastefulness','careful spending','generosity','poverty'], answer:1, explain:'Accomplishing more with less.'},
{q:'A reported question uses:', options:['inversion','if/whether without inversion','question marks','no change'], answer:1, explain:'“Are you ready?” → asked if I was ready.'},
{q:'Thinking long term means deciding for:', options:['days','years, not quarters','hours','seconds'], answer:1, explain:'Patience over quick wins.'},
{q:'The antonym of “patient” is:', options:['calm','hasty','kind','wise'], answer:1, explain:'Hasty is the opposite of patient.'},
{q:'Reported command: “Sit down,” she said →', options:['she said sit down','she told me to sit down','she asked sit down','she says to sit'], answer:1, explain:'Commands become told/asked + to-infinitive.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch6', title:'Say No to Drugs', urdu:'منشیات کو نہ کہو', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch6-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit warns against <strong>drug abuse</strong>: drugs (heroin, crystal meth/“ice”, cannabis, cigarettes) destroy <strong>health</strong> (lungs, brain, heart), <strong>families</strong> (trust broken, money wasted), <strong>studies/careers</strong> and <strong>society</strong> (crime rises). Addiction often starts with <strong>peer pressure</strong> or curiosity. <strong>Central idea:</strong> prevention is better than cure — <strong>say no firmly</strong>, choose good company, seek help early; Islam forbids all intoxicants.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch6-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Health and Choice Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Health and Choice Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>addiction</strong>, <strong>rehabilitation</strong> (recovery treatment), <strong>peer pressure</strong>, <strong>consequence</strong>, <strong>temptation</strong>. <strong>Modal verbs</strong> for advice: <strong>should/should not</strong> (“You should avoid bad company”), <strong>must</strong> (strong obligation), <strong>can</strong> (ability). <strong>Refusing politely but firmly:</strong> “No, thanks — I don’t do that.” Practise <strong>dialogue writing</strong> on resisting pressure.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch6-l3', title:'Writing: Dialogue and Formal Letters', html:'<h3>Writing: Dialogue and Formal Letters</h3><p><strong>Dialogue:</strong> new speaker = <strong>new line</strong>, speech in <strong>quotation marks</strong> with reporting verbs (said, replied, insisted). <strong>Formal letter/application</strong> format: sender’s address → date → receiver’s address → <strong>salutation</strong> (Respected Sir) → <strong>subject</strong> → body (3 paragraphs) → <strong>closing</strong> (“Yours obediently”) → name. <strong>Exam favourite:</strong> application to the principal for an anti-drug seminar.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Drug abuse harms first and most directly:', options:['roads','health — brain, lungs, heart','weather','books'], answer:1, explain:'Drugs attack the body’s vital systems.'},
{q:'Addiction often begins with:', options:['exams','peer pressure or curiosity','sports','reading'], answer:1, explain:'Friends’ pressure is the commonest entry point.'},
{q:'“You should avoid bad company” uses “should” for:', options:['order','advice','ability','past habit'], answer:1, explain:'Should gives advice or recommendation.'},
{q:'In dialogue writing, a new speaker starts on a:', options:['same line','new line','new page','new chapter'], answer:1, explain:'Change of speaker = new line in quotation marks.'},
{q:'A formal application ends with:', options:['Yours lovingly','Yours obediently','Bye','Cheers'], answer:1, explain:'Students close with “Yours obediently”.'},
{q:'Rehabilitation means:', options:['punishment','treatment to recover from addiction','arrest','fines'], answer:1, explain:'Rehab helps addicts return to normal life.'},
{q:'The salutation in a formal letter is:', options:['Hi','Respected Sir','Dear friend','Hello'], answer:1, explain:'Formal letters use respectful salutations.'},
{q:'Islam’s ruling on intoxicants is that they are:', options:['allowed','forbidden','recommended','neutral'], answer:1, explain:'All intoxicants are prohibited in Islam.'},
{q:'“Must” expresses:', options:['weak advice','strong obligation','ability','possibility'], answer:1, explain:'Must = necessity or strong duty.'},
{q:'Prevention is better than cure means:', options:['cure is easy','avoiding the problem beats fixing it','doctors are costly','medicines fail'], answer:1, explain:'Stopping drug use before it starts is wisest.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch7', title:'Mowing by Robert Frost', urdu:'مؤنگ (رابرٹ فراسٹ)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch7-l1', title:'Poem Summary and Theme', html:'<h3>Poem Summary and Theme</h3><p>In <strong>“Mowing”</strong>, Robert Frost’s speaker cuts hay with a <strong>scythe</strong>, listening to its whispering sound. He imagines what the scythe “says” — not dreamy fantasies but the <strong>simple truth of hard work</strong>: “the fact is the sweetest dream that labor knows.” <strong>Theme:</strong> dignity of honest labour; truth found in work, not idle dreaming. The poem is a <strong>sonnet</strong> (14 lines).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch7-l2', title:'Poetic Devices', html:'<h3>Poetic Devices</h3><p><strong>Personification:</strong> the scythe “whispering” is given a voice. <strong>Imagery:</strong> “the whispering scythe” appeals to hearing; sunlit hay to sight. <strong>Alliteration:</strong> repeated consonant sounds. <strong>Metaphor:</strong> the mown field as life’s work. <strong>Rhyme scheme</strong> and <strong>rhythm</strong> musicalise the labour. Exams ask: <em>identify the device and explain its effect</em> — always quote the line.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch7-l3', title:'Stanza Explanation Practice', html:'<h3>Stanza Explanation Practice</h3><p><strong>Method:</strong> 1) read the stanza twice; 2) paraphrase in simple words; 3) name devices; 4) state the theme. <strong>Reference to context:</strong> quote the lines, name the poem and poet, explain, then connect to the central idea. <strong>Sample:</strong> “My long scythe whispered” — the speaker finds meaning in the sound of honest work, showing labour itself can be poetry.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Mowing” is written by:', options:['Tennyson','Robert Frost','Wordsworth','Keats'], answer:1, explain:'The American poet Robert Frost.'},
{q:'The speaker in the poem is:', options:['sleeping','mowing hay with a scythe','fishing','travelling'], answer:1, explain:'He cuts hay and listens to the scythe.'},
{q:'“Mowing” has ___ lines (it is a sonnet):', options:['12','14','16','10'], answer:1, explain:'A sonnet has 14 lines.'},
{q:'Giving the scythe a whispering voice is:', options:['metaphor','personification','simile','irony'], answer:1, explain:'A non-human thing given human qualities.'},
{q:'The poem’s theme is:', options:['love','dignity of honest labour','war','travel'], answer:1, explain:'Truth is found in work, not idle dreams.'},
{q:'“The fact is the sweetest dream that labor knows” means:', options:['dreams beat work','real work brings the deepest satisfaction','sleep is sweet','facts are boring'], answer:1, explain:'Labour itself yields the truest fulfilment.'},
{q:'Alliteration is:', options:['rhyme at line ends','repetition of consonant sounds','comparison with like','exaggeration'], answer:1, explain:'Repeated initial consonants, e.g. whispering scythe.'},
{q:'In reference to context, you must NOT forget the:', options:['poet’s name','weather','page number','publisher'], answer:0, explain:'Name the poem and poet, then explain.'},
{q:'Imagery appeals to the:', options:['mind only','senses','grammar','rhyme'], answer:1, explain:'It paints pictures for sight, sound, touch.'},
{q:'The scythe is a tool for:', options:['digging','cutting grass/hay','fishing','writing'], answer:1, explain:'A long curved blade for mowing.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch8', title:'The Eagle by Alfred Tennyson', urdu:'عقاب (الفریڈ ٹینیسن)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch8-l1', title:'Poem Summary and Theme', html:'<h3>Poem Summary and Theme</h3><p>Tennyson’s <strong>“The Eagle”</strong> (6 lines, two stanzas) pictures an eagle <strong>clasping a crag</strong> with crooked hands, watching a wrinkled sea below, then falling <strong>“like a thunderbolt”</strong> to seize prey. <strong>Theme:</strong> power, majesty and swiftness of nature’s king of birds; the sudden dive shows strength in action. The poem’s brevity mirrors the eagle’s lightning strike.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch8-l2', title:'Poetic Devices', html:'<h3>Poetic Devices</h3><p><strong>Simile:</strong> “like a thunderbolt” (with <em>like</em>). <strong>Alliteration:</strong> “clasp… crag… crooked”. <strong>Personification:</strong> “crooked hands” for talons; the eagle “watches”. <strong>Imagery:</strong> lonely crag, azure (blue) world, wrinkled sea. <strong>Metaphor:</strong> the eagle as a monarch. Note the <strong>contrast</strong>: stillness on the crag vs violent speed of the dive.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch8-l3', title:'Appreciation and Exam Answers', html:'<h3>Appreciation and Exam Answers</h3><p><strong>Appreciation points:</strong> vivid imagery, musical alliteration, dramatic contrast, the simile’s force. <strong>Answer structure:</strong> central idea → device with quotation → effect → theme link. <strong>Sample Q:</strong> “What does the thunderbolt simile convey?” — <em>Answer:</em> the eagle’s terrifying speed and power as it strikes. Keep answers <strong>short, quoted and precise</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“The Eagle” is written by:', options:['Frost','Tennyson','Browning','Shelley'], answer:1, explain:'Alfred, Lord Tennyson.'},
{q:'The poem has ___ lines:', options:['4','6','8','14'], answer:1, explain:'Two short stanzas of three lines.'},
{q:'“Like a thunderbolt” is a:', options:['metaphor','simile','personification','pun'], answer:1, explain:'Comparison using “like”.'},
{q:'The eagle clasps the crag with:', options:['wings','crooked hands (talons)','beak','feet of steel'], answer:1, explain:'Personified “hands” grip the rock.'},
{q:'“Azure world” refers to the:', options:['sea only','blue sky','mountain','cloud'], answer:1, explain:'Azure means bright blue — the sky.'},
{q:'The poem’s central contrast is between:', options:['day and night','stillness and sudden speed','land and sea','eagle and man'], answer:1, explain:'Motionless watching vs the lightning dive.'},
{q:'Alliteration in “clasp… crag… crooked” repeats the:', options:['vowel','“c/k” sound','“s” sound','rhyme'], answer:1, explain:'Initial consonant repetition.'},
{q:'The eagle symbolises:', options:['weakness','power and majesty','fear','sadness'], answer:1, explain:'King of birds: strength, nobility, swiftness.'},
{q:'“Wrinkled sea” is an example of:', options:['simile','imagery','irony','hyperbole'], answer:1, explain:'It paints a sensory picture of the sea.'},
{q:'The eagle dives to:', options:['sleep','seize its prey','drink','play'], answer:1, explain:'The thunderbolt fall is a hunting strike.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch9', title:'Travel and Tourism', urdu:'سفر اور سیاحت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch9-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit celebrates <strong>travel and tourism</strong>: journeys broaden the mind, teach history and culture, and build the economy (Pakistan’s northern areas — Hunza, Swat, Skardu — draw visitors worldwide). <strong>Types:</strong> domestic/international; eco-tourism, cultural, adventure tourism. <strong>Central idea:</strong> travel educates and earns — but tourists must <strong>respect local culture and keep places clean</strong> (responsible tourism).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch9-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Travel Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Travel Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>itinerary</strong> (travel plan), <strong>destination</strong>, <strong>heritage</strong>, <strong>hospitality</strong>, <strong>scenic</strong>, <strong>expedition</strong>. <strong>Synonyms:</strong> journey ≈ voyage ≈ trip; tourist ≈ traveller. <strong>Prepositions of travel:</strong> travel <em>by</em> bus, <em>on</em> foot, <em>in</em> a car; arrive <em>at</em> a place, arrive <em>in</em> a country. <strong>Notice/advertisement writing</strong> often uses this vocabulary.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch9-l3', title:'Writing: Letters (Formal and Informal)', html:'<h3>Writing: Letters (Formal and Informal)</h3><p><strong>Informal letter</strong> (to a friend): warm tone, “Dear Ahmed,”, personal news, “Yours lovingly”. <strong>Formal letter</strong> (to an editor/manager): “Respected Sir,”, subject line, to-the-point paragraphs, “Yours faithfully”. <strong>Common exam letters:</strong> inviting a friend to visit the northern areas (informal); to the editor about promoting tourism (formal). <strong>Checklist:</strong> addresses, date, salutation, body, closing, name.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Tourism benefits a country by:', options:['pollution','earning foreign exchange and jobs','crowding','litter'], answer:1, explain:'Visitors spend on hotels, transport and crafts.'},
{q:'An itinerary is a:', options:['ticket','travel plan','passport','hotel'], answer:1, explain:'A scheduled plan of a journey.'},
{q:'We travel ___ bus but ___ foot:', options:['by, by','by, on','on, by','in, in'], answer:1, explain:'By + vehicle; on + foot.'},
{q:'An informal letter ends with:', options:['Yours faithfully','Yours lovingly','Yours obediently','Regards'], answer:1, explain:'Warm closings suit friends and family.'},
{q:'A formal letter to an editor needs a:', options:['joke','subject line','poem','drawing'], answer:1, explain:'The subject states the purpose crisply.'},
{q:'Responsible tourism means:', options:['littering','respecting culture and keeping places clean','bargaining hard','avoiding locals'], answer:1, explain:'Protect the places and people you visit.'},
{q:'The synonym of “journey” is:', options:['house','voyage','work','food'], answer:1, explain:'Both mean travelling from place to place.'},
{q:'Hospitality means:', options:['hospitals','warm welcome to guests','hostility','travel'], answer:1, explain:'Friendly reception of visitors.'},
{q:'“Arrive ___ Pakistan” uses:', options:['at','in','on','by'], answer:1, explain:'Arrive in + country; arrive at + specific place.'},
{q:'Eco-tourism focuses on:', options:['cities','nature with minimal harm','shopping','sports'], answer:1, explain:'Enjoying nature while protecting it.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch10', title:'Two Mothers Remembered by Joann Snow Duncanson', urdu:'دو مائیں یاد آئیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch10-l1', title:'Poem Summary and Theme', html:'<h3>Poem Summary and Theme</h3><p>In <strong>“Two Mothers Remembered”</strong>, the speaker honours <strong>two mothers</strong>: her own mother who gave her life, and her <strong>mother-in-law</strong>, who gave her “the man I love” — the speaker’s husband. <strong>Theme:</strong> gratitude toward both mothers; love multiplies rather than divides. It gently teaches respect for <strong>in-laws</strong> and the bonds that join families — a warm, human poem about thankfulness.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch10-l2', title:'Poetic Devices and Tone', html:'<h3>Poetic Devices and Tone</h3><p><strong>Tone:</strong> tender, grateful, reflective. <strong>Repetition/parallelism</strong> balances the two mothers’ gifts. <strong>Imagery:</strong> gentle domestic pictures of care. <strong>Contrast:</strong> birth-giving vs love-giving — different gifts, equal gratitude. <strong>Symbol:</strong> mothers as sources of life and love. Exams ask for the <strong>central idea in your own words</strong> — practise a 3-line summary.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch10-l3', title:'Summary Writing Skills', html:'<h3>Summary Writing Skills</h3><p>A <strong>summary</strong> condenses a text to about <strong>one-third</strong> its length: keep only main ideas, drop examples and repetition, use <strong>your own words</strong>, and give a <strong>title</strong>. Steps: read → underline key points → draft → check length and coverage. <strong>Do not</strong> add opinions or new examples. <strong>Practice:</strong> summarise this poem in 40–50 words focusing on gratitude to both mothers.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The poem honours:', options:['one mother','two mothers','three mothers','no mother'], answer:1, explain:'The speaker’s own mother and her mother-in-law.'},
{q:'The mother-in-law’s gift to the speaker was:', options:['money','the man she loves (her husband)','a house','advice'], answer:1, explain:'She raised the husband the speaker married.'},
{q:'The poem’s tone is:', options:['angry','tender and grateful','mocking','fearful'], answer:1, explain:'Warm thankfulness fills every line.'},
{q:'The theme of the poem is:', options:['revenge','gratitude toward both mothers','travel','war'], answer:1, explain:'Love and thankfulness for life-givers.'},
{q:'A summary should be about ___ of the original:', options:['half','one-third','twice','equal'], answer:1, explain:'Condense to roughly a third, keeping main ideas.'},
{q:'In a summary you must NOT:', options:['use own words','add personal opinions','give a title','drop examples'], answer:1, explain:'Summaries report the text, not your views.'},
{q:'Parallelism in the poem:', options:['confuses','balances the two mothers’ gifts','rhymes only','shortens lines'], answer:1, explain:'Matched structures give equal weight to both mothers.'},
{q:'The poem teaches respect for:', options:['strangers','in-laws and family bonds','teachers','rulers'], answer:1, explain:'It honours the mother-in-law’s role.'},
{q:'“Love multiplies rather than divides” means:', options:['love is maths','loving more people enriches the heart','love is painful','families split'], answer:1, explain:'Gratitude to both mothers enlarges love.'},
{q:'First step in summary writing is to:', options:['write immediately','read and underline key points','count words','add opinions'], answer:1, explain:'Understand fully before condensing.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch11', title:'The Metamorphosis', urdu:'کایا پلٹ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch11-l1', title:'Unit Overview and Theme', html:'<h3>Unit Overview and Theme</h3><p><strong>“The Metamorphosis”</strong> is presented as a <strong>novella extract</strong> — a story of dramatic transformation. <strong>Metamorphosis</strong> literally means a complete change of form (like caterpillar → butterfly). <strong>Theme:</strong> change is part of life; how we <strong>adapt</strong> to change — with courage and hope — defines us. The unit uses the narrative to build <strong>reading stamina</strong> for longer texts.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch11-l2', title:'Reading a Longer Narrative: Skills', html:'<h3>Reading a Longer Narrative: Skills</h3><p><strong>Strategies for long texts:</strong> preview headings, read in sections, <strong>pause and predict</strong> (“what happens next?”), track <strong>characters</strong> (who wants what?), note <strong>setting</strong> shifts, and summarise each section in one line. <strong>Vocabulary in context:</strong> guess unknown words from surrounding clues before reaching for a dictionary. <strong>Character analysis:</strong> what a character does, says and how others react reveals personality.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch11-l3', title:'Grammar: Conditionals', html:'<h3>Grammar: Conditionals</h3><p><strong>Type 1 (real):</strong> If + present, will + verb — “If you <em>work</em> hard, you <em>will succeed</em>.” <strong>Type 2 (unreal present):</strong> If + past, would + verb — “If I <em>were</em> rich, I <em>would travel</em>.” <strong>Type 3 (unreal past):</strong> If + past perfect, would have + participle — “If he <em>had tried</em>, he <em>would have won</em>.” <strong>Zero conditional:</strong> facts — “If you heat ice, it melts.” Match the type to reality.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Metamorphosis” literally means:', options:['travel','complete change of form','sleep','growth'], answer:1, explain:'Meta (change) + morph (form), as in caterpillar to butterfly.'},
{q:'The unit’s theme about change is:', options:['avoid change','adapt with courage and hope','change is impossible','fear change'], answer:1, explain:'How we respond to change defines us.'},
{q:'When reading long texts, a good strategy is to:', options:['read once fast','pause, predict and summarise sections','skip dialogues','memorise all words'], answer:1, explain:'Active reading builds comprehension and stamina.'},
{q:'Type 1 conditional: “If it rains, we ___ stay home.”', options:['would','will','would have','stayed'], answer:1, explain:'Real possibility: if + present, will + verb.'},
{q:'“If I were a bird, I would fly” is type:', options:['0','1','2','3'], answer:2, explain:'Unreal present: if + past, would + verb.'},
{q:'Type 3 conditional talks about:', options:['facts','real futures','unreal past regrets','orders'], answer:2, explain:'If + past perfect, would have + participle.'},
{q:'Character is revealed by:', options:['only appearance','actions, speech and others’ reactions','the title','page numbers'], answer:1, explain:'What characters do and say shows who they are.'},
{q:'Guessing vocabulary from surroundings is called using:', options:['a dictionary','context clues','translation','grammar'], answer:1, explain:'Neighbouring words hint at meaning.'},
{q:'Zero conditional expresses:', options:['dreams','general truths','regrets','wishes'], answer:1, explain:'“If you heat water, it boils” — always true.'},
{q:'A novella is:', options:['a poem','a short novel','a play','an essay'], answer:1, explain:'Longer than a story, shorter than a novel.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch12', title:'Good Health and Well-being', urdu:'اچھی صحت اور تندرستی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch12-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit teaches that <strong>health is wealth</strong>: good health needs <strong>balanced diet</strong> (all food groups), <strong>regular exercise</strong>, <strong>clean water</strong>, <strong>enough sleep</strong> (7–8 hours) and <strong>mental well-being</strong> (managing stress, positive thinking). <strong>Well-being</strong> covers physical, mental and social health together. <strong>Central idea:</strong> small daily habits — walk, drink water, sleep on time — build a healthy life; prevention beats cure.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch12-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Health Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Health Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>balanced diet</strong>, <strong>hygiene</strong>, <strong>immunity</strong>, <strong>obesity</strong>, <strong>well-being</strong>, <strong>sanitation</strong>. <strong>Word families:</strong> health → healthy → unhealthy; fit → fitness. <strong>Imperatives</strong> for advice: “<em>Drink</em> clean water.” “<em>Avoid</em> junk food.” <strong>Notice writing</strong> (lost/found, events) and <strong>posters</strong> on health themes use these words — exams love them.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c9-eng-ch12-l3', title:'Writing: Essays', html:'<h3>Writing: Essays</h3><p>An essay has <strong>introduction</strong> (hook + thesis), <strong>body paragraphs</strong> (one idea each, with examples) and <strong>conclusion</strong> (restate + closing thought). <strong>Outline first:</strong> “Health is Wealth” → intro → diet → exercise → sleep/mental health → conclusion. <strong>Tips:</strong> 250–300 words, varied sentences, linkers, no new ideas in the conclusion. <strong>Review exercises I–III</strong> at the book’s end revise all grammar — practise them fully.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Health is wealth” means:', options:['money buys health','good health is life’s greatest asset','doctors are rich','gyms are costly'], answer:1, explain:'Without health, nothing else can be enjoyed.'},
{q:'A balanced diet contains:', options:['only rice','all food groups in right amounts','only meat','only fruit'], answer:1, explain:'Carbs, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, water, fibre.'},
{q:'How much sleep do teens need?', options:['4 hours','7–8 hours','12 hours','2 hours'], answer:1, explain:'7–8 hours supports growth and learning.'},
{q:'“Drink clean water” is an:', options:['question','imperative','exclamation','passive'], answer:1, explain:'Commands giving advice use the imperative.'},
{q:'An essay’s conclusion should:', options:['add new ideas','restate the thesis and close','ask questions','list references'], answer:1, explain:'Wrap up without introducing new points.'},
{q:'Well-being includes:', options:['physical health only','physical, mental and social health','money only','grades only'], answer:1, explain:'True health covers body, mind and relationships.'},
{q:'The noun form of “healthy” used for the state is:', options:['health','healthiness','heal','healing'], answer:0, explain:'Health is the noun; healthy the adjective.'},
{q:'Obesity results mainly from:', options:['exercise','excess junk food and inactivity','sleep','water'], answer:1, explain:'More calories in than out stores fat.'},
{q:'An essay introduction contains a:', options:['conclusion','hook and thesis statement','summary','dialogue'], answer:1, explain:'It grabs interest and states the main argument.'},
{q:'Hygiene means:', options:['medicine','practices that keep us clean and healthy','hospital','exercise'], answer:1, explain:'Clean habits prevent disease.'}
]}
]});

/* ================= CLASS 10 — PHYSICS (FBISE / NBF, chapters 10–21) ================= */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class10-physics', board:'federal', title:'Physics', urdu:'طبیعیات', class:'Class 10', icon:'⚛️', desc:'FBISE Class 10 Physics — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch10', title:'Heat Capacity and Modes of Heat Transfer', urdu:'حرارتی گنجائش اور حرارت کی منتقلی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch10-l1', title:'Heat Capacity and Specific Heat Capacity', html:'<h3>Heat Capacity and Specific Heat Capacity</h3><p><strong>Heat capacity (C)</strong> is the heat needed to raise a body’s temperature by 1 K: <strong>C = Q/ΔT</strong> (J/K). <strong>Specific heat capacity (c)</strong> is per kilogram: <strong>c = Q/(m·ΔT)</strong> in <strong>J/(kg·K)</strong>. Water’s huge c (4200 J/kg·K) makes it a great coolant and stabilises coastal climates. <strong>Example:</strong> heating 2 kg of water by 5 K needs Q = 2 × 4200 × 5 = 42,000 J.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch10-l2', title:'Conduction, Convection and Radiation', html:'<h3>Conduction, Convection and Radiation</h3><p>Heat travels three ways. <strong>Conduction:</strong> particle-to-particle transfer in <strong>solids</strong> — metals are good conductors (free electrons), wood and air are <strong>insulators</strong>. <strong>Convection:</strong> bulk movement of <strong>fluids</strong> — warm air rises (sea breezes, room heaters). <strong>Radiation:</strong> <strong>electromagnetic waves</strong> needing no medium — the Sun warms Earth through space. <strong>Black surfaces</strong> absorb and emit radiation best; shiny ones reflect it (why tea stays hot in a shiny thermos).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch10-l3', title:'Calorimetry and Applications', html:'<h3>Calorimetry and Applications</h3><p><strong>Calorimetry</strong> measures heat exchange: heat lost by a hot body = heat gained by the cold body (+ calorimeter). <strong>Example:</strong> a hot metal dropped into water — solve m₁c₁(T₁−T) = m₂c₂(T−T₂) for the unknown c. Applications: <strong>thermos flasks</strong> (vacuum stops conduction/convection, shiny walls cut radiation), <strong>woollen clothes</strong> (trap air), <strong>refrigerator insulation</strong>, and car radiators using water’s high c.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Specific heat capacity is measured in:', options:['J/K','J/(kg·K)','W','J/kg'], answer:1, explain:'Per unit mass per kelvin.'},
{q:'Heat needed to warm 2 kg of water by 5 K (c = 4200) is:', options:['21,000 J','42,000 J','8,400 J','840 J'], answer:1, explain:'Q = mcΔT = 2 × 4200 × 5 = 42,000 J.'},
{q:'Conduction happens mainly in:', options:['gases','solids','vacuum','liquids only'], answer:1, explain:'Particle-to-particle transfer suits solids best.'},
{q:'Sea breezes are caused by:', options:['conduction','convection','radiation','evaporation'], answer:1, explain:'Warm air rises and cool sea air moves in — convection currents.'},
{q:'The Sun’s heat reaches Earth by:', options:['conduction','convection','radiation','diffusion'], answer:2, explain:'Radiation crosses empty space as EM waves.'},
{q:'Metals conduct heat well because of:', options:['heavy atoms','free electrons','low density','colour'], answer:1, explain:'Mobile electrons carry thermal energy quickly.'},
{q:'A shiny thermos keeps tea hot because shiny surfaces:', options:['absorb radiation','reflect radiation','conduct heat','trap air'], answer:1, explain:'Low emissivity cuts radiative heat loss.'},
{q:'In calorimetry, heat lost by the hot body:', options:['vanishes','equals heat gained by the cold body','doubles','is ignored'], answer:1, explain:'Energy conservation: lost = gained.'},
{q:'Woollen clothes keep us warm mainly by:', options:['generating heat','trapping air, a poor conductor','reflecting light','absorbing sweat'], answer:1, explain:'Still air in the fibres blocks conduction.'},
{q:'Water’s high specific heat capacity means it:', options:['boils easily','resists temperature change','freezes fast','evaporates fast'], answer:1, explain:'It absorbs/releases lots of heat with small ΔT.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch11', title:'Thermal Expansion and Change of State', urdu:'حرارتی پھیلاؤ اور حالت کی تبدیلی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch11-l1', title:'Thermal Expansion of Solids', html:'<h3>Thermal Expansion of Solids</h3><p>Heating makes particles vibrate more, so solids <strong>expand</strong>: <strong>linear expansion ΔL = α·L·ΔT</strong>, where α is the <strong>coefficient of linear expansion</strong>. Area and volume expand similarly (β ≈ 2α, γ ≈ 3α). <strong>Applications/problems:</strong> <strong>expansion gaps</strong> in railway tracks and bridges, <strong>sagging</strong> power lines in summer, and <strong>bimetallic strips</strong> (two metals, different α) bending with temperature — used in thermostats and fire alarms.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch11-l2', title:'Expansion of Liquids and Gases; Anomalous Water', html:'<h3>Expansion of Liquids and Gases; Anomalous Water</h3><p>Liquids expand more than solids (used in <strong>thermometers</strong>); gases expand most — <strong>Charles’s law</strong>: V ∝ T (kelvin) at constant pressure. Water is <strong>anomalous</strong>: it <strong>contracts</strong> from 0 °C to <strong>4 °C</strong> (maximum density), then expands. So lakes freeze <strong>top-down</strong>: 4 °C water sinks, ice floats and insulates — aquatic life survives winter under the ice.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch11-l3', title:'Change of State and Latent Heat', html:'<h3>Change of State and Latent Heat</h3><p>Melting, boiling, freezing, condensation, sublimation — temperature stays <strong>constant</strong> during change of state. <strong>Latent heat L = Q/m</strong> (J/kg): <strong>fusion</strong> of ice ≈ 3.34 × 10⁵ J/kg; <strong>vaporisation</strong> of water ≈ 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg. <strong>Example:</strong> melting 0.5 kg of ice needs 0.5 × 3.34×10⁵ = 1.67×10⁵ J. Steam burns are worse than boiling-water burns because steam releases <strong>latent heat</strong> on condensing. <strong>Evaporation</strong> cools (sweating) since the fastest molecules escape.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Linear expansion is given by:', options:['ΔL = α/L·ΔT','ΔL = α·L·ΔT','ΔL = L/(α·ΔT)','ΔL = α + L + ΔT'], answer:1, explain:'Extension ∝ original length and temperature rise.'},
{q:'Railway tracks have small gaps to:', options:['save steel','allow thermal expansion','reduce noise','look nice'], answer:1, explain:'Gaps prevent buckling on hot days.'},
{q:'A bimetallic strip bends because:', options:['it is weak','the two metals expand differently','it is heated unevenly','of magnetism'], answer:1, explain:'Different α values curve the strip with temperature.'},
{q:'Water is densest at:', options:['0 °C','4 °C','100 °C','−4 °C'], answer:1, explain:'Anomalous expansion gives maximum density at 4 °C.'},
{q:'Lakes freeze top-down because:', options:['ice sinks','ice floats and 4 °C water sinks','wind cools the top','fish heat the bottom'], answer:1, explain:'Cold surface water below 4 °C becomes less dense and stays up.'},
{q:'Latent heat of vaporisation of water is about:', options:['3.34 × 10⁵ J/kg','2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg','4200 J/kg','100 J/kg'], answer:1, explain:'Boiling needs far more energy than melting.'},
{q:'During melting, the temperature:', options:['rises','stays constant','falls','doubles'], answer:1, explain:'Energy breaks bonds (latent heat) instead of raising T.'},
{q:'Steam scalds worse than boiling water because:', options:['it is hotter','it releases latent heat on condensing','it is invisible','it moves faster'], answer:1, explain:'Condensation dumps extra 2.26 MJ/kg onto skin.'},
{q:'Melting 0.5 kg of ice (L = 3.34×10⁵) needs:', options:['6.68×10⁵ J','1.67×10⁵ J','3.34×10⁵ J','1.67×10⁶ J'], answer:1, explain:'Q = mL = 0.5 × 3.34×10⁵.'},
{q:'Sweating cools the body because:', options:['water is cold','evaporation removes the fastest molecules','skin shrinks','air blows'], answer:1, explain:'Escaping molecules carry away latent heat.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch12', title:'Waves', urdu:'لہریں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch12-l1', title:'Types of Waves', html:'<h3>Types of Waves</h3><p>A <strong>wave</strong> transfers <strong>energy without transferring matter</strong> — particles oscillate about fixed positions. <strong>Transverse</strong> waves oscillate <strong>perpendicular</strong> to travel (water ripples, light, rope waves). <strong>Longitudinal</strong> waves oscillate <strong>parallel</strong> to travel (sound: <strong>compressions and rarefactions</strong>). <strong>Mechanical</strong> waves need a medium; <strong>electromagnetic</strong> waves do not.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch12-l2', title:'Wave Terms and the Wave Equation', html:'<h3>Wave Terms and the Wave Equation</h3><p><strong>Amplitude</strong> (max displacement), <strong>wavelength λ</strong> (one full cycle length), <strong>frequency f</strong> (waves per second, Hz), <strong>period T = 1/f</strong>. The <strong>wave equation: v = f·λ</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> f = 50 Hz, λ = 2 m → v = 100 m/s. Higher frequency = shorter wavelength at fixed speed. <strong>Simple harmonic motion (SHM)</strong> — like a pendulum or mass on a spring — produces <strong>sine waves</strong>; its period is independent of amplitude (for small swings).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch12-l3', title:'Wave Behaviour', html:'<h3>Wave Behaviour</h3><p>Waves <strong>reflect</strong> (angle in = angle out), <strong>refract</strong> (bend on changing speed/medium), <strong>diffract</strong> (spread through gaps — most when gap ≈ λ), and <strong>interfere</strong> (superposition: crest+crest = bigger). <strong>Standing waves</strong> form on strings with <strong>nodes</strong> (still) and <strong>antinodes</strong> (max vibration) — the basis of musical instruments. <strong>Ripple-tank</strong> experiments demonstrate all these behaviours visually.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Waves transfer:', options:['matter','energy without matter','only heat','electrons'], answer:1, explain:'Particles oscillate in place; energy moves on.'},
{q:'Sound waves are:', options:['transverse','longitudinal','electromagnetic','standing'], answer:1, explain:'Air particles vibrate parallel to travel (compressions/rarefactions).'},
{q:'The wave equation is:', options:['v = f/λ','v = f·λ','v = λ/f','v = f + λ'], answer:1, explain:'Speed = frequency × wavelength.'},
{q:'A wave with f = 200 Hz and λ = 1.5 m has speed:', options:['300 m/s','133 m/s','201.5 m/s','3000 m/s'], answer:0, explain:'v = 200 × 1.5 = 300 m/s.'},
{q:'The period T relates to frequency as:', options:['T = f','T = 1/f','T = f²','T = 2f'], answer:1, explain:'Period is the time for one cycle.'},
{q:'Diffraction is greatest when the gap is:', options:['much larger than λ','about equal to λ','much smaller than λ','closed'], answer:1, explain:'Waves spread most when the gap matches the wavelength.'},
{q:'In a standing wave, points of zero displacement are:', options:['antinodes','nodes','crests','troughs'], answer:1, explain:'Nodes stay still; antinodes vibrate maximally.'},
{q:'Light is a ___ wave:', options:['longitudinal mechanical','transverse electromagnetic','sound','matter'], answer:1, explain:'EM waves oscillate perpendicular and need no medium.'},
{q:'Constructive interference happens when:', options:['crest meets trough','crest meets crest','waves stop','amplitude is zero'], answer:1, explain:'In-phase waves superpose to a larger amplitude.'},
{q:'Amplitude measures the wave’s:', options:['speed','maximum displacement','length','frequency'], answer:1, explain:'Height from rest position to crest.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch13', title:'Sound', urdu:'آواز', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch13-l1', title:'Production and Propagation of Sound', html:'<h3>Production and Propagation of Sound</h3><p>Sound is produced by <strong>vibrating</strong> objects (vocal cords, guitar strings, tuning forks) and travels as <strong>longitudinal waves</strong> through a <strong>medium</strong> — it <strong>cannot travel in a vacuum</strong> (bell-jar experiment). Speed: <strong>≈ 343 m/s in air</strong> (20 °C), faster in water (~1500 m/s), fastest in solids (~5000 m/s in steel) — denser, stiffer media transmit faster. <strong>Echo</strong> distance: d = (v·t)/2 for the round trip.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch13-l2', title:'Loudness, Pitch and Quality', html:'<h3>Loudness, Pitch and Quality</h3><p><strong>Loudness</strong> ∝ <strong>amplitude²</strong> (bigger vibrations = louder), measured in <strong>decibels (dB)</strong>. <strong>Pitch</strong> ∝ <strong>frequency</strong>: high f = shrill, low f = grave. <strong>Quality (timbre)</strong> distinguishes instruments playing the same note — different <strong>overtones</strong>. Human hearing: <strong>20 Hz – 20 kHz</strong>; below = <strong>infrasound</strong>, above = <strong>ultrasound</strong>. Prolonged sound above ~85 dB damages hearing.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch13-l3', title:'Echoes, Ultrasound and Noise', html:'<h3>Echoes, Ultrasound and Noise</h3><p><strong>Echoes</strong> (reflection) measure distances — <strong>sonar</strong> finds submarines and fish; bats navigate by <strong>echolocation</strong>. <strong>Ultrasound</strong> (> 20 kHz): medical <strong>scans</strong> (pregnancy), breaking kidney stones, industrial crack detection. <strong>Noise</strong> is unwanted sound — controlled with <strong>soundproofing</strong> (soft, porous materials absorb sound), silencers and green belts. <strong>Example:</strong> echo returns in 0.1 s → d = 343×0.1/2 ≈ 17 m.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Sound cannot travel through:', options:['air','water','vacuum','steel'], answer:2, explain:'Sound needs a material medium to vibrate.'},
{q:'Sound travels fastest in:', options:['air','water','steel','vacuum'], answer:2, explain:'~5000 m/s in steel vs 343 m/s in air.'},
{q:'Loudness depends on the wave’s:', options:['frequency','amplitude','speed','wavelength'], answer:1, explain:'Greater amplitude carries more energy — louder sound.'},
{q:'Pitch depends on:', options:['amplitude','frequency','medium','temperature'], answer:1, explain:'High frequency = high (shrill) pitch.'},
{q:'The audible range for humans is:', options:['0–20 Hz','20 Hz–20 kHz','20–200 kHz','1–10 MHz'], answer:1, explain:'Below 20 Hz is infrasound; above 20 kHz ultrasound.'},
{q:'An echo returning after 0.2 s (v = 340 m/s) means the wall is:', options:['68 m away','34 m away','17 m away','340 m away'], answer:1, explain:'d = v·t/2 = 340×0.2/2 = 34 m.'},
{q:'Ultrasound is used in:', options:['radio','medical scans','television','lighting'], answer:1, explain:'High-frequency reflections image soft tissue safely.'},
{q:'Timbre (quality) lets us distinguish:', options:['loud sounds','instruments playing the same note','high pitches','echoes'], answer:1, explain:'Different overtone mixes give each instrument its voice.'},
{q:'Sound is measured in:', options:['hertz','decibels','watts','metres'], answer:1, explain:'Loudness uses the decibel scale.'},
{q:'Bats navigate using:', options:['sight','echolocation','smell','magnetism'], answer:1, explain:'They emit ultrasound and listen for echoes.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch14', title:'Optics', urdu:'بصریات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch14-l1', title:'Reflection and Mirrors', html:'<h3>Reflection and Mirrors</h3><p><strong>Laws of reflection:</strong> angle of incidence = angle of reflection; incident ray, reflected ray and normal are <strong>coplanar</strong>. <strong>Plane mirrors</strong> give virtual, upright, same-size images as far behind as the object is in front (lateral inversion). <strong>Concave mirrors</strong> converge light (shaving mirrors, headlights); <strong>convex mirrors</strong> diverge it (wide view: car side mirrors, shop security). <strong>Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u</strong> (f = R/2).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch14-l2', title:'Refraction and Lenses', html:'<h3>Refraction and Lenses</h3><p><strong>Refraction</strong> bends light at a boundary due to speed change. <strong>Snell’s law:</strong> n = sin i / sin r; light bends <strong>toward</strong> the normal entering a denser medium. <strong>Refractive index</strong> of glass ≈ 1.5. <strong>Convex lenses</strong> converge (magnifying glasses, cameras); <strong>concave lenses</strong> diverge (correcting <strong>myopia</strong>/short sight). <strong>Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v − 1/u</strong>; power in <strong>dioptres = 1/f (metres)</strong>. <strong>Total internal reflection</strong> (angle > critical angle) powers <strong>fibre optics</strong> and sparkling diamonds.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch14-l3', title:'Dispersion, the Eye and Optical Instruments', html:'<h3>Dispersion, the Eye and Optical Instruments</h3><p>A <strong>prism disperses</strong> white light into <strong>VIBGYOR</strong> (violet bends most) — <strong>rainbows</strong> are natural dispersion. The <strong>human eye</strong>: cornea + lens focus light on the <strong>retina</strong>; the <strong>iris</strong> adjusts the pupil. Defects: <strong>myopia</strong> (concave lens), <strong>hyperopia</strong> (convex lens). <strong>Microscopes</strong> and <strong>telescopes</strong> use lens combinations for magnification; <strong>cameras</strong> focus real inverted images on film/sensors.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The first law of reflection states:', options:['i > r','i = r','i < r','i + r = 90°'], answer:1, explain:'Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection.'},
{q:'A plane mirror forms an image that is:', options:['real and inverted','virtual, upright, same size','magnified','on the surface'], answer:1, explain:'As far behind the mirror as the object is in front.'},
{q:'Car side mirrors are ___ to give a wide view:', options:['concave','convex','plane','cylindrical'], answer:1, explain:'Convex mirrors diverge light, widening the field.'},
{q:'Snell’s law is:', options:['n = sin r/sin i','n = sin i/sin r','n = i/r','n = c/v²'], answer:1, explain:'Refractive index = sin(incidence)/sin(refraction).'},
{q:'Light entering glass from air bends:', options:['away from the normal','toward the normal','not at all','backwards'], answer:1, explain:'Denser medium slows light, bending it toward the normal.'},
{q:'Myopia (short sight) is corrected with a:', options:['convex lens','concave lens','prism','plane mirror'], answer:1, explain:'Diverging lenses push the focus back onto the retina.'},
{q:'Total internal reflection needs:', options:['any angle','angle greater than the critical angle','a mirror','coloured light'], answer:1, explain:'Beyond the critical angle, all light reflects inside.'},
{q:'Fibre optics work on:', options:['refraction','total internal reflection','dispersion','diffraction'], answer:1, explain:'Light bounces along the fibre core by TIR.'},
{q:'In VIBGYOR, the most deviated colour is:', options:['red','violet','green','yellow'], answer:1, explain:'Shortest wavelength bends most in a prism.'},
{q:'Lens power of +2 D means focal length:', options:['2 m','0.5 m','200 cm','0.02 m'], answer:1, explain:'f = 1/P = 1/2 = 0.5 m.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch15', title:'Electrostatics', urdu:'الیکٹروسٹیٹکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch15-l1', title:'Electric Charge', html:'<h3>Electric Charge</h3><p><strong>Charge</strong> (coulomb, C) comes from <strong>electron transfer</strong>: rubbing removes electrons → <strong>positive</strong>; gaining electrons → <strong>negative</strong>. <strong>Like charges repel, unlike attract.</strong> <strong>Conductors</strong> (metals) let charge flow; <strong>insulators</strong> (plastic, rubber) hold it in place. <strong>Charging by induction:</strong> a charged rod polarises a neutral conductor, which can then be earthed to keep the charge. <strong>Lightning</strong> is a giant static discharge — <strong>lightning conductors</strong> (earthed spikes) protect buildings.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch15-l2', title:'Coulomb’s Law and Electric Field', html:'<h3>Coulomb’s Law and Electric Field</h3><p><strong>Coulomb’s law: F = k·q₁·q₂/r²</strong>, with <strong>k = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C²</strong>. Force grows with charge and shrinks with distance <strong>squared</strong> — doubling distance quarters the force. <strong>Example:</strong> 2 µC and 3 µC, 0.1 m apart: F = 9×10⁹ × 6×10⁻¹² / 0.01 = 5.4 N. The <strong>electric field</strong> E = F/q (N/C) maps the force on a positive test charge; <strong>field lines</strong> leave positive charges and enter negative ones, never crossing.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch15-l3', title:'Potential, Capacitance and the Electroscope', html:'<h3>Potential, Capacitance and the Electroscope</h3><p><strong>Electric potential</strong> V = W/q — work per coulomb, in <strong>volts</strong>. A <strong>capacitor</strong> stores charge: <strong>C = Q/V</strong> in <strong>farads (F)</strong>; energy stored = ½QV. Uses: camera flashes, smoothing power supplies, touchscreens. The <strong>gold-leaf electroscope</strong> detects charge: leaves <strong>diverge</strong> when charged (like charges repel) and collapse if the charge leaks away — also testing conductors vs insulators.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of electric charge is the:', options:['volt','coulomb','ampere','ohm'], answer:1, explain:'Charge is measured in coulombs.'},
{q:'A glass rod rubbed with silk becomes:', options:['negative','positive','neutral','magnetic'], answer:1, explain:'It loses electrons to the silk.'},
{q:'In Coulomb’s law, doubling the distance:', options:['doubles F','quarters F','halves F','no change'], answer:1, explain:'F ∝ 1/r², so 2× distance → F/4.'},
{q:'The value of k in Coulomb’s law is:', options:['6.67×10⁻¹¹','9×10⁹ N·m²/C²','1.6×10⁻¹⁹','3×10⁸'], answer:1, explain:'The electrostatic constant.'},
{q:'Electric field lines:', options:['cross freely','never cross','form circles only','start at negative charges'], answer:1, explain:'Each point has a single field direction.'},
{q:'Capacitance is defined as:', options:['Q×V','Q/V','V/Q','Q²/V'], answer:1, explain:'C = charge stored per volt, in farads.'},
{q:'A gold-leaf electroscope detects:', options:['current','charge','voltage','resistance'], answer:1, explain:'Leaves diverge when the cap is charged.'},
{q:'Lightning conductors protect buildings by:', options:['attracting clouds','providing an earthed path for discharge','repelling rain','insulating roofs'], answer:1, explain:'The earthed spike safely carries the strike to ground.'},
{q:'Charging by induction needs:', options:['touching','a nearby charged object and earthing','heating','a battery'], answer:1, explain:'Polarisation plus earthing leaves net charge.'},
{q:'Energy stored in a capacitor is:', options:['QV','½QV','Q/V','V²/Q'], answer:1, explain:'E = ½QV = ½CV².'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch16', title:'Current Electricity', urdu:'کرنٹ الیکٹرسٹی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch16-l1', title:'Current, Voltage and Ohm’s Law', html:'<h3>Current, Voltage and Ohm’s Law</h3><p><strong>Current</strong> I = Q/t — charge flow per second, in <strong>amperes (A)</strong>; conventional current flows <strong>+ to −</strong> (electrons move oppositely). <strong>Voltage</strong> (potential difference) pushes the charge. <strong>Ohm’s law: V = IR</strong> — current ∝ voltage for <strong>ohmic conductors</strong> (straight-line V–I graph). <strong>Example:</strong> 12 V across 4 Ω → I = 3 A. <strong>Filament bulbs</strong> are non-ohmic: resistance rises as they heat.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch16-l2', title:'Resistance and Resistivity', html:'<h3>Resistance and Resistivity</h3><p><strong>Resistance</strong> opposes current (ohm, Ω), measured by <strong>ohmmeter</strong> / multimeter. <strong>Resistivity: R = ρ·L/A</strong> — longer/thinner wires resist more; <strong>ρ</strong> (Ω·m) is the material’s property (copper ≈ 1.7×10⁻⁸, ideal for wiring). Resistance <strong>rises with temperature</strong> in metals. <strong>Variable resistors (rheostats)</strong> and <strong>thermistors/LDRs</strong> (temperature/light-sensitive) control circuits.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch16-l3', title:'Series and Parallel Circuits', html:'<h3>Series and Parallel Circuits</h3><p><strong>Series:</strong> one path — <strong>same current</strong> everywhere; <strong>R_total = R₁ + R₂ + …</strong>; voltage <strong>divides</strong>. One break kills the whole circuit (old fairy lights). <strong>Parallel:</strong> branches — <strong>same voltage</strong> across each; <strong>1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + …</strong>; current <strong>divides</strong>. Homes wire in <strong>parallel</strong> so each appliance gets full voltage and works independently. <strong>Example:</strong> two 6 Ω resistors in parallel → 3 Ω.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of current is the:', options:['volt','ampere','ohm','watt'], answer:1, explain:'Current = coulombs per second = amperes.'},
{q:'Ohm’s law states:', options:['I = V/R','V = IR','R = IV','P = VI'], answer:1, explain:'V = IR (equivalently I = V/R).'},
{q:'12 V across 6 Ω gives current:', options:['72 A','2 A','0.5 A','18 A'], answer:1, explain:'I = V/R = 12/6 = 2 A.'},
{q:'Conventional current flows from:', options:['− to +','+ to −','both ways','nowhere'], answer:1, explain:'Defined before electrons were known; electrons flow oppositely.'},
{q:'Resistivity ρ of a wire: R =', options:['ρA/L','ρL/A','L/(ρA)','ρ/LA'], answer:1, explain:'Longer and thinner means more resistance.'},
{q:'Copper is used for wiring because of its:', options:['high resistivity','low resistivity','high density','colour'], answer:1, explain:'Low ρ means little energy wasted as heat.'},
{q:'Two 6 Ω resistors in series give:', options:['3 Ω','12 Ω','6 Ω','36 Ω'], answer:1, explain:'R_total = 6 + 6 = 12 Ω.'},
{q:'The same two in parallel give:', options:['12 Ω','6 Ω','3 Ω','1 Ω'], answer:2, explain:'1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3.'},
{q:'Homes are wired in parallel so that:', options:['current is same','each appliance gets full voltage','it is cheaper','wires are short'], answer:1, explain:'Parallel branches each see the full mains voltage.'},
{q:'A filament bulb is non-ohmic because:', options:['it is old','resistance rises with temperature','it uses AC','it is dim'], answer:1, explain:'Heating increases resistance, curving the V–I graph.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch17', title:'Electric Circuits', urdu:'برقی سرکٹس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch17-l1', title:'Cells, EMF and Internal Resistance', html:'<h3>Cells, EMF and Internal Resistance</h3><p>A <strong>cell</strong> converts chemical energy to electrical. <strong>EMF (ε)</strong> is the energy per coulomb the cell <em>supplies</em>; <strong>terminal voltage</strong> V = ε − Ir is what the circuit <em>gets</em> — the difference is lost across the cell’s <strong>internal resistance r</strong>. <strong>Cells in series</strong> (head-to-tail) add EMFs for more voltage; <strong>in parallel</strong> (like terminals joined) keep voltage but last longer / supply more current.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch17-l2', title:'Electrical Power and Energy Bills', html:'<h3>Electrical Power and Energy Bills</h3><p><strong>Power P = V·I = I²R = V²/R</strong> (watts). <strong>Example:</strong> a 220 V, 5 A heater → P = 1100 W. Bills use the <strong>kilowatt-hour (kWh)</strong>: energy = power(kW) × time(h); <strong>1 kWh = 3.6 MJ</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 1.1 kW for 3 h = 3.3 kWh (“3.3 units”). Higher wattage or longer use = bigger bill — which is why LED bulbs (efficient) beat old incandescents.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch17-l3', title:'Circuit Safety: Fuses and Earthing', html:'<h3>Circuit Safety: Fuses and Earthing</h3><p><strong>Short circuits</strong> and overloads cause dangerous currents. A <strong>fuse</strong> is a thin wire that <strong>melts</strong> when current exceeds its rating, breaking the circuit — always in the <strong>live wire</strong>. <strong>Circuit breakers</strong> do the same job resetably. <strong>Earthing</strong> connects appliance bodies to the ground so fault current flows safely away. <strong>Double insulation</strong> (square-in-square symbol) protects without earth. Never touch switches with wet hands — water conducts.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'EMF of a cell is:', options:['voltage across terminals','energy supplied per coulomb','current output','internal resistance'], answer:1, explain:'ε = W/Q for the whole circuit.'},
{q:'Terminal voltage V equals:', options:['ε + Ir','ε − Ir','Ir','ε/I'], answer:1, explain:'Some EMF is lost across internal resistance.'},
{q:'Two 1.5 V cells in series give:', options:['1.5 V','3.0 V','0.75 V','1.5 A'], answer:1, explain:'Series EMFs add.'},
{q:'Electrical power P equals:', options:['V/I','V·I','I/V','V+I'], answer:1, explain:'P = VI = I²R = V²/R.'},
{q:'A 2 kW heater run for 3 hours uses:', options:['6 kWh','5 kWh','2/3 kWh','0.66 kWh'], answer:0, explain:'Energy = 2 × 3 = 6 kWh (6 units).'},
{q:'1 kWh equals:', options:['3600 J','3.6 MJ','1000 W','1 J'], answer:1, explain:'1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6×10⁶ J.'},
{q:'A fuse works by:', options:['tripping a switch','melting to break the circuit','cooling wires','raising voltage'], answer:1, explain:'Excess current melts the thin fuse wire.'},
{q:'The fuse is always fitted in the:', options:['neutral wire','live wire','earth wire','any wire'], answer:1, explain:'Breaking the live side fully isolates the appliance.'},
{q:'Earthing protects by:', options:['saving power','giving fault current a safe path to ground','raising voltage','cooling'], answer:1, explain:'Fault current flows to earth, not through a person.'},
{q:'Cells in parallel are used to:', options:['raise voltage','increase current capacity/life','reduce size','change AC to DC'], answer:1, explain:'Voltage stays same; they share the load longer.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch18', title:'Electronics', urdu:'الیکٹرانکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch18-l1', title:'Semiconductors and Diodes', html:'<h3>Semiconductors and Diodes</h3><p><strong>Semiconductors</strong> (silicon, germanium) conduct between conductors and insulators. <strong>Doping</strong> adds impurities: <strong>n-type</strong> (extra electrons, e.g. phosphorus) and <strong>p-type</strong> (holes, e.g. boron). A <strong>p-n junction diode</strong> conducts in <strong>forward bias</strong> (+ to p-side) and blocks in <strong>reverse bias</strong> — a one-way valve for current. <strong>LEDs</strong> emit light in forward bias (efficient lighting); <strong>photodiodes/solar cells</strong> do the reverse, turning light into electricity.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch18-l2', title:'Rectification: AC to DC', html:'<h3>Rectification: AC to DC</h3><p><strong>Rectification</strong> converts <strong>AC to DC</strong> using diodes. <strong>Half-wave</strong> (one diode) passes only half the cycles — pulsating DC. <strong>Full-wave bridge</strong> (four diodes) uses both halves; a <strong>capacitor smooths</strong> the output toward steady DC. This is how <strong>phone chargers and power adapters</strong> turn mains AC into the DC electronics need.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch18-l3', title:'Transistors and Logic Gates', html:'<h3>Transistors and Logic Gates</h3><p>A <strong>transistor</strong> (n-p-n or p-n-p) is a <strong>switch and amplifier</strong>: a tiny <strong>base</strong> current controls a large <strong>collector–emitter</strong> current — the heart of computers, radios and hearing aids. <strong>Logic gates</strong> process binary (0/1): <strong>AND</strong> (1 only if all inputs 1), <strong>OR</strong> (1 if any input 1), <strong>NOT</strong> (inverts). <strong>Integrated circuits (chips)</strong> pack millions of transistors — Moore’s law shrank computers from rooms to pockets.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Doping silicon with phosphorus makes it:', options:['p-type','n-type','insulating','magnetic'], answer:1, explain:'Phosphorus donates extra electrons (n = negative).'},
{q:'A diode conducts in:', options:['reverse bias','forward bias','no bias','both equally'], answer:1, explain:'Forward bias (+ to p-side) lets current through.'},
{q:'LEDs emit light when:', options:['reverse biased','forward biased','unbiased','heated'], answer:1, explain:'Forward current recombines electrons and holes, releasing photons.'},
{q:'Rectification converts:', options:['DC to AC','AC to DC','high to low voltage','sound to light'], answer:1, explain:'Diodes pass current one way, producing DC.'},
{q:'A bridge rectifier uses:', options:['one diode','two diodes','four diodes','no diodes'], answer:2, explain:'Four diodes in a bridge use both AC halves.'},
{q:'A smoothing capacitor:', options:['blocks DC','reduces ripple toward steady DC','increases voltage','stores AC'], answer:1, explain:'It charges and discharges to fill the gaps.'},
{q:'A transistor can act as a:', options:['resistor only','switch and amplifier','battery','fuse'], answer:1, explain:'Small base current controls large collector current.'},
{q:'An AND gate outputs 1 only when:', options:['any input is 1','all inputs are 1','inputs differ','input is 0'], answer:1, explain:'AND needs every input high.'},
{q:'A NOT gate:', options:['adds inputs','inverts the input','multiplies','stores charge'], answer:1, explain:'0 → 1 and 1 → 0.'},
{q:'Solar cells are essentially:', options:['LEDs','large photodiodes','transistors','capacitors'], answer:1, explain:'p-n junctions converting light to electricity.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch19', title:'Electromagnetism', urdu:'برقی مقناطیسیت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch19-l1', title:'Magnetic Fields from Currents', html:'<h3>Magnetic Fields from Currents</h3><p><strong>Oersted</strong> found that <strong>current creates a magnetic field</strong>: a compass deflects near a wire. Field around a straight wire forms <strong>concentric circles</strong> — direction by the <strong>right-hand grip rule</strong> (thumb = current, fingers = field). A <strong>solenoid</strong> (coil) makes a strong bar-magnet-like field, boosted by a <strong>soft-iron core</strong> — the <strong>electromagnet</strong> (cranes, bells, relays). Field strength grows with <strong>current and turns</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch19-l2', title:'Force on a Current and the Motor', html:'<h3>Force on a Current and the Motor</h3><p>A current-carrying wire in a magnetic field feels <strong>F = B·I·L</strong> (B = field strength, L = length in field) — direction by <strong>Fleming’s left-hand rule</strong> (First finger Field, seCond Current, thuMb Motion). A coil in a field <strong>rotates</strong> — the <strong>DC motor</strong>: the <strong>split-ring commutator</strong> reverses current every half-turn to keep it spinning one way. Uses: fans, drills, electric cars, starters.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch19-l3', title:'Electromagnetic Induction and Transformers', html:'<h3>Electromagnetic Induction and Transformers</h3><p><strong>Faraday:</strong> a <strong>changing</strong> magnetic field induces an <strong>EMF</strong> — move a magnet in a coil (or vice versa); faster motion / more turns / stronger magnet = bigger EMF (<strong>Lenz’s law</strong> opposes the change). <strong>Generators</strong> (dynamos) turn motion into electricity — power stations. <strong>Transformers</strong> (AC only): <strong>Vs/Vp = Ns/Np</strong>; <strong>step-up</strong> (more secondary turns) for grid transmission at <strong>high voltage, low current</strong> (less heat loss), <strong>step-down</strong> for safe home use.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Oersted discovered that:', options:['magnets attract iron','current produces a magnetic field','light is a wave','atoms have nuclei'], answer:1, explain:'A compass needle deflects near a current-carrying wire.'},
{q:'Field direction around a straight wire is found by the:', options:['left-hand rule','right-hand grip rule','screw gauge','compass'], answer:1, explain:'Thumb along current, curled fingers show the field.'},
{q:'A solenoid’s field is strengthened by:', options:['fewer turns','a soft-iron core','less current','longer wire'], answer:1, explain:'The core concentrates the magnetic field.'},
{q:'Force on a current in a magnetic field is:', options:['F = BIL','F = ma','F = kq₁q₂/r²','F = mg'], answer:0, explain:'Force = field × current × length in the field.'},
{q:'Fleming’s left-hand rule gives the direction of:', options:['current','field','force (motion)','voltage'], answer:2, explain:'ThuMb = Motion (force).'},
{q:'A DC motor keeps spinning one way using a:', options:['battery','split-ring commutator','fuse','resistor'], answer:1, explain:'It reverses coil current every half-turn.'},
{q:'Electromagnetic induction needs a ___ magnetic field:', options:['strong','changing','weak','steady'], answer:1, explain:'Only changing flux induces EMF (Faraday).'},
{q:'In a transformer, Vs/Vp equals:', options:['Np/Ns','Ns/Np','Is/Ip','1'], answer:1, explain:'Voltage ratio = turns ratio.'},
{q:'Power is transmitted at high voltage to:', options:['go faster','reduce heat loss (low current)','look impressive','avoid transformers'], answer:1, explain:'P_loss = I²R, so small current means small loss.'},
{q:'Transformers work on:', options:['DC only','AC only','both','batteries'], answer:1, explain:'They need the continuously changing field of AC.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch20', title:'Electromagnetic Waves', urdu:'برقی مقناطیسی لہریں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch20-l1', title:'The EM Spectrum', html:'<h3>The EM Spectrum</h3><p><strong>Maxwell</strong> predicted and <strong>Hertz</strong> confirmed <strong>electromagnetic waves</strong>: oscillating electric and magnetic fields needing <strong>no medium</strong>, all travelling at <strong>c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s</strong> in vacuum. In order of <strong>increasing frequency</strong> (decreasing λ): <strong>radio → microwave → infrared → visible → ultraviolet → X-rays → gamma rays</strong>. Same speed, different energies: <strong>v = f·λ</strong> still applies.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch20-l2', title:'Uses of EM Waves', html:'<h3>Uses of EM Waves</h3><p><strong>Radio:</strong> broadcasting, communications. <strong>Microwaves:</strong> ovens (heat water molecules), mobile phones, radar. <strong>Infrared:</strong> remote controls, thermal imaging, heaters. <strong>Visible:</strong> sight, photography, fibre optics. <strong>Ultraviolet:</strong> sterilisation, vitamin D (but causes sunburn — use sunscreen). <strong>X-rays:</strong> medical imaging, security scans. <strong>Gamma:</strong> cancer radiotherapy, sterilising equipment.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch20-l3', title:'Dangers and Safety', html:'<h3>Dangers and Safety</h3><p><strong>Ionising</strong> radiations (UV, X-rays, gamma) can <strong>damage cells and DNA</strong> — causing burns, cancer, mutations. Safety: <strong>limit exposure time</strong>, keep <strong>distance</strong>, use <strong>shielding</strong> (lead for X-rays), and wear protection (lead aprons, sunscreen). <strong>Non-ionising</strong> radio/microwaves are far less hazardous but still regulated. Risk ∝ dose: medical X-rays use the <strong>minimum effective dose</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'All EM waves travel in vacuum at:', options:['343 m/s','3×10⁸ m/s','1500 m/s','1.5×10⁸ m/s'], answer:1, explain:'The speed of light c.'},
{q:'Which has the longest wavelength?', options:['gamma rays','radio waves','X-rays','UV'], answer:1, explain:'Spectrum order: radio longest, gamma shortest.'},
{q:'Microwave ovens heat food by acting on:', options:['proteins','water molecules','fats only','air'], answer:1, explain:'Microwaves make polar water molecules vibrate.'},
{q:'X-rays are used for:', options:['cooking','medical imaging','broadcasting','heating'], answer:1, explain:'They penetrate soft tissue but not bone.'},
{q:'Which EM wave is ionising and most dangerous?', options:['radio','gamma','infrared','microwave'], answer:1, explain:'Highest frequency = most energetic photons.'},
{q:'Fibre-optic internet uses:', options:['radio waves','visible/infrared light','X-rays','gamma rays'], answer:1, explain:'Light pulses carry data by total internal reflection.'},
{q:'Sunscreen protects against:', options:['infrared','ultraviolet','radio','microwaves'], answer:1, explain:'UV causes sunburn and skin damage.'},
{q:'Radar uses:', options:['gamma rays','microwaves','X-rays','visible light'], answer:1, explain:'Microwave reflections detect objects and speed.'},
{q:'The correct spectrum order (low → high f) is:', options:['radio, microwave, infrared, visible, UV, X-ray, gamma','gamma … radio','visible first','infrared, radio, gamma'], answer:0, explain:'Increasing frequency: radio → gamma.'},
{q:'Safety from X-rays includes:', options:['longer exposure','lead shielding','standing closer','no protection'], answer:1, explain:'Time, distance and shielding minimise dose.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch21', title:'Nuclear Physics', urdu:'جوہری طبیعیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch21-l1', title:'The Nucleus and Isotopes', html:'<h3>The Nucleus and Isotopes</h3><p>The <strong>nucleus</strong> holds <strong>protons and neutrons</strong> (nucleons); <strong>atomic number Z</strong> = protons, <strong>mass number A</strong> = protons + neutrons. <strong>Isotopes</strong> share Z but differ in neutrons (carbon-12/14, uranium-235/238). Notation: <sup>A</sup><sub>Z</sub>X. <strong>Strong nuclear force</strong> binds nucleons against proton repulsion. <strong>Mass defect</strong> (lost mass → <strong>E = mc²</strong> binding energy) is the source of nuclear energy.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch21-l2', title:'Radioactivity', html:'<h3>Radioactivity</h3><p>Unstable nuclei decay, emitting: <strong>alpha (α)</strong> — helium nuclei, <strong>highly ionising, stopped by paper</strong>; <strong>beta (β)</strong> — fast electrons, stopped by <strong>aluminium</strong>; <strong>gamma (γ)</strong> — EM waves, need <strong>lead/concrete</strong>. <strong>Half-life</strong> = time for half the nuclei to decay (unaffected by temperature/pressure). <strong>Example:</strong> 80 g with 10-year half-life → 40 g after 10 y, 20 g after 20 y. <strong>Background radiation</strong> comes from rocks, cosmic rays and radon.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-phy-ch21-l3', title:'Fission, Fusion and Uses', html:'<h3>Fission, Fusion and Uses</h3><p><strong>Fission:</strong> a heavy nucleus (uranium-235) splits when hit by a neutron, releasing <strong>huge energy + more neutrons</strong> → <strong>chain reaction</strong> (controlled in reactors with <strong>control rods</strong> and <strong>moderator</strong>; uncontrolled in bombs). <strong>Fusion:</strong> light nuclei (hydrogen) join in the <strong>Sun/stars</strong>, releasing even more energy per kg — the hope for clean future power. <strong>Uses:</strong> nuclear power (Pakistan’s KANUPP, Chashma plants), <strong>medical tracers</strong>, <strong>radiotherapy</strong>, smoke detectors (americium-241), carbon dating. <strong>Safety:</strong> shielding, distance, strict waste disposal.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The mass number A equals:', options:['protons','neutrons','protons + neutrons','electrons'], answer:2, explain:'A counts all nucleons.'},
{q:'Isotopes differ in:', options:['protons','electrons','neutrons','charge'], answer:2, explain:'Same Z, different neutron number.'},
{q:'Alpha particles are stopped by:', options:['lead','paper','aluminium','concrete'], answer:1, explain:'Big, slow α particles ionise heavily but penetrate poorly.'},
{q:'Beta radiation is stopped by:', options:['paper','aluminium sheet','lead only','air'], answer:1, explain:'A few mm of aluminium absorbs β particles.'},
{q:'Half-life is the time for:', options:['all nuclei to decay','half the nuclei to decay','atoms to form','energy to double'], answer:1, explain:'Each half-life halves the remaining unstable nuclei.'},
{q:'80 g of a substance with 5-year half-life becomes ___ after 10 years:', options:['40 g','20 g','0 g','60 g'], answer:1, explain:'Two half-lives: 80 → 40 → 20 g.'},
{q:'Nuclear fission is:', options:['joining light nuclei','splitting heavy nuclei','electron loss','chemical burning'], answer:1, explain:'U-235 splits into lighter nuclei plus neutrons and energy.'},
{q:'The Sun’s energy comes from:', options:['fission','fusion','combustion','friction'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium, releasing energy.'},
{q:'Control rods in a reactor:', options:['speed the reaction','absorb neutrons to control the rate','cool water','make steam'], answer:1, explain:'Boron/cadmium rods soak up neutrons.'},
{q:'E = mc² shows:', options:['energy creates mass only','mass can convert to enormous energy','light is slow','atoms are indivisible'], answer:1, explain:'Tiny mass defect × c² = huge energy (Einstein).'}
]}
]});

