window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class10-chemistry', board:'federal', title:'Chemistry', urdu:'کیمسٹری', class:'Class 10', icon:'🧪', desc:'FBISE Class 10 Chemistry — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch1', title:'History of Chemistry', urdu:'کیمسٹری کی تاریخ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch1-l1', title:'From Alchemy to Chemistry', html:'<h3>From Alchemy to Chemistry</h3><p><strong>Alchemy</strong> (ancient–medieval) mixed mysticism with experiment — seeking the <strong>philosopher’s stone</strong> (turn lead to gold) and the <strong>elixir of life</strong>. Though its goals were mystical, alchemists invented <strong>distillation, filtration and furnaces</strong>. <strong>Jabir ibn Hayyan (Geber)</strong>, the father of chemistry, insisted on <strong>experimentation</strong> and described acids and apparatus. <strong>Al-Razi</strong> classified substances and wrote clinical chemical texts.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch1-l2', title:'The Scientific Revolution in Chemistry', html:'<h3>The Scientific Revolution in Chemistry</h3><p><strong>Robert Boyle</strong> defined elements scientifically; <strong>Lavoisier</strong>, father of modern chemistry, established the <strong>law of conservation of mass</strong> and named oxygen — ending the phlogiston theory. <strong>Dalton’s atomic theory</strong> (1808) gave chemistry its particle foundation; <strong>Mendeleev’s periodic table</strong> (1869) organised the elements predictively. Chemistry became a <strong>quantitative experimental science</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch1-l3', title:'Paradigms and Modern Chemistry', html:'<h3>Paradigms and Modern Chemistry</h3><p>Science advances through <strong>paradigms</strong> — accepted frameworks (like atomic theory) that guide research until <strong>anomalies</strong> force a <strong>paradigm shift</strong> (phlogiston → oxygen theory). <strong>Confidence</strong> in results comes from <strong>repeatability and reproducibility</strong> by independent labs. Modern chemistry spans <strong>nanotechnology, green chemistry</strong> (less waste, safer solvents) and <strong>computational modelling</strong> — with Pakistani chemists like <strong>Atta-ur-Rahman</strong> winning world renown in natural products chemistry.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Alchemists sought the philosopher’s stone to:', options:['make medicines','turn base metals into gold','build houses','purify water'], answer:1, explain:'Transmutation of lead into gold was their legendary goal.'},
{q:'Jabir ibn Hayyan is called the father of chemistry for:', options:['making gold','insisting on experimentation','discovering oxygen','inventing glass'], answer:1, explain:'Geber pioneered systematic experimental methods.'},
{q:'Lavoisier established the:', options:['atomic theory','law of conservation of mass','periodic table','pH scale'], answer:1, explain:'His careful weighing showed mass is conserved.'},
{q:'Dalton’s atomic theory appeared in:', options:['1708','1808','1908','1869'], answer:1, explain:'1808: atoms as the basis of chemistry.'},
{q:'A paradigm shift occurs when:', options:['a new book appears','anomalies overturn the old framework','funding increases','students graduate'], answer:1, explain:'E.g. phlogiston replaced by oxygen theory.'},
{q:'Repeatability builds:', options:['confusion','confidence in results','cost','mystery'], answer:1, explain:'Independent replication confirms findings.'},
{q:'Green chemistry aims to:', options:['make green dyes','reduce waste and use safer methods','paint labs green','grow plants'], answer:1, explain:'Environmentally benign chemical design.'},
{q:'Al-Razi is known for:', options:['astronomy','classifying substances and chemical medicine','poetry','mathematics'], answer:1, explain:'His works systematised chemical substances and their medical uses.'},
{q:'Mendeleev’s table (1869) organised elements by:', options:['colour','atomic mass','density','abundance'], answer:1, explain:'With gaps predicting undiscovered elements.'},
{q:'Boyle contributed by:', options:['defining elements scientifically','discovering electrons','naming oxygen','making urea'], answer:0, explain:'Boyle’s sceptical, experimental approach defined the element.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch2', title:'Matter', urdu:'مادہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch2-l1', title:'Changes of State and Heating Curves', html:'<h3>Changes of State and Heating Curves</h3><p>Heating a solid: temperature rises → <strong>melting</strong> (flat: latent heat of fusion) → liquid heats → <strong>boiling</strong> (flat: latent heat of vaporisation) → gas heats. <strong>Cooling curves</strong> mirror this. <strong>Evaporation</strong> (surface, any temperature) differs from <strong>boiling</strong> (whole liquid, fixed temperature): heat, wind, dry air and large surface speed evaporation. <strong>Sublimation</strong> (solid → gas, e.g. dry ice, naphthalene) skips the liquid phase.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch2-l2', title:'Kinetic Particle Theory and Gas Laws', html:'<h3>Kinetic Particle Theory and Gas Laws</h3><p><strong>Kinetic theory:</strong> particles in constant random motion; hotter = faster; gas pressure = particle collisions with walls. <strong>Boyle’s law:</strong> P ∝ 1/V (constant T) — <strong>P₁V₁ = P₂V₂</strong>. <strong>Charles’s law:</strong> V ∝ T in kelvin — <strong>V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 2 dm³ of gas at 300 K heated to 600 K → V = 4 dm³. <strong>Diffusion</strong> (gases mixing) is faster for lighter molecules — <strong>Graham’s law</strong>; ammonia reaches the nose before HCl in the classic tube experiment.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch2-l3', title:'Plasma and Properties of Matter', html:'<h3>Plasma and Properties of Matter</h3><p><strong>Plasma</strong>, the fourth state — ionised gas of free electrons and ions — makes up <strong>stars, lightning and neon signs</strong>. Matter’s properties split into <strong>physical</strong> (colour, density, melting point — no new substance) and <strong>chemical</strong> (flammability, reactivity — new substances form). <strong>Intensive</strong> properties (density, boiling point) ignore amount; <strong>extensive</strong> ones (mass, volume) scale with it. Tests identify substances by their characteristic properties.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Flat sections on a heating curve represent:', options:['thermometer faults','latent heat during change of state','cooling','no heating'], answer:1, explain:'Energy breaks bonds without raising temperature.'},
{q:'Evaporation differs from boiling because it occurs:', options:['at fixed temperature','at the surface at any temperature','only in vacuum','with bubbles'], answer:1, explain:'Surface molecules escape whenever they have enough energy.'},
{q:'Boyle’s law: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ holds at constant:', options:['volume','temperature','moles','pressure'], answer:1, explain:'Pressure × volume is constant at fixed T.'},
{q:'A gas at 300 K occupies 4 dm³; at 600 K (same P) it occupies:', options:['2 dm³','8 dm³','4 dm³','12 dm³'], answer:1, explain:'Charles’s law: V doubles as T doubles (kelvin).'},
{q:'Diffusion is faster for:', options:['heavier gases','lighter gases','cold gases','liquids'], answer:1, explain:'Lighter molecules move faster (Graham’s law).'},
{q:'Plasma is found in:', options:['ice','stars and lightning','wood','oil'], answer:1, explain:'Ionised gas — the commonest state in the universe.'},
{q:'Density is an ___ property:', options:['extensive','intensive','chemical','magnetic'], answer:1, explain:'It does not depend on the amount of substance.'},
{q:'Sublimation is:', options:['solid → liquid','solid → gas directly','gas → liquid','liquid → solid'], answer:1, explain:'E.g. dry ice and naphthalene balls.'},
{q:'Gas pressure results from:', options:['gravity','particle collisions with container walls','magnetism','diffusion'], answer:1, explain:'Kinetic theory: collisions exert force per area.'},
{q:'Flammability is a ___ property:', options:['physical','chemical','intensive','extensive'], answer:1, explain:'Burning creates new substances.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch3', title:'Stoichiometry', urdu:'سٹوکیومیٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch3-l1', title:'The Mole Revisited', html:'<h3>The Mole Revisited</h3><p><strong>1 mole = 6.022 × 10²³ entities</strong> (atoms, molecules, ions, electrons). <strong>Moles = mass ÷ molar mass</strong>; <strong>mass = moles × molar mass</strong>. For solutions: <strong>moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³)</strong> — so 0.5 dm³ of 2 mol/dm³ NaOH contains 1 mol. <strong>Example:</strong> 4 g NaOH (M = 40) = 0.1 mol. Master unit conversions: 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch3-l2', title:'Percentage Composition and Formulae', html:'<h3>Percentage Composition and Formulae</h3><p><strong>% composition</strong> = (total mass of element ÷ molar mass) × 100. <strong>Example:</strong> in H₂O, %H = 2/18 × 100 = 11.1%. <strong>Empirical formula from %:</strong> % → mass → ÷ atomic mass → ÷ smallest → whole-number ratio. If the ratio is 1:1.5, double to 2:3. <strong>Molecular formula:</strong> n = molar mass ÷ empirical mass; multiply the empirical formula by n.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch3-l3', title:'Limiting Reactants and Yield', html:'<h3>Limiting Reactants and Yield</h3><p>From a balanced equation, convert each reactant to <strong>moles</strong>, divide by its coefficient — the smallest result is the <strong>limiting reactant</strong>. <strong>Theoretical yield</strong> comes from the limiting reactant; <strong>percentage yield = actual ÷ theoretical × 100</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 4 g H₂ (2 mol) + 32 g O₂ (1 mol) → water: O₂ limits (needs 2 mol H₂ per 1 mol O₂ — exactly enough here, 36 g water). Industry maximises yield to cut costs.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'1 mole of electrons contains:', options:['1 electron','6.022×10²³ electrons','1000 electrons','1 g of electrons'], answer:1, explain:'A mole is 6.022×10²³ entities of anything.'},
{q:'Moles in 0.25 dm³ of 4 mol/dm³ solution:', options:['1','16','0.0625','4'], answer:0, explain:'n = cV = 4 × 0.25 = 1 mol.'},
{q:'% of oxygen in CO₂ (M = 44):', options:['27.3%','72.7%','50%','36.4%'], answer:1, explain:'32/44 × 100 = 72.7%.'},
{q:'The limiting reactant:', options:['is most expensive','produces the least product','is always a solid','is in excess'], answer:1, explain:'It runs out first, capping the yield.'},
{q:'Percentage yield =', options:['theoretical/actual ×100','actual/theoretical ×100','actual + theoretical','moles × 100'], answer:1, explain:'What you got vs the maximum possible.'},
{q:'To find an empirical formula from percentages, first:', options:['guess','treat % as mass in grams','divide by 100','multiply by Avogadro’s number'], answer:1, explain:'Then convert to moles and find the simplest ratio.'},
{q:'A mole ratio of 1 : 1.5 becomes:', options:['1:1','2:3','1:2','3:2'], answer:1, explain:'Multiply by 2 for whole numbers.'},
{q:'Molar mass of CaCO₃ is:', options:['68 g/mol','100 g/mol','84 g/mol','50 g/mol'], answer:1, explain:'40 + 12 + 48 = 100 g/mol.'},
{q:'Concentration units mol/dm³ are also called:', options:['molarity','molality','density','normality'], answer:0, explain:'Molarity = moles per cubic decimetre.'},
{q:'If actual yield is 8 g and theoretical is 10 g, % yield =', options:['125%','80%','8%','20%'], answer:1, explain:'8/10 × 100 = 80%.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch4', title:'Electrochemistry', urdu:'الیکٹرو کیمسٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch4-l1', title:'Electrochemical Cells and the Daniel Cell', html:'<h3>Electrochemical Cells and the Daniel Cell</h3><p>A <strong>galvanic cell</strong> converts chemical → electrical energy spontaneously. In the <strong>Daniel cell</strong>: Zn(s) | Zn²⁺ || Cu²⁺ | Cu(s) — zinc <strong>anode</strong> oxidises (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻), copper <strong>cathode</strong> reduces (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu); electrons flow Zn → Cu through the wire; the <strong>salt bridge</strong> completes the circuit with ion flow. <strong>Cell notation</strong> writes anode left, cathode right.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch4-l2', title:'Electrolysis in Detail', html:'<h3>Electrolysis in Detail</h3><p><strong>Electrolysis</strong> drives non-spontaneous reactions with electricity. <strong>At the cathode (−):</strong> cations gain electrons (<strong>reduction</strong>); <strong>at the anode (+):</strong> anions lose electrons (<strong>oxidation</strong>). <strong>Electrolysis of water</strong> (with H₂SO₄): H₂ at cathode, O₂ at anode, <strong>2:1 volume ratio</strong>. <strong>Molten NaCl:</strong> Na at cathode, Cl₂ at anode. <strong>Aqueous NaCl:</strong> H₂ at cathode (H⁺ discharged), Cl₂ at anode (concentrated) — the basis of the <strong>chlor-alkali industry</strong> (NaOH + Cl₂ + H₂).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch4-l3', title:'Electroplating, Batteries and Corrosion', html:'<h3>Electroplating, Batteries and Corrosion</h3><p><strong>Electroplating:</strong> object = cathode, plating metal = anode, metal-salt electrolyte — e.g. <strong>chromium-plating</strong> car parts, <strong>silver-plating</strong> cutlery. <strong>Batteries:</strong> <strong>dry cell</strong> (Zn/C, NH₄Cl paste, 1.5 V), <strong>lead-acid accumulator</strong> (rechargeable, cars, 12 V), <strong>lithium-ion</strong> (phones). <strong>Corrosion:</strong> iron rusts via an electrochemical cell needing <strong>oxygen + water</strong>; prevented by <strong>painting, oiling, galvanising</strong> (zinc — sacrificial protection) or <strong>alloying</strong> (stainless steel).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'In a galvanic cell, the anode is where:', options:['reduction occurs','oxidation occurs','electrons enter','salt sits'], answer:1, explain:'An Ox — oxidation at the anode.'},
{q:'In the Daniel cell, electrons flow:', options:['Cu → Zn','Zn → Cu','both ways','through the salt bridge'], answer:1, explain:'From the oxidised zinc anode to the copper cathode.'},
{q:'The salt bridge:', options:['makes electrons','allows ion flow to complete the circuit','adds voltage','cools the cell'], answer:1, explain:'Ions migrate to balance charge.'},
{q:'During electrolysis of water, the gas volume ratio H₂:O₂ is:', options:['1:1','2:1','1:2','1:8'], answer:1, explain:'2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂.'},
{q:'At the cathode during electrolysis:', options:['oxidation','reduction','neutralisation','sublimation'], answer:1, explain:'Cations gain electrons — Red Cat.'},
{q:'Electroplating coats objects by making them the:', options:['anode','cathode','electrolyte','battery'], answer:1, explain:'Metal ions reduce onto the cathode object.'},
{q:'Rusting needs:', options:['oxygen only','water only','oxygen and water','carbon dioxide'], answer:2, explain:'Both are required for the electrochemical rust cell.'},
{q:'Galvanising protects iron with:', options:['paint','zinc coating','oil','plastic'], answer:1, explain:'Zinc corrodes sacrificially instead of iron.'},
{q:'A lead-acid accumulator is:', options:['primary','rechargeable (secondary)','a fuel cell','dead'], answer:1, explain:'Its reactions reverse on charging.'},
{q:'Chlor-alkali industry products include:', options:['NaCl only','NaOH, Cl₂ and H₂','HCl','NH₃'], answer:1, explain:'Electrolysis of brine yields all three.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch5', title:'Reaction Kinetics', urdu:'تعامل حرکیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch5-l1', title:'Rate of Reaction', html:'<h3>Rate of Reaction</h3><p><strong>Rate = change in concentration ÷ time</strong> (mol/dm³/s), measured by gas volume, mass loss or colour change. <strong>Collision theory:</strong> particles must collide with <strong>enough energy (≥ activation energy)</strong> and the <strong>correct orientation</strong> — only a fraction of collisions are <strong>effective</strong>. Heating increases both collision frequency <em>and</em> the fraction with enough energy.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch5-l2', title:'Factors Affecting Rate', html:'<h3>Factors Affecting Rate</h3><p><strong>Concentration/pressure:</strong> more particles → more collisions → faster. <strong>Temperature:</strong> roughly <strong>doubles rate per 10 °C rise</strong>. <strong>Surface area:</strong> powdered solids react far faster than lumps (explosions in flour mills!). <strong>Catalysts</strong> provide an alternative pathway with <strong>lower activation energy</strong> — unchanged afterwards. <strong>Light</strong> speeds photochemical reactions (photosynthesis, Cl₂ + H₂ explosion in sunlight).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch5-l3', title:'Catalysts and the Food Industry', html:'<h3>Catalysts and the Food Industry</h3><p><strong>Homogeneous</strong> catalysts (same phase) vs <strong>heterogeneous</strong> (different phase, e.g. iron in Haber). <strong>Enzymes</strong> are biological catalysts — <strong>amylase</strong> in brewing, <strong>pectinase</strong> clarifying juice. Food industry uses kinetics: <strong>refrigeration</strong> slows spoilage, <strong>preservatives</strong> inhibit microbes, <strong>antioxidants</strong> stop rancidity, and <strong>modified-atmosphere packaging</strong> slows respiration of fresh produce.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Rate of reaction is measured in:', options:['mol/dm³','mol/dm³/s','seconds','grams'], answer:1, explain:'Concentration change per unit time.'},
{q:'For a collision to be effective, particles need:', options:['low energy','enough energy and correct orientation','large size','opposite charge'], answer:1, explain:'Collision theory’s two conditions.'},
{q:'Raising temperature by 10 °C roughly:', options:['halves the rate','doubles the rate','stops the reaction','no effect'], answer:1, explain:'More collisions exceed activation energy.'},
{q:'Powdered zinc reacts faster than lumps because of:', options:['more mass','greater surface area','higher temperature','catalyst'], answer:1, explain:'More exposed particles collide more often.'},
{q:'A catalyst increases rate by:', options:['being used up','lowering activation energy','raising temperature','adding reactants'], answer:1, explain:'An easier pathway; the catalyst is regenerated.'},
{q:'Activation energy is:', options:['total energy released','minimum energy for reaction','energy of products','heat of solution'], answer:1, explain:'The barrier colliding particles must clear.'},
{q:'Enzymes are:', options:['inorganic catalysts','biological catalysts','inhibitors','reactants'], answer:1, explain:'Protein catalysts in living systems and food industry.'},
{q:'Refrigeration preserves food by:', options:['killing all germs','slowing reaction/spoilage rates','adding flavour','drying food'], answer:1, explain:'Low temperature slows microbial and chemical reactions.'},
{q:'An iron catalyst in the Haber process is:', options:['homogeneous','heterogeneous','an enzyme','a reactant'], answer:1, explain:'Solid catalyst with gaseous reactants — different phases.'},
{q:'Light speeds up:', options:['neutralisation','photochemical reactions','rusting only','all reactions'], answer:1, explain:'E.g. photosynthesis and H₂ + Cl₂ in sunlight.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch6', title:'Salts', urdu:'نمکیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch6-l1', title:'What Salts Are', html:'<h3>What Salts Are</h3><p><strong>Salts</strong> are ionic compounds of a <strong>metal (or ammonium) cation</strong> and an <strong>acid anion</strong>: NaCl, KNO₃, NH₄Cl, CuSO₄. Formed by: acid + base (neutralisation), acid + metal, acid + carbonate, or <strong>precipitation</strong> (mix two solutions → insoluble salt). <strong>Solubility rules:</strong> all <strong>nitrates</strong> and <strong>sodium/potassium/ammonium</strong> salts are soluble; most <strong>chlorides</strong> soluble (except AgCl, PbCl₂); most <strong>sulphates</strong> soluble (except BaSO₄, PbSO₄); most <strong>carbonates</strong> insoluble (except Na/K/NH₄).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch6-l2', title:'Preparing Soluble and Insoluble Salts', html:'<h3>Preparing Soluble and Insoluble Salts</h3><p><strong>Soluble salts:</strong> react excess <strong>insoluble base/carbonate/metal</strong> with acid → <strong>filter</strong> off excess → <strong>evaporate/crystallise</strong> (e.g. CuSO₄ from CuO + H₂SO₄). <strong>Insoluble salts:</strong> <strong>precipitation</strong> — mix solutions of two soluble salts, <strong>filter, wash, dry</strong> (e.g. BaSO₄ from BaCl₂ + Na₂SO₄). <strong>Titration</strong> prepares soluble salts from <strong>acid + alkali</strong> using an indicator, then crystallisation.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch6-l3', title:'Physical States, Melting Points and Uses', html:'<h3>Physical States, Melting Points and Uses</h3><p>Salts are <strong>crystalline solids</strong> with <strong>high melting points</strong> (strong ionic lattices); they <strong>conduct when molten/aqueous</strong>. <strong>Water of crystallisation</strong> gives hydrated salts: CuSO₄·5H₂O (blue), Na₂CO₃·10H₂O (washing soda). Heating hydrated salts drives off water (<strong>thermal decomposition</strong> of carbonates: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂). <strong>Uses:</strong> NaCl (food, chlor-alkali), Na₂CO₃ (glass, soap), CaSO₄ (plaster of Paris), NH₄ fertilisers.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'A salt contains:', options:['only a metal','a metal/ammonium cation and an acid anion','two acids','only non-metals'], answer:1, explain:'E.g. Na⁺ with Cl⁻ in NaCl.'},
{q:'Which salt is insoluble?', options:['NaCl','KNO₃','BaSO₄','NH₄Cl'], answer:2, explain:'Barium sulphate is the classic insoluble sulphate.'},
{q:'All ___ are soluble:', options:['carbonates','nitrates','sulphates','chlorides'], answer:1, explain:'Every nitrate dissolves in water.'},
{q:'Insoluble salts are best prepared by:', options:['titration','precipitation','evaporation','filtration only'], answer:1, explain:'Mix two soluble salts; filter the precipitate.'},
{q:'To purify a soluble salt made from acid + insoluble base, you:', options:['distil','filter off excess base then crystallise','add more acid','freeze'], answer:1, explain:'Filtration removes unreacted solid; evaporation yields crystals.'},
{q:'CuSO₄·5H₂O is blue because of:', options:['copper metal','water of crystallisation','impurities','dye'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrated copper sulphate; anhydrous is white.'},
{q:'Heating calcium carbonate gives:', options:['Ca + CO₂','CaO + CO₂','CaC + O₂','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Thermal decomposition: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.'},
{q:'Salts conduct electricity when:', options:['solid','molten or aqueous','powdered','cold'], answer:1, explain:'Mobile ions carry the charge.'},
{q:'Silver chloride is:', options:['soluble','insoluble','a gas','an acid'], answer:1, explain:'AgCl is the notable insoluble chloride.'},
{q:'Washing soda is:', options:['NaCl','Na₂CO₃·10H₂O','CaSO₄','NH₄Cl'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrated sodium carbonate.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch7', title:'Nitrogen, Sulphur and Metals', urdu:'نائٹروجن، سلفر اور دھاتیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch7-l1', title:'Nitrogen and Its Compounds', html:'<h3>Nitrogen and Its Compounds</h3><p><strong>Nitrogen (N₂)</strong> is inert (triple bond) — used in food packaging and as liquid coolant. <strong>Ammonia (NH₃)</strong> via <strong>Haber</strong> (N₂ + 3H₂, Fe catalyst, ~450 °C, ~200 atm); its <strong>fountain experiment</strong> shows extreme solubility; it forms <strong>fertilisers</strong> (urea, ammonium nitrate). <strong>Nitric acid</strong> via <strong>Ostwald process</strong> (NH₃ → NO → NO₂ → HNO₃); uses: fertilisers, explosives (TNT). <strong>Acid rain</strong> (NOₓ) and <strong>photochemical smog</strong> are nitrogen-oxide problems.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch7-l2', title:'Sulphur and Sulphuric Acid', html:'<h3>Sulphur and Sulphuric Acid</h3><p>Sulphur (yellow solid) burns to <strong>SO₂</strong>; <strong>Contact process</strong> makes H₂SO₄: S → SO₂ → <strong>SO₃</strong> (V₂O₅ catalyst, ~450 °C) → absorbed in H₂SO₄ to <strong>oleum</strong>, then diluted — direct water absorption would mist dangerously. <strong>H₂SO₄</strong> is the <strong>king of chemicals</strong>: <strong>dehydrating agent</strong> (chars sugar: black carbon column), <strong>oxidising agent</strong> (hot, conc.), and in <strong>fertilisers, batteries, detergents</strong>. <strong>Acid rain</strong> from SO₂ damages the Taj Mahal’s marble.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch7-l3', title:'General Characteristics of Metals', html:'<h3>General Characteristics of Metals</h3><p>Metals: <strong>lustrous, malleable, ductile</strong>, good <strong>conductors</strong> (delocalised electrons), high melting points, form <strong>basic oxides</strong> and <strong>+ ions</strong>. <strong>Reactivity series:</strong> K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Pb > H > Cu > Ag > Au. <strong>Extraction:</strong> reactive metals by <strong>electrolysis</strong> (Al from bauxite); less reactive by <strong>reduction with carbon</strong> (Fe in the <strong>blast furnace</strong>: haematite + coke → iron + CO₂); unreactive found native. <strong>Alloys</strong> (steel, brass, bronze) improve properties.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Nitrogen gas is unreactive because of its:', options:['low mass','triple bond','colour','smell'], answer:1, explain:'The N≡N triple bond needs huge energy to break.'},
{q:'Ammonia is made industrially by the:', options:['Contact process','Haber process','Ostwald process','blast furnace'], answer:1, explain:'N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ with iron catalyst.'},
{q:'The Contact process catalyst is:', options:['iron','vanadium(V) oxide','nickel','platinum'], answer:1, explain:'V₂O₅ converts SO₂ to SO₃.'},
{q:'Concentrated H₂SO₄ chars sugar, showing it is a:', options:['oxidising agent','dehydrating agent','base','catalyst'], answer:1, explain:'It strips H and O as water, leaving carbon.'},
{q:'SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated H₂SO₄ (not water) to avoid:', options:['explosion','acid mist','cooling','dilution'], answer:1, explain:'Oleum forms smoothly; water would give unabsorbable mist.'},
{q:'The most reactive metal in the series K, Zn, Cu, Au is:', options:['Au','Cu','Zn','K'], answer:3, explain:'Potassium tops the reactivity series.'},
{q:'Iron is extracted in the:', options:['Haber process','blast furnace','Contact process','electrolytic cell'], answer:1, explain:'Haematite reduced by coke/carbon monoxide.'},
{q:'Aluminium is extracted by:', options:['carbon reduction','electrolysis of bauxite','heating only','acid leaching'], answer:1, explain:'Too reactive for carbon; electrolysis is needed.'},
{q:'Brass is an alloy of:', options:['iron and carbon','copper and zinc','copper and tin','aluminium and copper'], answer:1, explain:'Cu + Zn gives brass; Cu + Sn gives bronze.'},
{q:'Acid rain damages marble buildings because:', options:['water dissolves marble','acids react with calcium carbonate','rain is cold','wind erodes'], answer:1, explain:'SO₂/NOₓ acids attack CaCO₃.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch8', title:'Organic Chemistry', urdu:'نامیاتی کیمسٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch8-l1', title:'Structural Formulae and Isomerism', html:'<h3>Structural Formulae and Isomerism</h3><p><strong>Structural formulae</strong> show every atom and bond (full, condensed like CH₃CH₂OH, or skeletal). <strong>Isomers</strong> share a molecular formula but differ in structure: <strong>chain</strong> (butane vs methylpropane), <strong>position</strong> (propan-1-ol vs propan-2-ol), <strong>functional group</strong> (ethanol vs methoxymethane). Isomers have <strong>different properties</strong> — branching lowers boiling points. Always check: same atoms, different arrangement.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch8-l2', title:'Naming Organic Compounds', html:'<h3>Naming Organic Compounds</h3><p><strong>IUPAC steps:</strong> 1) longest chain = parent (meth/eth/prop/but…); 2) number for <strong>lowest locants</strong>; 3) name substituents alphabetically (bromo, chloro, methyl); 4) add suffix for the <strong>principal functional group</strong> (-ane, -ene, -yne, -ol, -al, -one, -oic acid). <strong>Examples:</strong> CH₃CH₂CH₂OH = propan-1-ol; CH₃COOH = ethanoic acid. The <strong>functional group outranks</strong> double bonds in suffix choice.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch8-l3', title:'Esters and Everyday Organics', html:'<h3>Esters and Everyday Organics</h3><p><strong>Esters</strong> (RCOOR′) form by <strong>esterification</strong>: carboxylic acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water (conc. H₂SO₄ catalyst) — <strong>sweet, fruity smells</strong> (perfumes, flavourings). <strong>Saponification:</strong> ester + NaOH → <strong>soap</strong> + glycerol. Everyday organics: <strong>ethanol</strong> (fuel, sanitiser), <strong>ethanoic acid</strong> (vinegar), <strong>plastics</strong> (polymers), <strong>detergents</strong>, <strong>medicines</strong> (aspirin, paracetamol) — organic chemistry runs modern life.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Butane and methylpropane are ___ isomers:', options:['position','chain','functional group','geometric'], answer:1, explain:'Same C₄H₁₀, different carbon skeleton.'},
{q:'Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are:', options:['chain isomers','position isomers','identical','polymers'], answer:1, explain:'The –OH sits on different carbons.'},
{q:'CH₃CH₂CH₂OH is named:', options:['propan-2-ol','propan-1-ol','ethanol','butanol'], answer:1, explain:'Three-carbon chain, –OH on carbon 1.'},
{q:'In IUPAC, substituents are listed:', options:['by size','alphabetically','by number','randomly'], answer:1, explain:'Alphabetical: bromo before chloro before methyl.'},
{q:'Esters are formed from:', options:['acid + base','carboxylic acid + alcohol','alkane + oxygen','salt + water'], answer:1, explain:'Esterification gives sweet-smelling esters.'},
{q:'Saponification makes:', options:['plastic','soap','ester','gasoline'], answer:1, explain:'Fat/oil + NaOH → soap + glycerol.'},
{q:'The catalyst for esterification is:', options:['iron','concentrated H₂SO₄','nickel','platinum'], answer:1, explain:'It absorbs water, driving equilibrium right.'},
{q:'Ethanoic acid’s common name is:', options:['formic acid','acetic acid (vinegar)','citric acid','oxalic acid'], answer:1, explain:'Vinegar is ~5% ethanoic acid.'},
{q:'Branching in isomers ___ boiling point:', options:['raises','lowers','does not change','doubles'], answer:1, explain:'Less surface contact weakens van der Waals forces.'},
{q:'The suffix for carboxylic acids is:', options:['-ol','-oic acid','-one','-al'], answer:1, explain:'-oic acid outranks -ol, -al, -one.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch9', title:'Hydrocarbons', urdu:'ہائیڈرو کاربن', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch9-l1', title:'Saturated vs Unsaturated Hydrocarbons', html:'<h3>Saturated vs Unsaturated Hydrocarbons</h3><p><strong>Saturated</strong> (alkanes, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂): single bonds only, <strong>unreactive</strong> except combustion and substitution. <strong>Unsaturated</strong> (alkenes CₙH₂ₙ, alkynes CₙH₂ₙ₋₂): multiple bonds, undergo <strong>addition reactions</strong>. <strong>Tests:</strong> <strong>bromine water</strong> (orange → colourless) and <strong>acidified KMnO₄</strong> (purple → colourless) — both decolourised by alkenes, unchanged by alkanes. <strong>Distinguishing</strong> them is a classic exam question.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch9-l2', title:'Reactions of Alkenes', html:'<h3>Reactions of Alkenes</h3><p><strong>Addition</strong> across C=C: <strong>hydrogenation</strong> (H₂, Ni catalyst → alkane; hardens vegetable oils into <strong>margarine</strong>), <strong>hydration</strong> (steam, H₃PO₄ catalyst → ethanol — industrial alcohol), <strong>bromination</strong>, <strong>hydrochlorination</strong> (Markovnikov: H adds to the carbon with more H’s). <strong>Polymerisation:</strong> ethene → <strong>poly(ethene)</strong> (addition polymer, high pressure, catalyst) — plastics from alkenes.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch9-l3', title:'Fossil Fuels and Fractional Distillation', html:'<h3>Fossil Fuels and Fractional Distillation</h3><p><strong>Crude oil</strong> (ancient marine organisms) is split by <strong>fractional distillation</strong>: <strong>refinery gas → petrol → naphtha → kerosene → diesel → lubricating oil → bitumen</strong> (rising boiling points). <strong>Cracking</strong> breaks long alkanes into <strong>shorter alkanes + alkenes</strong> (catalyst, heat) to meet petrol demand. <strong>Natural gas</strong> (methane) is the cleanest fossil fuel; <strong>coal</strong> the dirtiest. Combustion products: CO₂ (warming), CO/soot (incomplete), SO₂ (acid rain).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The general formula of alkenes is:', options:['CₙH₂ₙ₊₂','CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ₋₂','CₙHₙ'], answer:1, explain:'One double bond removes two hydrogens.'},
{q:'Bromine water with ethene:', options:['stays orange','decolourises','turns blue','forms precipitate'], answer:1, explain:'Br₂ adds across the double bond.'},
{q:'Hydrogenation of vegetable oil makes:', options:['soap','margarine','petrol','ethanol'], answer:1, explain:'Ni-catalysed H₂ addition saturates the oils.'},
{q:'Ethene → ethanol industrially needs:', options:['yeast','steam + phosphoric acid catalyst','sunlight','iron'], answer:1, explain:'Hydration of ethene at high T/P.'},
{q:'Cracking produces:', options:['only alkanes','shorter alkanes + alkenes','only gases','aromatics only'], answer:1, explain:'Long chains split, always yielding an alkene.'},
{q:'In fractional distillation, ___ comes off first:', options:['bitumen','refinery gas','diesel','lubricating oil'], answer:1, explain:'Smallest molecules have the lowest boiling points.'},
{q:'Markovnikov’s rule concerns addition of:', options:['bromine','HX to unsymmetrical alkenes','hydrogen','water'], answer:1, explain:'H adds to the carbon already bearing more H atoms.'},
{q:'Poly(ethene) is made by ___ polymerisation:', options:['condensation','addition','substitution','cracking'], answer:1, explain:'Alkene double bonds open to join chains.'},
{q:'The cleanest fossil fuel is:', options:['coal','natural gas','diesel','petrol'], answer:1, explain:'Methane burns to CO₂ + H₂O with least pollutants.'},
{q:'Incomplete combustion of hydrocarbons gives:', options:['CO₂ only','CO and soot','O₂','H₂'], answer:1, explain:'Limited oxygen yields poisonous CO and carbon.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch10', title:'Hydroxy Compounds', urdu:'ہائیڈروکسی مرکبات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch10-l1', title:'Alcohols: Structure and Manufacture', html:'<h3>Alcohols: Structure and Manufacture</h3><p><strong>Alcohols</strong> contain <strong>–OH</strong> (CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH): methanol, <strong>ethanol</strong> (CH₃CH₂OH), propanol. <strong>Manufacture of ethanol:</strong> 1) <strong>fermentation</strong> — glucose → ethanol + CO₂ using <strong>yeast</strong> (warm, anaerobic, ~15% max); 2) <strong>hydration of ethene</strong> (steam, H₃PO₄, high T/P — purer, unlimited). Ethanol is a <strong>renewable fuel</strong> (sugarcane) and superior <strong>solvent</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch10-l2', title:'Properties and Combustion of Alcohols', html:'<h3>Properties and Combustion of Alcohols</h3><p>Alcohols are <strong>neutral</strong>, mix with water (<strong>hydrogen bonding</strong>), and burn with a <strong>clean blue flame</strong>: C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O. <strong>Oxidation:</strong> ethanol → <strong>ethanal</strong> → <strong>ethanoic acid</strong> (acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, orange → green — the <strong>breathalyser</strong> test). <strong>Dehydration</strong> (conc. H₂SO₄, heat) removes water → <strong>ethene</strong>. Uses: <strong>sanitisers, fuels, solvents, perfumes</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch10-l3', title:'Alcohols in Industry, Health and Daily Life', html:'<h3>Alcohols in Industry, Health and Daily Life</h3><p><strong>Pharmaceutical:</strong> ethanol sterilises and carries medicines; <strong>methanol</strong> is a feedstock (but <strong>toxic</strong> — causes blindness). <strong>Cosmetics:</strong> perfumes, lotions (solvent, preservative). <strong>Public health:</strong> excessive drinking damages the <strong>liver</strong> (cirrhosis), impairs judgment and is <strong>forbidden in Islam</strong>; <strong>drink-driving kills</strong> — never mix alcohol and driving. <strong>Methylated spirit</strong> (ethanol + methanol, dyed) deters drinking of industrial alcohol.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The functional group of alcohols is:', options:['–COOH','–OH','C=O','–NH₂'], answer:1, explain:'The hydroxyl group defines alcohols.'},
{q:'Fermentation of glucose needs:', options:['oxygen','yeast, warmth, no air','sunlight','acid'], answer:1, explain:'Anaerobic yeast converts sugar to ethanol + CO₂.'},
{q:'Complete combustion of ethanol gives:', options:['CO + H₂O','CO₂ + H₂O','C + H₂O','CO₂ + H₂'], answer:1, explain:'C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.'},
{q:'Oxidation of ethanol with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ gives:', options:['ethene','ethanoic acid (via ethanal)','methanol','ester'], answer:1, explain:'Colour change orange → green confirms oxidation.'},
{q:'Dehydration of ethanol (conc. H₂SO₄, heat) gives:', options:['ethane','ethene','ethyne','methane'], answer:1, explain:'Water is removed across adjacent carbons.'},
{q:'Methanol is dangerous because it is:', options:['explosive','toxic (causes blindness)','radioactive','corrosive'], answer:1, explain:'Even small amounts damage the optic nerve.'},
{q:'Alcohols mix well with water due to:', options:['ionic bonds','hydrogen bonding','covalent networks','magnetism'], answer:1, explain:'The –OH group H-bonds with water.'},
{q:'In industry ethanol is made by:', options:['fermentation only','hydration of ethene (and fermentation)','cracking','distilling petrol'], answer:1, explain:'Both routes; hydration gives purer fuel-grade ethanol.'},
{q:'Methylated spirit contains methanol to:', options:['improve taste','deter drinking','add colour','sweeten'], answer:1, explain:'It poisons industrial alcohol against misuse.'},
{q:'Excessive alcohol harms mainly the:', options:['skin','liver','bones','teeth'], answer:1, explain:'The liver metabolises alcohol; overload causes cirrhosis.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch11', title:'Carboxylic Compounds', urdu:'کاربوکسلک مرکبات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch11-l1', title:'Carboxylic Acids: Formation and Properties', html:'<h3>Carboxylic Acids: Formation and Properties</h3><p><strong>Carboxylic acids</strong> contain <strong>–COOH</strong>: methanoic, <strong>ethanoic</strong> (CH₃COOH), propanoic. <strong>Formation:</strong> oxidation of alcohols (ethanol → ethanoic acid); <strong>vinegar</strong> is ~5% ethanoic acid from bacterial oxidation. <strong>Weak acids</strong> (partly ionised, pH 3–6): turn blue litmus red, fizz with <strong>carbonates</strong> (CO₂), react with metals and bases. <strong>Boiling points</strong> are high for their size (<strong>hydrogen bonding</strong>, often dimers).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch11-l2', title:'Reactions of Carboxylic Acids', html:'<h3>Reactions of Carboxylic Acids</h3><p>Typical acid reactions: + metal → <strong>salt + H₂</strong>; + base → <strong>salt + water</strong>; + carbonate → <strong>salt + CO₂ + H₂O</strong>. <strong>Esterification:</strong> acid + alcohol ⇌ <strong>ester + water</strong> (conc. H₂SO₄) — e.g. ethanoic acid + ethanol → <strong>ethyl ethanoate</strong> (fruity). <strong>Reduction</strong> is hard (strong agents to alcohols). <strong>Acid chlorides</strong> (SOCl₂) are more reactive derivatives for synthesis.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch11-l3', title:'Everyday and Industrial Uses', html:'<h3>Everyday and Industrial Uses</h3><p><strong>Everyday:</strong> vinegar (pickles, cooking), citric acid (soft drinks, <strong>vitamin C</strong>), <strong>aspirin</strong> (acetylsalicylic acid — painkiller), <strong>soaps</strong> from fatty acids. <strong>Industrial:</strong> ethanoic acid → <strong>plastics, fibres (acetate rayon), dyes</strong>; <strong>esters</strong> → perfumes, flavourings, <strong>solvents</strong>; methanoic acid in <strong>leather tanning</strong> and rubber. Carboxylic acids link the kitchen, pharmacy and factory.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The functional group –COOH belongs to:', options:['alcohols','carboxylic acids','esters','aldehydes'], answer:1, explain:'The carboxyl group defines the family.'},
{q:'Vinegar contains:', options:['methanoic acid','ethanoic acid','citric acid','HCl'], answer:1, explain:'~5% ethanoic (acetic) acid.'},
{q:'Carboxylic acids are weak because they:', options:['have no H⁺','partly ionise in water','are solids','lack oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'Only a fraction of molecules release H⁺.'},
{q:'Ethanoic acid + sodium carbonate gives:', options:['salt + H₂','salt + CO₂ + H₂O','ester','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Typical acid–carbonate fizz.'},
{q:'Esterification needs:', options:['iron catalyst','concentrated H₂SO₄','sunlight','high pressure'], answer:1, explain:'It removes water, shifting equilibrium to ester.'},
{q:'Ethanoic acid + ethanol →', options:['ethyl ethanoate + water','ethene','soap','vinegar'], answer:0, explain:'Ester + water; ethyl ethanoate smells fruity.'},
{q:'Aspirin is:', options:['an alcohol','acetylsalicylic acid','an ester only','a base'], answer:1, explain:'A carboxylic-acid-derived painkiller.'},
{q:'Carboxylic acids have high boiling points due to:', options:['ionic bonds','hydrogen bonding (dimers)','covalent networks','mass'], answer:1, explain:'Strong H-bonds, often paired dimers.'},
{q:'Citric acid is found in:', options:['vinegar','citrus fruits','soap','petrol'], answer:1, explain:'Lemons and oranges; also vitamin C products.'},
{q:'Esters are used as:', options:['acids','perfumes and flavourings','bases','fuels only'], answer:1, explain:'Their sweet smells suit fragrances and food.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch12', title:'Polymers', urdu:'پولیمر', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch12-l1', title:'What Polymers Are', html:'<h3>What Polymers Are</h3><p><strong>Polymers</strong> are long chains of repeating <strong>monomers</strong>: <strong>polymerisation</strong> joins thousands of small molecules. <strong>Natural:</strong> <strong>starch, cellulose, proteins, natural rubber, DNA</strong>. <strong>Synthetic:</strong> <strong>poly(ethene)</strong> (bags), <strong>PVC</strong> (pipes), <strong>nylon</strong> (clothes, ropes), <strong>polyester/terylene</strong> (fabric), <strong>bakelite</strong> (switches). Properties come from chain length and cross-linking — long chains = strong, flexible materials.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch12-l2', title:'Addition vs Condensation Polymerisation', html:'<h3>Addition vs Condensation Polymerisation</h3><p><strong>Addition:</strong> alkene monomers join by opening C=C — <strong>no by-product</strong> (poly(ethene), PVC, poly(propene)). <strong>Condensation:</strong> monomers with <strong>two functional groups</strong> join, expelling a <strong>small molecule (water/HCl)</strong> each link — <strong>nylon</strong> (diamine + dicarboxylic acid), <strong>polyester/terylene</strong> (diol + dicarboxylic acid). <strong>Drawing:</strong> addition repeat units keep all atoms; condensation repeat units lose H₂O per link.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch12-l3', title:'Plastics, Textiles and Disposal', html:'<h3>Plastics, Textiles and Disposal</h3><p><strong>Thermoplastics</strong> (polythene, PVC) <strong>melt and remould</strong> — recyclable. <strong>Thermosets</strong> (bakelite, melamine) <strong>set permanently</strong> — heat-resistant (pan handles, plugs). <strong>Textiles:</strong> nylon/polyester are strong, quick-drying synthetics blended with cotton. <strong>Disposal problem:</strong> plastics persist for centuries — <strong>reduce, reuse, recycle</strong>; Pakistan’s <strong>plastic-bag bans</strong> fight pollution; <strong>biodegradable</strong> polymers (PLA) are the greener future.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'A polymer is made of:', options:['atoms','repeating monomer units','salts','metals'], answer:1, explain:'Long chains of small repeating molecules.'},
{q:'Which is a natural polymer?', options:['nylon','starch','PVC','bakelite'], answer:1, explain:'Plants make starch and cellulose naturally.'},
{q:'Addition polymerisation:', options:['releases water','has no by-product','needs two functional groups','makes nylon'], answer:1, explain:'Alkene double bonds simply open and join.'},
{q:'Nylon is made by ___ polymerisation:', options:['addition','condensation','substitution','cracking'], answer:1, explain:'Diamine + dicarboxylic acid, losing water per link.'},
{q:'Thermoplastics can be:', options:['never melted','remelted and remoulded','burned only','dissolved in water'], answer:1, explain:'No cross-links, so heat softens them reversibly.'},
{q:'Bakelite is used for plug sockets because it is:', options:['cheap','a heat-resistant thermoset','flexible','transparent'], answer:1, explain:'Cross-linked thermosets do not melt.'},
{q:'PVC is used for:', options:['clothes','water pipes','food','fuel'], answer:1, explain:'Tough, waterproof poly(chloroethene).'},
{q:'Polyester is made from:', options:['ethene','a diol + a dicarboxylic acid','an alkene','cellulose'], answer:1, explain:'Condensation of two bifunctional monomers.'},
{q:'Plastic pollution is worst because plastics:', options:['are colourful','persist for centuries','are light','float'], answer:1, explain:'Non-biodegradable chains accumulate in the environment.'},
{q:'A biodegradable polymer is:', options:['PVC','PLA (polylactic acid)','nylon','bakelite'], answer:1, explain:'PLA breaks down via natural processes.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch13', title:'Biochemistry', urdu:'بائیو کیمسٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch13-l1', title:'Carbohydrates in Depth', html:'<h3>Carbohydrates in Depth</h3><p><strong>Monosaccharides</strong> (glucose, fructose — C₆H₁₂O₆) are the simplest sugars. <strong>Disaccharides</strong> (sucrose, maltose, lactose — C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) form by <strong>condensation</strong> (lose H₂O). <strong>Polysaccharides:</strong> <strong>starch</strong> (plant energy store), <strong>glycogen</strong> (animal store), <strong>cellulose</strong> (plant walls — <strong>fibre</strong> for digestion). <strong>Benedict’s test:</strong> reducing sugars (glucose) give a <strong>brick-red precipitate</strong> on heating; starch gives <strong>blue-black with iodine</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch13-l2', title:'Proteins, Lipids and Nucleic Acids', html:'<h3>Proteins, Lipids and Nucleic Acids</h3><p><strong>Proteins:</strong> amino-acid chains (<strong>peptide bonds</strong>); structure levels: primary → secondary → tertiary → quaternary (haemoglobin). <strong>Biuret test:</strong> violet with CuSO₄ + NaOH. <strong>Lipids:</strong> <strong>triglycerides</strong> (glycerol + 3 fatty acids); saturated vs unsaturated; <strong>phospholipids</strong> build membranes. <strong>Nucleic acids:</strong> <strong>DNA</strong> (double helix, A–T, G–C) stores heredity; <strong>RNA</strong> (single strand, uracil) builds proteins; <strong>ATP</strong> is the energy currency.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-che-ch13-l3', title:'Vitamins, Enzymes and Digestion', html:'<h3>Vitamins, Enzymes and Digestion</h3><p><strong>Vitamins:</strong> <strong>water-soluble</strong> B-complex and C (not stored — need daily); <strong>fat-soluble</strong> A, D, E, K (stored in liver/fat). Deficiencies: <strong>scurvy</strong> (C), <strong>rickets</strong> (D), <strong>night blindness</strong> (A). <strong>Enzymes</strong> (protein catalysts): <strong>amylase</strong> (mouth/pancreas, starch → maltose), <strong>pepsin</strong> (stomach, protein), <strong>lipase</strong> (fats → fatty acids + glycerol), <strong>trypsin</strong>. <strong>Bile</strong> (liver) <strong>emulsifies</strong> fats — no enzymes, but vital.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Sucrose is a:', options:['monosaccharide','disaccharide','polysaccharide','lipid'], answer:1, explain:'Two sugar units joined (glucose + fructose).'},
{q:'Benedict’s test for glucose gives:', options:['blue-black','brick-red precipitate','violet','yellow'], answer:1, explain:'Reducing sugars reduce blue Cu²⁺ to red Cu₂O.'},
{q:'Starch is tested with:', options:['Benedict’s','iodine (blue-black)','Biuret','litmus'], answer:1, explain:'Iodine fits into starch’s helix, turning blue-black.'},
{q:'Peptide bonds join:', options:['sugars','amino acids','fatty acids','nucleotides'], answer:1, explain:'Condensation links amino acids into proteins.'},
{q:'The Biuret test detects:', options:['sugar','protein (violet colour)','fat','starch'], answer:1, explain:'Cu²⁺ in alkaline solution turns violet with peptide bonds.'},
{q:'Fat-soluble vitamins are:', options:['B and C','A, D, E, K','only C','only B12'], answer:1, explain:'Stored in liver and fatty tissue.'},
{q:'Night blindness is caused by lack of vitamin:', options:['D','A','C','K'], answer:1, explain:'Vitamin A maintains the retina’s photoreceptors.'},
{q:'Bile’s role in digestion is to:', options:['digest protein','emulsify fats','absorb water','kill germs'], answer:1, explain:'It breaks fat into droplets for lipase.'},
{q:'ATP stands for:', options:['a protein','adenosine triphosphate','an amino acid','a vitamin'], answer:1, explain:'The cell’s energy currency.'},
{q:'Lipase digests fats into:', options:['glucose','fatty acids + glycerol','amino acids','starch'], answer:1, explain:'Enzymatic hydrolysis of triglycerides.'}
]}
]});

/* ================= CLASS 10 — BIOLOGY (FBISE / NBF, 11 chapters) ================= */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class10-biology', board:'federal', title:'Biology', urdu:'حیاتیات', class:'Class 10', icon:'🧬', desc:'FBISE Class 10 Biology — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch1', title:'Digestive System', urdu:'نظام انہضام', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch1-l1', title:'The Alimentary Canal', html:'<h3>The Alimentary Canal</h3><p>Food’s journey: <strong>mouth</strong> (teeth chew — <strong>mechanical digestion</strong>; saliva’s <strong>amylase</strong> starts starch digestion) → <strong>oesophagus</strong> (<strong>peristalsis</strong> — wave-like muscle contractions push food down) → <strong>stomach</strong> (churns; <strong>HCl</strong> kills germs and activates <strong>pepsin</strong> for protein) → <strong>small intestine</strong> (digestion completed, absorption) → <strong>large intestine</strong> (water absorbed) → <strong>egestion</strong>. Five stages: <strong>ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation, egestion</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch1-l2', title:'Chemical Digestion: Enzymes and Glands', html:'<h3>Chemical Digestion: Enzymes and Glands</h3><p><strong>Liver</strong> makes <strong>bile</strong> (stored in the <strong>gall bladder</strong>) — <strong>emulsifies fats</strong> and neutralises stomach acid; no enzymes. <strong>Pancreas</strong> secretes <strong>pancreatic juice</strong> (amylase, trypsin, lipase) and <strong>insulin</strong>. <strong>Intestinal glands</strong> finish digestion: <strong>maltase</strong> (maltose → glucose), <strong>peptidase</strong>, <strong>lipase</strong>. End products: starch → <strong>glucose</strong>, protein → <strong>amino acids</strong>, fats → <strong>fatty acids + glycerol</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch1-l3', title:'Absorption and Gut Disorders', html:'<h3>Absorption and Gut Disorders</h3><p><strong>Villi</strong> (finger-like folds with <strong>microvilli</strong>) give huge surface area; each villus has <strong>capillaries</strong> (absorb glucose, amino acids → blood) and a <strong>lacteal</strong> (absorbs fatty acids → lymph). Thin walls and rich blood supply speed uptake. <strong>Disorders:</strong> <strong>ulcers</strong> (acid damages stomach lining — <em>H. pylori</em>, NSAIDs), <strong>diarrhoea</strong> (water loss — treat with <strong>ORS</strong>), <strong>constipation</strong> (low fibre/water), <strong>appendicitis</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Peristalsis is:', options:['chewing','wave-like muscle contractions moving food','enzyme action','absorption'], answer:1, explain:'Rhythmic contractions push food along the gut.'},
{q:'Protein digestion begins in the:', options:['mouth','stomach','small intestine','large intestine'], answer:1, explain:'Pepsin in acidic gastric juice starts on proteins.'},
{q:'Bile’s role is to:', options:['digest starch','emulsify fats','absorb glucose','kill germs'], answer:1, explain:'It breaks fat into droplets; it has no enzymes.'},
{q:'Villi absorb nutrients because they have:', options:['thick walls','large surface area with capillaries','muscles','bones'], answer:1, explain:'Millions of villi maximise absorption area.'},
{q:'Fatty acids are absorbed into the:', options:['capillaries','lacteal','vein','artery'], answer:1, explain:'Lacteals (lymph vessels) take up fats.'},
{q:'Starch is finally digested to:', options:['sucrose','glucose','fructose','maltose only'], answer:1, explain:'Maltase converts maltose to glucose for absorption.'},
{q:'Diarrhoea is dangerous mainly due to:', options:['pain','water and salt loss','fever','hunger'], answer:1, explain:'Dehydration kills; ORS replaces fluids and salts.'},
{q:'The order of digestion stages is:', options:['ingestion, absorption, digestion, egestion','ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation, egestion','digestion, ingestion, egestion','absorption first'], answer:1, explain:'Eat → break down → absorb → use → expel.'},
{q:'HCl in the stomach:', options:['digests fat','kills germs and activates pepsin','absorbs water','makes bile'], answer:1, explain:'Acid pH ~2 suits pepsin and kills pathogens.'},
{q:'Insulin is secreted by the:', options:['liver','pancreas','gall bladder','stomach'], answer:1, explain:'Pancreatic islets release insulin to lower blood glucose.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch2', title:'Circulatory System', urdu:'نظام دوران خون', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch2-l1', title:'Blood and Its Components', html:'<h3>Blood and Its Components</h3><p>Blood (~5 litres) has <strong>plasma</strong> (55% — water, proteins, nutrients, hormones, waste) and cells: <strong>red blood cells</strong> (no nucleus, <strong>haemoglobin</strong> carries O₂; 120-day life), <strong>white blood cells</strong> (fight infection — <strong>phagocytes</strong> engulf germs, <strong>lymphocytes</strong> make antibodies), <strong>platelets</strong> (clotting). <strong>Clotting:</strong> damaged vessel → <strong>fibrin</strong> mesh traps cells → scab. <strong>Blood groups</strong> (ABO, Rh): O = universal donor, AB = universal recipient — matching prevents fatal clumping.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch2-l2', title:'The Heart', html:'<h3>The Heart</h3><p>The <strong>heart</strong> (fist-sized, <strong>cardiac muscle</strong>) has <strong>4 chambers</strong>: right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → body. <strong>Valves</strong> stop backflow: <strong>tricuspid</strong> (right), <strong>bicuspid/mitral</strong> (left), <strong>semi-lunar</strong> (arteries). The <strong>left ventricle</strong> wall is thickest (pumps to the whole body). <strong>Heartbeat:</strong> initiated by the <strong>pacemaker (SAN)</strong>; “lub-dup” = valves closing. <strong>Double circulation:</strong> <strong>pulmonary</strong> (heart↔lungs) + <strong>systemic</strong> (heart↔body).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch2-l3', title:'Vessels, Transport and Heart Disease', html:'<h3>Vessels, Transport and Heart Disease</h3><p><strong>Arteries</strong> (thick, elastic, carry blood <strong>away</strong> — mostly oxygenated), <strong>veins</strong> (thin, <strong>valves</strong>, toward heart), <strong>capillaries</strong> (one-cell thick — <strong>exchange</strong>). Blood transports <strong>O₂, CO₂, nutrients, hormones, urea</strong> and heat. <strong>Heart diseases:</strong> <strong>atherosclerosis</strong> (plaque narrows arteries), <strong>heart attack</strong> (blocked coronary artery), <strong>hypertension</strong>, <strong>stroke</strong>. <strong>Smoking</strong> damages vessels (nicotine constricts, CO reduces O₂); <strong>prevention:</strong> exercise, low-salt diet, no smoking.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Red blood cells carry oxygen using:', options:['plasma','haemoglobin','platelets','fibrin'], answer:1, explain:'Haemoglobin binds O₂ in the lungs, releases it in tissues.'},
{q:'White blood cells that engulf germs are:', options:['lymphocytes','phagocytes','platelets','erythrocytes'], answer:1, explain:'Phagocytes ingest pathogens.'},
{q:'The universal blood donor group is:', options:['AB','O','A','B'], answer:1, explain:'O has no A/B antigens to trigger reactions.'},
{q:'The pacemaker of the heart is the:', options:['AVN','SAN','aorta','valve'], answer:1, explain:'The sinoatrial node initiates each heartbeat.'},
{q:'The thickest heart wall belongs to the:', options:['right atrium','right ventricle','left ventricle','left atrium'], answer:2, explain:'It pumps blood around the entire body.'},
{q:'Valves in veins:', options:['pump blood','prevent backflow','make sound','filter germs'], answer:1, explain:'They keep low-pressure venous blood moving heartward.'},
{q:'Capillaries are one cell thick to:', options:['save space','allow rapid exchange','pump fast','resist pressure'], answer:1, explain:'Short diffusion distance for O₂, nutrients, waste.'},
{q:'A heart attack is caused by:', options:['fast heartbeat','blocked coronary artery','low sugar','infection'], answer:1, explain:'Starved heart muscle dies without oxygen.'},
{q:'Smoking harms the heart because:', options:['it cools blood','nicotine constricts vessels and CO cuts O₂','it adds iron','it thins blood'], answer:1, explain:'Both effects strain the cardiovascular system.'},
{q:'Double circulation means blood passes the heart ___ per circuit:', options:['once','twice','three times','never'], answer:1, explain:'Pulmonary loop + systemic loop.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch3', title:'Respiratory System', urdu:'نظام تنفس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch3-l1', title:'The Air Passageway', html:'<h3>The Air Passageway</h3><p>Air’s path: <strong>nostrils</strong> (hair and mucus trap dust; air warmed) → <strong>pharynx</strong> → <strong>larynx</strong> (voice box; <strong>epiglottis</strong> covers it when swallowing) → <strong>trachea</strong> (held open by <strong>C-shaped cartilage rings</strong>; lined with <strong>cilia</strong> sweeping mucus up) → <strong>bronchi</strong> → <strong>bronchioles</strong> → <strong>alveoli</strong> (tiny air sacs — the gas-exchange surface).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch3-l2', title:'Breathing Mechanism and Gas Exchange', html:'<h3>Breathing Mechanism and Gas Exchange</h3><p><strong>Inhalation:</strong> <strong>diaphragm contracts/flattens</strong> + <strong>ribs move up/out</strong> (external intercostals) → chest volume <strong>increases</strong> → pressure <strong>drops</strong> → air rushes in. <strong>Exhalation</strong> reverses it (diaphragm relaxes/domes). In the <strong>alveoli</strong> (huge surface, one-cell walls, dense capillaries), <strong>O₂ diffuses into blood</strong> and <strong>CO₂ diffuses out</strong>. Inspired air: ~21% O₂, 0.04% CO₂; expired: ~16% O₂, ~4% CO₂.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch3-l3', title:'Respiratory Diseases', html:'<h3>Respiratory Diseases</h3><p><strong>Asthma:</strong> airways narrow (spasms/mucus) — treated with <strong>inhalers (bronchodilators)</strong>. <strong>Bronchitis:</strong> inflamed bronchi, persistent cough (smoking is a top cause). <strong>Pneumonia:</strong> alveoli fill with fluid (bacterial — <strong>antibiotics</strong> help). <strong>Emphysema:</strong> alveolar walls break down (smoking). <strong>Lung cancer:</strong> strongly linked to <strong>smoking</strong>. <strong>Prevention:</strong> no smoking, clean air, masks in smog, vaccination.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The epiglottis:', options:['makes sound','covers the larynx during swallowing','warms air','pumps blood'], answer:1, explain:'It stops food entering the windpipe.'},
{q:'C-shaped cartilage in the trachea:', options:['makes mucus','keeps the airway open','absorbs O₂','moves ribs'], answer:1, explain:'Rings prevent collapse during pressure changes.'},
{q:'During inhalation, the diaphragm:', options:['relaxes and domes','contracts and flattens','stops','vibrates'], answer:1, explain:'Flattening increases chest volume, dropping pressure.'},
{q:'Gas exchange happens in the:', options:['bronchi','alveoli','trachea','larynx'], answer:1, explain:'Thin-walled sacs with huge total surface area.'},
{q:'Expired air contains about ___ CO₂:', options:['0.04%','4%','21%','16%'], answer:1, explain:'Up from 0.04% in inspired air.'},
{q:'Asthma is treated with:', options:['antibiotics','bronchodilator inhalers','surgery always','cough syrup only'], answer:1, explain:'Inhalers relax narrowed airways.'},
{q:'Emphysema damages the:', options:['bronchi','alveolar walls','diaphragm','ribs'], answer:1, explain:'Broken walls reduce gas-exchange surface.'},
{q:'Cilia in the airways:', options:['absorb oxygen','sweep mucus and dust upward','make sound','pump blood'], answer:1, explain:'The mucus escalator cleans inhaled air.'},
{q:'The main cause of lung cancer is:', options:['cold air','smoking','exercise','dust only'], answer:1, explain:'Tobacco carcinogens are the leading cause.'},
{q:'Air is warmed and filtered first in the:', options:['alveoli','nasal cavity','bronchi','lungs'], answer:1, explain:'Hairs, mucus and blood vessels condition incoming air.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch4', title:'Urinary System', urdu:'نظام بول', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch4-l1', title:'The Human Urinary System', html:'<h3>The Human Urinary System</h3><p>Organs: <strong>2 kidneys</strong> (bean-shaped, filter blood) → <strong>2 ureters</strong> (carry urine by peristalsis) → <strong>urinary bladder</strong> (stores) → <strong>urethra</strong> (expels). Kidneys also <strong>regulate water/salts</strong> (<strong>osmoregulation</strong>) and blood pressure. Each kidney has ~1 million <strong>nephrons</strong> — the filtering units. <strong>Urine</strong>: water, <strong>urea</strong>, excess salts.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch4-l2', title:'The Nephron: How Urine Forms', html:'<h3>The Nephron: How Urine Forms</h3><p>Three steps: 1) <strong>Glomerular filtration</strong> — high pressure forces water, salts, glucose, urea from the <strong>glomerulus</strong> into the <strong>Bowman’s capsule</strong> (cells/proteins stay). 2) <strong>Selective reabsorption</strong> — <strong>all glucose</strong>, most water and needed salts reabsorbed into blood (proximal tubule, loop of Henle). 3) <strong>Tubular secretion</strong> — extra H⁺/K⁺/drugs added. <strong>ADH</strong> (pituitary) controls water reabsorption — more ADH = concentrated urine.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch4-l3', title:'Kidney Disorders and Dialysis', html:'<h3>Kidney Disorders and Dialysis</h3><p><strong>Kidney stones</strong> (calcium oxalate — severe pain; treated with lithotripsy/fluids). <strong>Kidney failure</strong> (waste builds up — uraemia). <strong>Dialysis</strong> machine: blood flows past a <strong>semi-permeable membrane</strong> against <strong>dialysis fluid</strong>; urea and excess salts <strong>diffuse out</strong>; blood cells/proteins stay. <strong>Kidney transplant</strong> is the lasting cure (donor match vital). <strong>Prevention:</strong> drink water, limit salt, avoid NSAID overuse.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Urine is carried from kidney to bladder by the:', options:['urethra','ureters','nephrons','aorta'], answer:1, explain:'Ureters use peristalsis; the urethra exits the bladder.'},
{q:'The functional unit of the kidney is the:', options:['glomerulus alone','nephron','bladder','ureter'], answer:1, explain:'~1 million nephrons per kidney filter blood.'},
{q:'Filtration occurs at the:', options:['loop of Henle','glomerulus → Bowman’s capsule','collecting duct','ureter'], answer:1, explain:'Pressure forces small molecules into the capsule.'},
{q:'Normally, ___ is fully reabsorbed:', options:['urea','glucose','creatinine','excess salt'], answer:1, explain:'All glucose is reclaimed in the proximal tubule.'},
{q:'ADH increases:', options:['glucose','water reabsorption','urine volume','salt loss'], answer:1, explain:'Antidiuretic hormone concentrates the urine.'},
{q:'Dialysis removes waste by:', options:['filtration under pressure','diffusion across a semi-permeable membrane','evaporation','surgery'], answer:1, explain:'Urea diffuses from blood into dialysis fluid.'},
{q:'Kidney stones are often made of:', options:['sodium chloride','calcium oxalate','glucose','protein'], answer:1, explain:'Crystallised salts block the urinary tract painfully.'},
{q:'Urea is formed in the:', options:['kidney','liver','bladder','skin'], answer:1, explain:'The liver deaminates amino acids, making urea for kidney excretion.'},
{q:'Proteins in the filtrate are normally:', options:['present','absent (too large)','reabsorbed later','secreted'], answer:1, explain:'Glomerular filtration blocks cells and large proteins.'},
{q:'The bladder stores urine until released via the:', options:['ureter','urethra','nephron','capsule'], answer:1, explain:'The urethra carries urine out of the body.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch5', title:'Nervous System', urdu:'اعصابی نظام', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch5-l1', title:'Organisation of the Nervous System', html:'<h3>Organisation of the Nervous System</h3><p><strong>Central nervous system (CNS):</strong> <strong>brain + spinal cord</strong> — processing. <strong>Peripheral (PNS):</strong> <strong>cranial/spinal nerves</strong> — carrying messages; split into <strong>sensory</strong> (to CNS) and <strong>motor</strong> (from CNS). Motor divides into <strong>somatic</strong> (voluntary — skeletal muscles) and <strong>autonomic</strong> (involuntary — <strong>sympathetic</strong>: fight-or-flight; <strong>parasympathetic</strong>: rest-and-digest).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch5-l2', title:'Neurons, Impulses and the Reflex Arc', html:'<h3>Neurons, Impulses and the Reflex Arc</h3><p>A <strong>neuron</strong> has <strong>dendrites</strong> (receive), <strong>cell body</strong>, <strong>axon</strong> (conducts, insulated by <strong>myelin sheath</strong> for speed) and <strong>synaptic terminals</strong>. <strong>Nerve impulses</strong> are electrical along the axon, <strong>chemical across the synapse</strong> (<strong>neurotransmitters</strong> like acetylcholine). <strong>Reflex arc:</strong> receptor → sensory neuron → <strong>spinal cord</strong> (relay) → motor neuron → <strong>effector</strong> — e.g. jerking your hand from heat <strong>without waiting for the brain</strong>: fast protection.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch5-l3', title:'The Brain and Endocrine Glands', html:'<h3>The Brain and Endocrine Glands</h3><p><strong>Brain parts:</strong> <strong>cerebrum</strong> (thought, memory, voluntary movement — largest), <strong>cerebellum</strong> (balance, coordination), <strong>medulla</strong> (breathing, heartbeat — automatic). <strong>Endocrine system</strong> (slow, hormonal control via blood): <strong>pituitary</strong> (master gland — growth hormone), <strong>thyroid</strong> (thyroxine — metabolism; needs <strong>iodine</strong>), <strong>pancreas</strong> (insulin/glucagon — blood sugar), <strong>adrenals</strong> (adrenaline — emergency), <strong>gonads</strong> (sex hormones).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The CNS consists of the:', options:['nerves only','brain and spinal cord','glands','muscles'], answer:1, explain:'Central processing organs.'},
{q:'The myelin sheath:', options:['receives signals','speeds impulse conduction','makes neurotransmitters','stores energy'], answer:1, explain:'Insulation allows fast saltatory conduction.'},
{q:'Across a synapse, the impulse is:', options:['electrical','chemical (neurotransmitters)','magnetic','sound'], answer:1, explain:'Neurotransmitters cross the gap.'},
{q:'A reflex arc is fast because it:', options:['uses the brain','bypasses the brain via the spinal cord','uses hormones','is voluntary'], answer:1, explain:'Short path: receptor → cord → effector.'},
{q:'The cerebellum controls:', options:['thought','balance and coordination','breathing','vision'], answer:1, explain:'It fine-tunes movement and posture.'},
{q:'The medulla controls:', options:['memory','breathing and heartbeat','hearing','taste'], answer:1, explain:'Vital automatic functions.'},
{q:'The master gland is the:', options:['thyroid','pituitary','adrenal','pancreas'], answer:1, explain:'It controls other endocrine glands.'},
{q:'Thyroxine needs:', options:['iron','iodine','calcium','zinc'], answer:1, explain:'Iodine deficiency causes goitre.'},
{q:'Adrenaline is released in:', options:['sleep','emergencies (fight-or-flight)','digestion','rest'], answer:1, explain:'Adrenal glands prepare the body for action.'},
{q:'Insulin:', options:['raises blood sugar','lowers blood sugar','digests fat','makes bile'], answer:1, explain:'It drives glucose into cells; deficiency causes diabetes.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch6', title:'Reproduction', urdu:'تولید', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch6-l1', title:'Asexual vs Sexual Reproduction', html:'<h3>Asexual vs Sexual Reproduction</h3><p><strong>Asexual:</strong> one parent, no gametes — <strong>identical offspring</strong> (binary fission in Amoeba, budding in Hydra/yeast, spores, vegetative propagation). Fast, but <strong>no variation</strong>. <strong>Sexual:</strong> two parents, <strong>gametes fuse (fertilisation)</strong> — offspring show <strong>variation</strong>, aiding survival and evolution. Most animals (including humans) reproduce sexually; many plants do both.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch6-l2', title:'Human Reproductive Systems', html:'<h3>Human Reproductive Systems</h3><p><strong>Male:</strong> <strong>testes</strong> (make <strong>sperm</strong> + <strong>testosterone</strong>) → sperm travel via <strong>vas deferens</strong>; <strong>seminal vesicles/prostate</strong> add fluid (<strong>semen</strong>); <strong>penis</strong> delivers. <strong>Female:</strong> <strong>ovaries</strong> (release <strong>eggs/ova</strong> + <strong>oestrogen/progesterone</strong>) → <strong>oviduct (fallopian tube)</strong> — site of <strong>fertilisation</strong> → <strong>uterus</strong> (embryo implants, develops) → <strong>vagina</strong>. <strong>Puberty</strong> hormones trigger gamete production and secondary sexual characteristics.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch6-l3', title:'Fertilisation to Birth; STDs', html:'<h3>Fertilisation to Birth; STDs</h3><p><strong>Fertilisation</strong> (usually in the oviduct) → <strong>zygote</strong> → divides into <strong>embryo</strong> → implants in the <strong>uterus wall</strong> → <strong>placenta</strong> (nutrients/O₂ in, waste out) supports the <strong>foetus</strong>; <strong>pregnancy ≈ 9 months (40 weeks)</strong>, ending in <strong>birth (parturition)</strong>. <strong>Sexually transmitted diseases:</strong> <strong>AIDS (HIV)</strong>, <strong>syphilis</strong>, <strong>gonorrhoea</strong> — spread by unprotected contact; <strong>prevention:</strong> abstinence/faithfulness, safe practices; <strong>no cure for HIV</strong> (managed with antiretrovirals).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Budding in Hydra is:', options:['sexual','asexual reproduction','fertilisation','pollination'], answer:1, explain:'One parent produces a genetically identical bud.'},
{q:'Sexual reproduction’s advantage is:', options:['speed','genetic variation','simplicity','no energy cost'], answer:1, explain:'Variation helps populations adapt and evolve.'},
{q:'Sperm are produced in the:', options:['prostate','testes','vas deferens','urethra'], answer:1, explain:'Testes make sperm and testosterone.'},
{q:'Fertilisation in humans usually occurs in the:', options:['uterus','oviduct (fallopian tube)','ovary','vagina'], answer:1, explain:'The zygote then travels to implant in the uterus.'},
{q:'The placenta:', options:['makes sperm','exchanges nutrients, O₂ and waste','produces eggs','digests food'], answer:1, explain:'It links foetal and maternal blood without mixing.'},
{q:'Human pregnancy lasts about:', options:['6 months','9 months','12 months','3 months'], answer:1, explain:'~40 weeks from fertilisation to birth.'},
{q:'Oestrogen and progesterone are produced by the:', options:['testes','ovaries','placenta only','pituitary'], answer:1, explain:'Female sex hormones regulating the cycle and pregnancy.'},
{q:'HIV attacks the:', options:['liver','immune system','kidneys','lungs'], answer:1, explain:'It destroys helper T-cells, causing AIDS.'},
{q:'Semen is:', options:['sperm only','sperm plus glandular fluids','urine','blood'], answer:1, explain:'Vesicles and prostate add nourishing fluid.'},
{q:'Puberty is triggered by:', options:['growth hormone only','sex hormones','insulin','thyroxine'], answer:1, explain:'Testosterone/oestrogen start gamete production and adult traits.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch7', title:'Inheritance', urdu:'وراثت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch7-l1', title:'Mendel’s Laws', html:'<h3>Mendel’s Laws</h3><p><strong>Gregor Mendel</strong> (pea plants) founded genetics. <strong>Law of segregation:</strong> the two <strong>alleles</strong> of a gene separate into different gametes. <strong>Law of independent assortment:</strong> genes for different traits assort independently. <strong>Dominant</strong> alleles (T) mask <strong>recessive</strong> (t): Tt looks tall. <strong>Genotype</strong> = allele combo (TT, Tt, tt); <strong>phenotype</strong> = observable trait. <strong>Monohybrid cross</strong> Tt × Tt → <strong>3:1</strong> phenotype ratio (1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch7-l2', title:'Chromosomes, DNA and Sex Determination', html:'<h3>Chromosomes, DNA and Sex Determination</h3><p>Humans have <strong>46 chromosomes (23 pairs)</strong>: 22 <strong>autosome</strong> pairs + 1 pair of <strong>sex chromosomes</strong>. <strong>Female: XX; male: XY</strong> — the <strong>father’s sperm</strong> (X or Y) determines the child’s sex (50/50 chance). <strong>DNA</strong> segments called <strong>genes</strong> sit at <strong>loci</strong> on chromosomes; <strong>mutations</strong> (changes in DNA) create new alleles — source of variation, occasionally causing <strong>genetic disorders</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch7-l3', title:'Genetic Disorders and Variation', html:'<h3>Genetic Disorders and Variation</h3><p><strong>Down syndrome:</strong> extra chromosome 21 (<strong>trisomy</strong>). <strong>Haemophilia:</strong> X-linked recessive — mostly males, blood fails to clot. <strong>Thalassaemia:</strong> faulty haemoglobin (common in Pakistan — <strong>cousin marriages</strong> raise risk; screening matters). <strong>Colour blindness:</strong> X-linked. <strong>Continuous variation</strong> (height — many genes) vs <strong>discontinuous</strong> (blood group — single gene). Environment + genes shape every trait.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Mendel worked on:', options:['mice','pea plants','flies','humans'], answer:1, explain:'Pisum sativum gave clear trait ratios.'},
{q:'The law of segregation states alleles:', options:['stay together','separate into different gametes','disappear','multiply'], answer:1, explain:'Each gamete gets one allele per gene.'},
{q:'Tt × Tt gives phenotype ratio:', options:['1:1','3:1','9:3:3:1','1:2:1'], answer:1, explain:'3 dominant : 1 recessive (genotypes 1:2:1).'},
{q:'Genotype means:', options:['observable trait','allele combination','chromosome number','family tree'], answer:1, explain:'E.g. Tt; phenotype is what you see.'},
{q:'Human sex is determined by the:', options:['mother’s egg','father’s sperm (X or Y)','environment','diet'], answer:1, explain:'XX = female, XY = male.'},
{q:'Humans have ___ chromosomes:', options:['23','46','44','48'], answer:1, explain:'23 pairs: 22 autosome + 1 sex pair.'},
{q:'Haemophilia is:', options:['autosomal dominant','X-linked recessive','Y-linked','mitochondrial'], answer:1, explain:'Mostly males show it; females are usually carriers.'},
{q:'Down syndrome is:', options:['missing chromosome','trisomy 21','X-linked','a mutation in one gene'], answer:1, explain:'An extra copy of chromosome 21.'},
{q:'Height shows ___ variation:', options:['discontinuous','continuous','no','single-gene'], answer:1, explain:'Many genes + environment give a smooth range.'},
{q:'Thalassaemia affects:', options:['nerves','haemoglobin','bones','skin'], answer:1, explain:'Faulty globin chains reduce oxygen carriage.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch8', title:'Diseases', urdu:'بیماریاں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch8-l1', title:'Pathogens and Transmission', html:'<h3>Pathogens and Transmission</h3><p><strong>Pathogens</strong> cause disease: <strong>bacteria</strong> (cholera, TB — treatable with <strong>antibiotics</strong>), <strong>viruses</strong> (flu, HIV, dengue — <strong>antibiotics do NOT work</strong>; prevented by <strong>vaccines</strong>), <strong>fungi</strong> (ringworm), <strong>protozoa</strong> (malaria). <strong>Transmission:</strong> air (flu, TB), water/food (cholera, typhoid), vectors (mosquitoes → malaria/dengue), direct contact, blood. <strong>Hygiene</strong> breaks the chain.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch8-l2', title:'Common Diseases in Pakistan', html:'<h3>Common Diseases in Pakistan</h3><p><strong>Cholera/typhoid:</strong> contaminated water — <strong>boil water, ORS</strong>. <strong>Malaria:</strong> <em>Plasmodium</em> via <strong>Anopheles</strong> — nets, sprays, drain stagnant water. <strong>Dengue:</strong> <em>Aedes</em> mosquito (day-biter, clean stagnant water) — no stagnant water, repellents. <strong>Hepatitis B/C:</strong> blood/needles — <strong>vaccinate (B)</strong>, sterile syringes. <strong>TB:</strong> airborne — <strong>DOTS</strong> full antibiotic course; stopping early breeds <strong>drug resistance</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch8-l3', title:'Prevention and Control', html:'<h3>Prevention and Control</h3><p><strong>Vaccination</strong> (EPI programme: BCG, polio, measles…) gives <strong>active immunity</strong> — Pakistan fights to <strong>eradicate polio</strong>. <strong>Antibiotics</strong> kill bacteria (complete the course!). <strong>Antiseptics/disinfectants</strong> kill germs on skin/surfaces. <strong>Quarantine</strong> isolates the infected. <strong>Lifestyle:</strong> clean water, balanced diet, exercise and <strong>no smoking</strong> keep <strong>non-communicable diseases</strong> (diabetes, hypertension, cancer) at bay.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Antibiotics are effective against:', options:['viruses','bacteria','fungi only','all pathogens'], answer:1, explain:'They target bacterial structures, not viruses.'},
{q:'Malaria is caused by:', options:['a virus','Plasmodium (protozoan)','bacteria','fungus'], answer:1, explain:'Transmitted by Anopheles mosquitoes.'},
{q:'Dengue spreads via:', options:['dirty water','Aedes mosquitoes','air','touch'], answer:1, explain:'Day-biting Aedes breeding in clean stagnant water.'},
{q:'Vaccination provides:', options:['passive treatment','active immunity','antibiotics','surgery'], answer:1, explain:'It trains the immune system before exposure.'},
{q:'TB treatment (DOTS) must be:', options:['one dose','completed fully','skipped if better','only for children'], answer:1, explain:'Stopping early breeds drug-resistant TB.'},
{q:'Cholera spreads through:', options:['air','contaminated water','mosquitoes','touch'], answer:1, explain:'Waterborne Vibrio cholerae; ORS saves lives.'},
{q:'Hepatitis B is prevented by:', options:['antibiotics','vaccination','rest','diet'], answer:1, explain:'The Hep-B vaccine is part of EPI.'},
{q:'Polio in Pakistan is fought with:', options:['antibiotics','oral polio vaccine campaigns','surgery','quarantine only'], answer:1, explain:'Mass OPV drives aim at eradication.'},
{q:'A non-communicable disease is:', options:['flu','diabetes','cholera','malaria'], answer:1, explain:'It does not spread person to person.'},
{q:'Quarantine means:', options:['vaccinating','isolating the infected','fuming rooms','testing water'], answer:1, explain:'Separation stops transmission chains.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch9', title:'Immunity', urdu:'قوت مدافعت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch9-l1', title:'Lines of Defence', html:'<h3>Lines of Defence</h3><p><strong>First line (barriers):</strong> <strong>skin</strong>, <strong>mucus/cilia</strong>, <strong>stomach acid</strong>, <strong>tears/saliva</strong> (lysozyme). <strong>Second line (innate):</strong> <strong>phagocytes</strong> engulf invaders; <strong>inflammation</strong> (redness, swelling, heat) rushes defenders; <strong>fever</strong> hampers pathogens. <strong>Third line (adaptive):</strong> <strong>lymphocytes</strong> — B-cells make <strong>antibodies</strong> (Y-shaped proteins locking onto <strong>antigens</strong>), T-cells kill infected cells. <strong>Memory cells</strong> give faster second responses.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch9-l2', title:'Active vs Passive Immunity', html:'<h3>Active vs Passive Immunity</h3><p><strong>Active immunity:</strong> your body makes its own antibodies — <strong>natural</strong> (after infection) or <strong>artificial</strong> (vaccination); <strong>long-lasting</strong> via memory cells. <strong>Passive immunity:</strong> ready-made antibodies given — <strong>natural</strong> (mother’s milk/placenta to baby) or <strong>artificial</strong> (antiserum, e.g. anti-venom); <strong>immediate but short-lived</strong>. Vaccines contain <strong>weakened/killed pathogens or their antigens</strong> — they cannot cause the disease.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch9-l3', title:'Vaccines, Allergies and Organ Transplants', html:'<h3>Vaccines, Allergies and Organ Transplants</h3><p><strong>Herd immunity:</strong> high vaccination coverage protects those who cannot be vaccinated. <strong>Allergies</strong> are immune <strong>overreactions</strong> (pollen, dust — <strong>antihistamines</strong> help). <strong>Autoimmune diseases</strong> (e.g. type 1 diabetes) attack the body’s own cells. <strong>Transplants</strong> need <strong>tissue matching</strong>; recipients take <strong>immunosuppressants</strong> to stop rejection. <strong>Blood transfusion</strong> reactions show why <strong>ABO/Rh matching</strong> matters.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The first line of defence includes:', options:['antibodies','skin and mucus','phagocytes','fever'], answer:1, explain:'Physical and chemical barriers block entry.'},
{q:'Antibodies are made by:', options:['phagocytes','B-lymphocytes','platelets','red cells'], answer:1, explain:'B-cells produce Y-shaped antibody proteins.'},
{q:'Inflammation’s signs include:', options:['cold and pale','redness, swelling, heat','numbness','bleeding'], answer:1, explain:'Increased blood flow rushes defenders to the site.'},
{q:'Active immunity from vaccination is:', options:['immediate but brief','long-lasting via memory cells','passive','impossible'], answer:1, explain:'Memory cells remember the antigen for years.'},
{q:'Passive natural immunity for a baby comes from:', options:['vaccines','mother’s milk/placenta','infection','antibiotics'], answer:1, explain:'Ready-made maternal antibodies protect newborns.'},
{q:'Anti-venom is an example of ___ immunity:', options:['active natural','passive artificial','active artificial','innate'], answer:1, explain:'Pre-made antibodies injected for immediate protection.'},
{q:'Herd immunity protects:', options:['animals only','unvaccinated individuals via community coverage','doctors','hospitals'], answer:1, explain:'Disease cannot spread when most are immune.'},
{q:'An allergy is:', options:['an infection','an immune overreaction','a deficiency','a poison'], answer:1, explain:'Harmless antigens trigger exaggerated responses.'},
{q:'Transplant rejection is prevented by:', options:['antibiotics','immunosuppressants','vaccines','rest'], answer:1, explain:'They damp the immune attack on the new organ.'},
{q:'Antigens are:', options:['antibodies','foreign molecules triggering immunity','white cells','vaccines'], answer:1, explain:'Antibodies specifically bind their antigens.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch10', title:'Biotechnology', urdu:'بائیو ٹیکنالوجی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch10-l1', title:'What Biotechnology Is', html:'<h3>What Biotechnology Is</h3><p><strong>Biotechnology</strong> uses living organisms or their products for human benefit — ancient (bread, yoghurt, brewing) and modern (gene technology). <strong>Fermentation:</strong> yeast → <strong>ethanol + CO₂</strong> (baking, brewing); bacteria → <strong>yoghurt, cheese</strong>. <strong>Antibiotics</strong> (penicillin from <em>Penicillium</em> fungus — <strong>Fleming</strong>) revolutionised medicine. <strong>Single-cell protein</strong> and <strong>biogas</strong> (methane from waste) aid food and energy security.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch10-l2', title:'Genetic Engineering', html:'<h3>Genetic Engineering</h3><p><strong>Genetic engineering</strong> moves genes between organisms using <strong>restriction enzymes</strong> (molecular scissors) and <strong>vectors</strong> (plasmids). Steps: cut gene → insert into <strong>plasmid</strong> → into <strong>bacteria</strong> → bacteria make the product. <strong>Products:</strong> human <strong>insulin</strong> (diabetes), <strong>growth hormone</strong>, <strong>hepatitis B vaccine</strong>. <strong>GM crops:</strong> <strong>Bt cotton</strong> (pest-resistant — widely grown in Pakistan), <strong>golden rice</strong> (vitamin A). <strong>Cloning</strong> (Dolly the sheep) copies whole genomes.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch10-l3', title:'Tissue Culture and Applications', html:'<h3>Tissue Culture and Applications</h3><p><strong>Tissue culture (micropropagation):</strong> tiny plant pieces grown on <strong>sterile nutrient medium</strong> → <strong>callus</strong> → whole plants — producing <strong>thousands of identical, virus-free plants</strong> (banana, orchids, sugarcane) fast. <strong>Applications:</strong> <strong>medicine</strong> (vaccines, gene therapy), <strong>agriculture</strong> (high-yield, disease-resistant crops), <strong>environment</strong> (oil-eating bacteria, <strong>bioremediation</strong>), <strong>forensics</strong> (DNA fingerprinting). <strong>Ethics:</strong> safety testing, labelling GM food, and equitable access matter.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Biotechnology is:', options:['study of rocks','using living organisms for human benefit','making robots','mining'], answer:1, explain:'From brewing to genetic engineering.'},
{q:'Penicillin was discovered by:', options:['Pasteur','Fleming','Koch','Lister'], answer:1, explain:'From Penicillium mould killing bacteria (1928).'},
{q:'Restriction enzymes act as:', options:['glue','molecular scissors','vectors','hosts'], answer:1, explain:'They cut DNA at specific sequences.'},
{q:'In genetic engineering, plasmids serve as:', options:['scissors','vectors carrying genes','hosts','enzymes'], answer:1, explain:'Bacterial plasmids ferry genes into host cells.'},
{q:'Human insulin is now made by:', options:['pigs','genetically engineered bacteria','plants','chemical synthesis'], answer:1, explain:'Bacteria with the human insulin gene produce it.'},
{q:'Bt cotton is:', options:['organic cotton','pest-resistant GM cotton','coloured cotton','wild cotton'], answer:1, explain:'A bacterial gene makes it toxic to bollworms.'},
{q:'Tissue culture produces plants that are:', options:['all different','identical and virus-free','seeds','genetically modified always'], answer:1, explain:'Micropropagation clones elite plants rapidly.'},
{q:'Bioremediation uses:', options:['chemicals','microbes to clean pollution','bulldozers','fire'], answer:1, explain:'Oil-eating bacteria clean spills.'},
{q:'Fermentation by yeast gives:', options:['lactic acid only','ethanol + CO₂','methane','oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'Anaerobic respiration of sugars.'},
{q:'DNA fingerprinting is used in:', options:['farming','forensics','baking','mining'], answer:1, explain:'Unique DNA profiles identify individuals.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch11', title:'Biostatistics and Data Analysis', urdu:'حیاتیاتی شماریات اور ڈیٹا تجزیہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch11-l1', title:'What Biostatistics Is', html:'<h3>What Biostatistics Is</h3><p><strong>Biostatistics</strong> applies statistics to biology and medicine — testing drugs, tracking epidemics, analysing growth. <strong>Population</strong> (whole group) vs <strong>sample</strong> (studied subset — must be <strong>representative and random</strong>). <strong>Variables:</strong> <strong>quantitative</strong> (height, counted/measured) vs <strong>qualitative</strong> (blood group, gender). Good data needs clear <strong>aims</strong>, fair <strong>sampling</strong> and honest recording — the base of every medical trial.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch11-l2', title:'Summarising Data: Averages and Spread', html:'<h3>Summarising Data: Averages and Spread</h3><p><strong>Mean</strong> (average), <strong>median</strong> (middle), <strong>mode</strong> (commonest) summarise the centre. <strong>Spread</strong> matters too: <strong>range</strong> (max − min), and <strong>standard deviation</strong> (typical distance from the mean — small SD = consistent data). <strong>Example:</strong> two drug trials may share a mean recovery time, but the one with smaller SD is more reliable. <strong>Tables</strong> organise raw data; always include <strong>units</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-bio-ch11-l3', title:'Graphs and Interpreting Results', html:'<h3>Graphs and Interpreting Results</h3><p>Choose wisely: <strong>bar charts</strong> (categories), <strong>histograms</strong> (continuous data), <strong>line graphs</strong> (trends over time), <strong>scatter plots</strong> (relationships — look for <strong>correlation</strong>), <strong>pie charts</strong> (proportions). <strong>Correlation ≠ causation</strong> — ice-cream sales and drowning both rise in summer (heat causes both). Report <strong>conclusions</strong> tied to the aim, note <strong>limitations</strong>, and never overclaim from small samples.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Biostatistics is statistics applied to:', options:['business','biology and medicine','sports','weather'], answer:1, explain:'Trials, epidemics, growth studies.'},
{q:'A sample must be:', options:['large only','representative and random','biased','convenient only'], answer:1, explain:'Only fair samples generalise to the population.'},
{q:'Blood group is a ___ variable:', options:['quantitative','qualitative','continuous','measured'], answer:1, explain:'Categories, not numbers.'},
{q:'Standard deviation measures:', options:['the average','spread around the mean','the maximum','the total'], answer:1, explain:'Small SD = consistent, reliable data.'},
{q:'A scatter plot shows:', options:['categories','relationships/correlation','proportions','totals'], answer:1, explain:'Paired values reveal trends.'},
{q:'Correlation does not prove:', options:['a link','causation','a pattern','a trend'], answer:1, explain:'A third factor may drive both variables.'},
{q:'A histogram suits:', options:['categories','continuous grouped data','proportions','two variables'], answer:1, explain:'Touching bars for measured ranges.'},
{q:'The median is the:', options:['average','middle value when ordered','commonest value','largest value'], answer:1, explain:'Position-based, resisting outliers.'},
{q:'In a drug trial, the group getting no drug is the:', options:['sample','control group','population','variable'], answer:1, explain:'It isolates the drug’s true effect.'},
{q:'Raw data should first be:', options:['graphed','organised in tables with units','deleted','guessed'], answer:1, explain:'Tables structure data before analysis.'}
]}
]});

