window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class10-mathematics', board:'federal', title:'Mathematics', urdu:'ریاضی', class:'Class 10', icon:'📐', desc:'FBISE Class 10 Mathematics — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch1', title:'Complex Numbers', urdu:'مخلوط اعداد', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch1-l1', title:'Imaginary and Complex Numbers', html:'<h3>Imaginary and Complex Numbers</h3><p>No real number squares to −1, so mathematicians defined <strong>i = √(−1)</strong>, with <strong>i² = −1</strong>. A <strong>complex number</strong> z = <strong>a + bi</strong>: <strong>a</strong> = real part, <strong>b</strong> = imaginary part. <strong>Examples:</strong> 3 + 4i, −2 − i, 5 (real), 3i (pure imaginary). Complex numbers complete algebra — every quadratic now has solutions. They model <strong>AC circuits</strong> and <strong>signal processing</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch1-l2', title:'Operations on Complex Numbers', html:'<h3>Operations on Complex Numbers</h3><p><strong>Add/subtract:</strong> combine like parts — (3+4i) + (1−2i) = 4+2i. <strong>Multiply:</strong> expand, using i² = −1 — (2+3i)(1+i) = 2+2i+3i+3i² = <strong>−1+5i</strong>. <strong>Conjugate</strong> of a+bi is <strong>a−bi</strong>; <strong>z × z̄ = a²+b²</strong> (real). <strong>Divide</strong> by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate: (1+i)/(1−i) = (1+i)²/2 = <strong>i</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch1-l3', title:'Modulus and the Argand Plane', html:'<h3>Modulus and the Argand Plane</h3><p>The <strong>modulus</strong> |z| = <strong>√(a²+b²)</strong> — the distance from the origin: |3+4i| = 5. On the <strong>Argand plane</strong>, plot z = a+bi as the point <strong>(a, b)</strong>: real axis horizontal, imaginary vertical. Addition becomes <strong>vector addition</strong>; conjugates <strong>mirror across the real axis</strong>. The <strong>argument</strong> θ = tan⁻¹(b/a) gives the direction — polar form z = r(cos θ + i·sin θ).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'i² equals:', options:['1','−1','0','i'], answer:1, explain:'By definition, i = √(−1).'},
{q:'The imaginary part of 5 − 3i is:', options:['5','−3','3','3i'], answer:1, explain:'The coefficient of i (including its sign).'},
{q:'(2 + 3i) + (4 − i) equals:', options:['6 + 2i','6 + 4i','8 + 2i','6 − 3i'], answer:0, explain:'Add real and imaginary parts separately.'},
{q:'(1 + i)(1 − i) equals:', options:['0','2','1 + i²','2i'], answer:1, explain:'1 − i² = 1 + 1 = 2.'},
{q:'The conjugate of 3 − 4i is:', options:['−3 + 4i','3 + 4i','−3 − 4i','4 + 3i'], answer:1, explain:'Flip the sign of the imaginary part.'},
{q:'|3 + 4i| equals:', options:['7','5','25','12'], answer:1, explain:'√(9+16) = 5.'},
{q:'On the Argand plane, 2 − 5i is plotted at:', options:['(2, 5)','(2, −5)','(−2, 5)','(5, 2)'], answer:1, explain:'(real part, imaginary part).'},
{q:'z × z̄ always gives a:', options:['complex number','real number','imaginary number','zero'], answer:1, explain:'(a+bi)(a−bi) = a² + b².'},
{q:'i³ equals:', options:['1','−i','i','−1'], answer:1, explain:'i³ = i²·i = −i.'},
{q:'To divide complex numbers, multiply by the:', options:['reciprocal','conjugate of the denominator','modulus','imaginary part'], answer:1, explain:'This makes the denominator real.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch2', title:'Quadratic Equations', urdu:'دو درجی مساواتیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch2-l1', title:'Solving by Factorization', html:'<h3>Solving by Factorization</h3><p>Standard form: <strong>ax² + bx + c = 0</strong> (a ≠ 0). <strong>Factorize:</strong> x² + 5x + 6 = 0 → (x+2)(x+3) = 0 → <strong>x = −2 or x = −3</strong> (a product is zero only if a factor is). <strong>Extracting roots:</strong> x² = 25 → x = ±5. Always <strong>rearrange to standard form first</strong>: 2x² = 8x → 2x² − 8x = 0 → 2x(x−4) = 0 → x = 0 or 4.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch2-l2', title:'Completing the Square and the Formula', html:'<h3>Completing the Square and the Formula</h3><p><strong>Completing the square:</strong> x² + 6x + 5 = 0 → (x+3)² − 9 + 5 = 0 → (x+3)² = 4 → x = −1 or −5. This derives the <strong>quadratic formula: x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac)) / 2a</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 2x² + 5x − 3 = 0 → x = (−5 ± √(25+24))/4 = (−5 ± 7)/4 → x = 0.5 or x = −3. <strong>Check</strong> answers by substitution.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch2-l3', title:'Discriminant and Nature of Roots', html:'<h3>Discriminant and Nature of Roots</h3><p>The <strong>discriminant D = b² − 4ac</strong> reveals root nature: <strong>D > 0:</strong> two <strong>real distinct</strong> roots; <strong>D = 0:</strong> two <strong>equal</strong> (repeated) roots; <strong>D < 0:</strong> <strong>no real roots</strong> (two complex). <strong>Sum of roots = −b/a</strong>, <strong>product = c/a</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> x² − 4x + 4 = 0 → D = 16 − 16 = 0 → repeated root x = 2. Form equations from given roots: x² − (sum)x + product = 0.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The standard form of a quadratic is:', options:['ax + b = 0','ax² + bx + c = 0','ax³ + b = 0','a²x + b = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Degree 2 with a ≠ 0.'},
{q:'Solutions of x² − 9 = 0 are:', options:['x = 9','x = ±3','x = 3 only','no solution'], answer:1, explain:'x² = 9 gives two roots, ±3.'},
{q:'x² + 7x + 10 = 0 factorises to:', options:['(x+5)(x+2)','(x+10)(x+1)','(x−5)(x−2)','(x+7)(x+3)'], answer:0, explain:'Two numbers with product 10, sum 7: 5 and 2.'},
{q:'The quadratic formula is:', options:['x = (−b ± √(b²+4ac))/2a','x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac))/2a','x = (b ± √(b²−4ac))/2','x = −b/2a only'], answer:1, explain:'The standard formula.'},
{q:'For 2x² + 5x − 3 = 0, the roots are:', options:['3 and −0.5','0.5 and −3','5 and 3','−5 and 3'], answer:1, explain:'(−5 ± 7)/4 gives 0.5 and −3.'},
{q:'If D < 0, the equation has:', options:['two real roots','no real roots','one root','infinite roots'], answer:1, explain:'Negative discriminant → complex (non-real) roots.'},
{q:'Sum of roots of 3x² − 7x + 2 = 0 is:', options:['−7/3','7/3','2/3','−2/3'], answer:1, explain:'Sum = −b/a = 7/3.'},
{q:'Product of roots of x² + 4x − 5 = 0 is:', options:['4','−5','5','−4'], answer:1, explain:'Product = c/a = −5.'},
{q:'D = 0 means roots are:', options:['distinct','equal (repeated)','complex','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Zero discriminant gives a double root.'},
{q:'An equation with roots 2 and −6 is:', options:['x² + 4x − 12 = 0','x² − 4x − 12 = 0','x² + 4x + 12 = 0','x² − 8x + 12 = 0'], answer:0, explain:'x² − (sum)x + product = x² + 4x − 12.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch3', title:'Matrices and Determinants', urdu:'میٹرکس اور ڈیٹرمیننٹ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch3-l1', title:'Matrices: Types and Addition', html:'<h3>Matrices: Types and Addition</h3><p>A <strong>matrix</strong> is a rectangular array: <strong>order m×n</strong> (rows × columns). Types: <strong>row, column, square, diagonal, scalar, identity (I)</strong>, <strong>zero</strong>, <strong>symmetric</strong>. <strong>Add/subtract</strong> element-wise — orders must <strong>match</strong>. <strong>Scalar multiplication:</strong> multiply every element: 3×[[1,2],[3,4]] = [[3,6],[9,12]]. <strong>Transpose</strong> Aᵀ swaps rows and columns.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch3-l2', title:'Matrix Multiplication', html:'<h3>Matrix Multiplication</h3><p>Multiply <strong>row × column</strong>: (AB)ᵢⱼ = row i of A · column j of B. <strong>Order rule:</strong> (m×n)(n×p) = <strong>m×p</strong> — inner dimensions must match. <strong>Example:</strong> [[1,2],[3,4]] × [[5,6],[7,8]] = [[19,22],[43,50]]. <strong>Not commutative:</strong> AB ≠ BA generally. <strong>Identity:</strong> AI = IA = A. Used in <strong>computer graphics, cryptography</strong> and solving systems.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch3-l3', title:'Determinants and Inverses', html:'<h3>Determinants and Inverses</h3><p>For 2×2 [[a,b],[c,d]]: <strong>det = ad − bc</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> det[[3,8],[4,6]] = 18 − 32 = −14. A matrix is <strong>singular</strong> if det = 0 (no inverse). <strong>Inverse:</strong> A⁻¹ = <strong>(1/det) × [[d,−b],[−c,a]]</strong> (swap a,d; negate b,c). <strong>Solve systems:</strong> write AX = B → <strong>X = A⁻¹B</strong>. <strong>Cramer’s rule</strong> uses determinants: x = Dx/D, y = Dy/D.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'A 3×2 matrix has:', options:['3 columns, 2 rows','3 rows, 2 columns','6 rows','2 matrices'], answer:1, explain:'Order m×n = rows × columns.'},
{q:'Matrices can be added only if:', options:['they are square','orders match','they are 2×2','determinants are equal'], answer:1, explain:'Element-wise addition needs identical dimensions.'},
{q:'[[1,2],[3,4]] × [[5,6],[7,8]] top-left entry is:', options:['5','19','43','50'], answer:1, explain:'(1×5)+(2×7) = 19.'},
{q:'Matrix multiplication is generally:', options:['commutative','not commutative','always zero','impossible'], answer:1, explain:'AB ≠ BA in general.'},
{q:'det of [[3,8],[4,6]] is:', options:['50','−14','14','−50'], answer:1, explain:'ad − bc = 18 − 32 = −14.'},
{q:'A matrix with det = 0 is:', options:['invertible','singular','identity','symmetric'], answer:1, explain:'No inverse exists when det = 0.'},
{q:'The inverse of [[a,b],[c,d]] involves:', options:['dividing by (a+d)','(1/det)[[d,−b],[−c,a]]','swapping b and c','no change'], answer:1, explain:'Swap a,d; negate b,c; divide by determinant.'},
{q:'AI equals:', options:['0','A','I','2A'], answer:1, explain:'The identity matrix leaves A unchanged.'},
{q:'For (2×3)(3×4), the product order is:', options:['2×4','3×3','2×3','4×2'], answer:0, explain:'Inner 3s match; outer gives 2×4.'},
{q:'Cramer’s rule finds x as:', options:['D/Dx','Dx/D','Dy/D','D·Dx'], answer:1, explain:'Replace the x-column with constants, divide by D.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch4', title:'Linear and Quadratic Inequalities', urdu:'خطی اور دو درجی غیر مساواتیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch4-l1', title:'Solving Linear Inequalities', html:'<h3>Solving Linear Inequalities</h3><p>Solve like equations, remembering: <strong>× or ÷ by a negative reverses the sign</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> 3x − 7 < 11 → 3x < 18 → x < 6. <strong>Example:</strong> −2x + 5 ≥ 13 → −2x ≥ 8 → x ≤ −4 (sign flipped). Solutions are <strong>ranges</strong>, shown on <strong>number lines</strong> (open/filled circles) or in <strong>interval notation</strong>: x < 6 is (−∞, 6).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch4-l2', title:'Quadratic Inequalities', html:'<h3>Quadratic Inequalities</h3><p>Solve <strong>x² − 5x + 6 > 0</strong>: factor → (x−2)(x−3) > 0 → <strong>critical values</strong> 2, 3. The parabola opens upward, so it is positive <strong>outside</strong> the roots: <strong>x < 2 or x > 3</strong>. For < 0, take <strong>between</strong> the roots. <strong>Sign chart</strong> method: test each interval. <strong>Example:</strong> x² ≤ 9 → −3 ≤ x ≤ 3 (between, since ≤ and upward parabola).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch4-l3', title:'Graphical Solutions and Applications', html:'<h3>Graphical Solutions and Applications</h3><p><strong>Graph y = mx + c:</strong> the inequality <strong>y > mx + c</strong> is the region <strong>above</strong> the line (dashed line for strict </>). <strong>Systems:</strong> shade each inequality; the <strong>overlap</strong> is the solution (feasible region — basis of <strong>linear programming</strong>). <strong>Application:</strong> “A shop needs at least 100 items with budget Rs 5000” translates to inequalities solved for the feasible combination.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Solving −3x > 12 gives:', options:['x > −4','x < −4','x > 4','x < 4'], answer:1, explain:'Dividing by −3 reverses the sign.'},
{q:'x < 6 in interval notation is:', options:['[6, ∞)','(−∞, 6)','(−∞, 6]','(6, ∞)'], answer:1, explain:'Strict inequality: open bracket at 6.'},
{q:'For (x−2)(x−3) > 0, the solution is:', options:['2 < x < 3','x < 2 or x > 3','x > 3 only','all x'], answer:1, explain:'Positive outside the roots (upward parabola).'},
{q:'x² < 16 gives:', options:['x < 4','−4 < x < 4','x > 4','x < −4'], answer:1, explain:'Between the roots −4 and 4.'},
{q:'Critical values of x² − 7x + 10 ≥ 0 are:', options:['2 and 5','−2 and −5','7 and 10','0 and 7'], answer:0, explain:'Factor: (x−2)(x−5) = 0.'},
{q:'y > 2x + 1 is the region:', options:['below the line','above the line','on the line','nowhere'], answer:1, explain:'Greater y-values lie above.'},
{q:'A dashed boundary line means the inequality is:', options:['≤ or ≥','< or > (strict)','an equality','empty'], answer:1, explain:'Strict inequalities exclude the boundary.'},
{q:'The feasible region of a system is the:', options:['first inequality','overlap of all shadings','union','empty set'], answer:1, explain:'Points satisfying every inequality.'},
{q:'Solving 5 − 2x ≤ 11:', options:['x ≥ −3','x ≤ −3','x ≥ 3','x ≤ 3'], answer:0, explain:'−2x ≤ 6 → x ≥ −3 (flip).'},
{q:'Linear programming uses:', options:['equalities only','systems of inequalities','quadratics only','matrices only'], answer:1, explain:'The feasible region is defined by linear inequalities.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch5', title:'Algebraic Functions', urdu:'الجبری فنکشنز', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch5-l1', title:'Algebraic Fractions', html:'<h3>Algebraic Fractions</h3><p>Simplify by <strong>factoring first, then cancelling common factors</strong>: (x²−4)/(x²+4x+4) = (x−2)(x+2)/(x+2)² = <strong>(x−2)/(x+2)</strong>. <strong>Add/subtract:</strong> find the <strong>LCD</strong>: 1/x + 1/(x+1) = (2x+1)/(x(x+1)). <strong>Multiply:</strong> cancel across; <strong>divide:</strong> flip the second fraction. State <strong>excluded values</strong> (denominator ≠ 0).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch5-l2', title:'Partial Fractions', html:'<h3>Partial Fractions</h3><p>Split a complex fraction into simpler ones: <strong>(3x+5)/((x+1)(x+2)) = A/(x+1) + B/(x+2)</strong>. Multiply through: 3x+5 = A(x+2) + B(x+1). <strong>Cover-up method:</strong> set x = −1 → 2 = A(1) → A = 2; x = −2 → −1 = B(−1) → B = 1. So it equals <strong>2/(x+1) + 1/(x+2)</strong>. <strong>Repeated factors</strong> need A/(x+1) + B/(x+1)². Used in <strong>integration</strong> later.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch5-l3', title:'Rational Equations', html:'<h3>Rational Equations</h3><p>Solve <strong>(x+1)/(x−2) = 3</strong>: multiply by (x−2) → x+1 = 3x−6 → x = 3.5. <strong>Check for extraneous roots</strong> — reject any making a denominator zero. <strong>Example:</strong> 2/x + 3/(x+1) = 1 → multiply by x(x+1): 2(x+1) + 3x = x(x+1) → x² − 4x − 2 = 0 → x = 2 ± √6. <strong>Word problems:</strong> work-rate and travel problems often lead here.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'(x²−9)/(x−3) simplifies to:', options:['x + 3','x − 3','x + 9','3'], answer:0, explain:'Factor: (x−3)(x+3)/(x−3); x ≠ 3.'},
{q:'1/a + 1/b equals:', options:['2/(a+b)','(a+b)/ab','1/(a+b)','2/ab'], answer:1, explain:'Common denominator ab.'},
{q:'Dividing by (x+1)/(x−1) means multiplying by:', options:['(x+1)/(x−1)','(x−1)/(x+1)','(x+1)²','1'], answer:1, explain:'Flip the divisor.'},
{q:'In partial fractions, (3x+5)/((x+1)(x+2)), A equals:', options:['1','2','3','5'], answer:1, explain:'Cover-up at x = −1: 2 = A(1).'},
{q:'Excluded value in 1/(x−4) is:', options:['0','4','1','−4'], answer:1, explain:'Denominator zero is forbidden.'},
{q:'Solving (x+1)/(x−2) = 3 gives:', options:['x = 2','x = 3.5','x = −3.5','x = 7'], answer:1, explain:'x+1 = 3x−6 → x = 3.5.'},
{q:'An extraneous root is one that:', options:['is negative','makes a denominator zero','is a fraction','is large'], answer:1, explain:'It fails the original equation’s domain.'},
{q:'Partial fractions help with:', options:['graphing','integration','geometry','statistics'], answer:1, explain:'Simpler fractions integrate term by term.'},
{q:'(x²+5x+6)/(x+2) simplifies to:', options:['x + 4','x + 3','x + 2','x + 6'], answer:1, explain:'(x+2)(x+3)/(x+2) = x+3.'},
{q:'Repeated linear factors need terms like:', options:['A/(x+1) only','A/(x+1) + B/(x+1)²','A + B','1/(x+1)² only'], answer:1, explain:'Each power gets its own numerator.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch6', title:'Functions and Graphs', urdu:'فنکشنز اور گراف', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch6-l1', title:'Domain, Range and Types', html:'<h3>Domain, Range and Types</h3><p><strong>Domain</strong> = allowed inputs; <strong>range</strong> = actual outputs. <strong>Example:</strong> f(x) = √(x−2): domain x ≥ 2, range y ≥ 0. Types: <strong>one-to-one</strong> (distinct inputs → distinct outputs; passes the <strong>horizontal-line test</strong>), <strong>onto</strong> (range = whole codomain), <strong>bijective</strong> (both — invertible). <strong>Constant, linear, quadratic, cubic</strong> are polynomial functions; <strong>rational</strong> functions exclude denominator zeros.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch6-l2', title:'Composite and Inverse Functions', html:'<h3>Composite and Inverse Functions</h3><p><strong>Composite (fog)(x) = f(g(x))</strong> — apply g, then f. <strong>Example:</strong> f(x) = 2x, g(x) = x+3 → fog = 2(x+3) = 2x+6; gof = 2x+3 (order matters!). <strong>Inverse f⁻¹</strong> undoes f: <strong>swap x and y, solve for y</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> f(x) = 3x−2 → x = 3y−2 → f⁻¹(x) = (x+2)/3. <strong>Check:</strong> f(f⁻¹(x)) = x. Only <strong>bijective</strong> functions have inverses.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch6-l3', title:'Graphing Functions', html:'<h3>Graphing Functions</h3><p><strong>Linear:</strong> straight line (slope, intercepts). <strong>Quadratic:</strong> <strong>parabola</strong> — vertex at x = −b/2a; opens up if a > 0. <strong>Example:</strong> y = x² − 4x + 3: vertex (2, −1), roots 1 and 3. <strong>Transformations:</strong> y = f(x)+k shifts <strong>up</strong>; y = f(x−h) shifts <strong>right</strong>; y = −f(x) <strong>reflects</strong> in the x-axis; y = af(x) <strong>stretches</strong> vertically. Sketch by intercepts, vertex and shape.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The domain of f(x) = 1/(x−5) is:', options:['all reals','x ≠ 5','x > 5','x ≥ 5'], answer:1, explain:'Denominator cannot be zero.'},
{q:'A function is one-to-one if it passes the:', options:['vertical-line test','horizontal-line test','diagonal test','no test'], answer:1, explain:'Each y-value comes from exactly one x.'},
{q:'If f(x) = 2x and g(x) = x+3, then (fog)(2) =', options:['7','10','8','5'], answer:1, explain:'g(2) = 5, then f(5) = 10.'},
{q:'The inverse of f(x) = 3x − 2 is:', options:['(x−2)/3','(x+2)/3','3x+2','x/3+2'], answer:1, explain:'Swap: x = 3y−2 → y = (x+2)/3.'},
{q:'Only ___ functions have inverses:', options:['all','bijective','linear','quadratic'], answer:1, explain:'One-to-one and onto guarantees reversibility.'},
{q:'y = f(x) − 4 shifts the graph:', options:['up 4','down 4','right 4','left 4'], answer:1, explain:'Subtracting outside moves it down.'},
{q:'The vertex of y = x² − 6x + 5 is at x =', options:['−3','3','5','6'], answer:1, explain:'x = −b/2a = 6/2 = 3.'},
{q:'y = −f(x) is a reflection in the:', options:['y-axis','x-axis','origin','line y = x'], answer:1, explain:'Negating outputs flips vertically.'},
{q:'The range of f(x) = x² is:', options:['all reals','y ≥ 0','y ≤ 0','y > 1'], answer:1, explain:'Squares are never negative.'},
{q:'f(f⁻¹(x)) equals:', options:['0','x','1','f(x)'], answer:1, explain:'Inverse undoes the function.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch7', title:'Vectors in Plane', urdu:'مستوی میں ویکٹر', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch7-l1', title:'Vectors: Notation and Magnitude', html:'<h3>Vectors: Notation and Magnitude</h3><p>A <strong>vector</strong> has <strong>magnitude and direction</strong> (displacement, force, velocity). Notation: <strong>bold a</strong>, <strong>→AB</strong>, or components <strong>⟨x, y⟩</strong>. <strong>Magnitude</strong> |⟨x,y⟩| = <strong>√(x²+y²)</strong>: |⟨3,4⟩| = 5. <strong>Position vector</strong> of P(x,y) is ⟨x,y⟩ from the origin. <strong>→AB = b − a</strong> (position of B minus position of A). <strong>Unit vector</strong> â = a/|a| has magnitude 1.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch7-l2', title:'Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication', html:'<h3>Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication</h3><p><strong>Add</strong> component-wise: ⟨1,2⟩ + ⟨3,−1⟩ = ⟨4,1⟩. Geometrically: <strong>triangle/parallelogram law</strong> (head-to-tail). <strong>Scalar multiplication:</strong> k⟨x,y⟩ = ⟨kx,ky⟩ — scales magnitude; negative k <strong>reverses</strong> direction. <strong>Resultant</strong> of two vectors: magnitude by <strong>cosine rule</strong> if the angle is known. Vectors model <strong>forces, velocities</strong> (aeroplane + wind = resultant path).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch7-l3', title:'Dot Product and Applications', html:'<h3>Dot Product and Applications</h3><p><strong>Dot product: a·b = |a||b|cos θ = x₁x₂ + y₁y₂</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> ⟨2,3⟩·⟨4,−1⟩ = 8−3 = 5. <strong>Perpendicular</strong> vectors have <strong>a·b = 0</strong>. Find <strong>angles</strong>: cos θ = (a·b)/(|a||b|). <strong>Work</strong> in physics = <strong>F·d</strong>. Applications: <strong>navigation</strong> (resultant of ship + current), <strong>engineering forces</strong>, <strong>computer graphics</strong> (lighting via dot products).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'|⟨6, 8⟩| equals:', options:['14','10','48','100'], answer:1, explain:'√(36+64) = 10.'},
{q:'→AB where A(1,2), B(4,6) is:', options:['⟨5,8⟩','⟨3,4⟩','⟨−3,−4⟩','⟨4,6⟩'], answer:1, explain:'b − a = ⟨4−1, 6−2⟩.'},
{q:'⟨2,−3⟩ + ⟨−5,7⟩ equals:', options:['⟨−3,4⟩','⟨7,−10⟩','⟨−3,−10⟩','⟨3,4⟩'], answer:0, explain:'Add components: ⟨−3, 4⟩.'},
{q:'3⟨2,−1⟩ equals:', options:['⟨5,2⟩','⟨6,−3⟩','⟨2,−3⟩','⟨6,3⟩'], answer:1, explain:'Multiply each component by 3.'},
{q:'The dot product ⟨2,3⟩·⟨4,−1⟩ is:', options:['11','5','−5','14'], answer:1, explain:'(2×4) + (3×−1) = 5.'},
{q:'Two vectors are perpendicular if their dot product is:', options:['1','0','−1','maximum'], answer:1, explain:'cos 90° = 0.'},
{q:'A unit vector has magnitude:', options:['0','1','10','variable'], answer:1, explain:'By definition, |â| = 1.'},
{q:'Multiplying a vector by −2:', options:['doubles it same direction','doubles magnitude, reverses direction','halves it','rotates 90°'], answer:1, explain:'Negative scalars reverse direction.'},
{q:'Work done by a force is:', options:['F + d','F·d (dot product)','F×d','F/d'], answer:1, explain:'Only the parallel component does work.'},
{q:'Position vector of P(3,−4) is:', options:['⟨−3,4⟩','⟨3,−4⟩','⟨4,3⟩','⟨0,0⟩'], answer:1, explain:'From origin to P.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch8', title:'Application of Trigonometry', urdu:'مثلثیات کے اطلاقات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch8-l1', title:'Trigonometric Ratios and Identities', html:'<h3>Trigonometric Ratios and Identities</h3><p>Recap: <strong>sin θ = opp/hyp, cos θ = adj/hyp, tan θ = opp/adj</strong>. Identities: <strong>sin²θ + cos²θ = 1</strong>; <strong>1 + tan²θ = sec²θ</strong>; <strong>1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ</strong>. <strong>Prove</strong> identities by converting to sin/cos: e.g. tan θ·cos θ = (sin θ/cos θ)·cos θ = <strong>sin θ</strong>. Standard values for <strong>30°, 45°, 60°</strong> must be memorised.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch8-l2', title:'Heights and Distances', html:'<h3>Heights and Distances</h3><p><strong>Angle of elevation</strong> (looking up) and <strong>depression</strong> (looking down) unlock height problems. <strong>Method:</strong> draw the diagram, mark the right triangle, choose the ratio linking known and unknown. <strong>Example:</strong> from 50 m away, a tower’s top has 30° elevation → height = 50·tan 30° = 50/√3 ≈ 28.9 m. <strong>Two-triangle</strong> problems: set up two equations for a common height.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch8-l3', title:'Bearings and Practical Problems', html:'<h3>Practical Problems</h3><p><strong>Bearings:</strong> three-figure angles clockwise from north. <strong>Example:</strong> ship sails 060° for 12 km: north component 12·cos 60° = 6 km; east 12·sin 60° ≈ 10.4 km. <strong>Sine rule</strong> (a/sin A = b/sin B) and <strong>cosine rule</strong> (c² = a²+b²−2ab·cos C) solve <strong>non-right triangles</strong> in navigation and surveying. Always <strong>draw north lines</strong> at each point in bearing diagrams.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'1 + tan²θ equals:', options:['cosec²θ','sec²θ','sin²θ','cot²θ'], answer:1, explain:'Divide sin²+cos² = 1 by cos².'},
{q:'tan θ × cos θ simplifies to:', options:['cot θ','sin θ','1','sec θ'], answer:1, explain:'(sin/cos)×cos = sin.'},
{q:'A tower 20 m away subtends 45° elevation; its height is:', options:['10 m','20 m','40 m','28 m'], answer:1, explain:'h = 20·tan 45° = 20×1.'},
{q:'Angle of depression is measured:', options:['upward','downward from horizontal','from north','in radians'], answer:1, explain:'Looking down from the horizontal.'},
{q:'Bearings are measured:', options:['anticlockwise from south','clockwise from north','from east','in radians'], answer:1, explain:'Three-figure, clockwise from north.'},
{q:'The sine rule is:', options:['a/sin A = b/sin B','a² = b²+c²','sin²+cos² = 1','c = a+b'], answer:0, explain:'Sides proportional to sines of opposite angles.'},
{q:'The cosine rule finds:', options:['angles only','a side from two sides and included angle','areas','heights'], answer:1, explain:'c² = a²+b²−2ab·cos C.'},
{q:'sin 60° equals:', options:['1/2','√3/2','1','√2/2'], answer:1, explain:'Standard 30°–60°–90° value.'},
{q:'A ship on bearing 090° sails due:', options:['north','east','south','west'], answer:1, explain:'090° clockwise from north = east.'},
{q:'In heights problems, the first step is to:', options:['guess','draw and label the diagram','compute','memorise'], answer:1, explain:'A clear diagram reveals the right triangle and ratio.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch9', title:'Chord and Arcs of a Circle', urdu:'دائرے کے وتر اور قوس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch9-l1', title:'Chords: Key Theorems', html:'<h3>Chords: Key Theorems</h3><p>A <strong>chord</strong> joins two circle points. <strong>Theorem 1:</strong> the <strong>perpendicular from the centre bisects the chord</strong> (and converse: the line from centre bisecting a chord is perpendicular). <strong>Theorem 2:</strong> <strong>equal chords are equidistant</strong> from the centre (and converse). <strong>Example:</strong> chord length 8 cm, distance from centre 3 cm → half-chord 4, radius = √(16+9) = <strong>5 cm</strong> (right triangle!).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch9-l2', title:'Arcs, Sectors and Segments', html:'<h3>Arcs, Sectors and Segments</h3><p><strong>Arc length = (θ/360°) × 2πr</strong>; <strong>sector area = (θ/360°) × πr²</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> r = 7 cm, θ = 90° → arc = ¼ × 44 = 11 cm; sector = ¼ × 154 = 38.5 cm². A <strong>segment</strong> = sector minus the triangle. <strong>Radian</strong> alternative: arc = rθ, area = ½r²θ (θ in radians). Equal arcs subtend <strong>equal angles at the centre</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch9-l3', title:'Angles in Circles', html:'<h3>Angles in Circles</h3><p><strong>Theorem:</strong> the angle subtended by an arc at the <strong>centre is double</strong> that at the <strong>circumference</strong>. <strong>Angles in the same segment are equal.</strong> <strong>Angle in a semicircle = 90°</strong> — a favourite exam proof. <strong>Cyclic quadrilaterals:</strong> <strong>opposite angles sum to 180°</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> centre angle 80° → circumference angle 40°.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The perpendicular from the centre to a chord:', options:['trisects it','bisects it','misses it','equals the radius'], answer:1, explain:'The key chord theorem.'},
{q:'Chord 10 cm, distance from centre 12 cm → radius =', options:['13 cm','22 cm','17 cm','26 cm'], answer:0, explain:'√(5²+12²) = 13 cm.'},
{q:'Arc length for r = 7, θ = 90° (π = 22/7):', options:['22 cm','11 cm','44 cm','7 cm'], answer:1, explain:'(90/360)×2×(22/7)×7 = 11 cm.'},
{q:'Sector area for the same data is:', options:['77 cm²','38.5 cm²','154 cm²','11 cm²'], answer:1, explain:'(90/360)×(22/7)×49 = 38.5 cm².'},
{q:'Angle at the centre is ___ angle at the circumference:', options:['half','double','equal to','triple'], answer:1, explain:'Same arc: centre = 2 × circumference.'},
{q:'Angle in a semicircle is:', options:['45°','90°','180°','60°'], answer:1, explain:'It subtends a diameter (180° at centre).'},
{q:'In a cyclic quadrilateral, opposite angles:', options:['are equal','sum to 180°','sum to 90°','are zero'], answer:1, explain:'The cyclic quadrilateral theorem.'},
{q:'Equal chords of a circle are:', options:['parallel','equidistant from the centre','perpendicular','diameters'], answer:1, explain:'Equal chords lie the same distance from the centre.'},
{q:'A segment is the region between:', options:['two radii','a chord and its arc','two chords','arc and diameter'], answer:1, explain:'Sector minus the triangle.'},
{q:'Angles in the same segment are:', options:['supplementary','equal','complementary','90°'], answer:1, explain:'They subtend the same chord.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch10', title:'Tangents and Angles of a Circle', urdu:'مماس اور دائرے کے زاویے', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch10-l1', title:'Tangent Properties', html:'<h3>Tangent Properties</h3><p>A <strong>tangent</strong> touches the circle at <strong>exactly one point</strong>. <strong>Theorem 1:</strong> the <strong>tangent is perpendicular to the radius</strong> at the point of contact. <strong>Theorem 2:</strong> <strong>tangents from an external point are equal in length</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> two tangents from P touch at A, B with PA = 6 cm → PB = 6 cm. The line from the centre to P <strong>bisects</strong> the angle between the tangents.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch10-l2', title:'Alternate Segment Theorem', html:'<h3>Alternate Segment Theorem</h3><p><strong>Alternate segment theorem:</strong> the angle between a <strong>tangent and a chord</strong> through the contact point equals the angle in the <strong>alternate segment</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> tangent–chord angle = 50° → the angle the chord subtends in the opposite segment is also 50°. This is the most-tested circle theorem — <strong>identify the tangent, the chord, and the opposite arc</strong> before applying it.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch10-l3', title:'Circle Geometry Proofs', html:'<h3>Circle Geometry Proofs</h3><p><strong>Proof strategy:</strong> list <strong>given</strong>, state the <strong>required</strong>, then chain reasons: “tangent ⊥ radius”, “angles in the same segment”, “alternate segment theorem”, “cyclic quadrilateral”. <strong>Example proof:</strong> angle in a semicircle is 90° — the diameter subtends 180° at the centre, so 90° at the circumference. <strong>Common tangents</strong> to two circles: <strong>direct</strong> (same side) and <strong>transverse</strong> (crossing) — used in belt-drive problems.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'A tangent meets the radius at the contact point at:', options:['45°','90°','180°','0°'], answer:1, explain:'Tangent ⊥ radius theorem.'},
{q:'Tangents from one external point are:', options:['perpendicular','equal in length','parallel','unequal'], answer:1, explain:'Symmetry about the line to the centre.'},
{q:'The alternate segment theorem relates:', options:['two tangents','tangent–chord angle to the angle in the alternate segment','two chords','radius and diameter'], answer:1, explain:'Tangent–chord angle = angle in the opposite segment.'},
{q:'If tangent–chord angle is 60°, the alternate segment angle is:', options:['30°','60°','120°','90°'], answer:1, explain:'They are equal by the theorem.'},
{q:'A tangent touches a circle at ___ point(s):', options:['0','1','2','3'], answer:1, explain:'Exactly one point of contact.'},
{q:'The line from centre to an external point ___ the angle between its two tangents:', options:['doubles','bisects','trisects','ignores'], answer:1, explain:'Symmetry of the tangent configuration.'},
{q:'In a proof, each step needs a:', options:['diagram only','geometric reason','guess','number'], answer:1, explain:'Cite the theorem justifying each deduction.'},
{q:'Transverse common tangents:', options:['do not exist','cross between the circles','are parallel','touch once'], answer:1, explain:'They cross the line of centres between circles.'},
{q:'Angle in a semicircle is 90° because the diameter subtends:', options:['90° at centre','180° at the centre','360°','0°'], answer:1, explain:'Half of 180° at the circumference.'},
{q:'Belt drives use:', options:['chords','common tangents','diameters','arcs'], answer:1, explain:'Belts are common external tangents to pulleys.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch11', title:'Practical Geometry of Circles', urdu:'دائروں کی عملی جیومیٹری', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch11-l1', title:'Circumscribed Circles', html:'<h3>Circumscribed Circles</h3><p>To <strong>circumscribe</strong> a triangle: draw the <strong>perpendicular bisectors</strong> of two sides — they meet at the <strong>circumcentre</strong> (equidistant from all vertices). Set the compass to a vertex and draw the circle through all three. Works for <strong>any triangle</strong>; the circumcentre lies <strong>inside</strong> acute, <strong>outside</strong> obtuse, and at the <strong>hypotenuse midpoint</strong> for right triangles.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch11-l2', title:'Inscribed Circles', html:'<h3>Inscribed Circles</h3><p>To <strong>inscribe</strong> a circle: draw the <strong>angle bisectors</strong> of two angles — they meet at the <strong>incentre</strong> (equidistant from all sides). Drop a <strong>perpendicular</strong> from the incentre to one side for the radius; draw the circle touching all three sides. The incircle is the <strong>largest circle fitting inside</strong> the triangle — used in design and packing problems.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch11-l3', title:'Tangents from a Point and Escribed Circles', html:'<h3>Tangents from a Point and Escribed Circles</h3><p><strong>Tangents from external point P:</strong> join P to the centre O, draw the circle with <strong>diameter PO</strong> — where it cuts the given circle are the <strong>contact points</strong> (angle in a semicircle = 90° guarantees tangency). <strong>Escribed circle:</strong> touches one side externally and the extensions of the other two — centre at the meeting of <strong>two exterior angle bisectors</strong>. <strong>Check</strong> constructions by measuring.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The circumcentre is the meeting point of:', options:['angle bisectors','perpendicular bisectors of sides','medians','altitudes'], answer:1, explain:'Equidistant from all three vertices.'},
{q:'For a right triangle, the circumcentre lies:', options:['inside','at the hypotenuse midpoint','outside always','at the vertex'], answer:1, explain:'The hypotenuse is a diameter of the circumcircle.'},
{q:'The incentre is the meeting point of:', options:['medians','angle bisectors','perpendicular bisectors','sides'], answer:1, explain:'Equidistant from all three sides.'},
{q:'To draw tangents from an external point, first:', options:['guess','join P to centre, draw circle on PO as diameter','draw any line','measure angles'], answer:1, explain:'The semicircle construction gives 90° tangency points.'},
{q:'An escribed circle touches:', options:['all sides internally','one side externally and extensions of two','one vertex','no sides'], answer:1, explain:'It sits outside, tangent to one side and two extensions.'},
{q:'The incircle radius is found by dropping a ___ from incentre to a side:', options:['parallel','perpendicular','angle bisector','median'], answer:1, explain:'Perpendicular distance = radius.'},
{q:'Circumcentre of an obtuse triangle lies:', options:['inside','outside','on a side','at incentre'], answer:1, explain:'Outside for obtuse triangles.'},
{q:'The incircle is the ___ circle inside a triangle:', options:['smallest','largest','only','imaginary'], answer:1, explain:'Tangent to all three sides, maximal size.'},
{q:'Construction tools allowed are:', options:['protractor','compass and straightedge','ruler only','calculator'], answer:1, explain:'Classical constructions.'},
{q:'Angle in a semicircle is used in tangent construction to guarantee:', options:['equal lengths','90° at contact','parallel lines','bisection'], answer:1, explain:'Radius ⊥ tangent at the contact point.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch12', title:'Basic Statistics', urdu:'بنیادی شماریات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch12-l1', title:'Frequency Distributions', html:'<h3>Frequency Distributions</h3><p>Organise data into <strong>class intervals</strong> with <strong>boundaries</strong> (e.g. 9.5–19.5 for 10–19), <strong>class marks</strong> (midpoints) and <strong>frequencies</strong>. <strong>Cumulative frequency</strong> (“less than” totals) builds the <strong>ogive</strong>. <strong>Example:</strong> marks 12, 25, 33 → cumulative: 1, 2, 3. <strong>Relative frequency</strong> = f/total. Well-built tables are the foundation of every graph and average.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch12-l2', title:'Mean, Median, Mode of Grouped Data', html:'<h3>Mean, Median, Mode of Grouped Data</h3><p><strong>Mean:</strong> Σ(f·x)/Σf using class marks. <strong>Median:</strong> the <strong>(n/2)th</strong> value’s class — read from the <strong>ogive</strong> or interpolate. <strong>Mode:</strong> the class with <strong>highest frequency</strong> (modal class); estimate precisely with the formula. <strong>Example:</strong> classes 0–10 (f=5), 10–20 (f=8): mean ≈ (5×5 + 15×8)/13 ≈ 11.15. Grouped statistics are <strong>estimates</strong> — exact values are lost in grouping.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-mat-ch12-l3', title:'Histograms, Ogives and Pie Charts', html:'<h3>Histograms, Ogives and Pie Charts</h3><p><strong>Histogram:</strong> touching bars, area ∝ frequency (use <strong>frequency density</strong> = f/class width for unequal widths). <strong>Frequency polygon:</strong> join class-mark tops. <strong>Ogive</strong> (cumulative frequency curve): read the <strong>median</strong> at n/2, <strong>quartiles</strong> at n/4 and 3n/4; <strong>interquartile range = Q3 − Q1</strong> measures spread robustly. <strong>Pie chart:</strong> angle = (value/total) × 360°.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Class mark of 20–30 is:', options:['20','25','30','10'], answer:1, explain:'Midpoint: (20+30)/2.'},
{q:'Cumulative frequency is:', options:['f/total','running total of frequencies','max − min','f × x'], answer:1, explain:'“Less than” totals accumulate down the table.'},
{q:'The ogive is a graph of:', options:['frequency','cumulative frequency','class marks','pie sectors'], answer:1, explain:'Plot upper boundaries vs cumulative f.'},
{q:'Median from an ogive is read at:', options:['n/4','n/2','3n/4','n'], answer:1, explain:'The middle cumulative value.'},
{q:'Interquartile range =', options:['Q3 + Q1','Q3 − Q1','max − min','Q2 − Q1'], answer:1, explain:'Spread of the middle 50%.'},
{q:'For unequal class widths, a histogram uses:', options:['frequency','frequency density','cumulative f','class marks'], answer:1, explain:'Area ∝ frequency: height = f/width.'},
{q:'In a pie chart, 25% of the total is:', options:['45°','90°','180°','120°'], answer:1, explain:'0.25 × 360° = 90°.'},
{q:'The modal class is the one with:', options:['lowest frequency','highest frequency','widest interval','most marks'], answer:1, explain:'Mode = most frequent.'},
{q:'Grouped-data statistics are:', options:['exact','estimates','always wrong','integers'], answer:1, explain:'Grouping loses exact values.'},
{q:'Mean of grouped data uses:', options:['lower boundaries','class marks (f·x)/Σf','upper boundaries','medians'], answer:1, explain:'Midpoints represent each class.'}
]}
]});

