window.__saMergeCourse({id:'fed-class10-english', board:'federal', title:'English', urdu:'انگریزی', class:'Class 10', icon:'📖', desc:'FBISE Class 10 English — full NBF syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch1', title:'Animal Rights in Islam: Showing Compassion', urdu:'اسلام میں جانوروں کے حقوق', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch1-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit teaches <strong>compassion toward animals</strong> as an Islamic duty: the Prophet ﷺ forbade <strong>cruelty</strong> — overloading animals, <strong>branding faces</strong>, killing for sport — and promised <strong>reward for kindness</strong> (the famous hadith of the man who gave water to a thirsty dog). <strong>Central idea:</strong> mercy extends to <strong>all creation</strong>; how we treat the weak, including animals, reflects our character. Modern links: <strong>wildlife protection</strong> and humane farming.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch1-l2', title:'Vocabulary and Comprehension', html:'<h3>Vocabulary and Comprehension</h3><p>Key words: <strong>compassion</strong>, <strong>cruelty</strong>, <strong>humane</strong>, <strong>welfare</strong>, <strong>sentient</strong> (able to feel). <strong>Reading comprehension:</strong> identify the <strong>main idea</strong> vs <strong>supporting details</strong>; answer <strong>short questions</strong> in <strong>complete sentences</strong> using the text’s key terms. <strong>Reference words:</strong> “this kindness” points to the act just described. <strong>Synonyms:</strong> mercy ≈ compassion; cruelty ≠ kindness.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch1-l3', title:'Grammar: Tenses and Sentence Correction', html:'<h3>Grammar: Tenses and Sentence Correction</h3><p><strong>Tense consistency:</strong> “The Prophet ﷺ <em>forbade</em> cruelty and <em>taught</em> mercy” (both past). <strong>Common errors:</strong> subject–verb agreement (“Animals <em>deserve</em> care” — plural subject, no -s), <strong>article use</strong> (“<em>a</em> dog”, “<em>the</em> Prophet ﷺ”), <strong>prepositions</strong> (“kind <em>to</em> animals”, “cruel <em>to</em>”). <strong>Correct the sentence:</strong> “He give water to the dog” → “He <em>gave</em> water to the dog.”</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Islam teaches about animals:', options:['they have no rights','compassion and kindness toward them','they are unimportant','only usefulness matters'], answer:1, explain:'Mercy to animals is a rewarded duty.'},
{q:'The hadith of the thirsty dog shows:', options:['dogs are bad','kindness to animals is rewarded','water is scarce','dogs are pets'], answer:1, explain:'The man was forgiven for giving water to a dog.'},
{q:'“Sentient” means:', options:['silent','able to feel','strong','wild'], answer:1, explain:'Capable of feeling pain and pleasure.'},
{q:'“He give water” is wrong because:', options:['spelling','tense — should be “gave”','word order','article'], answer:1, explain:'Past narrative needs past simple.'},
{q:'The antonym of “cruelty” is:', options:['weakness','kindness','anger','fear'], answer:1, explain:'Opposite meanings.'},
{q:'Short answers in comprehension should be:', options:['one word','complete sentences','copied fully','skipped'], answer:1, explain:'Full sentences show understanding.'},
{q:'“Kind ___ animals” takes the preposition:', options:['with','to','for','at'], answer:1, explain:'Kind/cruel to someone.'},
{q:'A supporting detail:', options:['is the main idea','backs the main idea with evidence','is irrelevant','replaces the title'], answer:1, explain:'Details prove or illustrate the central point.'},
{q:'Modern application of the unit’s message includes:', options:['hunting sports','wildlife protection','animal testing only','caging birds'], answer:1, explain:'Compassion extends to conservation.'},
{q:'“Animals deserves care” is wrong due to:', options:['tense','subject–verb agreement','spelling','punctuation'], answer:1, explain:'Plural subject takes “deserve”.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch2', title:'Cultural Festivals of Pakistan: Unity in Diversity', urdu:'پاکستان کے ثقافتی میلے', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch2-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>Pakistan’s <strong>cultural festivals</strong> — <strong>Basant</strong> (Punjab’s kite spring), <strong>Shandur Polo Festival</strong> (Gilgit-Baltistan), <strong>Kalash festivals</strong> (Chitral), <strong>Sibi Mela</strong> (Balochistan), <strong>Urs</strong> celebrations (Sindh) — showcase <strong>unity in diversity</strong>: many cultures, one nation. Festivals preserve <strong>heritage</strong>, attract <strong>tourism</strong> and build <strong>national harmony</strong>. <strong>Central idea:</strong> celebrating differences strengthens Pakistan.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch2-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Culture Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Culture Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>heritage</strong>, <strong>diversity</strong>, <strong>tradition</strong>, <strong>harmony</strong>, <strong>vibrant</strong>, <strong>ritual</strong>. <strong>Word families:</strong> celebrate → celebration; diverse → diversity; unite → unity. <strong>Descriptive writing:</strong> use <strong>adjectives</strong> (colourful, melodious) and <strong>sensory details</strong> (sight, sound, taste) to bring a festival alive — “the sky blazed with kites.”</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch2-l3', title:'Writing: Descriptive Paragraphs', html:'<h3>Writing: Descriptive Paragraphs</h3><p><strong>Descriptive paragraph</strong> structure: <strong>topic sentence</strong> (what/where) → <strong>sensory details</strong> (what you see, hear, smell, taste) → <strong>feelings</strong> → closing line. <strong>Show, don’t just tell:</strong> “drums thundered” beats “there was music”. <strong>Present tense</strong> for general descriptions; <strong>past tense</strong> for a festival you attended. <strong>Exam task:</strong> describe a cultural festival of Pakistan in 100–120 words.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Unity in diversity” means:', options:['everyone is identical','many cultures, one nation','no festivals','one culture only'], answer:1, explain:'Diversity celebrated within national unity.'},
{q:'Shandur Polo Festival is held in:', options:['Punjab','Gilgit-Baltistan','Sindh','Balochistan'], answer:1, explain:'At the world’s highest polo ground.'},
{q:'Basant is the festival of:', options:['winter','spring kites','harvest dance','boats'], answer:1, explain:'Punjab’s spring kite festival.'},
{q:'The noun form of “diverse” is:', options:['diversity','diversion','diverseity','diversify'], answer:0, explain:'Diverse (adj) → diversity (noun).'},
{q:'Descriptive writing appeals mainly to the:', options:['mind','senses','grammar','logic'], answer:1, explain:'Sight, sound, smell, taste, touch.'},
{q:'“The sky blazed with kites” is better than “there were kites” because it:', options:['is longer','shows through vivid verbs','rhymes','is past tense'], answer:1, explain:'Show, don’t just tell.'},
{q:'Festivals help tourism by:', options:['closing hotels','attracting visitors','raising prices','blocking roads'], answer:1, explain:'Cultural events draw domestic and foreign tourists.'},
{q:'A descriptive paragraph’s closing line should:', options:['add new topics','wrap up the impression','ask questions','list facts'], answer:1, explain:'End with the overall feeling or significance.'},
{q:'The synonym of “vibrant” is:', options:['dull','lively','dark','quiet'], answer:1, explain:'Full of energy and colour.'},
{q:'Kalash festivals belong to:', options:['Punjab','Chitral','Karachi','Multan'], answer:1, explain:'The Kalash valleys of Chitral.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch3', title:'Media Literacy in the Modern Age', urdu:'جدید دور میں میڈیا کی سمجھ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch3-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p><strong>Media literacy</strong> is the ability to <strong>access, analyse, evaluate and create</strong> media. In the age of <strong>social media, MOOCs and 24-hour news</strong>, we must spot <strong>misinformation, bias and clickbait</strong>. <strong>Central idea:</strong> be a <strong>critical consumer</strong>, not a passive one — <strong>verify before sharing</strong>. Digital tools also <strong>democratise learning</strong> (free courses for all).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch3-l2', title:'Spotting Fake News: Vocabulary and Skills', html:'<h3>Spotting Fake News: Vocabulary and Skills</h3><p>Key words: <strong>bias</strong>, <strong>credible</strong>, <strong>source</strong>, <strong>viral</strong>, <strong>manipulate</strong>. <strong>Fact-check steps:</strong> 1) check the <strong>source</strong>; 2) look for <strong>other outlets</strong> reporting it; 3) check the <strong>date</strong>; 4) <strong>reverse-image search</strong> photos; 5) read <strong>beyond the headline</strong>. <strong>Loaded language</strong> (“shocking!”, “you won’t believe”) signals clickbait.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch3-l3', title:'Grammar: Reported Speech in Media', html:'<h3>Grammar: Reported Speech in Media</h3><p>News <strong>reports speech</strong>: Direct: The minister said, “We <em>will</em> act.” → Reported: The minister said they <em>would</em> act. <strong>Rules:</strong> backshift tenses (is → was, have → had), change pronouns/time (“today” → “that day”). <strong>Headlines</strong> use <strong>present simple</strong> for past events (“Minister <em>announces</em> plan”) and <strong>infinitives</strong> for future (“PM <em>to visit</em> Lahore”).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Media literacy means the ability to:', options:['make films','critically analyse media','avoid media','buy media'], answer:1, explain:'Access, analyse, evaluate and create media wisely.'},
{q:'First step in fact-checking is to:', options:['share quickly','check the source','like the post','comment'], answer:1, explain:'Credible sources come first.'},
{q:'Clickbait headlines use:', options:['calm facts','loaded, sensational language','long sentences','no verbs'], answer:1, explain:'“Shocking!” baits clicks.'},
{q:'Reported: She said, “I am busy” →', options:['she said she is busy','she said she was busy','she says she was busy','she said I was busy'], answer:1, explain:'Backshift: am → was; pronoun I → she.'},
{q:'Headlines use present simple to report:', options:['future','past events vividly','opinions','weather'], answer:1, explain:'“Minister announces…” = recent past.'},
{q:'“PM to visit Lahore” uses the infinitive for:', options:['past','future','orders','questions'], answer:1, explain:'Headline shorthand for future.'},
{q:'A credible source is one that is:', options:['popular','trustworthy and verifiable','funny','new'], answer:1, explain:'Established, evidence-based outlets.'},
{q:'MOOCs are:', options:['movies','massive open online courses','news apps','games'], answer:1, explain:'Free online courses democratising learning.'},
{q:'Reading beyond the headline matters because:', options:['headlines are long','headlines can mislead','it wastes time','it is fun'], answer:1, explain:'Headlines may distort the full story.'},
{q:'Bias in media means:', options:['fairness','one-sided slant','brevity','humour'], answer:1, explain:'Favouring one viewpoint unfairly.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch4', title:'Thank You, Ma’am by Langston Hughes', urdu:'شکریہ محترمہ (لینگسٹن ہیوز)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch4-l1', title:'Story Summary', html:'<h3>Story Summary</h3><p>A boy, <strong>Roger</strong>, tries to <strong>snatch Mrs. Luella Bates Washington Jones’s purse</strong>. Instead of handing him to police, she takes him home, <strong>feeds him</strong>, talks kindly, gives him <strong>$10 for the blue suede shoes</strong> he wanted, and sends him off with <strong>“behave yourself”</strong>. <strong>Theme:</strong> <strong>compassion reforms</strong> better than punishment; <strong>trust</strong> can transform a young offender. Roger leaves unable even to say “thank you” — overwhelmed.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch4-l2', title:'Character Sketch: Mrs. Jones', html:'<h3>Character Sketch: Mrs. Jones</h3><p><strong>Mrs. Jones:</strong> <strong>strong</strong> (drags Roger by his shirt), <strong>compassionate</strong> (feeds and counsels him), <strong>wise</strong> (understands poverty’s temptations — “I were young once”), <strong>trusting</strong> (leaves her door open, gives money). <strong>Roger:</strong> poor, tempted, but <strong>remorseful</strong> and <strong>respectful</strong> (washes his face as told). <strong>Character-sketch method:</strong> trait + <strong>evidence</strong> from the story.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch4-l3', title:'Moral and Exam Questions', html:'<h3>Moral and Exam Questions</h3><p><strong>Moral:</strong> kindness and second chances reform people; everyone deserves dignity. <strong>Common questions:</strong> Why did Mrs. Jones not call the police? (She believed <strong>guidance beats punishment</strong>.) Why couldn’t Roger say thank you? (Overwhelmed by <strong>unexpected kindness</strong>.) <strong>Answer tip:</strong> begin with a direct answer, add <strong>story evidence</strong>, end with the <strong>moral link</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Roger tried to steal:', options:['a watch','Mrs. Jones’s purse','shoes','money from a shop'], answer:1, explain:'He wanted money for blue suede shoes.'},
{q:'Mrs. Jones responded with:', options:['police','kindness and guidance','anger','indifference'], answer:1, explain:'She fed him, talked to him and gave him money.'},
{q:'She gave Roger:', options:['nothing','$10','new shoes','a job'], answer:1, explain:'$10 to buy the blue suede shoes.'},
{q:'The story’s theme is:', options:['crime pays','compassion reforms better than punishment','poverty is fate','shoes matter'], answer:1, explain:'Trust and kindness transform Roger.'},
{q:'Mrs. Jones shows wisdom when she says:', options:['“go away”','“I were young once too”','“you are bad”','nothing'], answer:1, explain:'She understands youthful temptation.'},
{q:'Roger couldn’t say thank you because he was:', options:['rude','overwhelmed by kindness','in a hurry','asleep'], answer:1, explain:'Unexpected grace left him speechless.'},
{q:'A character sketch needs:', options:['only appearance','traits with story evidence','the author’s name','dialogue only'], answer:1, explain:'Each trait must be backed by the text.'},
{q:'The author is:', options:['O. Henry','Langston Hughes','Mark Twain','Hemingway'], answer:1, explain:'The Harlem Renaissance writer.'},
{q:'Leaving her door open shows Mrs. Jones’s:', options:['carelessness','trust','wealth','fear'], answer:1, explain:'She trusted Roger despite the theft.'},
{q:'Roger washed his face when told, showing he was:', options:['defiant','respectful and remorseful','lazy','proud'], answer:1, explain:'He obeyed, ashamed of his act.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch5', title:'Mother Nature (Poem) by Emily Dickinson', urdu:'مادر فطرت (نظم)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch5-l1', title:'Poem Summary and Theme', html:'<h3>Poem Summary and Theme</h3><p>Dickinson’s <strong>“Mother Nature”</strong> personifies nature as a <strong>mother</strong> — gentle yet powerful, nurturing all living things through seasons. <strong>Theme:</strong> nature’s <strong>beauty, generosity and mystery</strong>; humans should <strong>respect and protect</strong> it. The poem invites <strong>wonder</strong> at creation — echoing the Islamic view of nature as <strong>signs (ayat)</strong> of the Creator.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch5-l2', title:'Poetic Devices', html:'<h3>Poetic Devices</h3><p><strong>Personification:</strong> Nature as “Mother” with human care. <strong>Metaphor:</strong> seasons as her moods/gifts. <strong>Imagery:</strong> vivid natural scenes appealing to the senses. <strong>Capitalisation</strong> of Nature/Mother shows <strong>reverence</strong>. <strong>Tone:</strong> affectionate, awestruck. <strong>Exam skill:</strong> quote a line, name the device, explain its <strong>effect</strong> on the reader.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch5-l3', title:'Appreciation', html:'<h3>Appreciation</h3><p><strong>Appreciation points:</strong> striking <strong>central metaphor</strong> (mother), <strong>musical language</strong>, emotional <strong>warmth</strong>, universal theme. <strong>Structure:</strong> central idea → two devices with quotations → personal response → theme link. <strong>Sample:</strong> “Calling nature ‘Mother’ makes us feel <strong>gratitude and responsibility</strong> — we do not harm our mother.” Keep it <strong>concise and quoted</strong>.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Mother Nature” is written by:', options:['Frost','Emily Dickinson','Tennyson','Wordsworth'], answer:1, explain:'The American poet of nature and inner life.'},
{q:'The poem personifies nature as a:', options:['queen','mother','teacher','child'], answer:1, explain:'Nurturing, powerful, deserving reverence.'},
{q:'The theme is:', options:['city life','beauty of nature and our duty to respect it','war','technology'], answer:1, explain:'Wonder at creation plus responsibility.'},
{q:'Capitalising “Mother” shows:', options:['grammar error','reverence','shouting','a name only'], answer:1, explain:'Respect for nature’s grandeur.'},
{q:'Personification means:', options:['comparing with like','giving human qualities to non-humans','exaggeration','rhyme'], answer:1, explain:'Nature given a mother’s care.'},
{q:'The poem’s tone is:', options:['angry','affectionate and awestruck','mocking','gloomy'], answer:1, explain:'Warm wonder at nature.'},
{q:'In appreciation, you must include:', options:['the poet’s age','quotations','page numbers','your marks'], answer:1, explain:'Quote lines as evidence for devices.'},
{q:'The seasons in the poem act as:', options:['enemies','the mother’s moods/gifts (metaphor)','accidents','clocks'], answer:1, explain:'Metaphorical expressions of nature.'},
{q:'Imagery in the poem appeals to the:', options:['intellect only','senses','grammar','plot'], answer:1, explain:'Vivid pictures of the natural world.'},
{q:'The Islamic parallel in the unit is that nature is:', options:['to be feared','signs (ayat) of the Creator','unimportant','man-made'], answer:1, explain:'Creation reflects divine wisdom.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch6', title:'How to Make Better Decisions About Your Career', urdu:'کیریئر کے بہتر فیصلے', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch6-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>Choosing a career is a <strong>major life decision</strong>: the unit advises <strong>knowing yourself</strong> (interests, strengths), <strong>researching options</strong> (what each field really involves), <strong>seeking guidance</strong> (teachers, mentors, professionals), and <strong>thinking long term</strong> (demand, growth). <strong>Central idea:</strong> decide by <strong>aptitude + interest + opportunity</strong> — not by <strong>peer pressure or trends</strong> alone.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch6-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Career Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Career Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>aptitude</strong> (natural ability), <strong>aspiration</strong>, <strong>mentor</strong>, <strong>prospect</strong>, <strong>vocation</strong>. <strong>Collocations:</strong> “career path”, “job market”, “skill set”. <strong>Formal vs informal:</strong> “I am writing to inquire about…” (formal) vs “I wanna ask…” (informal). <strong>Email writing</strong> (formal) uses this register — exams test it.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch6-l3', title:'Writing: Formal Emails', html:'<h3>Writing: Formal Emails</h3><p><strong>Formal email format:</strong> <strong>subject line</strong> (clear) → <strong>salutation</strong> (Respected Sir/Dear Madam) → <strong>introduction</strong> (purpose) → <strong>body</strong> (details, polite requests) → <strong>closing</strong> (“Looking forward to your reply”) → <strong>sign-off</strong> (“Regards,” + name). <strong>Tone:</strong> polite, concise, no slang or emojis. <strong>Exam task:</strong> email a counsellor asking for career guidance.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The three factors for career choice are:', options:['money, fame, luck','aptitude, interest, opportunity','friends, films, fun','marks only'], answer:1, explain:'Ability + passion + real prospects.'},
{q:'Aptitude means:', options:['grades','natural ability','luck','wealth'], answer:1, explain:'What you are naturally good at.'},
{q:'A mentor is:', options:['a friend','an experienced guide','a boss','a book'], answer:1, explain:'Someone who advises from experience.'},
{q:'A formal email needs a:', options:['joke','clear subject line','emoji','nickname'], answer:1, explain:'The subject states the purpose.'},
{q:'Formal sign-off is:', options:['“Cheers!”','“Regards,” + name','“Bye”','no sign-off'], answer:1, explain:'Polite professional closing.'},
{q:'“I wanna ask” is:', options:['formal','informal','correct email style','polite'], answer:1, explain:'Slang has no place in formal emails.'},
{q:'Researching a career means learning:', options:['its salary only','what the field really involves','its uniform','nothing'], answer:1, explain:'Daily realities, skills needed, prospects.'},
{q:'Deciding only by trends is risky because:', options:['trends pay well','trends fade; fit matters','friends agree','parents agree'], answer:1, explain:'Personal aptitude outlasts fads.'},
{q:'A “skill set” is:', options:['one skill','the range of skills you offer','a toolbox','a certificate'], answer:1, explain:'Your bundle of abilities.'},
{q:'The email body should be:', options:['long and chatty','concise and polite','empty','in slang'], answer:1, explain:'Respect the reader’s time.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch7', title:'The Alchemist (Extract) by Paulo Coelho', urdu:'الکیمسٹ (اقتباس)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch7-l1', title:'Story Summary', html:'<h3>Story Summary</h3><p>The extract follows <strong>Santiago</strong>, an Andalusian <strong>shepherd boy</strong>, who dreams of <strong>treasure near the Egyptian pyramids</strong> and sets out on a journey. Along the way he learns to follow <strong>omens</strong>, listen to his <strong>heart</strong>, and pursue his <strong>“Personal Legend”</strong> (life’s purpose). <strong>Theme:</strong> <strong>follow your dreams</strong>; the journey itself is the real treasure — <strong>“when you want something, the universe conspires to help you.”</strong></p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch7-l2', title:'Symbols and Language', html:'<h3>Symbols and Language</h3><p><strong>Symbols:</strong> the <strong>desert</strong> (life’s trials), <strong>treasure</strong> (one’s dream/purpose), the <strong>sheep</strong> (comfort zone), <strong>omens</strong> (guidance). <strong>Simple, fable-like language</strong> carries deep meaning — <strong>allegory</strong>. <strong>Key quotes:</strong> “Personal Legend”, “the Soul of the World”. <strong>Exam skill:</strong> explain what a symbol <strong>stands for</strong> and how it deepens the theme.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch7-l3', title:'Moral and Personal Response', html:'<h3>Moral and Personal Response</h3><p><strong>Moral:</strong> pursue your purpose with <strong>courage and persistence</strong>; obstacles teach. <strong>Personal response</strong> questions (“What would you do in Santiago’s place?”) need <strong>your view + reasons + text link</strong>. <strong>Structure:</strong> opinion → reason → example from the extract → concluding line. <strong>Islamic parallel:</strong> <strong>tawakkul</strong> (trust in Allah) with <strong>effort</strong> — tie your camel.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Santiago is a:', options:['king','shepherd boy','merchant','soldier'], answer:1, explain:'An Andalusian shepherd who dreams of treasure.'},
{q:'His “Personal Legend” means:', options:['a story','life’s purpose/dream','a lie','fame'], answer:1, explain:'The dream one is meant to pursue.'},
{q:'The treasure is buried near the:', options:['mountains','Egyptian pyramids','sea','forest'], answer:1, explain:'His recurring dream points there.'},
{q:'The desert symbolises:', options:['fun','life’s trials','wealth','home'], answer:1, explain:'Hardships on the journey to one’s dream.'},
{q:'Omens in the story are:', options:['bad luck','signs guiding the traveller','dreams only','people'], answer:1, explain:'He learns to read life’s guidance.'},
{q:'The central message is:', options:['stay home','follow your dreams persistently','dreams are useless','treasure is gold'], answer:1, explain:'The journey itself becomes the treasure.'},
{q:'An allegory is a story with:', options:['no meaning','a hidden deeper meaning','pictures','songs'], answer:1, explain:'Surface tale carrying symbolic truth.'},
{q:'The author is:', options:['Coelho','Paulo Coelho','Hemingway','Dickens'], answer:1, explain:'Brazilian author of The Alchemist.'},
{q:'A personal-response answer needs:', options:['summary only','opinion + reasons + text link','quotes only','no opinion'], answer:1, explain:'Your view, justified and tied to the text.'},
{q:'Tawakkul means:', options:['laziness','trust in Allah with effort','fear','prayer only'], answer:1, explain:'Strive, then trust — tie your camel.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch8', title:'Blue (Poem) by Brian A. Haycock', urdu:'نیلا (نظم)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch8-l1', title:'Poem Summary and Theme', html:'<h3>Poem Summary and Theme</h3><p><strong>“Blue”</strong> uses the <strong>colour blue</strong> as a <strong>central symbol</strong> to explore <strong>human emotions and relationships</strong> — calm, depth, sadness (“feeling blue”), vastness like sky and sea. <strong>Theme:</strong> colours carry <strong>emotional meanings</strong>; poetry looks <strong>beyond literal words</strong> to inner states. The poem teaches <strong>symbolic reading</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch8-l2', title:'Symbols and Poetic Devices', html:'<h3>Symbols and Poetic Devices</h3><p><strong>Symbolism:</strong> blue = calm/depth/melancholy. <strong>Imagery:</strong> sky, sea, eyes — blue things evoking feeling. <strong>Metaphor:</strong> emotions “painted” blue. <strong>Repetition</strong> of the colour builds mood. <strong>Contrast:</strong> blue’s calm vs its sadness. <strong>Exam skill:</strong> explain <strong>what the symbol suggests</strong> and <strong>why the poet chose it</strong> — always with a quoted line.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch8-l3', title:'Stanza Explanation', html:'<h3>Stanza Explanation</h3><p><strong>Method:</strong> 1) <strong>reference</strong> (poem + poet); 2) <strong>paraphrase</strong> the stanza simply; 3) <strong>devices</strong> (symbol, imagery) with quotes; 4) <strong>theme link</strong>. <strong>Sample:</strong> when the poet calls sorrow “blue”, he borrows the sea’s depth to show <strong>how deep sadness feels</strong>. Practise explaining <strong>two stanzas</strong> fully — quality beats quantity.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Blue” is written by:', options:['Dickinson','Brian A. Haycock','Frost','Tennyson'], answer:1, explain:'The poet of this symbolic poem.'},
{q:'In the poem, blue is a:', options:['literal colour only','central symbol of emotions','mistake','rhyme'], answer:1, explain:'It stands for calm, depth, sadness.'},
{q:'“Feeling blue” means feeling:', options:['happy','sad','angry','cold'], answer:1, explain:'The idiom for melancholy.'},
{q:'Symbolism is:', options:['listing colours','using one thing to stand for another','rhyming','counting syllables'], answer:1, explain:'Blue stands for emotional states.'},
{q:'The sea’s blueness suggests:', options:['shallowness','depth and vastness','warmth','noise'], answer:1, explain:'Its depth mirrors deep emotion.'},
{q:'Repetition of “blue” creates:', options:['confusion','mood and emphasis','boredom','rhyme'], answer:1, explain:'It deepens the symbolic atmosphere.'},
{q:'In stanza explanation, step one is:', options:['devices','reference to poem and poet','theme','quotes'], answer:1, explain:'Name the poem and poet first.'},
{q:'A symbol differs from a sign because it:', options:['is simpler','carries deeper suggested meaning','is always blue','has no meaning'], answer:1, explain:'Symbols evoke layers of meaning.'},
{q:'The poem teaches readers to look:', options:['at pictures','beyond literal words','for rhymes','for errors'], answer:1, explain:'Symbolic reading finds inner meaning.'},
{q:'Blue’s two emotional poles in the poem are:', options:['hot and cold','calm and sadness','love and hate','joy and fear'], answer:1, explain:'Peaceful depth vs melancholy.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch9', title:'The Menace of Drugs', urdu:'منشیات کا خطرہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch9-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit exposes drugs as a <strong>menace</strong>: <strong>“ice” (crystal meth), heroin, cannabis, tobacco</strong> destroy <strong>health</strong> (brain damage, organ failure), <strong>families</strong> (broken trust, poverty), <strong>education/careers</strong> and <strong>society</strong> (crime, terrorism funding). Traffickers target <strong>youth</strong>. <strong>Central idea:</strong> <strong>awareness + firm refusal + early help</strong> defeat the menace; a drug-free youth builds a strong Pakistan.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch9-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Formal Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Formal Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>menace</strong> (threat), <strong>trafficking</strong>, <strong>rehabilitation</strong>, <strong>deterrent</strong>, <strong>epidemic</strong>. <strong>Formal register:</strong> “The government <em>must curb</em> trafficking” (not “should stop the bad stuff”). <strong>Essay linkers:</strong> <em>firstly, furthermore, moreover, consequently, in conclusion</em>. <strong>Passive for formality:</strong> “Strict laws <em>are enforced</em>.”</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch9-l3', title:'Writing: Argumentative Essays', html:'<h3>Writing: Argumentative Essays</h3><p><strong>Structure:</strong> <strong>introduction</strong> (the problem + thesis) → <strong>body</strong> (causes, effects, solutions — one per paragraph with <strong>evidence</strong>) → <strong>conclusion</strong> (restate + call to action). <strong>Argue, don’t just list:</strong> “Because drugs fund crime, strict border control is essential.” <strong>Exam essay:</strong> “The Menace of Drugs” — 250–300 words, formal tone, linkers, strong closing appeal.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'“Ice” is the street name for:', options:['heroin','crystal meth','cannabis','tobacco'], answer:1, explain:'A highly addictive stimulant.'},
{q:'Drug trafficking harms society by:', options:['raising taxes','funding crime','building roads','creating jobs'], answer:1, explain:'The drug trade fuels organised crime.'},
{q:'Traffickers mainly target:', options:['elders','youth','babies','officials'], answer:1, explain:'Young people are most vulnerable to pressure.'},
{q:'The formal word for “threat” is:', options:['danger','menace','fear','risk'], answer:1, explain:'Menace = serious threat.'},
{q:'An argumentative essay’s body should:', options:['list points','argue each point with evidence','tell stories','ask questions'], answer:1, explain:'Claims need reasons and proof.'},
{q:'“Strict laws are enforced” is passive because:', options:['it is long','the doer is unimportant; focus is on laws','it is wrong','it is informal'], answer:1, explain:'Formal writing foregrounds the action.'},
{q:'A good essay conclusion has a:', options:['new argument','call to action','joke','question list'], answer:1, explain:'End by urging the reader to act.'},
{q:'Rehabilitation centres:', options:['punish addicts','treat and reintegrate addicts','sell drugs','are prisons'], answer:1, explain:'Medical and psychological recovery.'},
{q:'“Consequently” is a linker showing:', options:['contrast','result','addition','time'], answer:1, explain:'It introduces a consequence.'},
{q:'The unit’s central solution trio is:', options:['ignore, hide, wait','awareness, refusal, early help','punish only','pray only'], answer:1, explain:'Prevention plus timely treatment.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch10', title:'Earth and Environments', urdu:'زمین اور ماحولیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch10-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p>This unit warns that <strong>Earth is our only home</strong>: <strong>pollution</strong> (air, water, plastic), <strong>deforestation</strong>, and <strong>climate change</strong> threaten life. Pakistan faces <strong>smog, water scarcity, floods</strong> (2022 super-floods) and <strong>glacier melt</strong>. <strong>Central idea:</strong> <strong>act now</strong> — reduce, reuse, recycle; plant trees (<strong>Ten Billion Tree Tsunami</strong>); save water and energy. <strong>Stewardship (khilafah)</strong> of Earth is an Islamic duty.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch10-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Environment Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Environment Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>ecosystem</strong>, <strong>biodiversity</strong>, <strong>sustainable</strong>, <strong>emission</strong>, <strong>conservation</strong>. <strong>Word building:</strong> eco- (ecosystem), bio- (biodiversity), re- (recycle, reuse). <strong>Comprehension:</strong> distinguish <strong>causes</strong> (burning fossil fuels) from <strong>effects</strong> (rising temperatures) — exams test <strong>cause–effect chains</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch10-l3', title:'Writing: Speeches', html:'<h3>Writing: Speeches</h3><p><strong>Speech format:</strong> <strong>opening</strong> (“Respected principal, teachers and friends…”) → <strong>introduction</strong> (hook: a startling fact) → <strong>body</strong> (problems, solutions) → <strong>closing appeal</strong> (“Let us pledge…”). <strong>Rhetorical devices:</strong> <strong>repetition</strong> (“We must act — act now”), <strong>rhetorical questions</strong> (“What world will our children inherit?”), <strong>direct address</strong> (“my friends”). <strong>Exam speech:</strong> “Save Our Earth”.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'The unit’s core message is:', options:['Earth is fine','act now to protect the environment','technology will fix all','move planets'], answer:1, explain:'Urgent action on pollution and climate.'},
{q:'Pakistan’s major environmental threats include:', options:['snow','smog, floods, water scarcity','volcanoes','tides'], answer:1, explain:'Smog, glacier melt, floods, scarcity.'},
{q:'“Sustainable” means:', options:['expensive','meeting needs without harming the future','slow','artificial'], answer:1, explain:'Development that future generations can continue.'},
{q:'A cause–effect chain links:', options:['two effects','a cause to its result','two causes','opinions'], answer:1, explain:'Fossil fuels → emissions → warming.'},
{q:'A speech opening addresses:', options:['no one','the audience respectfully','the topic only','the writer'], answer:1, explain:'“Respected principal, teachers and friends…”'},
{q:'A rhetorical question:', options:['needs an answer','makes the audience think (no answer needed)','is rude','ends speeches'], answer:1, explain:'“What world will our children inherit?”'},
{q:'Khilafah in the unit means humans are Earth’s:', options:['owners','stewards/trustees','enemies','visitors'], answer:1, explain:'Islamic duty of care for creation.'},
{q:'The 3 R’s are:', options:['read, write, count','reduce, reuse, recycle','run, rest, repeat','rise, roar, rest'], answer:1, explain:'Waste hierarchy for conservation.'},
{q:'Ten Billion Tree Tsunami is Pakistan’s:', options:['flood','tree-planting drive','dam','festival'], answer:1, explain:'Massive afforestation against climate change.'},
{q:'Deforestation causes:', options:['more rain','soil erosion and habitat loss','cleaner air','cooling'], answer:1, explain:'Trees hold soil and shelter wildlife.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch11', title:'Adventure Sports', urdu:'مہم جوئی کے کھیل', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch11-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p><strong>Adventure sports</strong> — <strong>trekking, paragliding, rafting, rock climbing, skiing</strong> — test <strong>courage, fitness and teamwork</strong>. Pakistan’s <strong>Karakoram and Himalayas</strong> (K2, Nanga Parbat) attract world climbers; <strong>Malam Jabba</strong> offers skiing; the <strong>Indus</strong> offers rafting. <strong>Central idea:</strong> adventure builds <strong>confidence and discipline</strong> — but <strong>safety first</strong> (trained guides, proper gear).</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch11-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Sports and Safety Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Sports and Safety Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>adrenaline</strong>, <strong>terrain</strong>, <strong>summit</strong>, <strong>gear</strong>, <strong>precaution</strong>. <strong>Modal verbs for rules:</strong> <strong>must</strong> (“You <em>must</em> wear a helmet”), <strong>must not</strong> (“You <em>must not</em> climb alone”), <strong>should</strong> (advice). <strong>Instructions</strong> use <strong>imperatives</strong>: “Check your harness.” Safety vocabulary is exam-useful for <strong>notices</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch11-l3', title:'Writing: Notices', html:'<h3>Writing: Notices</h3><p><strong>Notice format:</strong> <strong>title/heading</strong> (NOTICE) → <strong>date</strong> → <strong>body</strong> (what, when, where, who may join, contact) → <strong>signature + designation</strong>. Keep it <strong>brief (50 words)</strong>, boxed, formal. <strong>Example:</strong> school trekking club announces a <strong>Margalla Hills hike</strong> — date, assembly point, gear list, teacher in charge. <strong>Checklist:</strong> all 5 W’s answered.</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Adventure sports build:', options:['laziness','confidence, fitness and discipline','fear','wealth'], answer:1, explain:'Challenge develops character and teamwork.'},
{q:'K2 lies in the:', options:['Himalayas','Karakoram','Hindu Kush','Pamir'], answer:1, explain:'The Karakoram range in Gilgit-Baltistan.'},
{q:'“You must wear a helmet” uses “must” for:', options:['advice','strong obligation/rule','ability','past habit'], answer:1, explain:'Safety rules demand must/must not.'},
{q:'A notice must answer the:', options:['3 R’s','5 W’s (what, when, where, who, contact)','ABC','XYZ'], answer:1, explain:'Complete information in brief form.'},
{q:'The summit is the:', options:['base','top of a mountain','valley','river'], answer:1, explain:'The peak climbers aim for.'},
{q:'“Check your harness” is an:', options:['question','imperative','exclamation','passive'], answer:1, explain:'Instructional command form.'},
{q:'Malam Jabba is known for:', options:['rafting','skiing','paragliding','diving'], answer:1, explain:'Swat’s ski resort.'},
{q:'Safety first in adventure means:', options:['avoiding fun','trained guides and proper gear','going alone','no planning'], answer:1, explain:'Preparation prevents tragedy.'},
{q:'Terrain means:', options:['weather','the physical land surface','a tent','a map'], answer:1, explain:'Mountains, valleys — the ground itself.'},
{q:'A notice is usually about ___ words:', options:['500','50','5','1000'], answer:1, explain:'Brief, boxed, to the point.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch12', title:'Importance of Life Skills', urdu:'زندگی کی مہارتوں کی اہمیت', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch12-l1', title:'Theme and Summary', html:'<h3>Theme and Summary</h3><p><strong>Life skills</strong> — <strong>communication, decision-making, time management, stress management, teamwork, critical thinking</strong> — matter as much as degrees. Employers hire <strong>attitude and skills</strong>, not just marks. <strong>Central idea:</strong> build life skills <strong>deliberately</strong>: speak up in class, manage your timetable, learn to say no, work in teams. <strong>Emotional intelligence</strong> (managing emotions) predicts success.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch12-l2', title:'Vocabulary: Skills Words', html:'<h3>Vocabulary: Skills Words</h3><p>Key words: <strong>resilience</strong> (bouncing back), <strong>empathy</strong>, <strong>assertive</strong>, <strong>procrastination</strong>, <strong>prioritise</strong>. <strong>Synonyms:</strong> resilient ≈ tough; empathy ≈ understanding. <strong>Antonyms:</strong> confidence ≠ timidity. <strong>Idioms:</strong> “think on your feet”, “keep your cool”. <strong>Dialogue writing</strong> on handling peer pressure practises assertiveness.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch12-l3', title:'Grammar: Phrases and Clauses', html:'<h3>Grammar: Phrases and Clauses</h3><p><strong>Phrase:</strong> no subject–verb (“in the morning”). Types: <strong>noun</strong> (“a great leader”), <strong>verb</strong> (“has been working”), <strong>prepositional</strong> (“under pressure”), <strong>adjectival</strong>. <strong>Clause:</strong> has subject–verb — <strong>main</strong> (stands alone) vs <strong>subordinate</strong> (“<em>because he practised</em>” — needs the main clause). <strong>Sentence types:</strong> simple, compound (joined by <em>and/but/so</em>), complex (main + subordinate).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Life skills include:', options:['only degrees','communication, time management, teamwork','marks only','sports only'], answer:1, explain:'Practical abilities for life and work.'},
{q:'Employers value:', options:['marks only','attitude and skills with knowledge','looks','luck'], answer:1, explain:'Skills and character decide hiring.'},
{q:'Resilience means:', options:['giving up','bouncing back from setbacks','resting','avoiding work'], answer:1, explain:'Recovering and continuing after failure.'},
{q:'Empathy is:', options:['sympathy only','understanding others’ feelings','apathy','pride'], answer:1, explain:'Seeing from another’s perspective.'},
{q:'Procrastination means:', options:['planning','delaying tasks needlessly','working fast','prioritising'], answer:1, explain:'Putting off what should be done.'},
{q:'A phrase differs from a clause because it has:', options:['a verb','no subject–verb pair','more words','a full stop'], answer:1, explain:'Phrases lack the subject+verb core.'},
{q:'“Because he practised” is a:', options:['main clause','subordinate clause','phrase','sentence'], answer:1, explain:'It cannot stand alone.'},
{q:'A complex sentence has:', options:['two mains','a main + a subordinate clause','no verb','two phrases'], answer:1, explain:'One independent + dependent clause(s).'},
{q:'“Keep your cool” means:', options:['stay cold','stay calm under pressure','drink water','leave'], answer:1, explain:'Idiom for composure.'},
{q:'Being assertive means:', options:['aggressive','expressing yourself firmly and respectfully','silent','rude'], answer:1, explain:'Firm yet respectful communication.'}
]},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch13', title:'The Oyster and the Pearl (Play) by William Saroyan', urdu:'سیپی اور موتی (ڈرامہ)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch13-l1', title:'Play Summary', html:'<h3>Play Summary</h3><p>In Saroyan’s one-act play, <strong>Clay</strong>, a poor barber, finds a <strong>pearl in an oyster</strong> — worth a fortune. <strong>Harry Van Dusen</strong>, a wealthy man, tries to <strong>buy it cheaply</strong> through tricks, but Clay, guided by his <strong>honest friend</strong> and his own <strong>integrity</strong>, refuses to be cheated. <strong>Theme:</strong> <strong>honesty over greed</strong>; true wealth is <strong>character</strong>, not money. The oyster’s hidden pearl symbolises <strong>unexpected fortune testing one’s values</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch13-l2', title:'Drama Terms and Characters', html:'<h3>Drama Terms and Characters</h3><p><strong>Drama terms:</strong> <strong>dialogue</strong> (characters’ speech), <strong>stage directions</strong> (in brackets — actions/settings), <strong>act/scene</strong>, <strong>protagonist</strong> (Clay), <strong>antagonist</strong> (Van Dusen’s greed). <strong>Characters:</strong> <strong>Clay</strong> — poor but <strong>honest and dignified</strong>; <strong>Van Dusen</strong> — rich but <strong>greedy and cunning</strong>; the <strong>friend</strong> — loyal adviser. <strong>Contrast</strong> between Clay’s integrity and Van Dusen’s greed drives the play.</p>'},
{id:'fed-c10-eng-ch13-l3', title:'Theme, Moral and Review', html:'<h3>Theme, Moral and Review</h3><p><strong>Moral:</strong> <strong>integrity is priceless</strong> — no fortune is worth selling one’s honesty. <strong>Symbol:</strong> the pearl = fortune testing character. <strong>Exam questions:</strong> Why does Clay refuse the money? (His <strong>self-respect</strong> outweighs greed.) What does the play teach about wealth? (Character > cash.) <strong>Play review:</strong> plot → characters → theme → <strong>your evaluation</strong> (“I admired Clay because…”).</p>'}
], quiz:[
{q:'Clay finds the pearl in:', options:['a shop','an oyster','a river','a box'], answer:1, explain:'The unexpected find drives the plot.'},
{q:'Harry Van Dusen wants to:', options:['help Clay','buy the pearl cheaply by trickery','admire it','donate it'], answer:1, explain:'His greed is the play’s conflict.'},
{q:'The play’s theme is:', options:['money is everything','honesty over greed','poverty is shameful','tricks win'], answer:1, explain:'Integrity proves priceless.'},
{q:'Stage directions appear:', options:['as dialogue','in brackets','as titles','nowhere'], answer:1, explain:'They guide actors’ movements and setting.'},
{q:'The protagonist is:', options:['Van Dusen','Clay','the friend','the pearl'], answer:1, explain:'The central honest character.'},
{q:'The pearl symbolises:', options:['food','fortune testing one’s values','the sea','a gift'], answer:1, explain:'Unexpected wealth tests character.'},
{q:'Clay refuses the money because:', options:['it is little','his self-respect outweighs greed','he is rich','he is afraid'], answer:1, explain:'Dignity matters more than cash to him.'},
{q:'A play review includes:', options:['only the plot','plot, characters, theme and evaluation','actors’ names only','ticket price'], answer:1, explain:'Summarise, analyse, then judge.'},
{q:'Van Dusen’s character shows:', options:['generosity','greed and cunning','honesty','wisdom'], answer:1, explain:'He schemes to cheat Clay.'},
{q:'The moral of the play is:', options:['get rich quick','integrity is priceless','trust strangers','avoid oysters'], answer:1, explain:'Character outlasts fortune.'}
]}
]});

